window.AKT_UNIT({"unit":"Respiratory health","slug":"respiratory","notes":[{"id":"rs-asthma-dx","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Asthma: making the diagnosis","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Asthma needs three things: variable symptoms, no better explanation, and objective proof of variable airflow or airway inflammation.","Proof on spirometry: FEV1 rises by at least 12% and at least 200 mL after a bronchodilator.","Test before starting a preventer where possible. Do not delay treatment that is needed now.","Normal spirometry in a person who is well does not exclude asthma. Normal spirometry while symptomatic points elsewhere.","Work-related symptoms are referred straight away."],"curriculum":["Diagnose asthma in adolescents and adults using history, examination and objective testing.","Perform and interpret spirometry, including bronchodilator responsiveness.","Recognise conditions that mimic asthma and know when to refer."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Asthma diagnosis: children 6 years and older, adolescents and adults. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"criteria","h":"What makes the diagnosis","body":[{"p":"No single symptom or test proves asthma. The diagnosis is made when all three of the following are present, and the basis is written in the record. [1,2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Requirement","What counts"],"r":[["Variable symptoms","Wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness or cough that comes and goes or varies in intensity"],["No better explanation","Other causes are unlikely after history and examination"],["Objective evidence","Variable expiratory airflow limitation on testing, or a raised exhaled nitric oxide (FeNO)"]]}},{"ul":["Wheeze and breathlessness are the symptoms most reliably linked to asthma. [1]","The examination is usually normal, so a normal chest does not exclude asthma. [1]","Evidence documented in the past counts. Variable airflow can be hard to show once a person is on an inhaled corticosteroid. [1]"]},{"key":"Symptoms suggest asthma. Only a test confirms it."}]},{"id":"history","h":"History that raises or lowers the odds","body":[{"tb":{"h":["More likely asthma","Less likely asthma"],"r":[["More than one of wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough","Chronic productive cough with no wheeze or breathlessness"],["Worse at night or in the early morning","Normal spirometry or a clear chest while symptomatic"],["Triggered by exercise, cold air or allergens","Voice change or throat tightness; worse with talking or laughing"],["Follows aspirin, NSAIDs or beta blockers","Dizziness, light-headedness or tingling fingers"],["Personal or family history of atopy","Symptoms only with colds, and none between"],["Widespread wheeze; unexplained blood eosinophilia","More than 20 pack-years of smoking; no response to a treatment trial"]]}},{"ul":["Pack-years: years smoked multiplied by cigarettes per day, divided by 20. [2]","Ask about home and work exposures, smoking, vaping and cannabis. [1]","Adolescents: see the young person alone for part of the visit to ask about smoking and vaping. [1]","Recurrent 'bronchitis' or a cough that drags on after infections should raise suspicion. [1]"]}]},{"id":"spirometry","h":"Spirometry with bronchodilator","body":[{"ul":["Who: every person aged 6 or over with suspected asthma. [2]","When: before a regular preventer is started where possible. Treatment makes the diagnosis harder to confirm later. [1,2]","How: FEV1 before, then 10 to 15 minutes after salbutamol 200 to 400 micrograms, with at least three blows each time. [1,2]","No spirometer in the practice: refer to an accredited laboratory. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Finding","Meaning"],"r":[["FEV1 up by 12% or more and 200 mL or more","Positive bronchodilator response: supports asthma"],["FEV1 up by more than 400 mL","Strongly supports asthma"],["Reduced FEV1/FVC ratio","Expiratory airflow limitation"],["Low FEV1 alone","Non-specific"],["Normal result while well","Does not exclude asthma: repeat when symptomatic"],["Normal result while symptomatic","Consider another diagnosis"]]}},{"ul":["Significant symptoms or exacerbations: start treatment now and test as soon as possible. [1,2]","New-onset symptoms in an acute presentation: treat as acute asthma, then investigate after recovery. [1]","Seasonal symptoms: test during the pollen season, and consider the risk of thunderstorm asthma. [2]"]},{"key":"Both parts are needed: 12% and 200 mL."}]},{"id":"next","h":"When the first test does not settle it","body":[{"p":"If asthma is still suspected after a non-diagnostic first test, the options are stepped. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Step","Detail"],"r":[["Repeat spirometry","On another day, ideally while symptomatic or in the early morning"],["FeNO","Measures airway inflammation. In adults, 40 ppb or more supports asthma when the picture fits. A normal result does not exclude it"],["Peak flow diary","Use if spirometry is delayed. Morning and about 4 pm for 2 to 4 weeks, best of three. Average daily variation over 10% supports asthma"],["Bronchial provocation","Accredited laboratory only. Not required for diagnosis"],["Specialist referral","If the diagnosis remains uncertain and symptoms persist"]]}},{"ul":["Daily peak flow variation: the day's highest minus lowest, divided by the mean of the two. [1]","Always use the same peak flow meter. A peak flow meter does not replace spirometry. [2]","Bronchial provocation is contraindicated in pregnancy and when FEV1 is under 60% of predicted. [1]","Before provocation testing, withhold salbutamol for 4 hours and formoterol or salmeterol for 24 hours. [1]"]}]},{"id":"trial","h":"Treatment trial","body":[{"ul":["Use: when a person cannot perform spirometry, or suspicion is strong and spirometry is normal. [2]","Method: a reliever alone, or with a regular low-dose inhaled corticosteroid. [2]","Supports asthma: a clear improvement in symptoms or lung function after at least 4 weeks of inhaled corticosteroid. [2]","No improvement after 4 weeks: consider another diagnosis. [2]","FEV1 rising by 200 mL and 12% after 4 weeks of inhaled corticosteroid also counts as objective evidence. [2]"]},{"key":"A treatment trial is a fallback, and it is judged at 4 weeks."}]},{"id":"nottests","h":"Tests that do not make the diagnosis","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Test","Role"],"r":[["Chest X-ray or CT","Not needed for diagnosis. Used for unusual features or to look for another cause"],["Blood eosinophil count","Not a primary diagnostic test. May support asthma in a symptomatic acute presentation"],["Allergy testing","Does not confirm or exclude asthma. Guides trigger management only"],["Peak flow meter alone","Cannot show reversibility or a reduced ratio"]]}}]},{"id":"mimics","h":"Mimics and special groups","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Clue","Think of"],"r":[["Throat tightness, voice change, inspiratory noise","Inducible laryngeal obstruction (vocal cord dysfunction)"],["Tingling, light-headedness, sighing","Dysfunctional breathing or anxiety"],["Over 20 pack-years, fixed obstruction","COPD, alone or with asthma"],["Dry cough on an ACE inhibitor","Drug-induced cough"],["Daily wet cough","Bronchiectasis or chronic bronchitis"],["Orthopnoea, oedema, crackles","Heart failure"],["Unilateral wheeze in a child","Inhaled foreign body"]]}},{"ul":["Work-related symptoms: refer immediately. Occupational asthma can cause rapid, permanent loss of lung function. [1]","Reaction to aspirin or NSAIDs: consider aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease and refer. [1]","Older adults: asthma can start at any age and may present as cough alone. [1]","Asthma with COPD features: an inhaled corticosteroid is needed. Long-acting bronchodilators alone carry higher risk. [1]","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander adults in remote areas: consider bronchiectasis, which needs high-resolution CT. [1]","Competitive athletes: refer, because sporting bodies set their own testing requirements. [1]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"p":"The Therapeutic Guidelines respiratory topics date from 2020 and are under review. This page follows the 2025 Australian Asthma Handbook where the two differ."},{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["FeNO","Recommended with spirometry; 40 ppb or more supports asthma in adults","A specialist test, not readily available in primary care"],["Peak flow diary","2 to 4 weeks; average daily variation over 10%","14 days; over 20% between the two highest and two lowest readings"]]}}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A young adult with episodic wheeze and normal spirometry on a well day: what next.","Reading a bronchodilator response: is 9% and 250 mL positive.","A person already started on a preventer elsewhere with no objective test on record.","New wheeze in a baker or spray painter.","Throat tightness and normal spirometry during symptoms.","Choosing between chest X-ray, allergy tests and spirometry as the first test."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-lungfn","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Spirometry, oximetry and blood gases","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Obstruction is a low FEV1/FVC ratio. Restriction is a low FVC with a normal or high ratio.","Spirometry is deferred for a week after a heart attack or eye, sinus or middle ear surgery, and for 4 weeks after chest or abdominal surgery.","A pulse oximeter says nothing about carbon dioxide, and reads falsely high in carbon monoxide poisoning.","An abnormal venous gas is followed by an arterial gas."],"curriculum":["Perform and interpret spirometry.","Know the uses and limits of peak flow, pulse oximetry and blood gas analysis.","Know which lung function tests need a respiratory laboratory."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Pulmonary function testing. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"measures","h":"What spirometry measures","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Measure","Meaning"],"r":[["FEV1","Volume breathed out in the first second of a forced breath"],["FVC","Total volume breathed out in a forced breath"],["FEV1/FVC ratio","FEV1 as a fraction of FVC, for example 0.7 or 70%"]]}},{"ul":["The manoeuvre: breathe in fully, then blow out as hard and as long as possible. [1]","Results are compared with reference values matched for age, sex, height and ethnicity. [1]","Spirometry can be done and read in general practice. Operator training is recommended first. [1]","Quality depends on maintained equipment, operator technique and patient effort with a good lip seal. [1]","Not every child can perform it reliably, and unwell patients may not manage the effort. [1]"]}]},{"id":"patterns","h":"Reading the pattern","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Pattern","Spirometry","Flow-volume loop","Examples"],"r":[["Obstructive","Low FEV1/FVC ratio","Concave (scooped) expiratory limb","Asthma, COPD, asthma with COPD"],["Restrictive","Low FVC; ratio normal or raised","Normal shape, small volume","Interstitial lung disease, respiratory muscle weakness, kyphoscoliosis, obesity"],["Obstruction with low FVC","Low ratio and low FVC","Scooped and small","Gas trapping, or mixed disease"]]}},{"ul":["Reversible obstruction: the loop returns almost to normal after a bronchodilator. [1]","Age changes the normal loop, so results are read against age-matched values. [1]","Suspected restriction is assessed further with static lung volumes in a laboratory. [1]"]},{"key":"Read the ratio first. Low ratio means obstruction. Normal ratio with a low FVC suggests restriction."}]},{"id":"contra","h":"When to defer spirometry","body":[{"p":"These are relative contraindications. Weigh risk against benefit. A respiratory laboratory can test higher-risk patients, and the referral should name the concern. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Timeframe","Condition"],"r":[["Previous week","Heart attack; eye, sinus or middle ear surgery; middle ear infection"],["Previous 4 weeks","Chest or abdominal surgery"],["Current or recent","Pneumothorax; haemoptysis; concussion with ongoing symptoms"],["Any time","Past fainting with forced breathing out or coughing"]]}},{"ul":["Current respiratory infection: apply infection control, such as filters and single-use mouthpieces. [1]"]}]},{"id":"pef","h":"Peak flow","body":[{"ul":["What it is: the fastest flow during a forced breath out, in litres per minute. [1]","Use: tracking variable obstruction and response to treatment in asthma. [1]","Same meter every time: readings differ a lot between meters. [1]","Not for diagnosis in place of spirometry: it cannot show reversibility or a reduced ratio. [1]","Not useful in COPD, where the obstruction does not vary. [1]","Unreliable in children. [1]"]},{"key":"Peak flow monitors known asthma. It does not diagnose it, and it has no role in COPD."}]},{"id":"oximetry","h":"Pulse oximetry and its traps","body":[{"p":"A pulse oximeter estimates haemoglobin oxygen saturation. It does not measure ventilation or carbon dioxide. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Situation","Effect on the reading"],"r":[["Carbon monoxide exposure, heavy smoking","Falsely high"],["Methaemoglobinaemia (dapsone, benzocaine, lidocaine, nitrates, some antimalarials)","Falsely high or low"],["Poor perfusion, cold hands, low cardiac output","Unreliable"],["Nail polish","Unreliable: turn the probe side to side on the finger"],["Dark skin","Less accurate"],["Severe anaemia, cyanide poisoning","Reading is correct but falsely reassuring: oxygen delivery is poor"]]}},{"key":"A normal saturation does not exclude carbon dioxide retention or carbon monoxide poisoning."}]},{"id":"gases","h":"Blood gases","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Measure","Normal arterial value"],"r":[["pH","7.35 to 7.45"],["PaO2","80 to 100 mmHg (varies with age and altitude)"],["PaCO2","35 to 45 mmHg"],["Bicarbonate","22 to 26 mmol/L"],["Base excess","-2 to +2 mmol/L"]]}},{"ul":["Arterial gas uses: severe acute illness in hospital, chronic respiratory failure, and assessment for home oxygen. [1]","Venous gas: values do not track arterial values directly, and its usefulness is debated. [1]","Venous gas alone is not enough to assess respiratory failure. If it is abnormal, take an arterial sample. [1]"]}]},{"id":"lab","h":"Tests that need a respiratory laboratory","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Test","What it shows","Typical use"],"r":[["Gas transfer (DLCO)","How well gas crosses from air sacs to blood","Unexplained breathlessness; baseline before amiodarone, methotrexate or leflunomide"],["Static lung volumes","Total lung capacity and residual volume","Confirming restriction or hyperinflation; before lung resection"],["Bronchial provocation","Airway hyperresponsiveness","Uncertain asthma; some pre-employment checks"],["Exercise testing","Cause and degree of exercise limitation","Unexplained breathlessness; fitness for surgery"],["Hypoxic challenge","Oxygen level in simulated altitude","Fitness to fly"]]}},{"ul":["Beta agonists, antimuscarinics and antihistamines interfere with provocation tests. Ask the laboratory what to withhold. [1]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A printed spirometry result: obstructive, restrictive or normal.","A request for spirometry five days after cataract surgery or two weeks after a laparoscopy.","A house-fire survivor with a headache and a saturation of 99%.","A drowsy patient with COPD and a normal saturation on oxygen.","Which test to order before starting amiodarone or methotrexate.","A patient with COPD asking for a peak flow meter."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-asthma-adult","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Asthma in adults and adolescents: choosing and adjusting treatment","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Every person aged 12 or over with asthma gets an inhaled corticosteroid. A blue reliever alone is no longer acceptable.","Most start on low-dose budesonide-formoterol taken only when needed.","Next step up is the same inhaler used every day and for relief (MART).","Check adherence, technique, triggers and the diagnosis before any step up.","Still uncontrolled on a medium-dose regimen: check blood eosinophils and refer."],"curriculum":["Start and adjust asthma treatment in adolescents and adults in line with current Australian guidelines.","Explain anti-inflammatory reliever and maintenance-and-reliever regimens.","Recognise difficult-to-treat asthma and refer appropriately."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Treatment levels; Initial asthma treatment after diagnosis; Adjusting treatment (adults and adolescents); tables of approved reliever and maintenance-and-reliever products and of inhaled corticosteroid doses.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"principles","h":"The 2025 change","body":[{"ul":["Inhaled corticosteroid for all: every treatment level for people aged 12 and over contains one. [1]","No reliever-only treatment: salbutamol or terbutaline alone is not recommended, even for infrequent symptoms. [1]","Why: reliever-only treatment carries a higher risk of severe flare-ups than as-needed budesonide-formoterol. [1]","Never a long-acting beta agonist without an inhaled corticosteroid. [1]","Three or more salbutamol canisters a year signals higher flare-up risk. Twelve or more signals higher risk of asthma death. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Term","Meaning"],"r":[["AIR (anti-inflammatory reliever)","A corticosteroid-formoterol inhaler taken only when symptoms occur"],["MART (maintenance and reliever therapy)","The same corticosteroid-formoterol inhaler taken every day and also for relief"],["Reliever-only treatment","Salbutamol or terbutaline with no corticosteroid: not recommended"]]}},{"key":"The blue puffer alone is out. The reliever of choice now carries a corticosteroid."}]},{"id":"levels","h":"Treatment levels","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Level","Recommended","Alternative"],"r":[["1","Low-dose budesonide-formoterol as needed","Daily low-dose corticosteroid plus salbutamol as needed"],["2","Low-dose MART","Daily low-dose corticosteroid-LABA plus salbutamol as needed"],["3","Medium-dose MART","Daily medium-dose corticosteroid-LABA plus salbutamol as needed"],["4","Specialist-directed: corticosteroid-LABA with add-ons such as a LAMA or a monoclonal antibody",""]]}},{"ul":["Medium-dose MART: more daily maintenance puffs of the low-dose inhaler. The reliever dose stays low. [1]","Only budesonide-formoterol is approved for as-needed use without maintenance. [1]","Only certain budesonide-formoterol and beclometasone-formoterol inhalers can be used as MART. Other combinations cannot. [1]","Daily low-dose corticosteroid alone is often poorly adhered to when symptoms are infrequent, which is why it is the alternative. [1]"]}]},{"id":"start","h":"Starting treatment","body":[{"tb":{"h":["At diagnosis","Start with"],"r":[["Most people","Low-dose budesonide-formoterol as needed"],["Frequent or severe symptoms","Low-dose or medium-dose MART"],["A flare-up needing oral corticosteroids in the past 12 months","MART"],["Any past life-threatening flare-up","MART"],["Diagnosed during a severe acute episode","MART, even if an oral corticosteroid course is given"]]}},{"ul":["Carry the reliever at all times. [1]","Before exercise: one dose of the usual reliever 15 minutes beforehand. No separate salbutamol inhaler is added. [1]","PBS: MART is not subsidised as the first treatment after diagnosis. Check restrictions. [1]","Spacer, not nebuliser: a pressurised inhaler with a spacer works as well and faster. [1]","Give a written action plan that matches the regimen, and check inhaler technique. [1]","Review within 2 to 3 months of starting. [1]"]},{"key":"Default start: budesonide-formoterol as needed. Start higher if symptoms are frequent or there has been a serious flare-up."}]},{"id":"doses","h":"Doses and daily limits","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Inhaler","As-needed dose","Low-dose MART maintenance","Maximum"],"r":[["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 dry powder","1 inhalation","2 a day, in one or two doses","6 at a time; 8 a day (12 briefly)"],["Budesonide-formoterol 100/3 pressurised","2 inhalations","2 twice a day","12 at a time; 16 a day (24 briefly)"],["Beclometasone-formoterol 100/6 pressurised (MART only, adults)","1 inhalation","1 twice a day","6 at a time; 8 a day"]]}},{"ul":["Medium-dose MART: 200/6 dry powder 2 twice a day, or 100/3 pressurised 4 twice a day. [1]","The daily maximum is rarely needed. Worsening over 3 days despite extra doses needs medical review. [1]","Most products are approved from age 12. Some are approved from 18 only. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Inhaled corticosteroid","Low daily dose (micrograms)","Medium","High"],"r":[["Beclometasone (extra-fine)","100 to 200","250 to 400","over 400"],["Budesonide","200 to 400","500 to 800","over 800"],["Ciclesonide","80 to 160","240 to 320","over 320"],["Fluticasone propionate","100 to 200","250 to 500","over 500"]]}},{"p":"Most of the benefit of an inhaled corticosteroid comes at low dose. Long-term high doses add risk, including cataract and pneumonia. [1]"}]},{"id":"stepup","h":"Before and during a step up","body":[{"p":"Poor control is not stepped up until four things are checked. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Check","What to look for"],"r":[["Adherence","Is the preventer actually being taken? Dispensing records help"],["Technique","Watch the person use the inhaler"],["Triggers","Smoke, allergens, viral infections, work exposures"],["Diagnosis and comorbidity","Rhinitis, obesity, deconditioning, heart disease, laryngeal obstruction"]]}},{"ul":["Move one level at a time. [1]","On a fixed corticosteroid-LABA with salbutamol: switch to MART before raising the dose. [1]","On as-needed budesonide-formoterol and needing it 3 or more times a week: add daily maintenance (low-dose MART). [1]","A LAMA add-on helps most when lung function is reduced. Spirometry guides it. [1]"]}]},{"id":"refer","h":"When to refer","body":[{"ul":["Uncontrolled on medium-dose MART or medium-dose corticosteroid-LABA, with good adherence and technique: refer. [1]","Order a blood eosinophil count while waiting. Add laboratory spirometry if the wait is long. [1]","Eosinophils 150 per microlitre or more on medium-dose treatment suggest persistent type 2 inflammation, which may suit a monoclonal antibody. [1]","Eosinophils 1,500 per microlitre or more need urgent specialist assessment for other disease. [1]","Level 4 treatment is specialist-directed. [1]"]},{"key":"Medium-dose regimen, taken properly, still uncontrolled: eosinophils and referral, not a higher steroid dose."}]},{"id":"stepdown","h":"Stepping down","body":[{"ul":["When: good control for 2 to 3 months and low flare-up risk. [1]","How: down one level, then review in 4 to 8 weeks. [1]","Not now: during a respiratory infection, allergen season, travel or pregnancy. [1]","Floor: never stop the inhaled corticosteroid entirely. The lowest treatment is as-needed budesonide-formoterol. [1]","Low-dose MART steps down to as-needed use with the same inhaler. [1]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"p":"This page follows the 2025 Australian Asthma Handbook. The Therapeutic Guidelines asthma topics date from 2020 and are under review."},{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["Reliever alone","Not recommended for anyone aged 12 or over","Accepted as one option for symptoms less than twice a month"],["First treatment","As-needed budesonide-formoterol","As-needed reliever, or daily low-dose corticosteroid, or as-needed budesonide-formoterol"],["Structure","Four levels, each with a MART or AIR option first","Five steps"]]}}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A newly diagnosed adult with symptoms twice a month: what to prescribe.","A patient who has used salbutamol alone for years and is 'fine'.","Poor control on low-dose fluticasone-salmeterol: the step before a higher dose.","Uncontrolled on medium-dose MART with good technique.","How many extra puffs are allowed in a day.","A request to stop the preventer after three good months, or during pregnancy."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-asthma-review","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Asthma in adults and adolescents: review, action plans and pregnancy","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Good control over 4 weeks: symptoms and reliever on 2 days a week or fewer, no night waking, no limits on activity.","Any one of those failing is poor control.","Everyone has a written action plan that says when to add oral corticosteroids and when to call an ambulance.","Flare-up not settling: prednisolone 37.5 to 50 mg each morning for 5 to 10 days, no taper.","Pregnancy: keep treating, step up if needed, do not step down."],"curriculum":["Assess asthma control and future risk at review.","Write and update an asthma action plan.","Manage asthma in pregnancy and address comorbidities and triggers."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Assessing and reviewing asthma in adults and adolescents; Adjusting treatment for adults and adolescents.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"control","h":"Is it controlled?","body":[{"p":"Control is judged over the previous 4 weeks. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Measure","Good control","Poor control (any one)"],"r":[["Daytime symptoms","2 days a week or fewer","More than 2 days a week"],["Reliever use (not counting before exercise)","2 days a week or fewer","More than 2 days a week"],["Night or waking symptoms","None","Any"],["Activity limitation","None","Any"]]}},{"ul":["Also ask about days off work or school, use of the action plan, and urgent visits. [1]","Reliever needed on more than 2 days a week: review soon, not at the next routine visit. [1]","Validated tools: the Asthma Control Test and the Asthma Control Questionnaire. [1]","Adolescents under-report. Ask about avoided sport, vaping and missed school. [1]"]},{"key":"Any night waking or any activity limit means poor control."}]},{"id":"risk","h":"Who is at risk of a flare-up","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Group","Risk factors"],"r":[["History","Poor symptom control; a severe flare-up in the past year; any past intensive care admission or intubation"],["Medicines","Reliever overuse; no corticosteroid; poor adherence or technique"],["Comorbidity","Obesity, chronic rhinosinusitis, reflux, confirmed food allergy, pregnancy"],["Exposure","Smoking, vaping, allergens, air pollution"],["Tests","Low FEV1; large bronchodilator response; raised eosinophils or FeNO"]]}},{"ul":["The two strongest predictors are poor symptom control and a severe flare-up in the past year. [1]","Food allergy rarely triggers asthma, but a confirmed food allergy is a risk factor for asthma death. [2]"]}]},{"id":"review","h":"The review visit","body":[{"ul":["How often: at least once a year, and 2 to 3 months after any new treatment. [1]","Every review: control, flare-ups, triggers, adherence, inhaler technique, side effects, comorbidities, and the action plan. [1]","Spirometry: at diagnosis, when control is poor, and about every 2 years even when well. [1]","Monitoring spirometry does not need bronchodilators withheld. Diagnostic spirometry does. [1]","Clinic peak flow is not used to assess lung function. [1]","Mismatch between symptoms and spirometry: reconsider the diagnosis or refer. [1]","Smoking history in everyone. Consider coexisting COPD in current and former smokers. [1]"]}]},{"id":"plan","h":"Written action plans","body":[{"p":"Every person with asthma has a written plan matched to their regimen. It is reviewed yearly and whenever treatment changes. [1,2]"},{"tb":{"h":["The plan states","Detail"],"r":[["Usual treatment","Maintenance and reliever doses"],["Reliever limits","Maximum per occasion and per 24 hours for that inhaler"],["Signs of worsening","What the person will notice"],["Oral corticosteroids","When to start a course"],["Help","When to see a doctor and when to call an ambulance"]]}},{"ul":["Symptom-based plans suit most people. [2]","Peak-flow plans: for poor perceivers, over-perceivers and people with sudden severe attacks. Use personal best, not predicted. [2]","On budesonide-formoterol: extra doses raise the corticosteroid automatically. Add prednisolone if that fails. [2]","On corticosteroid alone: quadruple the dose for 1 to 2 weeks. Doubling is not enough. [2]","On a fixed corticosteroid-LABA: a prednisolone course is usually more practical than adding a second inhaler. [2]"]}]},{"id":"ocs","h":"Oral corticosteroids for a flare-up","body":[{"ul":["When: symptoms return within 4 hours of the reliever, or do not settle promptly. [1]","Adults: prednisolone 37.5 to 50 mg each morning for 5 to 10 days. [1,2]","Adolescents: 1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) once daily for 3 days. [1]","No taper is needed after a short course. [1]","Prescribe the exact quantity with no repeats, and track how often courses are needed. [1]","Repeated courses carry long-term harm: osteoporosis, diabetes, cataract, weight gain. [1]"]},{"key":"A short course stops without a taper. Repeated courses are a reason to step up or refer."}]},{"id":"pregnancy","h":"Pregnancy","body":[{"ul":["Manage as for any adult, aiming for the best possible control. [1]","Step up when needed. Do not step down during pregnancy. [1]","Do not stop the inhaled corticosteroid. [1]","About 40% of pregnant women with asthma get worse, and at least 20% have a flare-up needing medical care. [1]","Flare-ups are linked to low birth weight, preterm birth and small-for-dates babies. [1]","Assess control at antenatal visits. [1]"]},{"key":"Uncontrolled asthma is the risk to the baby. The inhalers are not."}]},{"id":"general","h":"Comorbidities, triggers and lifestyle","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Issue","What to do"],"r":[["Smoking and vaping","Always address. Cigarette smoke is the one trigger to avoid completely"],["Obesity","Weight loss of 5 to 10% can improve control. Confirm asthma objectively before escalating"],["Rhinitis","Present in most people with allergic asthma. Treating it helps control"],["Reflux","Common, but treating it does not reliably improve asthma"],["Anxiety and depression","Screen. Both worsen control and adherence"],["Beta blockers","If one becomes indicated, start low under supervision"],["NSAIDs","Avoid in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease"]]}},{"ul":["Exercise is encouraged. Asthma is not a reason to avoid it. [2]","Breathlessness that worsens in the minutes after stopping exercise suggests asthma rather than poor fitness. [2]","Vitamin D and other supplements have no proven benefit. [2]","Hoarseness and thrush: use a spacer, and rinse and spit after maintenance doses. [1]","Montelukast: an alternative that is less effective than an inhaled corticosteroid. Warn about mood and behaviour change, and stop it if they occur. [2]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["Adolescent oral corticosteroid course","1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) for 3 days","Not read"],["Raising inhaled corticosteroid in a plan","Not read on the pages reviewed","Quadruple for 1 to 2 weeks if on corticosteroid alone"]]}},{"p":"The two sources agree on the adult prednisolone dose and duration."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["Classifying control from a 4-week history that includes one night waking.","What an action plan must contain.","A flare-up on budesonide-formoterol that has not settled after extra doses.","A pregnant woman who has stopped her preventer.","A request for a 'standby' prednisolone script with repeats.","A patient with asthma who now needs a beta blocker after a heart attack."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-asthma-acute","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Acute asthma in adults and adolescents","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Sort by speech and saturation: whole sentences and over 94% is mild to moderate.","Salbutamol through a spacer: 4 to 12 puffs if mild to moderate, 12 puffs if severe, every 20 minutes for the first hour.","Cannot finish a sentence, or saturation 94% or less: call an ambulance while treating.","Oral corticosteroids for every adult who presents, started within the hour.","Do not send home if reliever is still needed more often than every 4 hours. Review in 3 to 5 days."],"curriculum":["Assess and manage an acute asthma exacerbation in the community, including first aid.","Recognise severe and life-threatening asthma and arrange transfer.","Arrange post-acute care and follow-up, and advise people at risk of thunderstorm asthma."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care, with tables of immediate treatment and secondary severity assessment; Thunderstorm asthma.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Acute asthma. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"firstaid","h":"First aid in the community","body":[{"p":"The 4 x 4 x 4 plan is the standard community first aid for an asthma attack. [2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Step","Action"],"r":[["1","Give 4 separate puffs of salbutamol through a spacer, with 4 breaths after each puff"],["2","Wait 4 minutes. If no better, give 4 more puffs"],["3","Still no better: call an ambulance and say it is a severe asthma attack"],["4","Keep giving 4 puffs every 4 minutes until help arrives (up to 8 puffs at a time for an adult)"]]}},{"ul":["No salbutamol to hand, age 12 or over: budesonide-formoterol 2 inhalations, then 1 more every 4 minutes, to 6 in total. [2]","Reliever needed more often than every 3 to 4 hours after settling: same-day medical review. [2]"]}]},{"id":"severity","h":"Rapid severity assessment","body":[{"p":"Assess by looking, listening to speech and measuring saturation on room air. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Features","Where"],"r":[["Mild to moderate (all of)","Can walk; whole sentences in one breath; saturation over 94%","Manage in the practice"],["Severe (any of)","Cannot finish a sentence; accessory muscles, tracheal tug or recession; obvious distress; saturation 94% or less","Ambulance; treat while waiting"],["Life-threatening (any of)","Drowsy, collapsed or exhausted; cyanosis; poor effort; soft or absent breath sounds; saturation under 92%","Ambulance; treat while waiting"]]}},{"tb":{"h":["Sign on fuller assessment","Mild to moderate","Severe","Life-threatening"],"r":[["Respiratory rate","Under 25","25 or more","Slow (exhaustion)"],["Heart rate","Under 110","110 or more","Arrhythmia or bradycardia"],["Chest","Wheeze or normal","","Silent chest"],["Posture","Can lie flat","Cannot lie flat; hunched forward","Collapsed"]]}},{"key":"A quiet chest, a slowing pulse or a slowing breathing rate in a tiring patient is a sign of impending arrest, not recovery."}]},{"id":"treat","h":"Immediate treatment","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Salbutamol 100 microgram puffs through a spacer","Repeat"],"r":[["Mild to moderate","4 to 12 puffs","Every 20 to 30 minutes in the first hour if needed"],["Severe","12 puffs","At least every 20 minutes for the first hour"],["Life-threatening","12 puffs, or 2 x 5 mg nebules continuously, driven by oxygen","Continuous until help arrives"]]}},{"ul":["Technique: one puff at a time into the spacer, with normal (tidal) breathing. [1]","Nebuliser: only if the person cannot use a spacer. Use 5 mg for a severe attack. [1]","Nebulisers generate aerosols: apply infection control if a viral infection is likely. [1]","Not relieved within 5 minutes: repeat the dose and arrange transfer to an emergency department. [1]","Ipratropium: added to salbutamol in severe and life-threatening attacks, 8 puffs of 21 micrograms through a spacer. [2]","Magnesium sulfate 10 mmol intravenously over 20 minutes is a hospital step for attacks that do not respond. [2]"]},{"key":"A spacer with 12 puffs matches a nebuliser. Reach for the nebuliser only when a spacer cannot be used."}]},{"id":"oxygen","h":"Oxygen","body":[{"ul":["Start: when saturation is under 92%. [1,2]","Target: 92 to 96%. [1,2]","At risk of carbon dioxide retention: target 88 to 92%. [1]","Do not over-oxygenate adults. Saturation above the target range raises the risk of carbon dioxide retention. [1]"]}]},{"id":"steroids","h":"Corticosteroids","body":[{"ul":["Who: every adult and adolescent presenting with acute asthma, whatever the severity at first assessment. [1]","When: as soon as practical, and within the first hour. [2]","Adults: prednisolone 37.5 to 50 mg, then each morning for a total of 5 to 10 days. [1,2]","Adolescents: 1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) once daily for 3 to 5 days. [1]","Alternative for adults: dexamethasone 16 mg, repeated once the next day. [2]","Cannot swallow: hydrocortisone 100 mg intravenously every 6 hours. [2]"]},{"key":"Corticosteroids go in early for everyone who presents. They are not held back for the severe cases."}]},{"id":"anaphylaxis","h":"Is it anaphylaxis?","body":[{"ul":["Sudden breathlessness with hives, flushing or swelling is anaphylaxis. [2]","Also suspect it when sudden breathlessness comes with dizziness, low blood pressure, vomiting or diarrhoea, even without a rash. [2]","Treatment: intramuscular adrenaline first. Give it if anaphylaxis cannot be excluded. [1,2]","Food allergy rarely triggers asthma, but confirmed food allergy is a risk factor for fatal asthma. [1]"]},{"key":"Wheeze plus skin, gut or circulation signs: give adrenaline."}]},{"id":"after","h":"Observation, discharge and follow-up","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Step","Detail"],"r":[["Observe","At least 4 hours after distress has settled, in the practice or with a responsible adult"],["Do not send home if","Reliever is still needed more often than every 4 hours"],["Admit","Any life-threatening feature, or severe features that persist after first treatment"],["Also weigh","Whether the person can lie flat without breathlessness, time of day, distance from help, phone access, past severe attacks"],["First follow-up","Within 3 to 5 days"],["Full review","About 2 weeks later"]]}},{"ul":["Written instructions: preventer, reliever as needed (not by the clock), prednisolone timing, and when to call an ambulance. [1]","Preventer after an attack: start or continue a maintenance corticosteroid-LABA with at least a medium corticosteroid dose. [1]","Before leaving: check technique, adherence and triggers, and give an interim action plan. [1,2]","Consider specialist referral after a first presentation or any hospital admission. [2]","Near-fatal asthma: specialist follow-up for life. [2]"]}]},{"id":"storm","h":"Thunderstorm asthma","body":[{"ul":["What: spring and early summer storms in high grass-pollen regions break pollen into tiny particles that reach the small airways. [1]","Who: people with ryegrass allergy or springtime hay fever, including those who have never had asthma. [1]","Highest risk: asthma with grass pollen allergy and no inhaled corticosteroid, such as people self-treating with pharmacy salbutamol. [1]","Protect: an inhaled corticosteroid-containing regimen through the pollen season. [1]","Check the reliever is in date and has enough doses. [1]","On high-pollen storm days: stay inside with windows shut, especially in the gusts before the rain. [1]","Turn off evaporative coolers. In a car, close windows and recirculate. [1]","Nasal corticosteroid sprays: it is not known whether they protect. [1]"]},{"key":"Springtime hay fever is the risk marker. The protection is an inhaled corticosteroid."}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["Oxygen target","93 to 95% in the text; 92 to 96% in its table","92 to 96%"],["Corticosteroids","All who present, regardless of severity","All but the mildest cases"],["Ipratropium","Not on the primary care page","With salbutamol for severe and life-threatening attacks"],["First follow-up","Within 3 to 5 days","Within 2 to 3 days"],["Adolescent prednisolone","3 to 5 days","3 days (children)"]]}},{"p":"This page uses the 92 to 96% target, which both sources print."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["Classifying an attack from speech, saturation and pulse.","The salbutamol dose and interval for a severe attack in the treatment room.","A patient who has gone quiet and drowsy with a silent chest.","Wheeze with hives after a meal.","Whether a patient who settled after one dose needs prednisolone.","Who can go home, and when to see them again.","Advice for a hay fever sufferer before storm season."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-asthma-kids","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Wheeze and asthma in children: diagnosis and long-term treatment","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Asthma is not diagnosed or treated under 12 months. Persistent wheeze in an infant is referred.","Ages 1 to 5: no lung function test is possible. The evidence is a rapid response to salbutamol.","Ages 6 to 11: spirometry with bronchodilator, or FeNO of 25 ppb or more.","Under 12, salbutamol as needed is still level 1. Add low-dose inhaled corticosteroid for frequent symptoms or flare-ups.","No nebulisers at home, no oral salbutamol, no theophylline."],"curriculum":["Assess the wheezy child and diagnose asthma by age group.","Start and step asthma treatment in children, including when to refer.","Recognise features that point to another diagnosis."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Diagnosing asthma in children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years; Treatment levels and Initial asthma treatment for both age groups; tables of inhaled corticosteroid doses, alternative diagnoses and probability features.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Asthma diagnosis: children 6 years and older, adolescents and adults. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"ages","h":"Three age bands","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Age","How asthma is confirmed","Notes"],"r":[["Under 12 months","It is not diagnosed","Wheeze is usually bronchiolitis. Refer persistent wheeze. No asthma treatment"],["1 to 5 years","Signs that clear within minutes of salbutamol, then a trial of treatment","Lung function testing is not possible"],["6 to 11 years","Spirometry with bronchodilator, or FeNO 25 ppb or more","Peak flow is not used to diagnose"]]}},{"key":"The younger the child, the more the diagnosis rests on watching what salbutamol does."}]},{"id":"history","h":"History and examination","body":[{"ul":["Ask: wheeze, hard breathing, chest tightness or cough, and how often by day and by night. [1]","Ask whether symptoms come only with colds, or also between them. [1]","Describe increased work of breathing to parents: sucking in under the ribs or at the neck. [1]","Ask about smoke and vape exposure, pets, eczema, hay fever, food allergy and family history. [1]","Ask about prematurity, neonatal breathing problems and hospital stays for bronchiolitis. [1]","Examine: growth on a centile chart, chest shape, clubbing, eczema and signs of rhinitis. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Asthma more likely","Asthma less likely"],"r":[["More than one typical symptom, and often","Isolated cough with no wheeze or hard breathing"],["Worse at night and early morning","A moist cough"],["Set off by exercise, pets, cold air, laughing or emotion","Noisy breathing in during exercise"],["Symptoms between colds as well","Symptoms only with colds, never between"],["Atopy in the child or family","Wheeze in one spot; normal chest when symptomatic"],["Clear response to reliever","No response to an adequate trial"]]}},{"p":"Wheeze only with colds makes asthma less likely in a preschooler but does not rule it out. Viral infections are the commonest trigger of severe attacks at every age. [1]"}]},{"id":"redflags","h":"Clues to another diagnosis","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Finding","Consider"],"r":[["Symptoms from birth","Cystic fibrosis, airway malformation, primary ciliary dyskinesia"],["Sudden-onset cough; wheeze on one side","Inhaled foreign body: urgent"],["Persistent wet cough","Protracted bacterial bronchitis, bronchiectasis, cystic fibrosis"],["Dry cough in the daytime only","Habit (somatic) cough"],["Coughing blood","Urgent referral"],["Clubbing, nasal polyps, poor growth","Cystic fibrosis, immunodeficiency"],["Stridor","Croup, laryngeal obstruction"],["Wheeze that never varies","Structural airway problem"],["Heart murmur","Congenital heart disease"]]}},{"key":"One-sided wheeze or coughing blood in a child is referred urgently."}]},{"id":"young","h":"Ages 1 to 5: confirming and starting treatment","body":[{"ul":["Confirm a response: signs settle within minutes of salbutamol by spacer, seen by parents, the practice or an emergency department. [1]","Reliever: salbutamol 2 puffs by spacer, with a mask if needed. Give 2 more if not settled in a few minutes. [1]","Unclear diagnosis with significant symptoms: refer. [1]"]},{"p":"Start a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, with salbutamol as needed, if any of these apply. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Indication","Threshold"],"r":[["Frequent symptoms","Daytime twice a week or more, or night-time twice a month or more"],["Restricted life","Activity or sleep limited when the child is otherwise well"],["Flare-ups","Two or more treated with oral corticosteroids"]]}},{"ul":["Dose: fluticasone propionate 50 micrograms twice daily. Higher doses are for specialists. [1]","Review at about 3 months. [1]","Good response: consider a pause to see whether symptoms return. Some children outgrow it. [1]","No response: check technique, adherence and triggers such as household smoke, then question the diagnosis. [1]"]}]},{"id":"levels","h":"Treatment levels under 12","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Level","Ages 1 to 5","Ages 6 to 11"],"r":[["1","Salbutamol as needed","Salbutamol as needed"],["2","Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid","Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid"],["3","Medium-dose inhaled corticosteroid","Low-dose corticosteroid-LABA (or medium-dose corticosteroid)"],["4","Specialist","Medium-dose corticosteroid-LABA"],["5","","Specialist"]]}},{"ul":["Ages 6 to 11, starting a preventer: same symptom thresholds, or one flare-up needing oral corticosteroids in the past 12 months. Review in 8 to 12 weeks. [1]","Reliever, ages 6 to 11: 2 to 4 puffs for cough or wheeze; 6 to 12 puffs if working hard to breathe. [1]","Low daily doses, ages 6 to 11: fluticasone propionate 100 micrograms, budesonide 100 to 200, ciclesonide 80. [1]","Montelukast: an alternative at level 2 only if parents decline a corticosteroid. Warn about mood and behaviour change. [1]","A LABA combination is an option from age 4. [1]","Every child has a written action plan, with a copy for school or day care. [1]"]},{"key":"Under 12, the reliever is still salbutamol. Budesonide-formoterol as needed starts at 12."}]},{"id":"never","h":"What not to prescribe","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Avoid","Why or instead"],"r":[["Nebulised medicines for home use","Use a spacer, with a mask for small children"],["Oral salbutamol","Not recommended"],["Theophylline","Not recommended"],["Any asthma treatment under 12 months","Refer persistent wheeze"],["Oral corticosteroids for mild episodes","Keep for severe or life-threatening attacks"]]}}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["FeNO in ages 6 to 11","Used: 25 ppb or more supports asthma","A specialist test"],["Peak flow in ages 6 to 11","Not used to diagnose","A 14-day diary is an option when spirometry is non-diagnostic"]]}},{"p":"Within the Handbook, the corticosteroid-treated flare-up threshold for starting a preventer is two or more at ages 1 to 5 and one in 12 months at ages 6 to 11."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A 9-month-old with recurrent wheeze whose parents ask for a puffer.","A 3-year-old with night cough twice a week: does she need a preventer.","A 4-year-old who improved on a 3-month trial: what next.","A 7-year-old with a wet cough every day for six weeks.","A toddler with sudden cough and wheeze on one side.","Parents asking for a home nebuliser or a salbutamol syrup.","Choosing between salbutamol and budesonide-formoterol for an 8-year-old."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-asthma-kids-acute","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Acute asthma in children","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Severe if the child cannot finish a sentence, is using accessory muscles, or has a saturation of 94% or less.","Salbutamol by spacer: ages 1 to 5, 2 to 6 puffs (6 if severe). Ages 6 to 11, 4 to 12 puffs (12 if severe).","Severe attacks: repeat every 20 minutes for an hour and call an ambulance.","Oral corticosteroids: ages 6 to 11 unless the attack is mild; ages 1 to 5 only if going to hospital.","Prednisolone 1 mg/kg each morning for 3 days."],"curriculum":["Assess and manage acute asthma and wheeze in children in the community.","Use spacers and masks, and dose salbutamol by age.","Decide on corticosteroids, transfer and follow-up."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook, version 3.0. Melbourne: National Asthma Council Australia; September 2025. Managing acute asthma in children 1 to 5 years in primary care; Managing acute asthma in children 6 to 11 years in primary care; tables of immediate treatment and secondary severity assessment.","url":"https://www.asthmahandbook.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Acute asthma. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020 (under review, June 2026).","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"severity","h":"How sick is the child?","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Features"],"r":[["Mild to moderate (all of)","Walking or moving about; talking in sentences or phrases (a toddler vocalises); saturation over 94%"],["Severe (any of)","Cannot finish a sentence; accessory muscles, tracheal tug or recession; obvious distress; saturation 94% or less"],["Life-threatening (any of)","Drowsy, collapsed or exhausted; cyanosis; poor effort; soft or absent breath sounds; saturation under 90%"]]}},{"ul":["Assess while preparing the salbutamol. Do not delay the first dose. [1]","Slow breathing, a slow pulse or a silent chest in a tiring child means arrest is near. [1]","The life-threatening saturation cut-off is under 90% in children and under 92% in adults. [1]"]},{"key":"Recession, a sentence left unfinished, or 94% or less: this is severe."}]},{"id":"dose","h":"Salbutamol by age","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Ages 1 to 5","Ages 6 to 11"],"r":[["Mild to moderate","2 to 6 puffs","4 to 12 puffs"],["Severe","6 puffs","12 puffs"],["Life-threatening","2 x 2.5 mg nebules, continuous, with oxygen","2 x 5 mg nebules, continuous, with oxygen"],["Nebule if a spacer cannot be used","2.5 mg","5 mg"]]}},{"ul":["Device: pressurised inhaler and spacer, one puff at a time, normal breathing. Add a mask for small children. [1]","Repeat: severe attacks at least every 20 minutes for the first hour. Milder attacks every 20 to 30 minutes if needed. [1]","Not relieved within 5 minutes: repeat and arrange transfer to an emergency department. [1]","Never oral or intravenous salbutamol. [1]","High doses can cause a fast pulse, fast breathing, low potassium and acidosis. [1]","Severe attacks also get ipratropium: 4 puffs of 21 micrograms at ages 1 to 5, and 8 puffs from age 6. [2]"]},{"key":"Small child, small dose: 6 puffs is the severe dose under 6 years, 12 puffs from 6 years."}]},{"id":"oxygen","h":"Oxygen","body":[{"ul":["Start when saturation is under 92% on room air. [1]","Target 92 to 96%. [1]","Use a simple mask at 5 L/min or nasal prongs at 3 L/min. [1]"]}]},{"id":"steroids","h":"Corticosteroids","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Age","Who gets them","Dose"],"r":[["1 to 5 years","Only children who need hospital admission","Prednisolone 1 mg/kg each morning for 3 days"],["6 to 11 years","All except attacks graded mild at first assessment","Prednisolone 1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) each morning for 3 days"]]}},{"ul":["Why hold back in preschoolers: in mild to moderate wheeze they may give no benefit. [2]","Alternative: dexamethasone 0.6 mg/kg (maximum 16 mg), repeated the next day if needed. [2]","Start within the first hour when indicated. [2]"]},{"key":"A preschooler with mild to moderate wheeze who is going home does not need prednisolone."}]},{"id":"transfer","h":"When to call an ambulance","body":[{"ul":["Any severe or life-threatening feature: call while starting treatment. [1]","A child who cannot speak in whole sentences, or has a saturation of 94% or less, goes to an acute care facility. [1]","No relief within 5 minutes of the first dose: repeat and transfer. [1]","Sudden wheeze with hives, swelling, vomiting or collapse is anaphylaxis: intramuscular adrenaline. [2]","Hospital steps for attacks that do not respond include intravenous magnesium sulfate, which is not used under 2 years. [2]"]}]},{"id":"after","h":"After the attack","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Step","Detail"],"r":[["Observe","At least 4 hours after the breathing has settled"],["Not ready for home","Reliever still needed more often than every 4 hours"],["Follow-up","Within 3 to 5 days, then a full review about 2 weeks later"],["Written plan","Preventer dose, reliever as needed, prednisolone course, when to call an ambulance"]]}},{"ul":["Review inhaler and spacer technique, adherence and triggers, including smoke and vape exposure at home. [1,2]","Decide whether a regular preventer is now needed. Many children have only one episode. [2]","Confirmed food allergy is a risk factor for fatal asthma. [1]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Point","Australian Asthma Handbook 2025","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["Corticosteroids at ages 1 to 5","Only if hospital admission is required","For severe wheeze; may be withheld in mild to moderate wheeze"],["Corticosteroids at ages 6 to 11","All except attacks graded mild","All but the mildest cases"],["Repeat dose at ages 1 to 5, mild to moderate","Its table lists 4 to 12 puffs for repeat doses","2 to 6 puffs, repeated as required"],["Ipratropium","Not on the primary care pages","Added in severe and life-threatening attacks"]]}},{"p":"This page uses 2 to 6 puffs for repeat doses at ages 1 to 5, which matches the first dose in both sources."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["The salbutamol dose for a 3-year-old with recession and a saturation of 93%.","A 4-year-old with mild wheeze who is well after 4 puffs: prednisolone or not.","A 9-year-old with a moderate attack: which corticosteroid, what dose, how long.","A child who goes quiet and floppy during treatment.","A parent asking for salbutamol syrup.","When a child who has improved can go home."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-copd","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"COPD: diagnosis and long-term management","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["COPD is diagnosed on spirometry: post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC under 0.7.","Stopping smoking does more than any inhaler. Pulmonary rehabilitation is for everyone with symptoms.","Inhalers go in order: reliever, then one long-acting bronchodilator, then two, then add a corticosteroid.","A corticosteroid is added only for exacerbations (one hospital admission or two courses in a year) with ongoing symptoms on two bronchodilators.","Refer anyone with COPD before 40, and anyone with a resting saturation of 90% or less for oxygen assessment."],"curriculum":["Diagnose COPD and grade its severity.","Apply stepwise drug and non-drug management, including smoking cessation, rehabilitation and vaccination.","Recognise indications for referral, home oxygen and palliative care."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook: summary clinical practice guidelines for the management of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Version 1. Milton, Queensland: Lung Foundation Australia; 2024.","url":"https://lungfoundation.com.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"who","h":"Who to test","body":[{"ul":["Spirometry for anyone aged 35 or over with a smoking history, or another exposure, and any respiratory symptom. [1,2]","Symptoms: breathlessness, cough, sputum, wheeze or recurrent chest infections. [1,2]","Breathlessness may be the only symptom, and it creeps up over years. [2]","Other exposures: workplace dust and fumes, second-hand smoke, indoor cooking smoke, prematurity and childhood lung illness. [1,2]","About half of smokers develop some airflow limitation. [2]","Coughing blood, chest pain or weight loss need urgent investigation for another cause. [1]"]}]},{"id":"dx","h":"Diagnosis","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Test","Role"],"r":[["Spirometry after bronchodilator","Required. FEV1/FVC under 0.7 confirms COPD"],["Chest X-ray","Excludes other causes of breathlessness. Does not diagnose COPD"],["CT chest","Not needed for diagnosis. Shows emphysema and bronchiectasis"],["Lung volumes and gas transfer","Not routine. Show gas trapping and reduced transfer"],["Alpha-1 antitrypsin","Suspect deficiency when COPD starts before 40, especially with a family history"]]}},{"ul":["Symptoms or imaging alone are not enough. Repeat spirometry if the result is borderline. [1]","A large bronchodilator response (over 15% and over 400 mL) suggests asthma, alone or with COPD. [1]","Asthma with COPD needs an inhaled corticosteroid with a long-acting bronchodilator, never a LABA alone. [1]"]},{"key":"No spirometry, no diagnosis. The number is a post-bronchodilator ratio under 0.7."}]},{"id":"severity","h":"Severity","body":[{"p":"Grade severity when the person is stable, at least 4 weeks after any exacerbation. [2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Grade","FEV1 (% predicted)","Typical picture"],"r":[["Mild","60 to 80","Breathless on moderate exertion; few symptoms"],["Moderate","40 to 59","Breathless walking on the flat; exacerbations needing corticosteroids or antibiotics"],["Severe","Under 40","Breathless on minimal exertion; frequent, worsening exacerbations"]]}},{"ul":["The spirometry bands are a guide. Symptoms and exacerbation history count as much. [2]","Track symptoms with the COPD Assessment Test (CAT) and the mMRC breathlessness scale. [1,2]"]}]},{"id":"nondrug","h":"Measures for everyone","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Measure","Detail"],"r":[["Stop smoking","The single most important step. Counselling with nicotine replacement, varenicline or bupropion"],["Pulmonary rehabilitation","For anyone with symptoms. Improves exercise capacity and quality of life and reduces exacerbations"],["Physical activity","At least 150 minutes a week of moderate activity"],["Vaccination","Influenza every year, pneumococcal, and COVID-19"],["Nutrition","Review weight and diet"],["Breathlessness techniques","Leaning forward, pursed-lip breathing, a handheld fan"]]}},{"ul":["High nicotine dependence: a patch combined with a fast-acting form. [1]","Few symptoms and a CAT score under 10: these measures alone may be enough at first. [2]"]},{"key":"Smoking cessation is the single most important step. It limits lung damage and reduces deaths."}]},{"id":"steps","h":"Inhalers, step by step","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Step","Treatment"],"r":[["1","Short-acting bronchodilator as needed (salbutamol or ipratropium)"],["2","Add one long-acting bronchodilator: a LAMA or a LABA"],["3","Both together: LAMA with LABA"],["4","Add an inhaled corticosteroid (triple therapy)"]]}},{"p":"An inhaled corticosteroid is added only when both conditions are met. [1,2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Condition","Threshold"],"r":[["Exacerbations in the past 12 months","One needing hospital admission, or two treated with oral corticosteroids or antibiotics"],["Symptoms","Still significant on a LAMA with a LABA"]]}},{"ul":["Before each step: check inhaler technique and adherence. Up to 90% of people use their device incorrectly. [2]","Keep the number of device types small. [1]","Judge symptoms at about 6 weeks and exacerbations over about 6 months. [1]","Inhaled corticosteroids raise the risk of pneumonia in COPD. [2]","A response to oral corticosteroids does not predict a response to inhaled ones. [2]","Still symptomatic on triple therapy: refer. [2]"]},{"key":"Bronchodilators first in COPD. The corticosteroid is last, and only for exacerbations."}]},{"id":"avoid","h":"What not to use","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Avoid","Why"],"r":[["Inhaled corticosteroid alone","Not a COPD treatment unless asthma is also present"],["Long-term oral corticosteroids","Harm without benefit: bone loss, muscle weakness, falls"],["Theophylline","Not recommended"],["Ipratropium with a LAMA","The two are not combined"],["Oxygen for breathlessness without low oxygen","No benefit"]]}},{"ul":["Long-term low-dose macrolide: a specialist option for frequent exacerbations. [1,2]","Cardioselective beta blockers are safe in COPD when there is a cardiac indication. [1]"]}]},{"id":"oxygen","h":"Home oxygen","body":[{"ul":["Refer for assessment when resting saturation is 90% or less while stable. [1,2]","Assess at least 4 weeks after an exacerbation. An arterial blood gas on room air is required. [2]","Criteria: PaO2 consistently under 55 mmHg, or up to 59 mmHg with polycythaemia, pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure. [1]","Dose: more than 18 hours a day. It prolongs life in resting hypoxaemia. [1]","Not for current smokers, because of fire and burns. [1]","Persistent high carbon dioxide: refer for assessment for home non-invasive ventilation. [1,2]"]}]},{"id":"refer","h":"Referral and review","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Refer to a respiratory physician for","Notes"],"r":[["Uncertain diagnosis","Including possible asthma overlap"],["COPD before age 40","To test for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"],["Rapid fall in FEV1",""],["Frequent exacerbations or symptoms despite full treatment","Including after pulmonary rehabilitation"],["Coughing blood or other unusual symptoms",""],["Saturation 90% or less, or signs of right heart failure","Oxygen assessment"]]}},{"ul":["Review: at least yearly for mild to moderate disease and twice a year for severe disease. [2]","Spirometry every year for everyone with COPD. [2]","Each review: smoking, symptoms, exacerbations, technique, adherence, action plan, mood, weight and vaccination. [2]","Screen for heart disease, osteoporosis, anxiety, depression, lung cancer and sleep apnoea. [1]","Discuss advance care planning early, and involve palliative care for advanced disease. [1,2]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"p":"The two sources agree on diagnosis, severity bands, the order of inhalers and the threshold for adding an inhaled corticosteroid. The Therapeutic Guidelines topic draws on an earlier version of COPD-X."},{"tb":{"h":["Point","COPD-X Handbook 2024","Therapeutic Guidelines 2020"],"r":[["When to judge a change","Symptoms at 6 weeks; exacerbations at 6 months","Review 3 to 6 months after any change"],["Blood eosinophils to guide corticosteroids","Addressed in the full plan; no cut-off in the handbook summary read","Not mentioned in the sections read"]]}}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A 58-year-old smoker with a morning cough and a normal chest X-ray: the next test.","Reading a post-bronchodilator ratio of 0.64 with an FEV1 of 52%.","Breathless on a LAMA alone, no exacerbations: the next inhaler.","Two prednisolone courses this year on LAMA with LABA.","A 38-year-old non-smoker with emphysema.","Resting saturation of 88% at a routine review.","The intervention with the greatest effect on survival."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-copd-exac","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"COPD exacerbations","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["More breathless, more cough or more sputum than the usual day-to-day variation is an exacerbation.","First: more reliever, up to 8 puffs of salbutamol by spacer. Needing it more than every 3 hours means review.","Next: prednisolone 30 to 50 mg daily for 5 days, with no taper.","Antibiotics only for more purulent sputum together with more sputum or more breathlessness: amoxicillin or doxycycline for 5 days.","Oxygen is titrated to 88 to 92%. Nebulisers are driven by air."],"curriculum":["Recognise and manage a COPD exacerbation in the community.","Use antibiotics and corticosteroids appropriately.","Identify who needs hospital, and arrange care after an exacerbation."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) exacerbations. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Antibiotic management of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) exacerbations. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":3,"cite":"Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook: summary clinical practice guidelines for the management of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Version 1. 2024.","url":"https://lungfoundation.com.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"recognise","h":"Recognising an exacerbation","body":[{"ul":["Definition: a sudden worsening of breathlessness, cough or sputum beyond normal day-to-day variation. [1,3]","Other features: less exercise tolerance, fast breathing, fever, new ankle swelling. [1]","Triggers: viral and bacterial infection, air pollution, heart failure and pulmonary embolism. [1]","Viruses are common triggers. Consider a swab for influenza, RSV and COVID-19. [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Other diagnoses to consider","Note"],"r":[["Pneumonia","Fast breathing at rest, fast pulse, rigors, crackles that do not clear with coughing: get a chest X-ray"],["Heart failure, ischaemia, arrhythmia","Can cause the same breathlessness, fatigue and chest discomfort"],["Pulmonary embolism, pneumothorax, pleural effusion","On the list to exclude when the picture does not fit"]]}},{"key":"Not every breathless patient with COPD is having an exacerbation. Think heart, clot and pneumonia."}]},{"id":"treat","h":"Treatment at home or in the practice","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Step","Treatment","Notes"],"r":[["1","Salbutamol up to 8 puffs through a spacer, repeated as needed","A spacer is as effective as a nebuliser for most"],["2","Prednisolone 30 to 50 mg daily for 5 days","If the reliever is not enough. Stop without a taper"],["3","Antibiotic for 5 days","Only if the criteria below are met"]]}},{"ul":["Reliever needed more often than every 3 hours, or symptoms uncontrolled: medical review. [1]","Ipratropium (up to 4 puffs) is an alternative reliever, but not for people taking a LAMA. [1]","If a nebuliser is used, drive it with air, not oxygen. [1]","Repeated high doses of salbutamol can lower potassium and affect heart rhythm. [1]","Chest X-ray and sputum culture are not routine in the community. [2,3]","Review at about 5 days. Still unwell: reassess. Worse: hospital. [3]"]},{"key":"Five days of prednisolone, then stop. No taper."}]},{"id":"abx","h":"Antibiotics","body":[{"p":"Antibiotics are not routine. Most community exacerbations can be managed without them. [2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Situation","Antibiotic?"],"r":[["Sputum more purulent, plus more sputum or more breathlessness","Consider"],["Needs intensive care or ventilation, including non-invasive","Give"],["Less severe exacerbation managed in the community","Withhold"],["Low C-reactive protein","Supports withholding"]]}},{"tb":{"h":["Choice","Dose","Duration"],"r":[["Amoxicillin","500 mg every 8 hours, or 1 g every 12 hours","5 days"],["Doxycycline","100 mg daily","5 days"]]}},{"ul":["Not first line: amoxicillin with clavulanate, macrolides and cephalosporins. They work no better and do more harm. [2]","Doses are lower than for pneumonia. The aim is faster recovery, not clearing colonisation. [2]","Oral treatment unless the person cannot swallow or absorb. [2]"]},{"key":"Purulent sputum is the trigger for antibiotics. Breathlessness alone is not."}]},{"id":"slow","h":"Not improving","body":[{"ul":["Symptoms last 7 to 10 days in mild COPD and can last weeks in more severe disease. [2]","Slow recovery rarely needs a broader antibiotic. [2]","First check that bronchodilator and corticosteroid treatment are adequate. [2]","Then look again for a virus, pneumonia or heart failure. [2]","Severe COPD with no response at 48 to 72 hours and likely bacterial cause: amoxicillin with clavulanate 875+125 mg twice daily for 5 days. [2]","Pseudomonas in sputum now or within 3 months: ciprofloxacin 750 mg twice daily for 5 days. [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Sputum culture is considered when","Detail"],"r":[["Severe obstruction","FEV1 under 50% predicted"],["Frequent exacerbations","Two or more a year"],["Hospital","Current admission or one in the past 3 months"],["Pseudomonas","Isolated or colonised in the past 3 months"],["Home oxygen",""]]}},{"p":"A positive culture does not prove infection. Many people with COPD carry Haemophilus, Moraxella or pneumococcus all the time. [2]"}]},{"id":"hospital","h":"Who needs hospital","body":[{"p":"Many exacerbations are managed at home. These features point to hospital. [1,3]"},{"tb":{"h":["Domain","Indication"],"r":[["Response","Not improving with treatment at home"],["Function","Cannot walk between rooms, eat or sleep because of breathlessness; cannot cope at home"],["Oxygen","Saturation under 92% if not on home oxygen, or worse than usual; new or worse cyanosis"],["Brain","Confusion or drowsiness, which suggests carbon dioxide retention"],["Heart","New ankle swelling, new arrhythmia, chest pain, unstable blood pressure"],["Other illness","Pneumonia or a worsening comorbidity"]]}},{"p":"If there is an advance care plan, the person's wishes about hospital are taken into account. [1]"}]},{"id":"oxygen","h":"Oxygen and ventilation","body":[{"ul":["Give oxygen when saturation is under 88%, and titrate to 88 to 92%. [1]","Delivery: nasal prongs at 0.5 to 2 L/min. [3]","Too much oxygen raises carbon dioxide and increases deaths. [1]","Non-invasive ventilation: for PaCO2 over 45 mmHg with pH under 7.35 despite optimal treatment. [1,3]","Signs of respiratory failure: confusion, drowsiness, restlessness, cyanosis. [3]"]},{"key":"In COPD the oxygen target is 88 to 92%, not 'as high as possible'."}]},{"id":"after","h":"After the exacerbation","body":[{"ul":["GP review within 7 days of leaving hospital. [1,3]","Check technique and adherence, and reconsider the level of maintenance treatment. [1]","Update the written action plan and make sure it is understood. [1]","Refer to pulmonary rehabilitation. After an admission it can start within 2 to 4 weeks of discharge. [1,3]","Check vaccinations and smoking status. [3]","A hospital admission is a prompt to discuss advance care planning. [1]","Two exacerbations in 12 months needing corticosteroids or antibiotics: review earlier and step up. [3]","Frequent exacerbations despite full treatment: refer to consider a long-term macrolide. [2]"]},{"p":"Some people hold a prednisolone and antibiotic supply at home under their action plan, with instructions to arrange early review. [3]"}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where the sources differ","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Point","Therapeutic Guidelines","COPD-X Handbook 2024"],"r":[["Antibiotic trigger","More purulent sputum plus more volume or more breathlessness","Fever, or a change in sputum colour or volume"],["Start oxygen","Saturation under 88%","Hospital if under 92% and not on home oxygen; target 88 to 92%"],["Salbutamol at home","Up to 8 puffs, repeated as needed","4 to 8 puffs every 3 to 4 hours"]]}},{"p":"Questions follow the Therapeutic Guidelines antibiotic criteria, which were updated in December 2025."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["More breathless for two days with white sputum: antibiotics or not.","Green sputum and more of it: which antibiotic, what dose, how long.","How long to give prednisolone and whether to taper.","A drowsy patient on 6 litres of oxygen with a saturation of 99%.","Setting up a nebuliser for a patient with COPD in the treatment room.","Not better after three days of amoxicillin.","Deciding between home and hospital."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-cap","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Community-acquired pneumonia in adults","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Most coughs with fever are not pneumonia. Look for a fast pulse, fast breathing, low saturation and focal chest signs.","Any of these means possible admission: respiratory rate 22 or more, pulse over 100, systolic under 90, new confusion, saturation under 92%.","None of them: treat at home with amoxicillin 1 g three times a day for 5 days.","Penicillin allergy or a likely atypical organism: doxycycline 100 mg twice a day.","Review early. Fever should settle in days; cough and tiredness can last weeks."],"curriculum":["Diagnose pneumonia and distinguish it from acute bronchitis.","Assess severity and decide the place of care.","Prescribe empirical antibiotics for low-severity pneumonia, review response and prevent recurrence."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) in adults. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited; published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"features","h":"Is it pneumonia?","body":[{"p":"Cough and fever are common and non-specific. Among people who present to primary care with respiratory symptoms, only 0.4 to 5% have pneumonia. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Finding","More likely pneumonia when"],"r":[["Temperature","Over 37.8 degrees"],["Respiratory rate","18 or more at rest"],["Heart rate","Over 100"],["Oxygen saturation","Under 95% on room air"],["Symptoms","Breathlessness, rigors, pleuritic chest pain"],["Chest signs","Dullness, poor air entry, bronchial breathing, crackles that do not clear with coughing"]]}},{"ul":["Older people may present with confusion, diarrhoea or headache and few chest symptoms. [1]","Acute bronchitis is the main alternative. It does not cause severe hypoxaemia and settles without antibiotics. [1]","COPD or bronchiectasis: suspect pneumonia when the person is sicker than in their usual exacerbations. [1]"]},{"key":"Coloured sputum does not make it pneumonia. Abnormal vital signs and focal chest signs do."}]},{"id":"severity","h":"Severity and where to treat","body":[{"p":"Any one of these features means close observation and possible admission. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Feature","Threshold"],"r":[["Respiratory rate","22 or more"],["Heart rate","Over 100"],["Systolic blood pressure","Under 90 mmHg"],["Mental state","New confusion"],["Oxygen saturation","Under 92% on room air, or below the person's usual"],["Chest X-ray, if done","More than one lobe involved"],["Lactate, if done","Over 2 mmol/L"]]}},{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Definition","Usual place of care"],"r":[["Low","None of the features above","Home, with oral antibiotics and planned review"],["Moderate","At least one feature, no red flag","Hospital"],["High","A red flag for intensive care, such as respiratory rate 30 or more, saturation under 90%, or sepsis","Intensive care"]]}},{"ul":["These features call for more support. They do not by themselves call for broader antibiotics. [1]","Also weigh: who is at home, age, comorbidities, ability to swallow tablets, and function. [1]","People can worsen over the first 24 to 48 hours. Give clear instructions on when to return. [1]","Check for an advance care plan and the person's goals before arranging admission. [1]"]},{"key":"No admission feature means low severity and treatment at home."}]},{"id":"tests","h":"Investigations","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Do","Consider"],"r":[["Low","Oxygen saturation on room air","Chest X-ray; viral PCR swab"],["Moderate","Saturation, chest X-ray, blood cultures","Viral PCR; sputum culture"],["High","All of the above plus arterial gas and sputum culture","Urinary antigens for pneumococcus and Legionella"]]}},{"p":"New consolidation on a chest X-ray usually confirms the diagnosis. In low-severity disease, imaging is done when one of these applies. [1]"},{"ul":["The diagnosis is unclear, such as unexplained hypoxia or marked breathlessness. [1]","Underlying lung disease, immune compromise or advanced age. [1]","No improvement on treatment. [1]","A complication is suspected: effusion, empyema or abscess. [1]"]}]},{"id":"treat","h":"Antibiotics for low-severity pneumonia","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Situation","Antibiotic","Duration"],"r":[["Most adults","Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours","5 days"],["Atypical organism suspected","Doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours, or clarithromycin 500 mg every 12 hours","5 days"],["Penicillin hypersensitivity","Doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours, or clarithromycin 500 mg every 12 hours","5 days"],["Significant comorbidity and follow-up uncertain","Amoxicillin plus doxycycline or clarithromycin","5 days"]]}},{"ul":["Why amoxicillin: it targets pneumococcus, the commonest bacterial cause. Resistance to tetracyclines and macrolides is rising. [1]","One drug is enough for most. Two drugs from the start only for frailty or advanced organ disease when review may not happen. [1]","Combination with non-severe penicillin hypersensitivity: cefuroxime 500 mg every 12 hours replaces amoxicillin. [1]","COPD, bronchiectasis and tropical residence do not change the choice in low-severity disease. [1]","Influenza circulating and high risk of complications: consider adding an antiviral. [1]","The dose is higher than for a COPD exacerbation, where amoxicillin is 500 mg every 8 hours. [1]"]},{"key":"Amoxicillin 1 g three times a day for 5 days. Doxycycline if allergic or atypical."}]},{"id":"review","h":"Review and duration","body":[{"tb":{"h":["When","What to expect or do"],"r":[["First few days","Fever settles and appetite returns"],["Day 3","If stable (normal pulse, breathing rate, saturation and temperature), consider stopping antibiotics"],["Day 5","Usual end of treatment. Not stable by now: higher risk, so reassess and consider admission"],["Weeks","Cough, sputum and breathlessness can linger"],["Months","Tiredness can persist"]]}},{"ul":["Lingering symptoms are not a reason to extend antibiotics. [1]","A virus is confirmed as the cause: stop the antibiotic. [1]","Not improving on one drug: review the diagnosis first. If still pneumonia, consider adding the second drug and reassess for admission. [1]"]},{"key":"Five days is the course. A cough that lasts three weeks is expected, not a treatment failure."}]},{"id":"notbetter","h":"Not improving","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Think of","Examples"],"r":[["A complication","Lung abscess, parapneumonic effusion, empyema: persistent fever or chest discomfort"],["Another diagnosis","Heart failure, COPD exacerbation, pulmonary embolism, cancer, interstitial lung disease"],["An unusual organism","Viruses, Legionella, Mycoplasma, anaerobes (poor dentition, foul sputum), Pseudomonas"],["An immune problem","Test for HIV"]]}},{"ul":["Repeat or arrange a chest X-ray if none was done. [1]","In tropical northern Australia, consider melioidosis (Burkholderia pseudomallei), especially with risk factors. [1]","Seek specialist advice if the cause remains unclear. [1]"]}]},{"id":"prevent","h":"Preventing the next one","body":[{"ul":["Vaccinate: pneumococcal, influenza and COVID-19. [1]","Stop smoking. [1]","Exercise, nutrition and weight management. [1]","Review medicines linked to pneumonia: proton pump inhibitors, benzodiazepines and other sedatives. [1]","Assess and reduce aspiration risk. [1]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["Cough and green sputum with normal observations and a clear chest: antibiotics or not.","Choosing home or hospital from a set of observations.","The antibiotic, dose and duration for a well 45-year-old with lobar consolidation.","The same patient with a penicillin allergy.","Still coughing at three weeks but otherwise well.","Fever persisting on day 5 of amoxicillin.","What to review after recovery, including a long-term proton pump inhibitor."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-cough","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Cough in adults and acute bronchitis","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Acute cough lasts up to 3 weeks; chronic cough lasts more than 8.","Acute bronchitis: no antibiotics, no chest X-ray, no inhalers. Coloured sputum does not mean bacteria.","The cough of bronchitis usually clears by 3 to 4 weeks, and can take 8.","Every adult with a chronic cough gets a chest X-ray and spirometry.","Stop the ACE inhibitor. Refer a smoker or anyone over 50 with a new or changed cough or a hoarse voice."],"curriculum":["Assess acute and chronic cough and recognise red flags.","Manage acute bronchitis without antibiotics and explain the expected course.","Investigate chronic cough systematically and treat common causes."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Cough in adults. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Acute bronchitis. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"duration","h":"Sorting by duration","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Type","Duration","Usual meaning"],"r":[["Acute","Up to 3 weeks","Viral infection, acute bronchitis, a flare of asthma or COPD, pneumonia"],["Subacute","3 to 8 weeks","Often still resolving after infection. Start thinking about chronic causes"],["Chronic","More than 8 weeks","Needs a structured work-up"]]}},{"ul":["Always review the medicine list. [1]","Pertussis: consider when a cough lasts more than 2 weeks, with bouts of coughing, a whoop or vomiting after coughing. [1]"]}]},{"id":"redflags","h":"Red flags","body":[{"p":"Any of these calls for further investigation and early referral. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Group","Red flag"],"r":[["Symptoms","Coughing blood; breathlessness at rest or at night; hoarseness; trouble swallowing or choking"],["Sputum","Large volumes"],["Systemic","Fever; weight loss"],["History","Smoking, including e-cigarettes; recurrent or persistent chest infections"],["Findings","Abnormal chest examination; abnormal chest X-ray"]]}},{"key":"A new or changed cough in a smoker, or with a hoarse voice, is cancer until shown otherwise: refer urgently."}]},{"id":"bronchitis","h":"Acute bronchitis","body":[{"ul":["What: a self-limiting infection of the large airways in someone with no chronic lung disease and no pneumonia. [2]","Cause: almost always a virus. [2]","Symptoms: cough with or without sputum, sometimes wheeze, chest soreness from coughing, fever and a blocked nose. [2]","Coloured sputum does not predict bacterial infection. [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Time since onset","Proportion still coughing"],"r":[["3 weeks","Most have improved"],["4 weeks","About 1 in 10"],["Up to 8 weeks","That 1 in 10 can take this long, while airway inflammation settles"]]}},{"tb":{"h":["Do","Do not"],"r":[["Explain the expected course","Prescribe antibiotics"],["Paracetamol or an NSAID for pain or fever","Order a chest X-ray"],["Consider a viral swab if it would change management","Prescribe salbutamol without airflow limitation"],["Ask the person to return if not better at 3 weeks, or sooner if worse","Prescribe oral or inhaled corticosteroids"]]}},{"key":"Acute bronchitis: explain, do not prescribe. Three weeks is normal and eight is possible."}]},{"id":"exclude","h":"What to exclude in an acute cough","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Diagnosis","Clue"],"r":[["Pneumonia","Fast breathing at rest, fast pulse, persistent fever, rigors, low saturation, crackles that do not clear"],["Influenza or COVID-19","Systemic illness: swab if it changes management"],["Pertussis","Bouts of coughing, or contact with a case. Adults often lack the classic signs"],["New asthma","Wheeze"],["Heart failure","Oedema or weight gain with the cough"],["Flare of chronic lung disease","Known asthma, COPD or bronchiectasis"]]}},{"ul":["Pertussis testing: a posterior nasopharyngeal swab or aspirate for PCR. A throat or front-of-nose swab is less sensitive. [2]"]}]},{"id":"otc","h":"Cough medicines","body":[{"p":"Over-the-counter cough medicines are not generally recommended. Benefit is small or absent, and they have side effects. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Product","Evidence"],"r":[["Honey","Some benefit, safe: reasonable to try"],["Bromhexine","May reduce cough frequency"],["Guaifenesin, dextromethorphan","Conflicting"],["Antihistamines","No better than placebo"],["Codeine","No better than placebo"],["Echinacea, pelargonium","Uncertain benefit; severe allergic reactions reported"]]}}]},{"id":"chronic","h":"Chronic cough: work-up","body":[{"p":"Every adult with a cough lasting more than 8 weeks has the following. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Step","Detail"],"r":[["History and examination","Including medicines, smoking, reflux and nasal symptoms, and effect on daily life"],["Chest X-ray","For all"],["Spirometry","For all"],["Eosinophilic inflammation","Blood eosinophil count, and FeNO where available"]]}},{"ul":["Further tests are chosen case by case and are not routine. [1]","No cause found, or no response to treatment: refer to a respiratory physician or cough clinic. [1]"]},{"key":"Chronic cough: chest X-ray and spirometry for everyone."}]},{"id":"causes","h":"Chronic cough: causes and what to do","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Cause","Clue","Action"],"r":[["ACE inhibitor","About 15% of users; dry; onset can be delayed","Stop it. Use an angiotensin receptor blocker if still needed"],["Smoking","","Help to quit. Cough can briefly worsen after stopping"],["Asthma","Wheeze, tightness, atopy, reversibility, raised eosinophils","Short trial of inhaled corticosteroid, review at 2 to 4 weeks, then confirm the diagnosis"],["Reflux","Heartburn, night choking, worse after meals","Proton pump inhibitor with lifestyle change; review at 8 to 12 weeks and stop if no better"],["Chronic rhinosinusitis","Congestion, discharge, facial pressure, loss of smell","Treat the nose"],["Post-viral","Follows an infection","No antibiotics"],["COPD, bronchiectasis","Smoker; daily sputum; recurrent infections","Spirometry, then manage the underlying disease"],["Lung or laryngeal cancer","Smoker; over 50 with new or changed cough; voice change","Urgent referral"],["Tuberculosis","Has lived where it is endemic","Consider and investigate"],["Laryngeal obstruction","Voice change, lump in throat, brief episodes of breathlessness","Speech pathologist"],["Aspiration","Parkinson disease, stroke, dementia","Assess and reduce aspiration risk"]]}},{"ul":["Cough as the only symptom is not treated as asthma, reflux or sinus disease. [1]","A proton pump inhibitor is not used for cough without reflux symptoms. [1]"]},{"key":"No reflux symptoms, no proton pump inhibitor. No asthma features, no inhaler."}]},{"id":"refractory","h":"Refractory chronic cough","body":[{"ul":["Meaning: cough beyond 8 weeks that persists after identifiable causes are treated. It reflects an oversensitive cough reflex. [1]","Refer to a respiratory physician or cough clinic. [1]","Speech therapy reduces cough severity and is recommended. [1]","Specialist options: amitriptyline, gabapentin or pregabalin, and occasionally low-dose morphine. [1]","Set expectations: many people do not respond to current treatments. [1]","Voice care: avoid throat clearing, sip water and swallow hard, avoid smoke and overuse of the voice. [1]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["Ten days of cough with green sputum in a well adult who wants antibiotics.","A cough still present at five weeks after a cold, with a normal examination.","Which two tests every adult with a 10-week cough needs.","A dry cough six months after starting perindopril.","A 58-year-old smoker with a changed cough and a hoarse voice.","Chronic cough with no heartburn, already tried on a proton pump inhibitor.","What to say about cough mixtures."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-kids-airway","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Bronchiolitis, croup and stridor","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Bronchiolitis is a clinical diagnosis in the first year of life. Care is supportive: no salbutamol, no steroids, no antibiotics.","Admit an infant who is feeding less than half of normal, has a saturation of 92% or less, or has apnoeas.","Croup: one dose of oral steroid for every child, whatever the severity.","Stridor at rest that persists is severe croup: nebulised adrenaline and hospital.","A toxic-looking patient with stridor may have epiglottitis. Do not examine the throat: transfer with an escort."],"curriculum":["Diagnose and manage bronchiolitis and decide who needs hospital.","Assess the severity of croup and treat it.","Recognise other causes of stridor, including epiglottitis and foreign body."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Acute bronchiolitis. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Croup. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":3,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Acute epiglottitis. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"bronch","h":"Bronchiolitis: recognising it","body":[{"ul":["Who: infants under 12 months, most often under 6 months. [1]","Cause: respiratory syncytial virus most often. [1]","Picture: a runny nose and cough, then fast breathing, increased effort, and widespread crackles or wheeze. [1]","Course: worst on days 2 to 3, better over 7 to 10 days. The cough can last weeks. [1]","Diagnosis is clinical. A chest X-ray is not routine. [1]"]},{"key":"Bronchiolitis peaks on day 2 or 3. An infant seen on day 1 may be worse tomorrow."}]},{"id":"bronchadmit","h":"Bronchiolitis: who goes to hospital","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Admit if","Detail"],"r":[["Poor feeding","Less than half of usual intake over the past 12 hours"],["Work of breathing","Moderate to severe, or a markedly raised rate"],["Tiring","A marked fall in breathing rate"],["Apnoea","Any episode"],["Oxygen saturation","92% or less"]]}},{"p":"Consider early admission, even with mild illness, for infants at risk of rapid deterioration. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Risk group","Examples"],"r":[["Age","Under 2 months"],["Birth and growth","Born preterm; failure to thrive"],["Chronic disease","Congenital heart disease; chronic lung or neurological disease; immunodeficiency"],["Background","Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander infants; tobacco smoke at home"]]}}]},{"id":"bronchtreat","h":"Bronchiolitis: treatment","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Do","Do not"],"r":[["Small, frequent feeds","Salbutamol or other bronchodilators"],["Minimal handling","Oral or inhaled corticosteroids"],["Explain the course and give written information","Antibiotics"],["In hospital: oxygen to keep saturation at 92% or more","Nebulised hypertonic saline"],["In hospital: nasogastric feeds or intravenous fluids if not feeding","Adrenaline, except in arrest"]]}},{"key":"Nothing in the drug cupboard helps bronchiolitis. Feeding, oxygen and time do."}]},{"id":"croup","h":"Croup: recognising and grading it","body":[{"ul":["Picture: a viral prodrome, then a barking cough, hoarse voice and stridor. [2]","Who: 6 months to 6 years. A first episode after 6 suggests another diagnosis. [2]","Cause: parainfluenza virus most often. Lasts 2 to 5 days. [2]","Spasmodic croup: comes on at night with no prodrome, recurs, and is linked to asthma. Treated the same way. [2]","Keep the child calm on the parent's lap. Do not examine the throat. [2]","How loud the stridor is does not tell you how tight the airway is. [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Stridor","Other signs"],"r":[["Mild","None at rest","Mild chest wall retraction; normal breathing rate"],["Moderate","At rest","Moderate retraction, accessory muscles, fast breathing and pulse"],["Severe","Persisting at rest","Tiring; very fast or slowing breathing; poor air entry"],["Life-threatening","Any","Restless or drowsy, floppy, pale or blue"]]}}]},{"id":"crouptreat","h":"Croup: treatment","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Treatment","Then"],"r":[["Mild to moderate","One oral dose: prednisolone 1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) or dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg (maximum 12 mg)","Observe for at least 30 minutes. Home if settled"],["Severe","Nebulised adrenaline 1:1000, 5 mL, plus dexamethasone 0.6 mg/kg (maximum 12 mg) or prednisolone 2 mg/kg","Immediate transfer. Observe at least 4 hours"]]}},{"ul":["Cannot take oral steroid: nebulised budesonide 2 mg. [2]","Adrenaline can be repeated after 30 minutes. A second dose means intensive care is told and the child is admitted. [2]","Not better 30 minutes after the steroid: treat as severe. [2]","Going home: return to hospital if stridor at rest comes back. [2]","After severe croup: follow up within 24 hours. [2]","Not recommended: steam or humidified air, cough suppressants, antibiotics. [2]"]},{"key":"Every child with croup gets a steroid. Adrenaline is added when stridor at rest persists."}]},{"id":"stridor","h":"Other causes of stridor","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Clue","Think of","Action"],"r":[["Stridor after a choking episode","Inhaled foreign body","Urgent referral"],["Stridor with systemic signs of infection (looks toxic)","Epiglottitis, bacterial tracheitis or a deep neck abscess","Urgent escorted transfer"],["Stridor from the first weeks of life, high-pitched, on breathing in","Laryngomalacia","Refer. Settles by 1 to 2 years"],["Stridor starting at 6 to 8 weeks and worsening, on breathing in and out","Subglottic haemangioma","Refer"]]}},{"ul":["Croup may bring fever, but not the systemic signs of serious bacterial infection. [2]","Higher risk of severe croup: under 6 months, Down syndrome or other narrow airway, past severe croup, return visit within 24 hours. [2]"]}]},{"id":"epiglottitis","h":"Epiglottitis","body":[{"ul":["Now rare in children because of Hib vaccination, and seen more often in adults. [3]","The airway can close quickly. The priority is the airway, not the diagnosis. [3]","Arrange urgent escorted transfer to a hospital that can manage a difficult airway. [3]","Hospital treatment: intravenous ceftriaxone with flucloxacillin, or cefotaxime, and early ENT review. [3]","Other possibilities: anaphylaxis, a foreign body, severe pharyngitis. [3]"]},{"key":"Suspected epiglottitis: keep the patient calm, and move them with someone who can manage an airway."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A 4-month-old on day 2 of bronchiolitis feeding at a third of normal.","Parents asking for salbutamol or antibiotics for bronchiolitis.","A 7-week-old ex-premature infant with mild bronchiolitis.","A 2-year-old with a barking cough and no stridor at rest: what to give.","Stridor at rest that persists after the steroid.","A toddler with sudden stridor while eating peanuts.","A febrile, toxic-looking 5-year-old with stridor."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-kids-cough","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Cough and pneumonia in children","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["In children, chronic cough means more than 4 weeks, not 8.","A wet cough for 4 weeks in a well child is protracted bacterial bronchitis: amoxicillin with clavulanate for 2 weeks.","Most childhood pneumonia is viral. If an antibiotic is needed for mild disease: amoxicillin 25 mg/kg three times a day for 3 days.","No cough and cold medicines under 6 years. Honey only after 12 months.","Sudden cough while eating or playing is a foreign body until shown otherwise."],"curriculum":["Classify and assess cough in children and identify red flags.","Diagnose and treat protracted bacterial bronchitis and low-severity pneumonia.","Advise parents on cough medicines and when to return."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Cough in children. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Protracted bacterial bronchitis in children. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":3,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) in children 2 months or older. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"classify","h":"Classifying cough in children","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Type","Children","Adults, for comparison"],"r":[["Acute","Up to 2 weeks","Up to 3 weeks"],["In between","2 to 4 weeks (protracted acute)","3 to 8 weeks (subacute)"],["Chronic","More than 4 weeks","More than 8 weeks"]]}},{"ul":["Most acute cough is a viral infection. It settles in 7 to 10 days, and 9 in 10 children are cough-free by 3 weeks. [1]","Fever, weight loss or poor growth with a cough needs investigation or referral. [1]"]},{"key":"A child's cough becomes chronic at 4 weeks."}]},{"id":"acute","h":"Acute cough: what not to miss","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Clue","Think of","Action"],"r":[["Persistent fever, fast breathing at rest, increased effort, lethargy","Bacterial pneumonia","Assess severity"],["Sudden onset while eating or playing with small objects","Inhaled foreign body","Urgent referral"],["Barking cough","Croup","Oral steroid"],["Infant with runny nose, wheeze and crackles","Bronchiolitis","Supportive care"],["Bouts of coughing, whoop, vomiting after coughing","Pertussis","Swab for PCR"]]}}]},{"id":"chronic","h":"Chronic cough: causes","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Cause","Clue"],"r":[["Protracted bacterial bronchitis","Wet cough alone in a well child: the most likely cause"],["Asthma","Wheeze, breathlessness and night cough together"],["Tobacco smoke at home","Cough improves when carers stop smoking"],["Post-viral","Follows an infection; up to 8 weeks"],["Retained foreign body","Sudden onset: urgent referral"],["Bronchiectasis or cystic fibrosis","Recurrent wet cough, clubbing, chest deformity, poor growth"],["Tic (habit) cough","Suppressible, distractible, variable"],["Somatic cough","Odd honking cough; diagnosis of exclusion"],["Airway malformation","Tracheomalacia, vascular ring"],["Infection","Pertussis, tuberculosis, lung abscess"]]}},{"p":"Chronic cough that continues after a treatment trial is referred to a paediatric respiratory physician. [1]"}]},{"id":"pbb","h":"Protracted bacterial bronchitis","body":[{"ul":["Definition: a wet cough for 4 weeks or more that clears with 2 to 4 weeks of antibiotics. [2]","Who: any age, most often 5 years or younger. [2]","Picture: a rattly chest, cough on most days and worse lying flat, in a child who is otherwise well. [2]","A cough that is only ever dry excludes it. [2]","First exclude asthma, bronchiectasis, aspiration with feeds and a foreign body. [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Situation","Antibiotic (every 12 hours)"],"r":[["First line","Amoxicillin with clavulanate 22.5+3.2 mg/kg (maximum 875+125 mg)"],["Non-severe penicillin hypersensitivity","Cefuroxime 15 mg/kg (maximum 500 mg)"],["Severe penicillin hypersensitivity","Trimethoprim with sulfamethoxazole 4+20 mg/kg (maximum 160+800 mg)"]]}},{"tb":{"h":["At review","Action"],"r":[["Cough gone after 2 weeks","Diagnosis confirmed. Stop"],["Not gone after 2 weeks","Discuss with a paediatrician and continue to 4 weeks in total"],["Not gone after 4 weeks","Refer"],["Three or more episodes in a year","Refer, to look for bronchiectasis"]]}},{"key":"Two weeks of antibiotic, not five days. Shorter courses relapse."}]},{"id":"cap","h":"Pneumonia in children aged 2 months and over","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Severity","Features","Place of care"],"r":[["Low","Minimal fast breathing, normal pulse, saturation 95% or more","Home"],["Moderate","Increased effort, fast breathing and pulse, saturation 95% or lower","Hospital"],["High","Grunting or nasal flaring, marked distress, saturation 90% or lower","Intensive care"]]}},{"ul":["Most pneumonia in children is viral, and the examination cannot tell viral from bacterial. [3]","Under 12 months with cough, wheeze and distress: bronchiolitis is more likely. [3]","Widespread wheeze or crackles with no focal X-ray change points to a virus. [3]","A chest X-ray can be considered to confirm the diagnosis. [3]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Low-severity pneumonia","Treatment"],"r":[["All children","Paracetamol or ibuprofen for fever or pain; fluids"],["If an antibiotic is needed","Amoxicillin 25 mg/kg (maximum 1 g) every 8 hours for 3 days"],["Non-severe penicillin hypersensitivity","Cefuroxime 15 mg/kg (maximum 500 mg) every 12 hours for 3 days"],["Severe penicillin hypersensitivity","Azithromycin 10 mg/kg (maximum 500 mg) daily for 3 days"]]}},{"ul":["Review if not improving at 48 to 72 hours, or sooner if worse. [3]","Not improving: reassess the diagnosis and severity, and consider admission. [3]","Short courses of doxycycline do not stain teeth and can be used at any age. [3]"]},{"key":"Three days of amoxicillin for a child. Five days for an adult."}]},{"id":"meds","h":"Cough medicines for children","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Age","Advice"],"r":[["Under 12 months","No honey"],["Over 12 months","Honey may be tried"],["Under 6 years","No cough and cold medicines of any kind"],["6 to 11 years","Only on the advice of a doctor, pharmacist or nurse practitioner"]]}},{"ul":["These products have little or no proven benefit, cost money and can cause harm. [1]","Antibiotics are for bacterial pneumonia and bacterial causes of chronic cough only. [1]","Salt rooms and salt inhalation have no evidence. [1]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A 3-year-old with a wet cough for five weeks and a normal examination.","The antibiotic and duration for protracted bacterial bronchitis.","A wet cough that returns for the fourth time in a year.","A well 4-year-old with focal crackles and a saturation of 97%: drug, dose, days.","A parent asking for cough syrup for a 3-year-old.","A toddler who started coughing suddenly at a birthday party.","At what duration a child's cough counts as chronic."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-urti","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Sore throat and acute rhinosinusitis","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Most sore throats and nearly all 'sinus infections' are viral and settle without antibiotics.","Sore throat in someone at high risk of rheumatic fever: always give antibiotics.","Not at high risk: antibiotics only for severe symptoms or a scarlet fever rash.","When treating: phenoxymethylpenicillin 500 mg twice a day for 10 days.","Sinus symptoms: antibiotics only if very unwell, worse after improving, or no better at 10 days."],"curriculum":["Assess sore throat, identify people at high risk of acute rheumatic fever and prescribe appropriately.","Recognise airway and deep neck space emergencies.","Manage acute rhinosinusitis and recognise complications."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Sore throat. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Melbourne: Therapeutic Guidelines Limited.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Acute rhinosinusitis. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"danger","h":"Sore throat: who needs hospital now","body":[{"p":"These features suggest airway obstruction or a deep neck space infection. Transfer urgently. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Group","Feature"],"r":[["Voice and breathing","Muffled voice, stridor, snoring breath sounds, respiratory distress"],["Mouth","Drooling; unable to open the mouth fully (trismus)"],["Neck","Swelling, severe pain, head held twisted to one side"],["Throat","Pain on one side only"],["Systemic","Signs of sepsis"]]}},{"ul":["Causes include epiglottitis, peritonsillar abscess (quinsy), retropharyngeal abscess and severe glandular fever. [1]","Unable to swallow fluids, or severe pain: may also need hospital. [1]"]},{"key":"One-sided throat pain or trismus points to a deep neck infection such as a peritonsillar abscess."}]},{"id":"cause","h":"Viral or streptococcal?","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Suggests a virus","Suggests group A streptococcus"],"r":[["Cough","No cough"],["Runny or blocked nose","No nasal symptoms"],["Hoarse voice, conjunctivitis","Abrupt onset with fever over 38 degrees"],["Rash of a viral type, diarrhoea","Tender neck glands; exudate on the tonsils"],["Any age","School-age children and adolescents most often"]]}},{"ul":["Viruses are the commonest cause at every age. Symptoms settle within 7 days. [1]","Streptococcal sore throat is also self-limiting, lasting about 7 days. [1]","The concern with streptococcus is acute rheumatic fever in high-risk groups. [1]"]}]},{"id":"arf","h":"High risk of acute rheumatic fever","body":[{"p":"A person is at high risk if they are under 40 and any one of the following applies. [1]"},{"tb":{"h":["Group","Detail"],"r":[["Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people","Living in a rural or remote area"],["Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, Maori or Pacific people","Living with household overcrowding or socioeconomic disadvantage"],["Personal history","Past acute rheumatic fever or rheumatic heart disease"],["Household","A member with recent acute rheumatic fever or rheumatic heart disease"]]}},{"ul":["Also weigh: ages 5 to 20, overcrowding, and time spent in a high-rate setting. [1]","High-rate settings: northern Queensland, the Northern Territory, the Kimberley and Pilbara, and low and middle income countries. [1]"]},{"key":"High risk of rheumatic fever: treat every sore throat with antibiotics, even if it looks viral."}]},{"id":"decide","h":"Sore throat: antibiotics or not","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Patient","Looks viral","Looks streptococcal"],"r":[["High risk of rheumatic fever","Antibiotics","Antibiotics"],["Not high risk","Symptomatic care only","Symptomatic care; antibiotics only for severe symptoms or a scarlet fever rash"]]}},{"ul":["In people not at high risk, antibiotics shorten symptoms by less than a day. [1]","Antibiotics are not given to prevent post-streptococcal kidney disease. [1]","Take a throat swab if antibiotics are being considered, and review the decision with the result. [1]","On benzathine benzylpenicillin prophylaxis with a new sore throat: treat if the last dose was 7 or more days ago. [1]","Ask the person to return if not improving in 3 to 7 days, or if worse. [1]"]}]},{"id":"treat","h":"Sore throat: treatment","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Situation","Antibiotic","Duration"],"r":[["First line","Phenoxymethylpenicillin 500 mg (child 15 mg/kg) every 12 hours","10 days"],["Child who cannot take it","Amoxicillin 50 mg/kg (maximum 1 g) once daily","10 days"],["Adherence doubtful","Benzathine benzylpenicillin by intramuscular injection: adult 1.2 million units","Single dose"],["Non-severe penicillin hypersensitivity","Cefalexin 1 g (child 25 mg/kg) every 12 hours","10 days"],["Severe penicillin hypersensitivity","Azithromycin 500 mg (child 12 mg/kg) daily","5 days"]]}},{"ul":["Ten days is needed to clear streptococcus and prevent rheumatic fever. [1]","Not at high risk and treated for symptoms: stopping at 5 days is reasonable once better. [1]","Everyone: paracetamol or an NSAID. Lozenges or sprays for adults and adolescents. [1]","Severe pain: a single dose of dexamethasone 10 mg, or prednisolone 50 mg for 1 to 2 days. [1]","School and childcare: stay home while unwell, and for 24 hours after the first antibiotic dose if streptococcus is confirmed. [1]"]},{"key":"Penicillin V, twice a day, ten days."}]},{"id":"sinus","h":"Acute rhinosinusitis","body":[{"ul":["Diagnosis: two or more of blocked nose, nasal discharge, facial pain or pressure, and reduced smell. [2]","No imaging is needed. [2]","Cause: a virus. Fewer than 2% develop bacterial infection. [2]","Course: often worse after day 5, better by 7 to 14 days. [2]","Longer than 12 weeks is chronic rhinosinusitis. [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Symptom relief","Detail"],"r":[["Analgesia","Paracetamol or an NSAID"],["Saline","Spray or rinse"],["Intranasal corticosteroid","Daily"],["Decongestant spray","Oxymetazoline for 3 to 5 days at most; longer causes rebound congestion"],["Oral antihistamine","Only if allergy is contributing"]]}},{"p":"Antibiotics may help a little between days 3 and 7, make no difference by day 10, and do not prevent complications. Consider them only in three situations. [2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Consider antibiotics if","Regimen"],"r":[["Systemically very unwell","Amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours for 5 days"],["Fever or facial pain worsening after first improving","Same"],["No improvement after 10 days of symptomatic care","Same"]]}},{"ul":["Children: amoxicillin 15 mg/kg every 8 hours. [2]","Penicillin hypersensitivity: cefuroxime if non-severe, doxycycline if severe. [2]"]},{"key":"Sinus symptoms under 10 days and improving: no antibiotics."}]},{"id":"sinuscomp","h":"Complicated rhinosinusitis","body":[{"p":"Any of these means infection has spread beyond the sinuses. The person needs intravenous antibiotics and urgent surgical review. [2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Site","Feature"],"r":[["Eye","Swollen or red eyelids, bulging eye, double vision, reduced vision, pain on eye movement"],["Brain","Confusion, drowsiness, neck stiffness, severe headache, light sensitivity"],["Nerves","Cranial nerve palsy"]]}},{"ul":["It is rare, arises early in the illness, and is not prevented by oral antibiotics. [2]","Immune compromise with sinus symptoms: think invasive fungal infection. [2]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["An 8-year-old Aboriginal child in a remote community with a sore throat and a cough.","A 30-year-old city office worker with tonsillar exudate and no cough.","The antibiotic, dose and duration for streptococcal tonsillitis.","A sore throat with trismus and a muffled voice.","Blocked nose and facial pressure for six days in a well adult.","Sinus symptoms that improved, then fever and pain returned on day 8.","Eyelid swelling and double vision with sinusitis."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-flu","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Influenza and pertussis","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Influenza antivirals are for people admitted to hospital and for pregnancy, and are considered for others at high risk.","Oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days, started within 48 hours if possible.","Pertussis: a cough for more than 2 weeks with coughing bouts, a whoop or vomiting.","Treat pertussis only within 3 weeks of onset: azithromycin for 5 days. Stay away from others until 5 days of treatment.","Pertussis is notifiable. Prophylaxis is aimed at protecting infants under 6 months."],"curriculum":["Diagnose influenza and decide who benefits from antiviral treatment or prophylaxis.","Diagnose, treat and notify pertussis, and manage contacts.","Advise on vaccination and exclusion."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Influenza. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Pertussis. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"fludx","h":"Influenza: diagnosis","body":[{"ul":["Picture: sudden cough, fever, headache, muscle aches and marked malaise. [1]","In season it is a clinical diagnosis. Cough with fever is the most telling pair. [1]","It cannot be told apart from COVID-19 on symptoms alone. [1]","Being vaccinated this year does not rule it out. [1]","Most illness lasts under 7 days. [1]","Test when the result changes management or infection control: PCR on a nose and throat swab. [1]","Test everyone admitted to hospital and anyone with immune compromise. [1]","Rapid antigen tests are specific but miss cases. [1]"]},{"key":"A vaccinated person can still have influenza."}]},{"id":"flutreat","h":"Influenza: who gets an antiviral","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Group","Antiviral?"],"r":[["Healthy adult, low risk","Not routinely: about 1 day less illness if started within 48 hours"],["Admitted to hospital","Recommended, up to 7 days after onset"],["Pregnant, or up to 2 weeks after birth","Recommended"],["Other higher-risk groups","Consider"]]}},{"tb":{"h":["Higher-risk groups","Detail"],"r":[["Age","65 and over; under 5, especially under 2"],["Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people","Any age"],["Residential care","Aged care and long-term facilities"],["Chronic disease","Heart, lung, kidney or neurological disease; diabetes; immune compromise"]]}},{"tb":{"h":["Drug","Treatment dose"],"r":[["Oseltamivir","75 mg every 12 hours for 5 days (children by weight)"],["Baloxavir","Single dose, age 12 and over: 40 mg if under 80 kg, 80 mg if 80 kg or more"],["Zanamivir","10 mg inhaled, age 5 and over"]]}},{"ul":["Pregnancy: use a neuraminidase inhibitor such as oseltamivir. There are no data for baloxavir. [1]","Antiviral side effects: nausea, vomiting, headache and neuropsychiatric events. [1]","Everyone: rest, fluids and paracetamol. [1]","No aspirin under 16 years, because of Reye syndrome. [1]","Bacterial pneumonia can follow influenza, from pneumococcus or Staphylococcus aureus. [1]"]},{"key":"Pregnant with influenza: treat with oseltamivir."}]},{"id":"fluprev","h":"Influenza: prevention","body":[{"ul":["Yearly vaccination is the most important measure. [1]","Post-exposure prophylaxis is considered for high-risk people exposed in the past 48 hours who are not protected by vaccine. [1]","Not protected means: not vaccinated this season, vaccinated under 2 weeks ago, unlikely to respond, or strain mismatch. [1]","Also used in outbreaks in hospitals and residential care. [1]","Prophylaxis dose: oseltamivir 75 mg once daily for 10 days. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Oseltamivir","Dose","Duration"],"r":[["Treatment","75 mg twice daily","5 days"],["Prophylaxis","75 mg once daily","10 days"]]}}]},{"id":"pertdx","h":"Pertussis: diagnosis","body":[{"ul":["Classic: cough for more than 2 weeks with bouts of coughing, a whoop on breathing in, or vomiting after coughing. [2]","Adults and older children may have a persistent cough and nothing else. [2]","Infants under 12 months are at highest risk of death. They may present with apnoea. [2]","Test: PCR on a posterior nasopharyngeal swab or aspirate. [2]","PCR is most sensitive in the first 3 to 4 weeks of cough. [2]","Notify the public health unit. [2]"]},{"key":"A whoop is not needed. Two weeks of coughing bouts is enough to test."}]},{"id":"perttreat","h":"Pertussis: treatment","body":[{"p":"Antibiotics do not clearly shorten the illness. They clear the organism and stop spread. [2]"},{"tb":{"h":["Timing","Action"],"r":[["Within 3 weeks of cough onset","Treat"],["More than 3 weeks","No antibiotic: rarely infectious"],["On treatment","Avoid others, especially infants, until 5 days have been taken"]]}},{"tb":{"h":["Patient","First line","Alternatives"],"r":[["Adult","Azithromycin 500 mg on day 1, then 250 mg daily for 4 days","Clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily for 7 days; trimethoprim with sulfamethoxazole for 7 days"],["Child 6 months and over","Azithromycin 10 mg/kg on day 1, then 5 mg/kg daily for 4 days","As above, by weight"],["Infant under 6 months","Azithromycin 10 mg/kg daily for 5 days","May need hospital"]]}},{"ul":["Roxithromycin has no evidence. Erythromycin is poorly tolerated. [2]"]}]},{"id":"pertcontacts","h":"Pertussis: contacts","body":[{"ul":["A contact: within 1 metre of an infectious case for more than 1 hour. [2]","Infectious: less than 3 weeks since onset and fewer than 5 days of antibiotics. [2]","Prophylaxis exists to protect infants under 6 months. [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Give prophylaxis to contacts who are","Notes"],"r":[["Infants under 6 months",""],["In the last month of pregnancy",""],["Living with an infant under 6 months or with someone in the last month of pregnancy","If the case lives there too, treat the whole household"],["In childcare with the case","Ask the public health unit"]]}},{"ul":["Regimen: the same as for treatment. [2]","Start within 14 days of first contact. Up to 21 days for the highest-risk contacts. [2]","Check that vaccinations are up to date. [2]"]},{"key":"Pertussis prophylaxis is about the baby in the house."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A pregnant woman with influenza on day 2.","A healthy 30-year-old with influenza asking for an antiviral on day 4.","An aged-care resident exposed to a roommate with influenza.","A father with three weeks of coughing fits and a newborn at home.","Which swab to take for pertussis.","A patient with pertussis diagnosed five weeks into the cough.","When a child with pertussis can return to school."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-bronch","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Bronchiectasis","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Think of it with a chronic wet cough, coarse crackles and chest infections that keep coming back.","High-resolution CT makes the diagnosis. A chest X-ray can be normal.","Airway clearance and exercise are the core of treatment, not inhalers.","Antibiotics are for exacerbations: 3 or more of six features for at least 48 hours. Send sputum first.","Empirical choice: amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours or doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours, guided by past cultures."],"curriculum":["Recognise and investigate bronchiectasis in adults and children.","Manage stable bronchiectasis in general practice and know when to refer.","Treat exacerbations with antibiotics only when criteria are met."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Bronchiectasis. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Antibiotic management of bronchiectasis in adults. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published December 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"suspect","h":"When to suspect it","body":[{"ul":["Bronchiectasis is abnormally widened airways with chronic cough and repeated bronchial infection. [1]","Typical picture: daily productive cough, purulent sputum, recurrent or slow-to-clear chest infections. [1]","Sputum may be blood-streaked. Fatigue, breathlessness and pleuritic pain also occur. [1]","Coarse crackles are common. Clubbing is seen in under 5%. [1]","Also suspect it when sputum grows Pseudomonas aeruginosa or Haemophilus influenzae. [1]","It often coexists with asthma, COPD, sinusitis, reflux, anxiety or depression. [1]"]},{"key":"Wet cough, coarse crackles and recurrent infections: think bronchiectasis."}]},{"id":"diagnose","h":"Making the diagnosis","body":[{"ul":["High-resolution CT is the gold standard. The bronchus is wider than its neighbouring vessel. [1]","A chest X-ray helps with other causes of cough but may be normal. [1]","Spirometry is normal in about half. Only about a third show airflow obstruction. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["If the cause is unclear, test","Looking for"],"r":[["Full blood count with differential","Clues to infection or immune disease"],["Total IgE and Aspergillus IgE and IgG","Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis"],["IgG, IgA and IgM","Antibody deficiency"],["Sputum culture, including mycobacteria","Colonising organisms, nontuberculous mycobacteria"],["Sweat chloride","Cystic fibrosis: children, and adults with suggestive symptoms"],["Rheumatoid factor, antinuclear antibodies","Connective tissue disease, when the history fits"]]}},{"ul":["Most cases are idiopathic or follow infection. Pneumonia, often in childhood, is the commonest cause. [1]"]}]},{"id":"kids","h":"Children","body":[{"ul":["Airway widening may be reversible in children if found and treated early. [1]","Refer to a paediatric specialist for: a wet cough over 4 weeks despite antibiotics, or recurrent episodes each over 4 weeks. [1]","Also refer for clubbing, chest wall deformity, persistent crackles, recurrent pneumonia, or X-ray changes that persist. [1]","Children are more sensitive to radiation. The specialist decides on CT. [1]"]}]},{"id":"manage","h":"Stable disease","body":[{"ul":["Aims: fewer symptoms, fewer exacerbations, slower progression. [1]","Airway clearance: refer to a respiratory physiotherapist for an individual program. [1]","Exercise regularly. Refer for pulmonary rehabilitation if exercise capacity is reduced. [1]","Give a written action plan. [1]","Pneumococcal and yearly influenza vaccines. Stop smoking. Look after nutrition. [1]","Spirometry once a year. [1]","Do not routinely prescribe bronchodilators or inhaled corticosteroids unless asthma or COPD is also present. [1]","Nebulised hypertonic saline can cause bronchospasm. It is started after a supervised challenge. [1]","Do not treat colonising organisms in a stable patient. [2]"]},{"key":"Physiotherapy and exercise first. Inhalers only for another indication."}]},{"id":"exac","h":"Exacerbations","body":[{"ul":["An exacerbation is a deterioration from baseline lasting 48 hours or more that needs a change in management. [2]","Consider antibiotics when 3 or more of these are present for at least 48 hours: [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["The six features"],"r":[["More cough"],["More sputum, or thicker sputum"],["More purulent sputum"],["More breathlessness or less exercise tolerance"],["Fatigue or malaise"],["Haemoptysis"]]}},{"ul":["Collect sputum for culture before the first dose. [2]","Check the last 12 months of sputum results and choose the antibiotic to match. [2]","No recent results: amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours, or doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours. [2]","Recent beta-lactamase-producing Haemophilus or Moraxella: amoxicillin with clavulanate 875+125 mg every 12 hours. [2]","Penicillin hypersensitivity: doxycycline. [2]","The picture can mimic pneumonia. Consider a chest X-ray. [2]","Hospital and intravenous therapy for worsening respiratory distress or new hypoxaemia, or failure of oral treatment. [2]"]}]},{"id":"refer","h":"Referral and haemoptysis","body":[{"ul":["Refer adults with rapid progression, a hospital admission, severe symptoms or poor response to treatment. [1]","Refer for more than three exacerbations a year. Long-term macrolides are a specialist decision. [1]","Refer when sputum grows Pseudomonas aeruginosa or nontuberculous mycobacteria. [1]","Streaks of blood in purulent sputum are common. Assess for infection and review NSAIDs and anticoagulants. [1]","Recurrent small bleeds of bright blood: specialist assessment. [1]","Massive haemoptysis: immediate transfer to hospital. [1]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where sources differ","body":[{"ul":["The 2020 Respiratory topic asks for all three of more sputum, more purulence and more cough before antibiotics. [1]","The December 2025 Antibiotic topic asks for 3 or more of six features for at least 48 hours. [2]","This page follows the 2025 topic because it is more recent."]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A woman with years of wet cough, a normal chest X-ray and normal spirometry.","Which test confirms bronchiectasis.","A stable patient whose sputum grows Haemophilus influenzae.","An exacerbation: what to do before the first antibiotic dose.","A request for a preventer inhaler in a patient with no asthma or COPD.","A child with a wet cough that has not cleared after 4 weeks of antibiotics.","Four exacerbations in a year."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-tb","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Tuberculosis","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Suspect pulmonary tuberculosis with possible exposure and a cough over 3 weeks, fever, night sweats or weight loss.","Ask the laboratory for acid-fast bacilli microscopy, mycobacterial culture and PCR. Routine culture will miss it.","Standard treatment is 6 months: four drugs for 2 months, then isoniazid and rifampicin for 4 months.","Stop all drugs for jaundice, or transaminases 5 times normal, or 3 times normal with symptoms.","A positive skin test or interferon gamma release assay means: exclude active disease first, then consider preventive treatment."],"curriculum":["Recognise and investigate suspected tuberculosis and notify it.","Share care during treatment: adherence, adverse effects and monitoring.","Interpret screening for tuberculosis infection and know who is offered preventive treatment."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Clinical presentations of tuberculosis. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published April 2026.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Investigations for tuberculosis. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published April 2026.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":3,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Principles of managing tuberculosis. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published April 2026.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":4,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Short-course treatments for tuberculosis. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published April 2026.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":5,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Monitoring tuberculosis treatment. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published April 2026.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":6,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Tuberculosis infection (previously known as latent tuberculosis). In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic. Published April 2026.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"suspect","h":"When to suspect it","body":[{"ul":["Pulmonary disease is the usual form, classically upper lobe with cavities. [1]","Suspect it in someone who may have been exposed and has a cough over 3 weeks, fevers, night sweats or weight loss. [1]","Disease outside the lungs is more likely in children, people with HIV and people with immune compromise. [1]","Lymph nodes are a common site. [1]","Genitourinary disease: frequency, dysuria, haematuria or sterile pyuria, usually without fever or weight loss. [1]","Central nervous system disease, including meningitis, carries high mortality. [1]"]},{"key":"Sterile pyuria in someone with risk factors: think genitourinary tuberculosis."}]},{"id":"test","h":"Investigation","body":[{"ul":["Standard microscopy and culture do not usually detect it. Name tuberculosis on the request. [2]","Ask for three things: acid-fast bacilli microscopy, mycobacterial culture and PCR. [2]","Rapid PCR can also detect drug resistance. [2]","Pulmonary: chest X-ray, plus sputum for microscopy and rapid PCR. Culture confirms and tests susceptibility. [2]","No sputum: bronchoscopy may be needed. [2]","Children under 10 rarely produce sputum. Gastric aspirates, induced sputum or other samples are used. [2]","Lymph node: fine-needle aspirate or excision for histology, culture and PCR. [2]","Genitourinary: large-volume early-morning urine for microscopy, culture and PCR. [2]"]}]},{"id":"publichealth","h":"Notification and infection control","body":[{"ul":["Management is specialist-led. Notify the public health authority promptly. [3]","Contact tracing is usually run by the tuberculosis program. [3]","Pulmonary disease is usually infectious. [3]","Fully susceptible disease is usually non-infectious after 5 days of daily standard treatment. [3]","Children under 10 are usually considered non-infectious. [3]","Disease outside the lungs, without lung involvement, is generally not an infection risk. [3]"]}]},{"id":"treat","h":"Standard treatment","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Phase","Drugs","Length"],"r":[["Intensive","Isoniazid, rifampicin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide","2 months"],["Continuation","Isoniazid, rifampicin","4 months"]]}},{"ul":["Cure rates exceed 98% for drug-susceptible disease. [4]","Before starting: weight, liver and kidney function, full blood count, visual acuity and colour vision. [3]","Also test for HIV and hepatitis B and C. Tuberculosis is an indicator condition for HIV testing. [3]","Discuss contraception. Rifampicin interacts with oral contraceptives. [3]","Pyridoxine is given with isoniazid to people at risk of peripheral neuropathy. [4]","Adherence matters most: education, close follow-up and dose aids. [3]","Directly observed therapy has not been shown to improve outcomes in most cases. [3]","Interruptions under 2 weeks do not extend the course. [5]"]}]},{"id":"monitor","h":"Monitoring and adverse effects","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Symptom","Think of","Usual drug"],"r":[["Nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, jaundice","Hepatotoxicity","Isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide"],["Blurred vision or altered colour vision","Optic neuritis","Ethambutol"],["Petechial rash","Thrombocytopenia","Rifampicin"],["Severe or blistering rash","Severe cutaneous reaction","Rifampicin most often"]]}},{"ul":["Liver function in adults: baseline, then 2, 4 and 8 weeks, then every 1 to 2 months. [5]","A minor rise in transaminases is common and treatment continues. [5]","Stop all drugs for clinical jaundice. [5]","Stop all drugs for transaminases 5 times the upper limit, or 3 times with symptoms. [5]","Drugs are reintroduced once ALT falls below 2 times the upper limit. [5]","Ethambutol: check visual acuity and colour vision monthly. It accumulates in kidney impairment. [5]","Any visual change: stop ethambutol at once and refer to an ophthalmologist. [5]","After treatment, look for lasting lung disease such as bronchiectasis. [3]"]},{"key":"Nausea on tuberculosis treatment is hepatitis until liver tests say otherwise."}]},{"id":"infection","h":"Tuberculosis infection (latent)","body":[{"ul":["Infection without disease has no symptoms. About 5 to 10% progress to active disease in their lifetime. [6]","It is found by a tuberculin skin test or an interferon gamma release assay. [6]","Usual settings: contact tracing, screening before immunosuppression, and migrant or workplace screening. [6]","A positive test: exclude active disease with history, examination and chest X-ray. Sputum if productive. [6]","Either test can be falsely negative in immune compromise, HIV and children under 5. [6]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Preventive treatment is considered for a positive test in"],"r":[["People with HIV"],["Recent converters, within 2 years"],["Close contacts of smear-positive pulmonary disease"],["Children"],["Healthcare workers"],["People with, or about to have, immune suppression"],["Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people in communities with local transmission"]]}},{"ul":["Adult options: rifampicin daily for 4 months, or rifampicin with isoniazid daily for 3 months. [6]","Isoniazid alone for 9 months is second line. [6]","Choice weighs the liver risk of isoniazid, which rises with age, against rifampicin interactions. [6]","If treatment is declined, watch for active disease. Close contacts are followed for at least 2 years. [6]","In pregnancy, treatment is often deferred until after birth. Seek expert advice. [6]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A recent migrant with two months of cough, night sweats and weight loss.","What to write on the sputum request.","A positive interferon gamma release assay before starting a biologic.","Nausea and vomiting in week 3 of treatment.","Blurred vision on four-drug treatment.","A woman on the oral contraceptive pill starting rifampicin.","Sterile pyuria in a man with urinary frequency."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-pleural","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Sudden pleuritic pain and breathlessness: think pneumothorax and get an inspiratory chest X-ray.","Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis in an unstable patient. Decompress with a needle; do not wait for an X-ray.","Primary spontaneous pneumothorax of any size starts with conservative care if the patient is stable.","Lung disease underneath changes everything: hospital and usually a chest drain.","A pleural effusion is sampled under ultrasound to find the cause."],"curriculum":["Recognise and give emergency treatment for tension pneumothorax.","Assess and arrange care for spontaneous pneumothorax, and advise on recurrence.","Investigate a pleural effusion."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Pneumothorax. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Pleural effusion in adults. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"types","h":"Types and diagnosis","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Type","Meaning"],"r":[["Primary spontaneous","No lung disease"],["Secondary spontaneous","Lung disease present, most often COPD"],["Traumatic","Chest injury, or a medical procedure"]]}},{"ul":["Usual story: sudden pleuritic chest pain and breathlessness. [1]","Signs depend on size and can be hard to find. [1]","Large: absent breath sounds, hyperresonance, less chest movement, trachea pushed to the other side. [1]","Test: an inspiratory chest X-ray. [1]","Expiratory films are not recommended. They exaggerate the size. [1]","CT may be needed if the patient cannot sit upright or has lung disease. [1]"]}]},{"id":"tension","h":"Tension pneumothorax","body":[{"ul":["A rapid, large pneumothorax in an unstable patient: severe breathlessness, hypoxaemia, hypotension, collapse. [1]","The diagnosis is clinical, not radiological. [1]","It usually follows trauma or lung disease. It is very rare in primary spontaneous pneumothorax. [1]","Think of it when a patient on positive pressure ventilation suddenly deteriorates. [1]","Treatment: urgent needle decompression, then a chest drain. [1]","Site: above the third rib in the midclavicular line. [1]","Do not delay for sterile equipment or local anaesthetic. [1]","Mediastinal shift on X-ray in a stable patient is not a reason to decompress. [1]"]},{"key":"Unstable with a pneumothorax: needle first, X-ray later."}]},{"id":"primary","h":"Primary spontaneous pneumothorax","body":[{"ul":["Usually not dangerous. Symptoms often settle in 24 to 48 hours. [1]","First make sure there is no underlying lung disease. [1]","All sizes are managed conservatively at first: analgesia, and oxygen only if hypoxaemic. [1]","Analgesia often relieves the breathlessness, which is largely pain on breathing. [1]","Aspiration or a drain is for the clinically unstable patient. [1]","In a trial, conservative care meant fewer hospital days, less surgery and less early recurrence. [1]","After discharge, review every 2 weeks until the X-ray has cleared. [1]"]},{"key":"Stable primary pneumothorax: pain relief and observation, whatever the size."}]},{"id":"recur","h":"Recurrence","body":[{"ul":["About 30 to 50% recur. [1]","Continuing to smoke raises the risk. Advise stopping. [1]","A recurrence in the same lung makes further episodes much more likely. Refer for pleurodesis. [1]"]}]},{"id":"secondary","h":"Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax","body":[{"ul":["Lung disease leaves less reserve. Admission and early intervention are more often needed. [1]","Start with analgesia, and oxygen if hypoxaemic. [1]","A chest drain is usually recommended. Aspiration is less likely to work. [1]","Do not use CPAP or bilevel ventilation with an untreated pneumothorax. [1]"]}]},{"id":"effusion","h":"Pleural effusion","body":[{"ul":["Management depends on the cause. [2]","First question: transudate or exudate, decided on pleural fluid by Light's criteria. [2]","Most transudates come from heart failure, cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome or peritoneal dialysis. [2]","Sample by aspiration under direct ultrasound guidance. [2]","Send fluid for biochemistry (pH, LDH, protein, glucose), cytology and culture, including mycobacteria. [2]","Note the look of the fluid: clear, cloudy, bloody or pus. [2]","A malignant effusion signals a poor prognosis and commonly refills after drainage. [2]","Options for a recurrent malignant effusion: pleurodesis or an indwelling tunnelled catheter. [2]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A tall young man with sudden pleuritic pain who is comfortable at rest.","A patient with COPD and a small pneumothorax.","A collapsed patient after chest trauma: where the needle goes.","Which X-ray view to request.","A second pneumothorax on the same side.","A new one-sided effusion: how to sample it and what to send."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-osa","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Ask about snoring, witnessed apnoeas, waking choking and daytime sleepiness. Ask the bed partner too.","A GP can order a subsidised sleep study if the Epworth score is 8 or more and a screening questionnaire is positive.","More than 5 events an hour is significant; more than 30 is severe.","CPAP is the most effective treatment. Weight loss and less alcohol help everyone.","Severe daytime sleepiness: no driving until stable on treatment."],"curriculum":["Recognise obstructive sleep apnoea and its associated conditions.","Order and interpret a sleep study.","Manage treatment, including advice on driving."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"suspect","h":"When to suspect it","body":[{"ul":["Typical patient: overweight, middle-aged, male, with a short thick neck. [1]","Women and thin young people get it too, especially with a narrow airway. [1]","Night: loud snoring, witnessed pauses in breathing, waking with choking, nocturia, sweating. [1]","Day: sleepiness, irritability, poor concentration, morning headache. [1]","Also reflux and erectile dysfunction. [1]","Ask the bed partner. Patients often underrate their own sleepiness. [1]","A family history of sleep apnoea makes it more likely. [1]","Nearly all adults with Down syndrome have it. Consider it even without symptoms. [1]"]}]},{"id":"risks","h":"Why it matters","body":[{"ul":["Untreated disease is linked to hypertension and to arrhythmias such as paroxysmal atrial fibrillation. [1]","It is linked to cardiovascular and cerebrovascular death. [1]","In pregnancy it is linked to hypertension and pre-eclampsia. [1]","Motor vehicle crashes, especially single-vehicle crashes at night. [1]","Hypertension in a younger patient should prompt the question. [1]"]}]},{"id":"test","h":"Testing","body":[{"ul":["The Epworth Sleepiness Scale measures daytime sleepiness. [1]","STOP-Bang, OSA50 and the Berlin questionnaire estimate the chance of moderate to severe disease. [1]","The diagnostic test is an overnight sleep study, at home or in a laboratory. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["A GP can order a subsidised study when","Threshold"],"r":[["Epworth Sleepiness Scale","8 or more"],["plus one of: STOP-Bang","4 or more"],["OSA50","5 or more"],["Berlin questionnaire","High risk"]]}},{"ul":["Strong suspicion without meeting these scores: refer to a sleep or respiratory physician. [1]","A home study suits most people. [1]","Choose a laboratory study for heart failure, lung or neuromuscular disease, suspected respiratory failure, or disability. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Apnoea-hypopnoea index","Meaning"],"r":[["More than 5 an hour","Significant sleep apnoea"],["More than 30 an hour","Severe"]]}}]},{"id":"general","h":"Measures for everyone","body":[{"ul":["Weight loss. [1]","Stop smoking. [1]","Avoid alcohol and sedating drugs. [1]","Good sleep habits. Sleep loss worsens daytime function. [1]","An intranasal corticosteroid lowers nasal resistance. [1]","Sleep on the side if apnoea is mainly when supine. Raise the bed head 5 to 8 cm. [1]","These may be enough for mild disease with no sleepiness and no hypertension. [1]","Nasal strips and anti-snoring pillows have little evidence. [1]"]}]},{"id":"cpap","h":"CPAP and alternatives","body":[{"ul":["CPAP splints the upper airway open. It is the most effective treatment. [1]","Consider it for severe disease, for moderate disease with daytime symptoms, and for sleep apnoea with hypertension. [1]","It lowers blood pressure. A benefit on cardiovascular events has not been shown in a randomised trial. [1]","Mild to moderate disease without daytime symptoms: watching and general measures are reasonable. [1]","Dry nose: add humidification or an intranasal corticosteroid. [1]","Dry mouth means a mouth leak: try a chin strap. [1]","A mandibular advancement splint suits mild to moderate disease. A dentist fits and reviews it. [1]","Surgery is for selected anatomy. Palatal surgery is rarely used now. [1]"]}]},{"id":"drive","h":"Driving","body":[{"ul":["Sleepiness raises crash risk. [1]","Severe daytime sleepiness: stop driving and operating machinery until stable on treatment. [1]","Weigh other factors too: shift work, sleep loss, alcohol and sedating medicines. [1]","Use the Austroads standards, and seek expert advice if unsure. [1]"]},{"key":"Sleepy drivers stop driving until treatment is working."}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A truck driver who snores and nods off at traffic lights.","Whether a GP can order the sleep study directly.","A sleep study result of 12 events an hour in a patient with no symptoms.","A patient who cannot tolerate CPAP because of a dry mouth.","A man of 32 with hypertension who snores.","An adult with Down syndrome and new daytime behaviour change."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-ild","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Interstitial and occupational lung disease","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Slowly worsening breathlessness with a dry cough and fine 'velcro' crackles: think interstitial lung disease.","Spirometry is restrictive: low FVC with a normal or high ratio.","The history finds the cause: drugs, jobs, birds, smoking, joint and skin disease.","Refer every suspected case. High-resolution CT is the key test.","Ask every stonemason and benchtop worker about silica dust."],"curriculum":["Recognise interstitial lung disease and begin investigation.","Identify drug, occupational and environmental causes.","Share care in pulmonary fibrosis, sarcoidosis and occupational lung disease."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Interstitial lung disease. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"suspect","h":"When to suspect it","body":[{"ul":["Symptoms are non-specific: breathlessness on exertion and a dry cough. Diagnosis is often late. [1]","Fine, velcro-like crackles. Coarser crackles suggest bronchiectasis. [1]","Clubbing occurs in a minority, mostly in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. [1]","Look at the hands and skin for connective tissue disease: Raynaud phenomenon, tight skin, arthritis. [1]","Late disease: fast breathing, fast pulse, low oxygen saturation. [1]"]}]},{"id":"cause","h":"Finding the cause","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Ask about","Because"],"r":[["Medicines","Amiodarone, methotrexate, leflunomide, nitrofurantoin and some cancer drugs"],["Work","Coal, asbestos, silica: miners and stonemasons"],["Home and hobbies","Birds, compost, spas: hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],["Smoking","Smoking-related disease and idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"],["Joints, skin, muscles","Rheumatoid arthritis, systemic sclerosis, polymyositis"],["Radiotherapy to the chest","Radiation injury"],["Family","Familial disease presents younger"]]}},{"ul":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is typical over 65 and in men who have smoked. [1]","Connective tissue disease and sarcoidosis are commoner in younger people. [1]"]}]},{"id":"workup","h":"First tests and referral","body":[{"ul":["In the practice: oxygen saturation, spirometry, chest X-ray and blood tests. [1]","Spirometry is restrictive: reduced FVC, with a normal or raised FEV1/FVC ratio. [1]","Bloods: full blood count with eosinophils, ANA, ENA, anti-dsDNA, rheumatoid factor and calcium. [1]","Exclude other causes of breathlessness such as anaemia and heart failure. [1]","A chest X-ray can be normal in early disease. [1]","High-resolution CT is the pivotal test, usually after an abnormal X-ray or spirometry. [1]","Refer all suspected cases. Diagnosis is made by a multidisciplinary team. [1]","A 10% fall in FVC suggests progression. [1]"]},{"key":"Low FVC with a normal ratio is restriction, not COPD."}]},{"id":"ipf","h":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","body":[{"ul":["Progressive fibrosis of unknown cause. Untreated, death usually comes within 3 to 5 years. [1]","CT shows the usual interstitial pneumonia pattern: basal, subpleural reticulation with honeycombing. [1]","Biopsy is not needed in most cases. [1]","Nintedanib and pirfenidone slow progression. They are specialist drugs with eligibility criteria. [1]","Prednisolone with azathioprine and acetylcysteine is no longer used. It increased deaths. [1]","Also: stop smoking, pulmonary rehabilitation, and oxygen for resting hypoxaemia. [1]","Opioids can ease breathlessness. Refer early for transplant assessment and palliative care. [1]","Treat reflux, heart disease and sleep-disordered breathing. [1]","An acute exacerbation with respiratory failure carries a mortality over 50%. [1]"]}]},{"id":"sarcoid","h":"Sarcoidosis","body":[{"ul":["A multi-organ granulomatous disease of unknown cause. The lungs are involved in up to 90%. [1]","It is often found by chance as enlarged hilar lymph nodes. [1]","Symptoms are exertional breathlessness and cough. Clubbing is not a feature. [1]","Many cases resolve without treatment, so observation usually comes first. [1]","Oral corticosteroids are for progressive symptoms or falling lung function. [1]","Look outside the lungs: eyes, skin, heart, nerves and kidneys. [1]","Check calcium. Hypercalcaemia can occur. Consider an eye review. [1]"]}]},{"id":"exposure","h":"Exposure-related disease","body":[{"ul":["Hypersensitivity pneumonitis follows repeated inhalation of an antigen: bird fancier's lung, hot tub lung. [1]","Treatment is avoiding the exposure. No cause is found in about 40%. [1]","Silicosis now comes mostly from cutting artificial stone benchtops, especially dry cutting. [1]","It ranges from symptomless nodules to progressive massive fibrosis and death. [1]","Asbestosis and coal worker's pneumoconiosis follow asbestos and coal dust. [1]","Smoking-related interstitial disease is treated first by stopping smoking. [1]","Take a full job history, past and present, from every patient with unexplained breathlessness. [1]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A 70-year-old ex-smoker with a dry cough, clubbing and basal crackles.","Reading restrictive spirometry.","A woman on nitrofurantoin prophylaxis with new breathlessness.","A 30-year-old stonemason with a normal examination.","Hilar lymphadenopathy found by chance in a well young adult.","A pigeon breeder with breathlessness and squeaks on auscultation."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-rhinitis","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Allergic rhinitis","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Sneezing, itch, a runny or blocked nose on both sides, often with itchy eyes. Always ask about asthma.","One-sided blockage, pain, or bloody discharge is not hay fever. Look for another cause.","Mild or intermittent: a less-sedating oral antihistamine.","Moderate to severe, or a blocked nose: an intranasal corticosteroid, used daily with good technique.","Decongestants have no role. Refer for immunotherapy when sprays and tablets are not enough."],"curriculum":["Diagnose allergic rhinitis and recognise features of another cause.","Choose and teach treatment, including nasal spray technique.","Identify coexisting asthma and risk of thunderstorm asthma, and know when to refer."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Allergic rhinitis. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Last updated June 2026.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"dx","h":"Diagnosis","body":[{"ul":["About one in five Australians has it. [1]","Symptoms: sneezing, runny nose, itchy nose and palate, blockage, mouth breathing, snoring, reduced smell. [1]","Seasonal triggers are pollens. Year-round triggers are dust mite, pet dander and mould. [1]","It often travels with asthma, allergic conjunctivitis and eczema. [1]","Medicines can cause rhinitis: aspirin, NSAIDs, some antihypertensives, estrogens and decongestant sprays. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Pattern","Definition"],"r":[["Intermittent","Under 4 days a week, or under 4 weeks in a row"],["Persistent","Over 4 days a week and over 4 weeks in a row"],["Mild","Not troublesome; sleep, work, school and sport unaffected"],["Moderate to severe","Any one of those is affected"]]}}]},{"id":"flags","h":"When it is something else","body":[{"ul":["One-sided blockage or discharge. [1]","Pain. [1]","Bloody or purulent discharge. [1]","Recurrent nosebleeds. [1]","No response to standard treatment: recheck the diagnosis. [1]","CT is not for uncomplicated rhinitis. Consider it, with ENT referral, when another diagnosis is suspected. [1]"]},{"key":"One-sided symptoms are not allergy until proven otherwise."}]},{"id":"asthma","h":"Asthma and thunderstorms","body":[{"ul":["Asthma occurs in 15 to 38% of people with allergic rhinitis. [1]","Over 80% of people with allergic asthma have rhinitis. [1]","Assess everyone with rhinitis for asthma. [1]","Rye grass allergy raises the risk of thunderstorm asthma. [1]","Assess that risk in people living where rye grass pollen is high. [1]"]}]},{"id":"oral","h":"Antihistamines","body":[{"ul":["Less-sedating oral antihistamines work for sneezing, itch, runny nose and eye symptoms. [1]","They are weaker for a blocked nose. [1]","Fast onset, so they suit as-needed use for intermittent symptoms. [1]","Adult doses: cetirizine 10 mg, loratadine 10 mg, desloratadine 5 mg or fexofenadine 120 to 180 mg daily. [1]","All work equally well. Cetirizine is the most likely to sedate. [1]","Sedating antihistamines are not recommended, especially for children. [1]","Antihistamine nasal sprays, such as azelastine, beat tablets for blockage. [1]","Tablet plus antihistamine spray adds nothing over either alone. [1]"]}]},{"id":"incs","h":"Intranasal corticosteroids","body":[{"ul":["The most effective single treatment, especially for blockage. They help eye symptoms too. [1]","Use for moderate to severe disease. [1]","Relief is not immediate. It starts in days and peaks at 2 to 4 weeks of daily use. [1]","For bad pollen seasons, start at least 2 weeks before the season. [1]","Start at a higher dose for 2 to 4 weeks, then step down to the lowest effective dose. [1]","Combined steroid and antihistamine sprays suit moderate to severe disease. Stop the oral antihistamine. [1]","Local effects: minor nosebleeds, dryness, stinging. Systemic effects are unlikely. [1]","Give each new treatment about 4 weeks. Check adherence and technique before stepping up. [1]"]}]},{"id":"technique","h":"Nasal spray technique","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Step","What to do"],"r":[["1","Shake the bottle. Prime it if new"],["2","Blow the nose gently first"],["3","Tilt the head forward and look down"],["4","Use the opposite hand for each nostril"],["5","Aim outwards, towards the ear, away from the septum"],["6","Spray and sniff gently"]]}},{"ul":["Aiming away from the septum reduces bleeding and the risk of septal perforation. [1]","A hard sniff sends the dose down the throat. [1]","Wait 10 minutes between two different sprays, and after a saline rinse. [1]","Large-volume saline rinses work better than saline sprays. [1]"]}]},{"id":"eyes","h":"Eyes, decongestants and montelukast","body":[{"ul":["Oral antihistamines and intranasal corticosteroids often settle the eyes. [1]","If not, use drops with antihistamine and mast cell stabilising action: ketotifen, olopatadine or azelastine. [1]","Cromoglycate drops take about 2 weeks to work. [1]","Avoid vasoconstrictor eye drops. They cause rebound redness. [1]","Corticosteroid eye drops are for specialists. [1]","Decongestants have no role in allergic rhinitis. [1]","Decongestant sprays beyond 3 to 5 days cause rebound congestion (rhinitis medicamentosa). [1]","Oral decongestants raise blood pressure. Avoid them in pregnancy, older adults and children under 6. [1]","Montelukast is weaker than an antihistamine. Warn about mood and behaviour changes. [1]"]}]},{"id":"refer","h":"Immunotherapy and referral","body":[{"ul":["Allergen immunotherapy is the only treatment that changes the course of the disease. [1]","Refer for poorly controlled moderate to severe symptoms, or severe or persistent disease. [1]","Refer also when allergens cannot be avoided or the person wants less medication. [1]","Sensitisation is confirmed by skin prick or blood testing. [1]","A course lasts 3 to 5 years, by injection or under the tongue. [1]","Injections are always given under medical supervision because of anaphylaxis. [1]","Under-the-tongue treatment is supervised for the first dose, then taken at home. [1]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A student with spring sneezing and itchy eyes before exams.","A blocked nose not helped by an antihistamine tablet.","A patient who says the steroid spray 'did nothing' after three days.","Nosebleeds on a nasal spray.","Months of daily decongestant spray.","A one-sided blocked nose with blood-stained discharge.","Hay fever in Melbourne and a history of wheeze in spring."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-devices","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Inhaler devices","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Poor technique is common and looks like treatment failure. Watch the patient use the device.","Check technique at every visit, and always before stepping up treatment.","Children: a puffer with a spacer. Add a face mask under 4 years.","Dry powder inhalers need a strong breath in. Avoid them when inspiratory flow is weak.","A puffer with a spacer delivers reliever better than a nebuliser in an acute attack."],"curriculum":["Choose an inhaler device to suit the patient.","Teach and check inhaler technique.","Know the place of spacers and nebulisers."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Inhalational drug delivery devices. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020, amended January 2025.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"principles","h":"Principles","body":[{"ul":["Poor technique is common. It causes treatment failure and more adverse effects. [1]","Check technique at every opportunity, even in long-term users. [1]","Always check technique before stepping up treatment. [1]","Choose by what the patient prefers and can use: age, breath strength, cognition and hand function. [1]","Keep the number of different device types to a minimum. [1]","If technique stays poor despite teaching, change the device. [1]","Inspect the inhaler, spacer and mask for breakage or blockage. [1]","Dry powder inhalers have a smaller carbon footprint than pressurised puffers. [1]"]},{"key":"Before stepping up, watch the patient take a dose."}]},{"id":"types","h":"The devices","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Device","Examples","Remember"],"r":[["Pressurised metered dose inhaler (puffer)","Salbutamol, many preventers","Prime when new. Use with a spacer"],["Breath-actuated puffer","Autohaler","No spacer. No dose counter"],["Mist inhaler","Respimat","Load the cartridge first. Can use a spacer"],["Multi-dose dry powder","Turbuhaler, Accuhaler, Ellipta","Strong breath in. Keep dry. No spacer"],["Capsule dry powder","Breezhaler, Handihaler","Load a capsule each dose. Strong breath in"],["Nebuliser","Salbutamol, ipratropium","Higher doses, slower, needs servicing"]]}},{"ul":["Moisture clumps dry powder. Store it dry and never breathe out into the device. [1]"]}]},{"id":"spacers","h":"Spacers","body":[{"ul":["A spacer is used with a puffer or a mist inhaler. [1]","It removes the need to time the press with the breath. [1]","It reduces drug left in the mouth and throat, so fewer local effects from inhaled corticosteroids. [1]","It improves delivery to the lungs. [1]","A small-volume spacer is usually preferred. [1]","Adults: one slow deep breath is best. Otherwise 2 normal breaths from a small spacer, 3 from a large one. [1]","Children: 4 to 6 normal breaths per puff. [1]","A face mask must fit tightly. Wash the face after inhaled corticosteroid given by mask. [1]"]}]},{"id":"kids","h":"Children","body":[{"ul":["A puffer with a spacer is usually preferred for all children. [1]","Under 4 years, or unable to seal the lips: add a face mask. [1]","Over 10 years, a range of devices can be used, depending on ability. [1]","Teach the parent or carer as well as the child. [1]","Lung delivery varies in children even with good technique. [1]"]}]},{"id":"older","h":"Older adults","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Problem","Choose","Avoid"],"r":[["Weak breath in","Puffer, with or without spacer","Dry powder and breath-actuated inhalers"],["Arthritic or weak hands","Breath-actuated inhaler, or puffer left attached to a spacer; a Haleraid","-"],["Cannot time press and breath","Puffer with spacer, or a breath-actuated device","Puffer alone"],["Cannot seal lips","Puffer with spacer and face mask","-"]]}}]},{"id":"nebs","h":"Nebulisers","body":[{"ul":["A puffer with a spacer is more effective than a nebuliser for reliever in an acute wheezing episode. [1]","A nebuliser may help in severe attacks needing large doses over a long time. [1]","Nebulisers need higher doses, so more adverse effects. [1]","They need regular servicing and can become contaminated. [1]","Do not use one in a patient with an acute infection. It can spread organisms. [1]","Nebulised ipratropium by mask can precipitate acute angle-closure glaucoma. Use a mouthpiece. [1]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A woman with arthritis who cannot press her puffer.","A frail man on a dry powder inhaler with worsening COPD control.","Which device for a 2-year-old.","A parent asking for a nebuliser for home.","A preventer puffer used without a spacer.","Asthma 'not responding' to a higher dose."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-oxygen","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["Home oxygen is given to prolong life in chronic hypoxaemia, not to treat breathlessness.","Threshold: PaO2 55 mmHg or less on room air when stable, or 56 to 59 with complications of hypoxaemia.","It is not prescribed to people who still smoke, and must be used for at least 15 hours a day.","Flying: stable disease, no past problems and resting SpO2 of 95% or more needs no further testing.","No flying with a pneumothorax. Wait 1 week after a spontaneous pneumothorax has fully cleared on X-ray."],"curriculum":["Identify who should be assessed for home oxygen and how it is used and reviewed.","Advise patients with lung disease about air travel.","Know the absolute barriers to flying."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Domiciliary oxygen therapy. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"},{"n":2,"cite":"Therapeutic Guidelines. Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease. In: Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory. Published December 2020.","url":"https://www.tg.org.au"}],"sections":[{"id":"who","h":"Who gets home oxygen","body":[{"ul":["The purpose of long-term oxygen is to prolong life. [1]","It is not prescribed for breathlessness without hypoxaemia. [1]","COPD is the usual reason in adults. [1]","Assess only when the patient is stable, on best treatment, and has not smoked for at least 1 month. [1]","SpO2 under 90%: consider arterial blood gases. [1]","Blood gases on room air are needed to establish eligibility. [1]"]},{"tb":{"h":["PaO2 at rest, awake, on air","Oxygen indicated"],"r":[["55 mmHg or less","Yes"],["56 to 59 mmHg","If polycythaemia, pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure"],["Over 60 mmHg","No"]]}},{"ul":["It is usually prescribed by a specialist. Rules differ between states. [1]"]},{"key":"Oxygen treats hypoxaemia. It is not a treatment for breathlessness."}]},{"id":"not","h":"Who does not","body":[{"ul":["People who continue to smoke, because of the risk of fire. [1]","People whose main problem is breathlessness with a PaO2 over 60 mmHg. [1]","People whose underlying disease has not been adequately treated. [1]","In heart failure there is no evidence that home oxygen helps breathlessness, admissions or survival. [1]"]}]},{"id":"use","h":"Using it","body":[{"ul":["Start at 2 litres a minute through nasal cannulae. The usual range is 1 to 3. [1]","Add 1 litre a minute overnight and during exertion if needed. [1]","Use for at least 15 hours a day, preferably 18 or more, including sleep. [1]","An oxygen concentrator is the usual source. Cylinders are for backup and going out. [1]","Fire risk: no smoking near oxygen. [1]","Nasal dryness or bleeding: saline spray or a water-based lubricant. [1]","Carbon dioxide retention is rare. [1]","Night-time oxygen: do a sleep study first to exclude sleep-disordered breathing. [1]"]}]},{"id":"review","h":"Review","body":[{"ul":["Over a third of people started on oxygen at hospital discharge no longer need it 4 to 8 weeks later. [1]","Oxygen started at discharge is short term. Arrange early follow-up. [1]","Specialist review within 4 months of starting, when stable, with blood gases on room air. [1]","Then every year, by the GP or specialist. [1]","At review: smoking status, hours of use, saturation on oxygen, quality of life. [1]","If it is stopped, tell the patient it is because they have improved. [1]"]}]},{"id":"fly","h":"Flying: the basics","body":[{"ul":["The cabin is pressurised to the equivalent of about 2400 metres. Oxygen falls to the equivalent of 15%. [2]","Trapped gas expands by about one-third. [2]","No further tests if the disease is stable, there were no past in-flight problems, and resting SpO2 is 95% or more. [2]","Carry the reliever inhaler in hand luggage. [2]","Severe asthma or COPD with FEV1 under 30% predicted: respiratory specialist assessment. [2]","Lung bullae are not a barrier to flying. [2]","A blocked nose risks ear barotrauma, mostly on descent. A decongestant before take-off and descent can help. [2]","Children: drink, chew or suck during descent. Breastfeed infants. [2]"]}]},{"id":"nofly","h":"Flying: barriers and oxygen","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Situation","Advice"],"r":[["Current pneumothorax","Do not fly"],["Spontaneous pneumothorax, resolved","Wait 1 week after full resolution on X-ray"],["Pneumothorax after trauma or chest surgery","Wait 2 weeks after full resolution on X-ray"],["Pulmonary tuberculosis","Do not fly until non-infectious"],["Cannot walk 50 metres on the flat","Unfit to fly"],["Needs 4 litres a minute or more at sea level","Unfit to fly"]]}},{"ul":["Consider in-flight oxygen for resting SpO2 under 95%, FEV1 under 30%, or a past in-flight problem. [2]","Also within 6 weeks of a hospital admission for respiratory illness, or with heart or cerebrovascular disease. [2]","SpO2 under 95%: consider a hypoxic challenge test in a respiratory laboratory. [2]","In-flight oxygen is usually indicated if SpO2 falls below 85% during the test. [2]","The usual in-flight flow is 2 litres a minute. Arrange it with the airline well ahead. [2]","Patients already on home oxygen: discuss with their respiratory specialist. [2]"]}]},{"id":"differ","h":"Where sources differ","body":[{"ul":["The 2020 fitness-to-fly topic says tuberculosis is usually non-infectious after at least 2 weeks of treatment. [2]","The April 2026 tuberculosis topics say about 5 days for fully susceptible disease.","For flying, the decision belongs to the tuberculosis specialist. This page gives no number of days."]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A breathless man with COPD and a PaO2 of 64 mmHg asking for oxygen.","A patient on home oxygen who has started smoking again.","Oxygen started in hospital six weeks ago: what now.","A holiday flight three days after a pneumothorax cleared.","A woman with COPD and a resting SpO2 of 92% planning a long-haul flight.","A child with a cold flying tomorrow."]}]}]},{"id":"rs-lungca","unit":"Respiratory health","title":"Lung cancer: symptoms and screening","updated":"18 November 2026","checked":"November 2026","brief":["An unexplained cough, breathlessness, chest pain or weight loss lasting over 3 weeks needs a chest X-ray, sooner in a smoker.","A normal chest X-ray does not exclude lung cancer. If suspicion remains, arrange CT and refer.","Any haemoptysis that persists or is unexplained needs investigation. Massive haemoptysis or stridor goes to hospital now.","Screening: age 50 to 70, no symptoms, 30 pack-years, and still smoking or quit within 10 years.","The screening test is a low-dose CT every 2 years. People with symptoms are investigated, not screened."],"curriculum":["Recognise symptoms and signs of lung cancer and investigate them promptly.","Assess eligibility for the National Lung Cancer Screening Program and act on results.","Discuss benefits and harms of screening and support smoking cessation."],"refs":[{"n":1,"cite":"Cancer Australia. Investigating symptoms of lung cancer: a guide for all health professionals. May 2020, amended for the screening program.","url":"https://www.canceraustralia.gov.au/ISLCguide"},{"n":2,"cite":"Australian Government Department of Health, Disability and Ageing. National Lung Cancer Screening Program: general practitioner resource guide. 2025.","url":"https://www.health.gov.au/our-work/nlcsp/for-healthcare-providers"}],"sections":[{"id":"risk","h":"Who is at risk","body":[{"ul":["Smoking, current or past, is the main risk factor. Passive smoking counts. [1]","Work exposures: asbestos, silica, diesel exhaust, radon. [1]","Older age, family history, and a previous cancer. [1]","Chronic lung disease such as COPD or pulmonary fibrosis. [1]","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are twice as likely to be diagnosed with and to die from lung cancer. [2]","Stigma about smoking delays people seeking help. Ask without judgement. [1]"]}]},{"id":"symptoms","h":"Symptoms and signs","body":[{"tb":{"h":["Symptoms","Signs and findings"],"r":[["New or changed cough","Finger clubbing"],["Breathlessness","Neck or supraclavicular lymph nodes"],["Chest or shoulder pain","Abnormal chest signs, pleural effusion"],["Hoarseness","Thrombocytosis"],["Weight loss, poor appetite, fatigue","Deep vein thrombosis"],["Haemoptysis","Features of spread: brain, bone, liver, skin"],["Chest infection that persists or recurs","Superior vena cava obstruction"]]}},{"ul":["Symptoms overlap with COPD and heart disease, which delays diagnosis. [1]","Investigate anything unexplained that lasts more than 3 weeks. [1]","Investigate earlier with known risk factors or more than one symptom or sign. [1]"]},{"key":"A changed cough in a smoker is a symptom, not 'just the smoking'."}]},{"id":"investigate","h":"Investigation and referral","body":[{"ul":["Massive haemoptysis or stridor: emergency department immediately. [1]","Otherwise: urgent chest X-ray, with the result back to the patient within 1 week. [1]","A chest X-ray misses up to 25% of lung cancers. [1]","Normal X-ray but suspicion remains: arrange CT of the chest. [1]","CT is with contrast unless contraindicated. [1]","X-ray or CT suggests cancer: urgent CT if not done, and refer at the same time. [1]","Refer to a specialist linked to a lung cancer multidisciplinary team. First appointment within 2 weeks. [1]","Consolidation that fits the picture: treat, then repeat the X-ray within 6 weeks. [1]","Consolidation or symptoms still present at 6 weeks: refer. [1]","A nodule on X-ray: compare with old films. New or changed means CT and referral. [1]"]}]},{"id":"eligible","h":"Screening: who is eligible","body":[{"tb":{"h":["All four must be met"],"r":[["Aged 50 to 70 years"],["No symptoms or signs suggesting lung cancer"],["Smokes now, or quit within the last 10 years"],["At least 30 pack-years of cigarette smoking"]]}},{"ul":["One pack-year is 20 cigarettes a day for one year. [2]","Once enrolled, smoking history is not reassessed. [2]","A person with symptoms is not screened. They are investigated. [2]","Quitting is not a condition of taking part. Offer help to quit at every contact. [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Defer the scan if"],"r":[["A full chest CT in the last 12 months, or one planned within 3 months"],["A symptomatic chest infection in the last 12 weeks"],["Unable to lie flat for 5 minutes with arms above the head"],["Weight over 200 kg"]]}}]},{"id":"process","h":"Screening: how it works","body":[{"ul":["The test is a low-dose CT without contrast, every 2 years. [2]","The GP checks eligibility, completes the program request form and enrols the person in the National Cancer Screening Register. [2]","The scan is bulk-billed. [2]","Use shared decision making before the first scan. [2]"]},{"tb":{"h":["Result","Meaning","Next"],"r":[["Very low risk","No significant finding","Screen again in 2 years"],["Low risk","Small nodule","Scan in 12 months"],["Low to moderate risk","Nodule to watch","Scan in 6 months"],["Moderate risk","Nodule to watch","Scan in 3 months"],["High or very high risk","Suspicious nodule","Refer to a respiratory physician linked to a lung cancer team"],["Incomplete","Infection, or lung not fully seen","Repeat when reminded"],["Additional findings","Outside the nodule question, such as emphysema","Assess and manage on their merits"]]}}]},{"id":"harms","h":"Screening: benefits and harms","body":[{"ul":["Benefit: cancers are found earlier, when more can be treated. [2]","Nodules are common: 22 to 51% of people screened have one. Most are not cancer. [2]","About 3% have a high-risk nodule. About half of those turn out to have cancer. [2]","Overdiagnosis: about 1 in 30 screen-found cancers would never have caused harm. [2]","Radiation from one scan is less than a year of background exposure. [2]","Incidental findings can cause worry and tests, and can also find real disease early. [2]"]}]},{"id":"tested","h":"How this is tested","body":[{"ul":["A 62-year-old smoker with a cough that has changed over six weeks.","A normal chest X-ray in a patient with weight loss and haemoptysis.","Pneumonia on X-ray in a 66-year-old smoker: what happens after the antibiotics.","A 55-year-old with 35 pack-years who quit 4 years ago and feels well.","A patient who asks for 'the lung screening scan' because of a new cough.","A screening report recommending a 3-month scan.","Working out pack-years."]}]}]}],"cards":[{"id":"rs1-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Asthma diagnosis needs variable symptoms, no better explanation, and {{c1::objective evidence}}.","e":"Variable expiratory airflow limitation on testing, or a raised FeNO. Symptoms alone are not enough.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#criteria","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"The two symptoms most reliably linked to asthma are wheeze and {{c1::breathlessness}}.","e":"Cough and chest tightness count, but are less specific.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#criteria","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Does a normal chest examination exclude asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"The examination is usually normal between episodes.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#criteria","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"A positive bronchodilator response is an FEV1 rise of at least {{c1::12%}} and at least 200 mL.","e":"Both parts are required. 9% with 250 mL is negative.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"A positive bronchodilator response is an FEV1 rise of at least 12% and at least {{c1::200 mL}}.","e":"A rise over 400 mL strongly supports asthma.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Post-bronchodilator FEV1 is measured {{c1::10 to 15 minutes}} after salbutamol.","e":"Salbutamol 200 to 400 micrograms; at least three blows before and after.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"On spirometry, expiratory airflow limitation is shown by a reduced {{c1::FEV1/FVC ratio}}.","e":"A low FEV1 alone is non-specific.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Spirometry is normal while the person is well. Is asthma excluded? {{c1::No}}","e":"Repeat when symptomatic or in the early morning.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Spirometry is normal while the person is breathless and wheezy. This points to {{c1::another diagnosis}}.","e":"Contrast: normal spirometry on a well day excludes nothing.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Ideally, spirometry is done {{c1::before::before or after}} a regular preventer is started.","e":"Treatment makes variable airflow harder to show. If symptoms are significant, treat now and test as soon as possible.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"In adults, a FeNO of {{c1::40 ppb}} or more supports asthma.","e":"Only when the clinical picture fits. A normal FeNO does not exclude asthma.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"FeNO is a measure of {{c1::airway inflammation}}.","e":"Fractional exhaled nitric oxide. Added to the diagnostic work-up in the 2025 Handbook.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"A peak flow diary supports asthma when average daily variation exceeds {{c1::10%}}.","e":"Morning and about 4 pm, best of three, for 2 to 4 weeks. Variation = (highest - lowest) / mean.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"A diagnostic peak flow diary runs for {{c1::2 to 4 weeks}}.","e":"Used when spirometry cannot be done in a reasonable time. Same meter throughout.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Bronchial provocation testing is contraindicated when FEV1 is under {{c1::60%}} of predicted.","e":"The other contraindication is pregnancy.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Suspected asthma in pregnancy with non-diagnostic spirometry: the test to avoid is {{c1::bronchial provocation}}.","e":"Options are repeat spirometry, laboratory spirometry with FeNO, or referral.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Before bronchial provocation, salbutamol is withheld for at least {{c1::4 hours}}.","e":"Formoterol and salmeterol: 24 hours. Laboratories set their own rules.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"A treatment trial with inhaled corticosteroid is judged after at least {{c1::4 weeks}}.","e":"Clear improvement supports asthma. No improvement: look for another diagnosis.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#trial","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Strong suspicion of asthma, normal spirometry, repeat testing not possible: the fallback is a {{c1::treatment trial}}.","e":"Also used when a person cannot perform spirometry.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#trial","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Is a chest X-ray needed to diagnose asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"Reserve imaging for unusual features or a suspected alternative diagnosis.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#nottests","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Does allergy testing confirm or exclude asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"It only guides management of triggers.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#nottests","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Pack-years = years smoked x cigarettes per day, divided by {{c1::20}}.","e":"More than 20 pack-years lowers the probability of asthma and raises that of COPD.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#history","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Throat tightness, voice change and symptoms worse with talking suggest {{c1::inducible laryngeal obstruction::diagnosis}}.","e":"Also called vocal cord dysfunction. Spirometry is normal during symptoms.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Breathlessness with dizziness and tingling fingers suggests {{c1::dysfunctional breathing::diagnosis}}.","e":"Hyperventilation. These features lower the probability of asthma.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"New asthma symptoms that are related to work: the next step is {{c1::immediate specialist referral}}.","e":"Occupational asthma can cause rapid, irreversible loss of lung function.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Wheeze after aspirin or NSAIDs suggests {{c1::aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease::diagnosis}}.","e":"Refer. An aspirin challenge is done only in a specialist setting.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Asthma with COPD features must be treated with an {{c1::inhaled corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"Long-acting bronchodilators alone carry higher risk when asthma is present.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs1-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Unilateral wheeze in a child suggests {{c1::inhaled foreign body::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"eTG Respiratory: Asthma diagnosis, 6 years and older (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs1-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","o":1,"x":"Suspected asthma in an Aboriginal adult from a remote community: the coexisting diagnosis to consider is {{c1::bronchiectasis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Needs high-resolution CT.","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs2-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"FEV1 is the volume breathed out in the {{c1::first second}} of a forced breath.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungfn#measures","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Spirometry reference values are matched for age, sex, ethnicity and {{c1::height}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungfn#measures","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A low FEV1/FVC ratio indicates an {{c1::obstructive::obstructive or restrictive}} defect.","e":"Asthma, COPD, or both. The expiratory loop is scooped.","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A low FVC with a normal or raised FEV1/FVC ratio indicates a {{c1::restrictive::obstructive or restrictive}} defect.","e":"Interstitial lung disease, muscle weakness, kyphoscoliosis, obesity.","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A concave (scooped) expiratory flow-volume loop indicates {{c1::airflow obstruction}}.","e":"A normal-shaped but small loop suggests restriction.","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A low ratio with a low FVC suggests mixed disease or {{c1::gas trapping}}.","e":"Static lung volumes separate the two.","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Suspected restriction on spirometry is assessed further with {{c1::static lung volumes::test}}.","e":"Laboratory test. Also shows hyperinflation.","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"After a heart attack, spirometry is deferred for {{c1::1 week}}.","e":"A relative contraindication: weigh risk and benefit.","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"After eye, sinus or middle ear surgery, spirometry is deferred for {{c1::1 week}}.","e":"A middle ear infection in the previous week is also a reason to defer.","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"After chest or abdominal surgery, spirometry is deferred for {{c1::4 weeks}}.","e":"Contrast: 1 week for eye, sinus and ear surgery.","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Is current haemoptysis a reason to defer spirometry? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"As are a current or recent pneumothorax and concussion with ongoing symptoms.","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A patient who must have spirometry despite a relative contraindication is tested in a {{c1::respiratory laboratory}}.","e":"Experienced operator with emergency care available. Name the concern on the referral.","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Is a peak flow meter useful for monitoring COPD? {{c1::No}}","e":"The obstruction in COPD does not vary.","nt":"rs-lungfn#pef","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Can a peak flow meter replace spirometry for diagnosing asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"It cannot show reversibility or a reduced FEV1/FVC ratio.","nt":"rs-lungfn#pef","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Serial peak flow readings must use the same {{c1::meter}}.","e":"Readings vary widely between devices.","nt":"rs-lungfn#pef","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"In carbon monoxide poisoning, pulse oximetry reads falsely {{c1::high::high or low}}.","e":"Carboxyhaemoglobin is read as oxyhaemoglobin. Heavy smoking has a smaller effect of the same kind.","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A pulse oximeter gives no information about {{c1::carbon dioxide}}.","e":"It measures saturation, not ventilation. Hypercapnia needs an arterial gas.","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Dapsone and benzocaine make oximetry unreliable by causing {{c1::methaemoglobinaemia}}.","e":"Readings may be falsely high or low. Lidocaine, nitrates and some antimalarials do the same.","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Nail polish is affecting an oximeter reading. The fix is to {{c1::rotate the probe}}.","e":"Side to side on the finger.","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"In severe anaemia the oximeter reading is accurate but {{c1::falsely reassuring}}.","e":"Saturation is normal while oxygen delivery is poor. Cyanide poisoning is similar.","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Normal arterial PaCO2 is {{c1::35 to 45}} mmHg.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Normal arterial pH is {{c1::7.35 to 7.45}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Normal arterial PaO2 is {{c1::80 to 100}} mmHg.","e":"Varies with age and altitude.","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Normal arterial bicarbonate is {{c1::22 to 26}} mmol/L.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"A venous blood gas is abnormal in a patient with suspected respiratory failure. The next test is an {{c1::arterial blood gas::test}}.","e":"Venous values do not track arterial values reliably.","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"The lung function test that measures gas exchange is {{c1::DLCO::test}}.","e":"Diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide. A baseline is useful before amiodarone, methotrexate or leflunomide.","nt":"rs-lungfn#lab","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"The laboratory test used to assess fitness to fly is the {{c1::hypoxic challenge test::test}}.","e":"Also called the high-altitude simulation test.","nt":"rs-lungfn#lab","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs2-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung function tests","o":1,"x":"Bronchial provocation testing detects {{c1::airway hyperresponsiveness}}.","e":"Beta agonists, antimuscarinics and antihistamines interfere: ask the laboratory what to withhold.","nt":"rs-lungfn#lab","src":"eTG Respiratory: Pulmonary function testing (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs3-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Every treatment level for asthma in people aged 12 and over contains an {{c1::inhaled corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"The 2025 Handbook change. Applies even when symptoms are infrequent.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Is salbutamol alone acceptable treatment for an adult with mild, infrequent asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"Reliever-only treatment carries a higher risk of severe flare-ups than as-needed budesonide-formoterol.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"AIR stands for {{c1::anti-inflammatory reliever}}.","e":"A corticosteroid-formoterol inhaler taken only when symptoms occur.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"MART stands for {{c1::maintenance and reliever therapy}}.","e":"One corticosteroid-formoterol inhaler used daily and for relief.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Salbutamol use of {{c1::3 or more}} canisters a year signals a higher risk of severe flare-ups.","e":"12 or more a year is linked to a higher risk of asthma death.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Salbutamol use of {{c1::12 or more}} canisters a year is linked to a higher risk of asthma death.","e":"3 or more already signals higher flare-up risk.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"A long-acting beta agonist is never used in asthma without an {{c1::inhaled corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Recommended level 1 treatment: low-dose {{c1::budesonide-formoterol::drug}} as needed.","e":"The only combination approved for as-needed use without maintenance.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Recommended level 2 treatment is {{c1::low-dose MART}}.","e":"Alternative: daily low-dose corticosteroid-LABA plus salbutamol as needed.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Recommended level 3 treatment is {{c1::medium-dose MART}}.","e":"More maintenance puffs of the low-dose inhaler. The reliever dose stays low.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Besides budesonide-formoterol, the combination approved for MART is {{c1::beclometasone-formoterol::drug}}.","e":"Adults only, and not approved for as-needed use without maintenance. Fluticasone-salmeterol cannot be used as MART.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Newly diagnosed asthma with a flare-up needing oral corticosteroids in the past year: start with {{c1::MART}}.","e":"Also for frequent or severe symptoms, or any past life-threatening episode. Everyone else starts with as-needed budesonide-formoterol.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Asthma is first diagnosed during a severe acute episode. Is maintenance treatment started straight away? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"MART, even if an oral corticosteroid course is also given.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"For predictable exercise symptoms, the usual reliever is taken {{c1::15 minutes}} before exercise.","e":"A person on budesonide-formoterol uses that. A separate salbutamol inhaler is not added.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"After starting asthma treatment, the first review is within {{c1::2 to 3 months}}.","e":"Reassess control, adherence and technique.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"For routine asthma treatment, a pressurised inhaler is used with a {{c1::spacer}} rather than a nebuliser.","e":"As effective, and faster.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 dry powder, as-needed dose: {{c1::1 inhalation}}.","e":"Up to 6 on one occasion.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 dry powder: usual maximum of {{c1::8}} inhalations a day.","e":"Up to 12 in a day for a short period. Maintenance and reliever doses both count.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Budesonide-formoterol 100/3 pressurised inhaler, as-needed dose: {{c1::2 inhalations}}.","e":"Usual maximum 16 a day, 24 briefly. Twice the puffs of the 200/6 dry powder inhaler.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Low-dose MART with budesonide-formoterol 200/6: maintenance of {{c1::2 inhalations}} a day.","e":"In one dose or two. Medium-dose MART is 2 twice a day.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"A patient on as-needed budesonide-formoterol worsens over {{c1::3 days}} despite extra doses: arrange medical review.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"A low daily dose of budesonide is {{c1::200 to 400}} micrograms.","e":"Medium 500 to 800; high over 800.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"A low daily dose of fluticasone propionate is {{c1::100 to 200}} micrograms.","e":"Medium 250 to 500; high over 500. Half the budesonide figures.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Before stepping up asthma treatment, check adherence, triggers, comorbidities and {{c1::inhaler technique}}.","e":"Watch the person use the device.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepup","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Poor control on fixed-dose corticosteroid-LABA plus salbutamol: before raising the dose, switch to {{c1::MART}}.","e":"At the same corticosteroid dose, MART gives fewer flare-ups.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepup","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"On as-needed budesonide-formoterol and using it {{c1::3 or more}} times a week: add daily maintenance.","e":"That is low-dose MART, using the same inhaler.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepup","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Uncontrolled on medium-dose MART despite good adherence and technique: order {{c1::blood eosinophils::test}} and refer.","e":"Do not simply raise the corticosteroid dose.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#refer","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"On medium-dose inhaled corticosteroid, blood eosinophils of {{c1::150}} per microlitre or more suggest persistent type 2 inflammation.","e":"These patients may benefit from a monoclonal antibody.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#refer","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Blood eosinophils of {{c1::1,500}} per microlitre or more in asthma need urgent specialist assessment.","e":"Looks for other disease, such as allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#refer","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-30","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Asthma treatment can be stepped down after good control for {{c1::2 to 3 months}}.","e":"And low flare-up risk. Then review in 4 to 8 weeks.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepdown","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-31","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"After stepping asthma treatment down, review in {{c1::4 to 8 weeks}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepdown","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-32","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"The lowest acceptable asthma treatment for an adult is {{c1::as-needed budesonide-formoterol}}.","e":"The inhaled corticosteroid is never withdrawn completely.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepdown","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs3-33","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","o":1,"x":"Is pregnancy a time to step asthma treatment down? {{c1::No}}","e":"Nor during a respiratory infection, allergen season or travel.","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepdown","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Asthma control is assessed over the previous {{c1::4 weeks}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-review#control","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Good asthma control: daytime symptoms on no more than {{c1::2 days}} a week.","e":"The same threshold applies to reliever use, not counting doses before exercise.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#control","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"One night waking with asthma in the past 4 weeks. Is control good? {{c1::No}}","e":"Any night symptom or any activity limitation means poor control.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#control","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"The strongest predictor of a future asthma flare-up is {{c1::poor symptom control}}.","e":"Next: a severe flare-up in the past year.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#risk","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"A confirmed {{c1::food allergy}} is a risk factor for asthma death, although it rarely triggers asthma.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-review#risk","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Minimum frequency of asthma review: {{c1::once a year}}.","e":"And 2 to 3 months after any new treatment.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#review","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"In well-controlled asthma, spirometry is repeated about every {{c1::2 years}}.","e":"Also at diagnosis and whenever control is poor.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#review","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Are bronchodilators withheld before spirometry done to monitor known asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"They are withheld for diagnostic spirometry.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#review","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"An asthma action plan is reviewed at least {{c1::yearly}} and whenever treatment changes.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Peak-flow action plans are set against the person's {{c1::personal best}}, not the predicted value.","e":"For poor perceivers, over-perceivers and people with sudden severe attacks. Most people use a symptom-based plan.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Worsening asthma on inhaled corticosteroid alone: the plan {{c1::quadruples}} the dose for 1 to 2 weeks.","e":"Doubling is not enough. If no response, start prednisolone.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Worsening asthma on a fixed corticosteroid-LABA inhaler: the practical step-up in the plan is {{c1::oral prednisolone::drug}}.","e":"Raising the inhaled dose would need a second, corticosteroid-only inhaler.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"An action plan states the maximum reliever dose per occasion and per {{c1::24 hours}}.","e":"Specific to that person's inhaler.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Oral corticosteroids are started when symptoms return within {{c1::4 hours}} of the reliever.","e":"Or do not settle promptly.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Adult asthma flare-up: prednisolone {{c1::37.5 to 50 mg}} each morning.","e":"For 5 to 10 days, without a taper.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Adult asthma flare-up: prednisolone is given for {{c1::5 to 10 days}}.","e":"37.5 to 50 mg each morning.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Does a 7-day prednisolone course for asthma need a taper? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Adolescent asthma flare-up: prednisolone 1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) daily for {{c1::3 days}}.","e":"Adults: 5 to 10 days.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Prednisolone for asthma flare-ups is prescribed with {{c1::no repeats::how many repeats}}.","e":"Exact quantity only, so that each course is counted.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"During pregnancy, about {{c1::40%}} of women with asthma have worsening symptoms.","e":"At least 20% have a flare-up needing medical care.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#pregnancy","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"A pregnant woman asks whether to stop her inhaled corticosteroid. {{c1::Continue it}}","e":"Flare-ups are linked to low birth weight and preterm birth. Step up if needed; do not step down.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#pregnancy","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Asthma flare-ups in pregnancy are linked to preterm birth and {{c1::low birth weight}}.","e":"Also small-for-dates babies.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#pregnancy","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"In an obese person with asthma, weight loss of {{c1::5 to 10%}} can improve control.","e":"Confirm asthma with lung function before escalating: obesity itself causes breathlessness.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"A person with asthma now needs a beta blocker after a heart attack. Start it at a {{c1::low dose}} under supervision.","e":"An indication for a beta blocker is not overridden by asthma.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Breathlessness that worsens in the minutes after stopping exercise suggests {{c1::asthma::asthma or unfitness}}.","e":"Breathlessness from poor fitness eases as soon as exercise stops.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"The adverse effects to warn about with montelukast are {{c1::neuropsychiatric}}.","e":"Behaviour change, depression, suicidal thoughts. Stop the drug if they occur.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs4-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"After a maintenance dose of inhaled corticosteroid, the person should {{c1::rinse and spit}}.","e":"Reduces thrush and hoarseness. Use a spacer with pressurised inhalers.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025)"},{"id":"rs4-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","o":1,"x":"Does treating reflux reliably improve asthma control? {{c1::No}}","e":"Reflux is common in asthma, but treating it does not necessarily help the asthma.","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","src":"eTG Respiratory: Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Asthma first aid: salbutamol {{c1::4 puffs}} through a spacer, repeated after 4 minutes.","e":"One puff at a time, 4 breaths after each. Still no better: call an ambulance and keep going.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#firstaid","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In the 4 x 4 x 4 plan, the wait between sets of salbutamol puffs is {{c1::4 minutes}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#firstaid","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"After an asthma attack settles, reliever needed more often than every {{c1::3 to 4 hours}} means same-day medical review.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#firstaid","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Acute asthma, speaking whole sentences, saturation 96%: severity is {{c1::mild to moderate}}.","e":"All of: can walk, whole sentences in one breath, saturation over 94%.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, a room-air saturation of {{c1::94%}} or less makes the attack at least severe.","e":"Under 92% is life-threatening.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, a room-air saturation under {{c1::92%}} is a life-threatening feature.","e":"It is also the threshold for starting oxygen.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Acute asthma with speech limited to a few words per breath: severity is {{c1::severe}}.","e":"Cannot speak at all is life-threatening.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, a heart rate of {{c1::110}} or more is a severe feature.","e":"Respiratory rate threshold: 25 or more.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, a respiratory rate of {{c1::25}} or more is a severe feature.","e":"A slow rate in a tiring patient signals exhaustion.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"A silent chest in acute asthma means the attack is {{c1::life-threatening}}.","e":"So do drowsiness, cyanosis, poor effort and bradycardia.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Bradycardia in a patient with acute asthma warns of imminent {{c1::respiratory arrest}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Mild to moderate acute asthma in an adult: salbutamol {{c1::4 to 12}} puffs through a spacer.","e":"Repeat every 20 to 30 minutes in the first hour if needed.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Severe acute asthma in an adult: salbutamol {{c1::12}} puffs through a spacer.","e":"At least every 20 minutes for the first hour, and call an ambulance.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In severe acute asthma, salbutamol is repeated every {{c1::20 minutes}} for the first hour.","e":"Three doses in the hour.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Severe acute asthma and the patient cannot use a spacer: nebulised salbutamol {{c1::5 mg}}.","e":"Life-threatening: 2 x 5 mg nebules continuously, driven by oxygen.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"The bronchodilator added to salbutamol in severe acute asthma is {{c1::ipratropium::drug}}.","e":"Adults: 8 puffs of 21 micrograms through a spacer every 20 minutes for the first hour.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Severe asthma not responding to bronchodilators in hospital: the intravenous drug added is {{c1::magnesium sulfate::drug}}.","e":"10 mmol over 20 minutes in adults.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Acute asthma not relieved within {{c1::5 minutes}} of the first dose: repeat it and arrange transfer.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, oxygen is started when saturation is under {{c1::92%}}.","e":"Target 92 to 96%.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#oxygen","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Oxygen saturation target in acute asthma: {{c1::92 to 96%}}.","e":"88 to 92% if at risk of carbon dioxide retention. Do not over-oxygenate.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#oxygen","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"An adult's asthma attack settles fully after one salbutamol dose in the practice. Is prednisolone still given? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"The 2025 Handbook gives systemic corticosteroids to all who present, regardless of initial severity.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#steroids","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"In acute asthma, corticosteroids are started within {{c1::1 hour}} of presentation.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#steroids","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Alternative to prednisolone in adult acute asthma: dexamethasone {{c1::16 mg}}, repeated once the next day.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#steroids","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Sudden breathlessness with hives or facial swelling is {{c1::anaphylaxis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Give intramuscular adrenaline first.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#anaphylaxis","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Wheeze where anaphylaxis cannot be excluded: the first drug is {{c1::intramuscular adrenaline::drug}}.","e":"Suspect it with skin, gut or circulation features alongside sudden breathlessness.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#anaphylaxis","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"After an asthma attack settles in the practice, the patient is observed for at least {{c1::4 hours}}.","e":"In the practice or with a responsible adult who can give salbutamol and call an ambulance.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"A patient is not sent home after acute asthma while reliever is needed more often than every {{c1::4 hours}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"First follow-up after an acute asthma episode is within {{c1::3 to 5 days}}.","e":"Then a full asthma review about 2 weeks later.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"After an acute attack, the maintenance inhaled corticosteroid dose should be at least {{c1::medium}}.","e":"As a corticosteroid-LABA, with the reliever used as needed, not by the clock.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-30","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Near-fatal asthma needs specialist follow-up for {{c1::life}}.","e":"After any severe attack: at least a year.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs5-31","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"The marker of thunderstorm asthma risk in a person without asthma is {{c1::springtime hay fever}}.","e":"Ryegrass pollen allergy.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#storm","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-32","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"The treatment that protects against severe thunderstorm asthma is an {{c1::inhaled corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"Highest risk: asthma, grass pollen allergy, and no preventer.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#storm","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-33","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"The grass most often implicated in thunderstorm asthma is {{c1::ryegrass}}.","e":"Storms fragment the pollen into particles small enough to reach the small airways.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#storm","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs5-34","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","o":1,"x":"Thunderstorm asthma exposure is greatest in the {{c1::wind gusts}} just before the rain.","e":"Stay indoors with windows shut; turn off evaporative coolers.","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#storm","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in primary care"},{"id":"rs6-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Asthma is not diagnosed in children younger than {{c1::12 months}}.","e":"Wheeze at this age is usually bronchiolitis. Persistent wheeze is referred.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Persistent wheeze in a 9-month-old: the next step is {{c1::specialist referral::next step}}.","e":"No asthma diagnosis and no asthma treatment under 12 months.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child aged 1 to 5, the evidence for asthma is signs that clear within minutes of {{c1::salbutamol}}.","e":"Lung function testing is not possible at this age.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In children aged 6 to 11, a FeNO of {{c1::25 ppb}} or more supports asthma.","e":"Adults: 40 ppb.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Is peak flow used to diagnose asthma in a child aged 6 to 11? {{c1::No}}","e":"Spirometry with bronchodilator, with FeNO if done in a laboratory.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A 7-year-old with suspected asthma and normal spirometry and FeNO: the next step is {{c1::specialist referral::next step}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a preschooler, wheeze only with colds makes asthma {{c1::less likely::more or less}}.","e":"It does not rule asthma out. Viral infection is the commonest trigger of severe attacks at every age.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#history","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A child's chronic cough is moist. This makes asthma {{c1::less likely::more or less}}.","e":"Think protracted bacterial bronchitis.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#history","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Sudden-onset cough with wheeze on one side in a toddler suggests {{c1::inhaled foreign body::diagnosis}}.","e":"Urgent referral.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A child with a persistent wet cough and no wheeze most likely has {{c1::protracted bacterial bronchitis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Also consider bronchiectasis and cystic fibrosis.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A child's dry cough occurs in the daytime only and stops in sleep. This suggests {{c1::habit cough::diagnosis}}.","e":"Also called somatic cough.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A wheezy child with nasal polyps and poor growth should be tested for {{c1::cystic fibrosis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Clubbing points the same way.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Wheeze in a child that never varies suggests a {{c1::structural airway problem}}.","e":"Asthma wheeze comes and goes.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Usual reliever dose for a child aged 1 to 5: salbutamol {{c1::2 puffs}} by spacer.","e":"With a mask if needed. Two more if not settled in a few minutes.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child under 12, daytime asthma symptoms {{c1::twice a week}} or more are an indication for a preventer.","e":"Or night symptoms twice a month or more, or activity or sleep restricted.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child under 12, night-time asthma symptoms {{c1::twice a month}} or more are an indication for a preventer.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Low-dose inhaled corticosteroid for a child aged 1 to 5: fluticasone propionate {{c1::50 micrograms}} twice daily.","e":"Higher doses only under a specialist.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A preventer trial in a child aged 1 to 5 is reviewed at about {{c1::3 months}}.","e":"Ages 6 to 11: 8 to 12 weeks.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A 4-year-old has done well on a 3-month inhaled corticosteroid trial. Consider {{c1::pausing treatment}} to see if symptoms return.","e":"Remission occurs in some children.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Level 1 asthma treatment for a child under 12 is {{c1::salbutamol as needed}}.","e":"Budesonide-formoterol as needed is for ages 12 and over.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Level 2 asthma treatment for a child under 12 is a low-dose {{c1::inhaled corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"Plus salbutamol as needed.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Level 3 for a child aged 6 to 11: low-dose {{c1::corticosteroid-LABA}}.","e":"Alternative: medium-dose corticosteroid alone. At ages 1 to 5, level 3 is medium-dose corticosteroid.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A LABA combination can be used in asthma from age {{c1::4 years}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Montelukast at level 2 in children is reserved for when parents {{c1::decline corticosteroids}}.","e":"Warn about mood and behaviour change. Switch to an inhaled corticosteroid when possible.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Reliever for a child aged 6 to 11 who is working hard to breathe: salbutamol {{c1::6 to 12}} puffs.","e":"For cough or wheeze without increased effort: 2 to 4 puffs.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A copy of a child's asthma action plan goes to the {{c1::school or day care}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Should a home nebuliser be prescribed for a child with asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"Use a spacer, with a mask for small children.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#never","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs6-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Should oral salbutamol be prescribed for a child with asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"Nor theophylline.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#never","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): children"},{"id":"rs7-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child with acute asthma, a saturation of {{c1::94%}} or less makes the attack severe.","e":"Same threshold as adults.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child with acute asthma, a saturation under {{c1::90%}} is life-threatening.","e":"Adults: under 92%.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Subcostal recession in a child with acute asthma makes the attack {{c1::severe}}.","e":"So does tracheal tug or an unfinished sentence.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#severity","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Mild to moderate acute asthma, age 1 to 5: salbutamol {{c1::2 to 6}} puffs.","e":"By spacer, with a mask if needed.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Severe acute asthma, age 1 to 5: salbutamol {{c1::6}} puffs.","e":"At least every 20 minutes for the first hour, and call an ambulance.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Mild to moderate acute asthma, age 6 to 11: salbutamol {{c1::4 to 12}} puffs.","e":"The adult doses apply from age 6.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Severe acute asthma, age 6 to 11: salbutamol {{c1::12}} puffs.","e":"Under 6 years: 6 puffs.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Nebulised salbutamol dose for a child aged 1 to 5 who cannot use a spacer: {{c1::2.5 mg}}.","e":"From age 6: 5 mg.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Is oral salbutamol used for acute asthma in children? {{c1::No}}","e":"Nor intravenous salbutamol in primary care.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Repeated high doses of salbutamol can cause low serum {{c1::potassium}}.","e":"Also tachycardia, fast breathing and metabolic acidosis.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Ipratropium dose in severe acute asthma, age 1 to 5: {{c1::4 puffs}} of 21 micrograms.","e":"From age 6: 8 puffs.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs7-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"In a child with acute asthma, oxygen is started when saturation is under {{c1::92%}}.","e":"Target 92 to 96%.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#oxygen","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Oxygen by simple mask for a child with acute asthma runs at {{c1::5 L/min}}.","e":"Nasal prongs: 3 L/min.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#oxygen","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Acute asthma, age 1 to 5: oral corticosteroids are given only if the child needs {{c1::hospital admission}}.","e":"In mild to moderate preschool wheeze they may give no benefit.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Acute asthma, age 6 to 11: oral corticosteroids are given unless the attack is graded {{c1::mild}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Prednisolone dose for acute asthma in a child: {{c1::1 mg/kg}} each morning.","e":"Maximum 50 mg, for 3 days.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Prednisolone course length for acute asthma in a child: {{c1::3 days}}.","e":"Adults: 5 to 10 days.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Alternative to prednisolone in a child with acute asthma: dexamethasone {{c1::0.6 mg/kg}}.","e":"Maximum 16 mg, repeated the next day if needed.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs7-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Intravenous magnesium sulfate for acute asthma is not used under age {{c1::2 years}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#transfer","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute asthma (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs7-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"A child's acute asthma is not relieved within {{c1::5 minutes}} of the first dose: repeat and transfer.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#transfer","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"After a child's asthma attack settles, observe for at least {{c1::4 hours}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#after","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs7-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Acute asthma in children","o":1,"x":"Follow-up after a child's asthma attack is within {{c1::3 to 5 days}}.","e":"Then a full review about 2 weeks later.","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#after","src":"Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025): acute asthma in children, primary care"},{"id":"rs8-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"COPD is confirmed by a post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC ratio under {{c1::0.7}}.","e":"Symptoms or a chest X-ray alone cannot make the diagnosis.","nt":"rs-copd#dx","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"The spirometry ratio that diagnoses COPD is measured {{c1::after::before or after}} a bronchodilator.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#dx","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Case-finding spirometry for COPD starts at age {{c1::35}} in people with a smoking history and symptoms.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#who","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Is a CT scan needed to diagnose COPD? {{c1::No}}","e":"It shows emphysema and bronchiectasis, and is ordered for red flags.","nt":"rs-copd#dx","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"COPD diagnosed before age 40 suggests {{c1::alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency::diagnosis}}.","e":"Especially with a family history. Refer.","nt":"rs-copd#dx","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"In COPD, an FEV1 rise over 15% and over 400 mL after a bronchodilator suggests coexisting {{c1::asthma}}.","e":"Overlap needs an inhaled corticosteroid with a long-acting bronchodilator.","nt":"rs-copd#dx","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Mild COPD: FEV1 of {{c1::60 to 80%}} predicted.","e":"Moderate 40 to 59%; severe under 40%.","nt":"rs-copd#severity","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Severe COPD: FEV1 under {{c1::40%}} predicted.","e":"Breathless on minimal exertion.","nt":"rs-copd#severity","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"COPD severity is graded at least {{c1::4 weeks}} after an exacerbation.","e":"The same wait applies before assessing for home oxygen.","nt":"rs-copd#severity","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"The questionnaire used to track COPD symptoms is the {{c1::COPD Assessment Test}}.","e":"CAT. Breathlessness is graded with the mMRC scale.","nt":"rs-copd#severity","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"The single most important intervention in COPD is {{c1::smoking cessation}}.","e":"Limits lung damage and reduces deaths.","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"The non-drug program recommended for anyone with symptomatic COPD is {{c1::pulmonary rehabilitation}}.","e":"Improves exercise capacity and quality of life and reduces exacerbations.","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Vaccines recommended in COPD: influenza, COVID-19 and {{c1::pneumococcal}}.","e":"Influenza every year.","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Physical activity target in COPD: at least {{c1::150 minutes}} a week.","e":"Moderate intensity. Any activity is better than none.","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Smoker with high nicotine dependence: combine a patch with a {{c1::fast-acting nicotine}} product.","e":"Gum, lozenge, spray or inhalator, with counselling.","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"COPD, breathless despite a reliever alone: add a {{c1::long-acting bronchodilator::drug class}}.","e":"A LAMA or a LABA.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"COPD, still breathless on a LAMA alone, no exacerbations: the next step is {{c1::LAMA plus LABA}}.","e":"Not an inhaled corticosteroid.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"In COPD an inhaled corticosteroid is added after {{c1::2}} moderate exacerbations in 12 months.","e":"Or one needing hospital admission, and only with ongoing symptoms on LAMA plus LABA.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"In COPD an inhaled corticosteroid is added after {{c1::1}} exacerbation needing hospital admission.","e":"Or two moderate ones in 12 months, with ongoing symptoms on dual bronchodilators.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"The infection made more likely by inhaled corticosteroids in COPD is {{c1::pneumonia::diagnosis}}.","e":"Also hoarseness and oral thrush.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Does a good response to oral prednisolone predict benefit from an inhaled corticosteroid in COPD? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Proportion of people with COPD who use their inhaler incorrectly: up to {{c1::90%}}.","e":"Check technique at every visit and before any step up.","nt":"rs-copd#steps","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Is an inhaled corticosteroid alone a treatment for COPD? {{c1::No}}","e":"Unless asthma is also present.","nt":"rs-copd#avoid","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Is theophylline recommended for COPD? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#avoid","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"A person with COPD has a heart attack. Can a cardioselective beta blocker be used? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"Safe in COPD when there is a cardiac indication.","nt":"rs-copd#avoid","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Refer for home oxygen assessment when resting saturation is {{c1::90%}} or less.","e":"When stable, at least 4 weeks after an exacerbation. An arterial gas on room air is needed.","nt":"rs-copd#oxygen","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Long-term oxygen is indicated when PaO2 is consistently under {{c1::55 mmHg}}.","e":"Or up to 59 mmHg with polycythaemia, pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure.","nt":"rs-copd#oxygen","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Long-term oxygen is used for more than {{c1::18 hours}} a day.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#oxygen","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Home oxygen is contraindicated in people who {{c1::still smoke}}.","e":"Fire and burns.","nt":"rs-copd#oxygen","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs8-30","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Spirometry in established COPD is repeated every {{c1::year}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd#refer","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs8-31","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: long-term management","o":1,"x":"Severe COPD is reviewed at least {{c1::twice a year}}.","e":"Mild to moderate: at least yearly.","nt":"rs-copd#refer","src":"eTG Respiratory: Stable COPD (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs9-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"COPD exacerbation, first step: salbutamol up to {{c1::8 puffs}} through a spacer.","e":"Repeated as needed.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"In a COPD exacerbation, reliever needed more often than every {{c1::3 hours}} means medical review.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"COPD exacerbation: prednisolone {{c1::30 to 50 mg}} daily.","e":"For 5 days, then stop.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"COPD exacerbation: prednisolone is given for {{c1::5 days}}.","e":"No taper. Asthma in adults: 5 to 10 days.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Ipratropium should not be given to a person already taking a {{c1::LAMA::drug class}}.","e":"Long-acting muscarinic antagonist, such as tiotropium.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"In COPD, a nebuliser is driven by {{c1::air::air or oxygen}}.","e":"Oxygen-driven nebulisers risk dangerous over-oxygenation.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Is sputum culture routine in a COPD exacerbation? {{c1::No}}","e":"Many patients are colonised, so a positive result does not prove infection.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"The sputum change that prompts antibiotics in a COPD exacerbation is increased {{c1::purulence}}.","e":"Together with more sputum volume or more breathlessness.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"COPD exacerbation with more breathlessness but unchanged white sputum: antibiotics? {{c1::No}}","e":"Bronchodilator, and prednisolone if needed.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"First-line antibiotics for a COPD exacerbation: amoxicillin or {{c1::doxycycline::drug}}.","e":"Not amoxicillin with clavulanate, macrolides or cephalosporins.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Amoxicillin dose for a COPD exacerbation: {{c1::500 mg}} every 8 hours.","e":"Or 1 g every 12 hours. Five days.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Doxycycline dose for a COPD exacerbation: {{c1::100 mg daily}}.","e":"For 5 days.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Antibiotic course length for a COPD exacerbation: {{c1::5 days}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"A COPD exacerbation needing non-invasive ventilation: are antibiotics given? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"They reduce treatment failure and deaths in this group.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"In a COPD exacerbation, a low {{c1::C-reactive protein}} supports withholding antibiotics.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"In mild COPD, exacerbation symptoms usually last {{c1::7 to 10 days}}.","e":"Weeks in moderate to severe disease. Slow recovery rarely needs a broader antibiotic.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Severe COPD, no response to amoxicillin at 48 to 72 hours, bacterial cause still likely: switch to {{c1::amoxicillin with clavulanate::drug}}.","e":"875+125 mg twice daily for 5 days.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"COPD exacerbation not responding, with Pseudomonas in recent sputum: use {{c1::ciprofloxacin::drug}}.","e":"750 mg twice daily for 5 days.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"In a COPD exacerbation, sputum culture is considered when FEV1 is under {{c1::50%}} predicted.","e":"Also with two or more exacerbations a year, recent hospital stay, Pseudomonas, or home oxygen.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Confusion or drowsiness during a COPD exacerbation suggests {{c1::carbon dioxide retention}}.","e":"An indication for hospital.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#hospital","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Oxygen target in a COPD exacerbation: {{c1::88 to 92%}}.","e":"Start when saturation is under 88%.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#oxygen","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Non-invasive ventilation in a COPD exacerbation: PaCO2 over 45 mmHg with pH under {{c1::7.35}}.","e":"Despite optimal treatment, including controlled oxygen.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#oxygen","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"After hospital discharge for a COPD exacerbation, GP review is within {{c1::7 days}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd-exac#after","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs9-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"After an admission for COPD, pulmonary rehabilitation can start within {{c1::2 to 4 weeks}} of discharge.","e":"","nt":"rs-copd-exac#after","src":"COPD-X Handbook (Lung Foundation Australia, 2024)"},{"id":"rs9-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"Frequent COPD exacerbations despite full treatment: the specialist option is a long-term low-dose {{c1::macrolide::drug class}}.","e":"After excluding non-tuberculous mycobacteria. Watch hearing and the QT interval.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#after","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs9-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"COPD: exacerbations","o":1,"x":"A hospital admission for COPD should prompt discussion of {{c1::advance care planning}}.","e":"A sentinel event.","nt":"rs-copd-exac#after","src":"eTG Respiratory: COPD exacerbations"},{"id":"rs10-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"First-line antibiotic for low-severity pneumonia in an adult: {{c1::amoxicillin::drug}}.","e":"1 g every 8 hours for 5 days. Targets pneumococcus.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Amoxicillin dose for low-severity pneumonia in an adult: {{c1::1 g}} every 8 hours.","e":"Twice the dose used for a COPD exacerbation.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Usual antibiotic duration for low-severity pneumonia: {{c1::5 days}}.","e":"Consider stopping at day 3 if clinically stable.","nt":"rs-cap#review","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Low-severity pneumonia with penicillin hypersensitivity: {{c1::doxycycline::drug}} 100 mg every 12 hours.","e":"Or clarithromycin 500 mg every 12 hours. Five days.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Doxycycline dose for pneumonia: 100 mg every {{c1::12 hours}}.","e":"For a COPD exacerbation it is once daily.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Low-severity pneumonia where Mycoplasma is suspected: use doxycycline or {{c1::clarithromycin::drug}}.","e":"Atypical organisms are intrinsically resistant to beta-lactams.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"The commonest bacterial cause of community-acquired pneumonia is {{c1::Streptococcus pneumoniae}}.","e":"Resistance to tetracyclines and macrolides is rising, hence amoxicillin.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Two antibiotics from the start in low-severity pneumonia: only with significant comorbidity and uncertain {{c1::follow-up}}.","e":"Amoxicillin plus doxycycline or clarithromycin.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In combination treatment for pneumonia, the oral cephalosporin for non-severe penicillin hypersensitivity is {{c1::cefuroxime::drug}}.","e":"500 mg every 12 hours. Better pneumococcal activity than cefalexin.","nt":"rs-cap#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In pneumonia, a respiratory rate of {{c1::22}} or more is a feature that may need admission.","e":"30 or more is a red flag for intensive care.","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In pneumonia, a heart rate over {{c1::100}} is a feature that may need admission.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In pneumonia, a systolic blood pressure under {{c1::90 mmHg}} is a feature that may need admission.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In pneumonia, a room-air saturation under {{c1::92%}} is a feature that may need admission.","e":"Or below the person's usual. Under 90% is a red flag for intensive care.","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"New confusion in an adult with pneumonia makes the severity at least {{c1::moderate}}.","e":"One admission feature is enough.","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Pneumonia with none of the admission features is {{c1::low severity}}.","e":"Treat at home with oral antibiotics and planned review.","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Pneumonia can worsen over the first {{c1::24 to 48 hours}}.","e":"Plan a review and give clear return advice.","nt":"rs-cap#severity","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"The acute chest infection that settles without antibiotics and is the main alternative to pneumonia is {{c1::acute bronchitis::diagnosis}}.","e":"No severe hypoxaemia, no focal signs.","nt":"rs-cap#features","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"A resting respiratory rate of {{c1::18}} or more raises the likelihood of pneumonia.","e":"Along with temperature over 37.8, pulse over 100 and saturation under 95%.","nt":"rs-cap#features","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Crackles that {{c1::do not clear::clear or not}} with coughing suggest pneumonia.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#features","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"In low-severity pneumonia, the one routine measurement is {{c1::oxygen saturation}}.","e":"Chest X-ray and viral PCR are considered, not mandatory.","nt":"rs-cap#tests","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"The finding that usually confirms pneumonia is new {{c1::consolidation}} on chest X-ray.","e":"PA and lateral views if possible.","nt":"rs-cap#tests","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Blood cultures are taken in pneumonia from {{c1::moderate}} severity upward.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#tests","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"A patient is well on day 5 of treatment for pneumonia but still coughing. Extend the antibiotic? {{c1::No}}","e":"Cough can last weeks and tiredness months.","nt":"rs-cap#review","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"A viral cause is confirmed in a patient started on amoxicillin for pneumonia. The antibiotic is {{c1::stopped}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#review","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Pneumonia not clinically stable by day {{c1::5}} carries a higher risk of death.","e":"Reassess severity and consider admission.","nt":"rs-cap#review","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Persistent fever and chest discomfort on treatment for pneumonia suggest {{c1::empyema::diagnosis}}.","e":"Or a parapneumonic effusion or lung abscess.","nt":"rs-cap#notbetter","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Pneumonia with poor dentition and foul-smelling sputum suggests {{c1::anaerobes}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#notbetter","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Pneumonia not improving in tropical northern Australia: the organism to consider is {{c1::Burkholderia pseudomallei}}.","e":"Melioidosis.","nt":"rs-cap#notbetter","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"The acid-suppressing drug class linked to a higher incidence of pneumonia is {{c1::proton pump inhibitors::drug class}}.","e":"Also benzodiazepines and other sedatives. Review them after an episode.","nt":"rs-cap#prevent","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs10-30","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumonia in adults","o":1,"x":"Vaccines reviewed after pneumonia: influenza, COVID-19 and {{c1::pneumococcal}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-cap#prevent","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Community-acquired pneumonia in adults (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs11-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Acute cough lasts up to {{c1::3 weeks}}.","e":"Subacute 3 to 8 weeks; chronic more than 8.","nt":"rs-cough#duration","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Chronic cough lasts more than {{c1::8 weeks}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-cough#duration","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Consider pertussis when a cough has lasted more than {{c1::2 weeks}}.","e":"With bouts of coughing, a whoop or vomiting after coughing. Adults often lack the classic signs.","nt":"rs-cough#duration","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Is an antibiotic indicated for acute bronchitis? {{c1::No}}","e":"It is almost always viral and self-limiting.","nt":"rs-cough#bronchitis","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute bronchitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs11-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Is a chest X-ray indicated for acute bronchitis? {{c1::No}}","e":"Only if pneumonia is suspected.","nt":"rs-cough#bronchitis","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute bronchitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs11-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"In acute bronchitis, does coloured sputum predict bacterial infection? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-cough#bronchitis","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute bronchitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs11-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"In acute bronchitis, 90% of coughs have resolved within {{c1::4 weeks}}.","e":"Usual duration up to 3 weeks.","nt":"rs-cough#bronchitis","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute bronchitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs11-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"The cough of acute bronchitis can last up to {{c1::8 weeks}} in about 1 in 10.","e":"Beyond that, investigate as chronic cough.","nt":"rs-cough#bronchitis","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute bronchitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs11-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Is salbutamol useful in acute bronchitis without airflow limitation? {{c1::No}}","e":"Nor oral or inhaled corticosteroids.","nt":"rs-cough#bronchitis","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute bronchitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs11-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"A patient with acute bronchitis should return if not better at {{c1::3 weeks}}.","e":"Or sooner if fever persists, symptoms worsen or new ones appear.","nt":"rs-cough#bronchitis","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute bronchitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs11-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"The best sample for pertussis PCR is a {{c1::posterior nasopharyngeal}} swab.","e":"Or aspirate. Throat and front-of-nose swabs are less sensitive.","nt":"rs-cough#exclude","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute bronchitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs11-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Acute cough with ankle oedema and weight gain suggests {{c1::heart failure::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-cough#exclude","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute bronchitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs11-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"The over-the-counter remedy that is safe and reasonable to try for cough is {{c1::honey}}.","e":"Most cough medicines have little or no benefit.","nt":"rs-cough#otc","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Is codeine better than placebo for cough? {{c1::No}}","e":"Antihistamines are no better than placebo either.","nt":"rs-cough#otc","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"The two tests every adult with chronic cough needs are a chest X-ray and {{c1::spirometry::test}}.","e":"Plus blood eosinophils, and FeNO where available.","nt":"rs-cough#chronic","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"The drug class that most often causes chronic cough is {{c1::ACE inhibitors::drug class}}.","e":"About 15% of users. Onset can be delayed, and it can take time to settle after stopping.","nt":"rs-cough#causes","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"About {{c1::15%}} of adults taking an ACE inhibitor develop a persistent dry cough.","e":"","nt":"rs-cough#causes","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"ACE inhibitor cough, and the drug is still needed: switch to an {{c1::angiotensin receptor blocker::drug class}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-cough#causes","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Chronic cough with features of asthma: trial an inhaled corticosteroid and review at {{c1::2 to 4 weeks}}.","e":"If it helps, confirm the diagnosis with testing.","nt":"rs-cough#causes","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Chronic cough is the only symptom. Is it treated as asthma? {{c1::No}}","e":"Nor as reflux or sinus disease.","nt":"rs-cough#causes","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Chronic cough with heartburn: trial a proton pump inhibitor and review at {{c1::8 to 12 weeks}}.","e":"Stop it if there is no response.","nt":"rs-cough#causes","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Chronic cough with no reflux symptoms: is a proton pump inhibitor trial recommended? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-cough#causes","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"A smoker over 50 with a changed cough and a hoarse voice needs {{c1::urgent referral::next step}}.","e":"For lung or laryngeal cancer.","nt":"rs-cough#causes","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Hoarseness in an adult with cough is a {{c1::red flag}}.","e":"So are coughing blood, weight loss, swallowing difficulty and a smoking history.","nt":"rs-cough#redflags","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"Chronic cough with voice change, a lump in the throat and brief breathless episodes: refer to a {{c1::speech pathologist}}.","e":"Inducible laryngeal obstruction.","nt":"rs-cough#causes","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"After stopping smoking, a chronic cough may temporarily {{c1::increase::increase or decrease}}.","e":"Warn people so they do not relapse.","nt":"rs-cough#causes","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs11-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","o":1,"x":"The allied health treatment recommended for refractory chronic cough is {{c1::speech therapy}}.","e":"Specialists may add amitriptyline, gabapentin or pregabalin.","nt":"rs-cough#refractory","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in adults"},{"id":"rs12-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"The commonest virus causing bronchiolitis is {{c1::respiratory syncytial virus}}.","e":"RSV. Also rhinovirus, metapneumovirus, parainfluenza and influenza.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronch","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute bronchiolitis (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Bronchiolitis is usually diagnosed only in children under {{c1::12 months}}.","e":"Peak under 6 months.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronch","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute bronchiolitis (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Bronchiolitis symptoms are worst around day {{c1::2 to 3}}.","e":"Then improve over 7 to 10 days. Cough can last weeks.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronch","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute bronchiolitis (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Is a chest X-ray routine in bronchiolitis? {{c1::No}}","e":"The diagnosis is clinical.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronch","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute bronchiolitis (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Admit an infant with bronchiolitis whose feeding is under {{c1::half}} of usual over 12 hours.","e":"","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronchadmit","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute bronchiolitis (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Admit an infant with bronchiolitis whose saturation is {{c1::92%}} or less.","e":"In hospital, oxygen is given to keep it at 92% or more.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronchadmit","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute bronchiolitis (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"In bronchiolitis, consider early admission even if mild when the infant is younger than {{c1::2 months}}.","e":"Also preterm, heart or lung disease, immunodeficiency, Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander infants, smoke exposure.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronchadmit","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute bronchiolitis (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Any {{c1::apnoea}} in an infant with bronchiolitis means admission.","e":"So does a marked fall in breathing rate, which signals tiring.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronchadmit","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute bronchiolitis (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Is salbutamol recommended for bronchiolitis? {{c1::No}}","e":"It does not shorten the illness or reduce the need for oxygen.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronchtreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute bronchiolitis (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Are corticosteroids recommended for bronchiolitis? {{c1::No}}","e":"Nor antibiotics, hypertonic saline or adrenaline.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronchtreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute bronchiolitis (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Feeding advice for an infant with mild bronchiolitis at home: {{c1::small, frequent feeds}}.","e":"With minimal handling.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronchtreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Acute bronchiolitis (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Croup usually affects children aged 6 months to {{c1::6 years}}.","e":"A first croup-like illness after that suggests another diagnosis.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#croup","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"The commonest virus causing croup is {{c1::parainfluenza}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-kids-airway#croup","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Croup with no stridor at rest is graded {{c1::mild}}.","e":"Stridor at rest is at least moderate.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#croup","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Is loud stridor a reliable sign of severe airway obstruction in croup? {{c1::No}}","e":"Judge by stridor at rest, retraction, fatigue and air entry.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#croup","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Croup that comes on at night with no viral prodrome and recurs is {{c1::spasmodic croup}}.","e":"Often with asthma. Treated the same way.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#croup","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Mild croup: is a corticosteroid given? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"A single oral dose reduces admissions and return visits.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Prednisolone dose for mild to moderate croup: {{c1::1 mg/kg}} as a single dose.","e":"Maximum 50 mg. Alternative: dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Dexamethasone dose for mild to moderate croup: {{c1::0.15 mg/kg}} as a single dose.","e":"Maximum 12 mg. Severe croup: 0.6 mg/kg.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Dexamethasone dose for severe croup: {{c1::0.6 mg/kg}}.","e":"Maximum 12 mg. Asthma uses the same 0.6 mg/kg.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"After a steroid for mild to moderate croup, observe for at least {{c1::30 minutes}}.","e":"No improvement: treat as severe.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"The nebulised drug for severe croup is {{c1::adrenaline::drug}}.","e":"5 mL of 1:1000, repeated after 30 minutes if needed. Plus a steroid, and transfer.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Nebulised adrenaline dose for severe croup: {{c1::5 mL}} of 1:1000 solution.","e":"The 1 mg/mL ampoule strength.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"After treatment of severe croup, observe for at least {{c1::4 hours}}.","e":"Home only if there is no stridor at rest. Follow up within 24 hours.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Is steam inhalation recommended for croup? {{c1::No}}","e":"No benefit, and a risk of scalds.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Oral steroid not tolerated in croup: use nebulised {{c1::budesonide::drug}}.","e":"2 mg.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Acute stridor straight after a choking episode suggests {{c1::inhaled foreign body::diagnosis}}.","e":"Urgent referral.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#stridor","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"High-pitched inspiratory stridor present from the first weeks of life is {{c1::laryngomalacia::diagnosis}}.","e":"Resolves by 1 to 2 years. Chronic stridor in an infant is referred.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#stridor","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Stridor that first appears at 6 to 8 weeks of age and steadily worsens suggests {{c1::subglottic haemangioma::diagnosis}}.","e":"Becomes present on breathing in and out.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#stridor","src":"eTG Respiratory: Croup (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs12-30","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Epiglottitis became rare in children because of the {{c1::Hib}} vaccine.","e":"Haemophilus influenzae type b. It is now seen more in adults.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#epiglottitis","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute epiglottitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs12-31","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","o":1,"x":"Suspected epiglottitis in the community: arrange urgent {{c1::escorted transfer}} to hospital.","e":"The airway can close quickly. Do not examine the throat.","nt":"rs-kids-airway#epiglottitis","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute epiglottitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"In a child, chronic cough lasts more than {{c1::4 weeks}}.","e":"Adults: 8 weeks.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#classify","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in children (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs13-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"In a child, acute cough lasts up to {{c1::2 weeks}}.","e":"Adults: 3 weeks.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#classify","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in children (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs13-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"After a viral infection, 90% of children are cough-free within {{c1::3 weeks}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-kids-cough#classify","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in children (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs13-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Sudden cough in a child who was eating or playing with small objects suggests {{c1::inhaled foreign body::diagnosis}}.","e":"Urgent referral, even weeks later.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#acute","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in children (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs13-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"The most likely cause of an isolated chronic wet cough in a well child is {{c1::protracted bacterial bronchitis::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-kids-cough#chronic","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in children (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs13-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"A child's cough that can be suppressed and stops with distraction is a {{c1::tic cough}}.","e":"Formerly habit cough.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#chronic","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in children (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs13-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"A child's chronic cough improves when the parents stop {{c1::smoking}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-kids-cough#chronic","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in children (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs13-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Protracted bacterial bronchitis: a wet cough lasting {{c1::4 weeks}} or more.","e":"That clears with 2 to 4 weeks of antibiotics.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#pbb","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Protracted bacterial bronchitis in children (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"A child's chronic cough is only ever dry. Can it be protracted bacterial bronchitis? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-kids-cough#pbb","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Protracted bacterial bronchitis in children (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"First-line antibiotic for protracted bacterial bronchitis: {{c1::amoxicillin with clavulanate::drug}}.","e":"22.5+3.2 mg/kg every 12 hours for 2 weeks.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#pbb","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Protracted bacterial bronchitis in children (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Antibiotic duration for protracted bacterial bronchitis: {{c1::2 weeks}}.","e":"Extended to 4 weeks if the cough has not cleared. Shorter courses relapse.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#pbb","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Protracted bacterial bronchitis in children (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Protracted bacterial bronchitis not cleared after {{c1::4 weeks}} of antibiotics: refer.","e":"","nt":"rs-kids-cough#pbb","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Protracted bacterial bronchitis in children (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Refer a child with {{c1::3 or more}} episodes of protracted bacterial bronchitis in a year.","e":"To exclude bronchiectasis.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#pbb","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Protracted bacterial bronchitis in children (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Recurrent protracted bacterial bronchitis raises the risk of {{c1::bronchiectasis::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-kids-cough#pbb","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Protracted bacterial bronchitis in children (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Protracted bacterial bronchitis with severe penicillin hypersensitivity: {{c1::trimethoprim with sulfamethoxazole::drug}}.","e":"Non-severe hypersensitivity: cefuroxime.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#pbb","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Protracted bacterial bronchitis in children (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Most pneumonia in children over 2 months is caused by {{c1::viruses}}.","e":"Examination cannot separate viral from bacterial.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#cap","src":"eTG Antibiotic: CAP in children 2 months or older (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Low-severity pneumonia in a child: saturation of {{c1::95%}} or more.","e":"With minimal fast breathing and a normal pulse.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#cap","src":"eTG Antibiotic: CAP in children 2 months or older (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"High-severity pneumonia in a child: saturation of {{c1::90%}} or lower.","e":"With grunting, nasal flaring and marked distress.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#cap","src":"eTG Antibiotic: CAP in children 2 months or older (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Amoxicillin dose for low-severity pneumonia in a child: {{c1::25 mg/kg}} every 8 hours.","e":"Maximum 1 g, for 3 days.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#cap","src":"eTG Antibiotic: CAP in children 2 months or older (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Antibiotic duration for low-severity pneumonia in a child: {{c1::3 days}}.","e":"Adults: 5 days.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#cap","src":"eTG Antibiotic: CAP in children 2 months or older (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"A child with low-severity pneumonia is reviewed if not improving at {{c1::48 to 72 hours}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-kids-cough#cap","src":"eTG Antibiotic: CAP in children 2 months or older (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Child with pneumonia and severe penicillin hypersensitivity: {{c1::azithromycin::drug}} 10 mg/kg daily for 3 days.","e":"Non-severe: cefuroxime.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#cap","src":"eTG Antibiotic: CAP in children 2 months or older (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Does a short course of doxycycline stain a young child's teeth? {{c1::No}}","e":"Courses under 21 days can be used at any age.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#cap","src":"eTG Antibiotic: CAP in children 2 months or older (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs13-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Cough and cold medicines should not be given to children under {{c1::6 years}}.","e":"Ages 6 to 11: only on professional advice.","nt":"rs-kids-cough#meds","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in children (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs13-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","o":1,"x":"Honey can be tried for cough in children older than {{c1::12 months}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-kids-cough#meds","src":"eTG Respiratory: Cough in children (Dec 2020)"},{"id":"rs14-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"The commonest cause of sore throat at every age is {{c1::viral infection}}.","e":"Settles within 7 days.","nt":"rs-urti#cause","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"The commonest bacterial cause of sore throat is {{c1::group A streptococcus}}.","e":"Streptococcus pyogenes. Most often in school-age children and adolescents.","nt":"rs-urti#cause","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"In a sore throat, the presence of cough makes streptococcal infection {{c1::less likely::more or less}}.","e":"So do a runny nose, hoarseness and conjunctivitis.","nt":"rs-urti#cause","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Sore throat with trismus and one-sided pain suggests {{c1::peritonsillar abscess::diagnosis}}.","e":"Urgent hospital transfer. Muffled voice, drooling and neck swelling are in the same group.","nt":"rs-urti#danger","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"High risk of acute rheumatic fever applies to people younger than {{c1::40 years}} in defined groups.","e":"Peak concern is ages 5 to 20.","nt":"rs-urti#arf","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"An Aboriginal child in a remote community has a sore throat with cough and runny nose. Antibiotics? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"High risk of rheumatic fever: treat every sore throat, even if it looks viral.","nt":"rs-urti#arf","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"A person with past rheumatic heart disease and a sore throat is at {{c1::high risk}} of acute rheumatic fever.","e":"So is anyone living with a person who has recently had it.","nt":"rs-urti#arf","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"In a person not at high risk of rheumatic fever, antibiotics shorten streptococcal sore throat by less than {{c1::1 day}}.","e":"No difference by day 7.","nt":"rs-urti#decide","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Not at high risk, sore throat with a scarlet fever rash: antibiotics? {{c1::Consider}}","e":"To reduce spread of toxin-producing strains. The other indication is severe symptoms.","nt":"rs-urti#decide","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"First-line antibiotic for streptococcal sore throat: {{c1::phenoxymethylpenicillin::drug}}.","e":"500 mg (child 15 mg/kg) every 12 hours for 10 days.","nt":"rs-urti#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Phenoxymethylpenicillin for streptococcal sore throat is given every {{c1::12 hours}}.","e":"Twice-daily dosing works and improves adherence.","nt":"rs-urti#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Antibiotic duration to prevent rheumatic fever after streptococcal sore throat: {{c1::10 days}}.","e":"Azithromycin is the exception at 5 days.","nt":"rs-urti#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Streptococcal sore throat in a high-risk patient unlikely to finish tablets: give {{c1::benzathine benzylpenicillin::drug}}.","e":"A single intramuscular dose. Adults: 1.2 million units.","nt":"rs-urti#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Streptococcal sore throat with non-severe penicillin hypersensitivity: {{c1::cefalexin::drug}}.","e":"1 g every 12 hours for 10 days. Severe hypersensitivity: azithromycin for 5 days.","nt":"rs-urti#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"A child with confirmed streptococcal sore throat can return to school {{c1::24 hours}} after the first antibiotic dose.","e":"If well.","nt":"rs-urti#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Severe throat pain: a single oral dose of {{c1::dexamethasone::drug}} can be considered.","e":"10 mg in adults. Or prednisolone 50 mg for 1 to 2 days.","nt":"rs-urti#treat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Sore throat"},{"id":"rs14-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Acute rhinosinusitis is diagnosed on {{c1::2 or more}} of four nasal and facial features.","e":"Blocked nose, discharge, facial pain or pressure, reduced smell.","nt":"rs-urti#sinus","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs14-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Is imaging needed to diagnose uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-urti#sinus","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs14-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Proportion of acute rhinosinusitis that becomes bacterial: under {{c1::2%}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-urti#sinus","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs14-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Acute rhinosinusitis usually resolves within {{c1::7 to 14 days}}.","e":"Often worse after day 5 first.","nt":"rs-urti#sinus","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs14-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Rhinosinusitis lasting longer than {{c1::12 weeks}} is chronic.","e":"","nt":"rs-urti#sinus","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs14-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Antibiotics are considered for acute rhinosinusitis that has not improved after {{c1::10 days}}.","e":"Or if very unwell, or worsening after first improving.","nt":"rs-urti#sinus","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs14-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Sinus symptoms improve, then fever and facial pain return. Is this a reason to consider antibiotics? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"Worsening after initial improvement.","nt":"rs-urti#sinus","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs14-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Antibiotic for acute bacterial rhinosinusitis: amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours for {{c1::5 days}}.","e":"Or 1 g every 12 hours.","nt":"rs-urti#sinus","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs14-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"A decongestant nasal spray is limited to {{c1::5 days}}.","e":"Longer causes rebound congestion.","nt":"rs-urti#sinus","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs14-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Do oral antihistamines relieve acute rhinosinusitis without allergy? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-urti#sinus","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs14-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Sinusitis with eyelid swelling and double vision needs {{c1::urgent surgical referral::next step}}.","e":"And intravenous antibiotics: infection has spread beyond the sinuses.","nt":"rs-urti#sinuscomp","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs14-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","o":1,"x":"Do oral antibiotics for uncomplicated rhinosinusitis prevent orbital or intracranial spread? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-urti#sinuscomp","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Acute rhinosinusitis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Does this season's vaccination rule out influenza? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-flu#fludx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"The preferred test for influenza is {{c1::PCR}}.","e":"On a nose and throat or nasopharyngeal swab. Rapid antigen tests are less sensitive.","nt":"rs-flu#fludx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"In a healthy adult, an influenza antiviral started within 48 hours shortens illness by about {{c1::1 day}}.","e":"Not routinely recommended.","nt":"rs-flu#flutreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Is an antiviral recommended for a pregnant woman with influenza? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"Also up to 2 weeks after birth. Use oseltamivir.","nt":"rs-flu#flutreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"For a patient admitted with influenza, antivirals can be started up to {{c1::7 days}} after onset.","e":"Benefit is greatest within 48 hours.","nt":"rs-flu#flutreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Oseltamivir treatment dose for an adult: 75 mg {{c1::twice daily}}.","e":"For 5 days. Prophylaxis is once daily for 10 days.","nt":"rs-flu#flutreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Oseltamivir treatment course: {{c1::5 days}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-flu#flutreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"The single-dose oral antiviral for influenza is {{c1::baloxavir::drug}}.","e":"Age 12 and over. No pregnancy data.","nt":"rs-flu#flutreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"The antipyretic to avoid in children with influenza is {{c1::aspirin::drug}}.","e":"Under 16 years: Reye syndrome.","nt":"rs-flu#flutreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"For influenza, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people are in the higher-risk group at {{c1::any age}}.","e":"Others: 65 and over, under 5, pregnancy, residential care, chronic disease.","nt":"rs-flu#flutreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Oseltamivir prophylaxis after influenza exposure: 75 mg once daily for {{c1::10 days}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-flu#fluprev","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Influenza post-exposure prophylaxis is considered within {{c1::48 hours}} of exposure.","e":"For high-risk people not protected by vaccine.","nt":"rs-flu#fluprev","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Influenza (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Pertussis is suspected when a cough has lasted more than {{c1::2 weeks}} with typical features.","e":"Bouts of coughing, inspiratory whoop, or vomiting after coughing.","nt":"rs-flu#pertdx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"In a young infant, pertussis may present with {{c1::apnoea}}.","e":"Infants under 12 months have the highest risk of death.","nt":"rs-flu#pertdx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Pertussis PCR is most sensitive in the first {{c1::3 to 4 weeks}} of cough.","e":"","nt":"rs-flu#pertdx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Is pertussis notifiable? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"","nt":"rs-flu#pertdx","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Antibiotics for pertussis are given only within {{c1::3 weeks}} of cough onset.","e":"After that the person is rarely infectious.","nt":"rs-flu#perttreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"First-line antibiotic for pertussis: {{c1::azithromycin::drug}}.","e":"Adults: 500 mg on day 1, then 250 mg daily for 4 days.","nt":"rs-flu#perttreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"A person with pertussis avoids contact with others until {{c1::5 days}} of antibiotics have been taken.","e":"","nt":"rs-flu#perttreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Do antibiotics clearly shorten the cough of pertussis? {{c1::No}}","e":"They clear the organism and reduce spread.","nt":"rs-flu#perttreat","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"A pertussis contact is someone within 1 metre of an infectious case for more than {{c1::1 hour}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-flu#pertcontacts","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Pertussis prophylaxis is aimed at protecting infants younger than {{c1::6 months}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-flu#pertcontacts","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Pertussis prophylaxis is given to contacts in the last {{c1::month}} of pregnancy.","e":"","nt":"rs-flu#pertcontacts","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"Pertussis prophylaxis should start within {{c1::14 days}} of first contact.","e":"Up to 21 days for the highest-risk contacts.","nt":"rs-flu#pertcontacts","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs15-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Influenza and pertussis","o":1,"x":"A household with a newborn has a member with pertussis. Who gets prophylaxis? {{c1::The whole household}}","e":"","nt":"rs-flu#pertcontacts","src":"eTG Antibiotic: Pertussis (Dec 2025)"},{"id":"rs16-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Chronic productive cough, coarse crackles and recurrent chest infections suggest {{c1::bronchiectasis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Sputum is usually purulent and may be blood-streaked.","nt":"rs-bronch#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Sputum growing Pseudomonas aeruginosa or Haemophilus influenzae should prompt you to consider {{c1::bronchiectasis::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-bronch#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"In bronchiectasis, finger clubbing is {{c1::uncommon::common or uncommon}}.","e":"Under 5% of patients.","nt":"rs-bronch#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"The crackles of bronchiectasis are typically {{c1::coarse::fine or coarse}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-bronch#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"The gold standard test for bronchiectasis is {{c1::high-resolution CT::test}}.","e":"The bronchus is wider than the adjacent vessel.","nt":"rs-bronch#diagnose","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Does a normal chest X-ray exclude bronchiectasis? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-bronch#diagnose","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Does normal spirometry exclude bronchiectasis? {{c1::No}}","e":"About half have normal spirometry; about a third have obstruction.","nt":"rs-bronch#diagnose","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"The most common identifiable cause of bronchiectasis is previous {{c1::pneumonia}}.","e":"Often in childhood. Many cases are idiopathic.","nt":"rs-bronch#diagnose","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"In bronchiectasis of unclear cause, total IgE and Aspergillus antibodies look for {{c1::ABPA::diagnosis}}.","e":"Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis.","nt":"rs-bronch#diagnose","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"In bronchiectasis of unclear cause, serum IgG, IgA and IgM look for {{c1::antibody deficiency}}.","e":"For example common variable immunodeficiency.","nt":"rs-bronch#diagnose","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"The test for cystic fibrosis in a child with bronchiectasis is {{c1::sweat chloride::test}}.","e":"Also for adults with suggestive symptoms.","nt":"rs-bronch#diagnose","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"A child's wet cough persists beyond {{c1::4 weeks}} despite antibiotics. Refer to a paediatric specialist.","e":"To investigate for bronchiectasis.","nt":"rs-bronch#kids","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"In children, the airway widening of bronchiectasis may be {{c1::reversible}} with early treatment.","e":"","nt":"rs-bronch#kids","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"For airway clearance in bronchiectasis, refer to a {{c1::respiratory physiotherapist}}.","e":"For an individual program.","nt":"rs-bronch#manage","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Bronchiectasis with reduced exercise capacity: refer for {{c1::pulmonary rehabilitation}}.","e":"As well as encouraging regular exercise.","nt":"rs-bronch#manage","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Bronchiectasis without asthma or COPD. Should an inhaled corticosteroid be prescribed routinely? {{c1::No}}","e":"The same applies to bronchodilators.","nt":"rs-bronch#manage","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"In stable bronchiectasis, spirometry is repeated every {{c1::year}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-bronch#manage","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"A stable patient with bronchiectasis grows Haemophilus influenzae in sputum. Antibiotics? {{c1::No}}","e":"Colonising organisms are not treated in stable patients.","nt":"rs-bronch#manage","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of bronchiectasis in adults"},{"id":"rs16-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Nebulised hypertonic saline can cause {{c1::bronchospasm}}.","e":"It is started after a supervised challenge.","nt":"rs-bronch#manage","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Consider antibiotics for a bronchiectasis exacerbation with {{c1::3}} or more of six features.","e":"More cough, more sputum, more purulence, more breathlessness, fatigue, haemoptysis.","nt":"rs-bronch#exac","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of bronchiectasis in adults"},{"id":"rs16-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Features of a bronchiectasis exacerbation must be present for at least {{c1::48 hours}} before antibiotics are considered.","e":"","nt":"rs-bronch#exac","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of bronchiectasis in adults"},{"id":"rs16-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Before antibiotics for a bronchiectasis exacerbation, collect {{c1::sputum culture::test}}.","e":"And review results from the last 12 months.","nt":"rs-bronch#exac","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of bronchiectasis in adults"},{"id":"rs16-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Empirical oral penicillin for a bronchiectasis exacerbation: {{c1::amoxicillin::drug}} 1 g every 8 hours.","e":"Doxycycline is the equal alternative.","nt":"rs-bronch#exac","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of bronchiectasis in adults"},{"id":"rs16-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Bronchiectasis exacerbation with penicillin hypersensitivity: {{c1::doxycycline::drug}} 100 mg every 12 hours.","e":"","nt":"rs-bronch#exac","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of bronchiectasis in adults"},{"id":"rs16-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Amoxicillin for a bronchiectasis exacerbation: 1 g every {{c1::8 hours}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-bronch#exac","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of bronchiectasis in adults"},{"id":"rs16-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"More than {{c1::three}} exacerbations of bronchiectasis a year: refer to a respiratory physician.","e":"Long-term macrolides may be considered by the specialist.","nt":"rs-bronch#refer","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Sputum in bronchiectasis grows Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Next step: {{c1::refer}}.","e":"To a respiratory physician.","nt":"rs-bronch#refer","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Massive haemoptysis in bronchiectasis: {{c1::immediate hospital transfer::next step}}.","e":"Bronchial artery embolisation may be needed.","nt":"rs-bronch#refer","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs16-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Bronchiectasis","o":1,"x":"Blood-streaked purulent sputum in bronchiectasis: review use of NSAIDs and {{c1::anticoagulants::drug class}}.","e":"And assess for infection.","nt":"rs-bronch#refer","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"},{"id":"rs17-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Consider pulmonary tuberculosis when a cough lasts more than {{c1::3 weeks}} in someone possibly exposed.","e":"With fevers, night sweats or weight loss.","nt":"rs-tb#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Pulmonary tuberculosis classically affects the {{c1::upper::upper or lower}} lobes.","e":"With cavities.","nt":"rs-tb#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Sterile pyuria with urinary frequency in a person with risk factors suggests {{c1::genitourinary tuberculosis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Fever and weight loss are often absent.","nt":"rs-tb#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"The three sputum tests for tuberculosis are acid-fast microscopy, mycobacterial culture and {{c1::PCR::test}}.","e":"Routine culture does not detect it.","nt":"rs-tb#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"The sputum test that can rapidly detect drug resistance in tuberculosis is {{c1::PCR::test}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Suspected tuberculous lymph node: {{c1::fine-needle aspirate::test}} or excision biopsy.","e":"For histology, culture and PCR.","nt":"rs-tb#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Children under {{c1::10}} years rarely produce sputum for tuberculosis testing.","e":"Gastric aspirates or induced sputum are used.","nt":"rs-tb#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Urine sample for genitourinary tuberculosis: large-volume {{c1::early-morning}} urine.","e":"For acid-fast microscopy, culture and PCR.","nt":"rs-tb#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Is tuberculosis notifiable? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"Notify promptly; the tuberculosis program runs contact tracing.","nt":"rs-tb#publichealth","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis is usually non-infectious after {{c1::5 days}} of standard treatment.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#publichealth","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Standard tuberculosis treatment lasts {{c1::6 months}}.","e":"Two months of four drugs, then four months of two.","nt":"rs-tb#treat","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"The two drugs continued for the full 6 months of tuberculosis treatment are isoniazid and {{c1::rifampicin::drug}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#treat","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"The two drugs stopped after 2 months of tuberculosis treatment are ethambutol and {{c1::pyrazinamide::drug}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#treat","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"The intensive phase of tuberculosis treatment lasts {{c1::2 months}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#treat","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Everyone diagnosed with tuberculosis should be tested for {{c1::HIV}}.","e":"Tuberculosis is an indicator condition. Hepatitis B and C serology too.","nt":"rs-tb#treat","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"The tuberculosis drug that reduces the effect of oral contraceptives is {{c1::rifampicin::drug}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#treat","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"To prevent peripheral neuropathy, isoniazid is given with {{c1::pyridoxine::drug}} in people at risk.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#treat","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Before starting ethambutol, test visual acuity and {{c1::colour vision}}.","e":"Repeated monthly.","nt":"rs-tb#treat","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Blurred vision during tuberculosis treatment: stop {{c1::ethambutol::drug}} and refer to an ophthalmologist.","e":"Optic neuritis.","nt":"rs-tb#monitor","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Ethambutol accumulates in {{c1::kidney impairment}}.","e":"Raising the risk of ocular toxicity.","nt":"rs-tb#monitor","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain on tuberculosis treatment: check {{c1::liver function::test}}.","e":"Isoniazid, rifampicin and pyrazinamide are all hepatotoxic.","nt":"rs-tb#monitor","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Stop all tuberculosis drugs if transaminases reach {{c1::5}} times the upper limit without symptoms.","e":"Or 3 times with symptoms, or any jaundice.","nt":"rs-tb#monitor","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Stop all tuberculosis drugs if transaminases reach {{c1::3}} times the upper limit with symptoms.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#monitor","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"A petechial rash on tuberculosis treatment suggests thrombocytopenia from {{c1::rifampicin::drug}}.","e":"Check the platelet count.","nt":"rs-tb#monitor","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Tuberculosis drugs are reintroduced once ALT falls below {{c1::2}} times the upper limit.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#monitor","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"A positive interferon gamma release assay. First step: exclude {{c1::active tuberculosis}}.","e":"History, examination and chest X-ray.","nt":"rs-tb#infection","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Lifetime risk that tuberculosis infection progresses to active disease: {{c1::5 to 10%}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#infection","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Single-drug preventive treatment for tuberculosis infection in adults: {{c1::rifampicin::drug}} daily for 4 months.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#infection","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"Rifampicin alone for tuberculosis infection is taken for {{c1::4 months}}.","e":"With isoniazid, 3 months.","nt":"rs-tb#infection","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-30","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"In tuberculosis infection, the liver risk of isoniazid {{c1::rises}} with age.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#infection","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs17-31","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Tuberculosis","o":1,"x":"A close contact declines preventive treatment for tuberculosis. Monitor for at least {{c1::2 years}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-tb#infection","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), tuberculosis topics"},{"id":"rs18-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"Sudden pleuritic chest pain and breathlessness in a healthy young adult suggests {{c1::pneumothorax::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-pleural#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"A spontaneous pneumothorax in a person with COPD is called {{c1::secondary::primary or secondary}}.","e":"Primary means no underlying lung disease.","nt":"rs-pleural#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"The chest X-ray for suspected pneumothorax is taken in {{c1::inspiration::inspiration or expiration}}.","e":"Expiratory films exaggerate the size.","nt":"rs-pleural#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"In a large pneumothorax the trachea deviates {{c1::away from}} the affected side.","e":"","nt":"rs-pleural#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"The percussion note over a large pneumothorax is {{c1::hyperresonant}}.","e":"With absent breath sounds.","nt":"rs-pleural#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"The most common lung disease behind a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax is {{c1::COPD}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-pleural#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"Is an X-ray needed to diagnose tension pneumothorax? {{c1::No}}","e":"An unstable patient: severe breathlessness, hypoxaemia, hypotension.","nt":"rs-pleural#tension","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"Immediate treatment of tension pneumothorax: {{c1::needle decompression::next step}}.","e":"Followed by a chest drain.","nt":"rs-pleural#tension","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"Needle decompression: insert the cannula above the {{c1::third}} rib in the midclavicular line.","e":"The second intercostal space.","nt":"rs-pleural#tension","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"Needle decompression is performed in the {{c1::midclavicular}} line.","e":"Above the third rib.","nt":"rs-pleural#tension","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"A stable patient's X-ray shows a large pneumothorax with mediastinal shift. Is needle decompression needed? {{c1::No}}","e":"Decompression is for clinical instability.","nt":"rs-pleural#tension","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"A patient on CPAP suddenly deteriorates. Consider {{c1::tension pneumothorax::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-pleural#tension","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"A stable patient with a primary spontaneous pneumothorax of any size is first managed {{c1::conservatively}}.","e":"Analgesia, and oxygen only if hypoxaemic.","nt":"rs-pleural#primary","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"In primary spontaneous pneumothorax, breathlessness often improves with {{c1::analgesia}}.","e":"Much of it is pain on breathing.","nt":"rs-pleural#primary","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"In primary spontaneous pneumothorax, pleural intervention is for patients who are clinically {{c1::unstable}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-pleural#primary","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"After a spontaneous pneumothorax, review every {{c1::2 weeks}} until it has resolved.","e":"","nt":"rs-pleural#primary","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"The recurrence risk after a primary spontaneous pneumothorax is about {{c1::30 to 50%}}.","e":"Higher in people who keep smoking.","nt":"rs-pleural#recur","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"A pneumothorax recurs in the same lung. Refer for {{c1::pleurodesis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-pleural#recur","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"The lifestyle change that lowers the risk of pneumothorax recurrence is {{c1::stopping smoking}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-pleural#recur","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"The usual intervention for a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax is {{c1::a chest drain}}.","e":"Aspiration is less likely to succeed.","nt":"rs-pleural#secondary","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"Should CPAP be used with an untreated pneumothorax? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-pleural#secondary","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"},{"id":"rs18-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"Pleural fluid is classed as transudate or exudate using {{c1::Light's}} criteria.","e":"","nt":"rs-pleural#effusion","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pleural effusion in adults"},{"id":"rs18-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"A pleural effusion from heart failure is usually a {{c1::transudate::transudate or exudate}}.","e":"Also cirrhosis, nephrotic syndrome and peritoneal dialysis.","nt":"rs-pleural#effusion","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pleural effusion in adults"},{"id":"rs18-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"Pleural aspiration is performed under direct {{c1::ultrasound}} guidance.","e":"","nt":"rs-pleural#effusion","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pleural effusion in adults"},{"id":"rs18-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","o":1,"x":"Pleural fluid culture should include a request for {{c1::mycobacteria}}.","e":"With biochemistry and cytology.","nt":"rs-pleural#effusion","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pleural effusion in adults"},{"id":"rs19-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"Loud snoring, witnessed pauses in breathing and daytime sleepiness suggest {{c1::obstructive sleep apnoea::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-osa#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"The genetic condition in which nearly all adults have obstructive sleep apnoea is {{c1::Down syndrome}}.","e":"Consider it even without symptoms.","nt":"rs-osa#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"In suspected sleep apnoea, also take a history from the {{c1::bed partner}}.","e":"Patients underrate their own sleepiness.","nt":"rs-osa#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"The arrhythmia most associated with untreated sleep apnoea is {{c1::atrial fibrillation}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-osa#risks","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"In pregnancy, untreated sleep apnoea is associated with hypertension and {{c1::pre-eclampsia}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-osa#risks","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"Daytime sleepiness is scored with the {{c1::Epworth}} Sleepiness Scale.","e":"","nt":"rs-osa#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"The diagnostic test for obstructive sleep apnoea is {{c1::a sleep study::test}}.","e":"Polysomnography, at home or in a laboratory.","nt":"rs-osa#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"A GP can order a subsidised sleep study if the Epworth score is at least {{c1::8}}, with a positive screening questionnaire.","e":"STOP-Bang 4 or more, OSA50 5 or more, or high-risk Berlin.","nt":"rs-osa#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"The STOP-Bang threshold for a GP-ordered sleep study is {{c1::4}} or more.","e":"With an Epworth score of 8 or more.","nt":"rs-osa#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"The OSA50 threshold for a GP-ordered sleep study is {{c1::5}} or more.","e":"With an Epworth score of 8 or more.","nt":"rs-osa#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"Significant sleep apnoea: an apnoea-hypopnoea index above {{c1::5}} events an hour.","e":"","nt":"rs-osa#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"Severe sleep apnoea: an apnoea-hypopnoea index above {{c1::30}} events an hour.","e":"","nt":"rs-osa#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"Suspected sleep apnoea with heart failure: the sleep study should be done {{c1::in a laboratory}}.","e":"Also for lung or neuromuscular disease and suspected respiratory failure.","nt":"rs-osa#test","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"The nasal spray that can help sleep apnoea by lowering nasal resistance is an intranasal {{c1::corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-osa#general","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"Positional sleep apnoea: raise the head of the bed by {{c1::5 to 8 cm}}.","e":"And sleep on the side.","nt":"rs-osa#general","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"The most effective treatment for obstructive sleep apnoea is {{c1::CPAP}}.","e":"It splints the upper airway open.","nt":"rs-osa#cpap","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"Has CPAP been shown in a randomised trial to reduce cardiovascular events? {{c1::No}}","e":"It does lower blood pressure and sleepiness.","nt":"rs-osa#cpap","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"A dry mouth on CPAP usually means a {{c1::mouth leak}}.","e":"Try a chin strap.","nt":"rs-osa#cpap","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"Mild sleep apnoea, no daytime symptoms, normal blood pressure. Is CPAP required? {{c1::No}}","e":"General measures and watching are reasonable.","nt":"rs-osa#cpap","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"A mandibular advancement splint is effective only in {{c1::mild to moderate}} sleep apnoea.","e":"Fitted and reviewed by a dentist.","nt":"rs-osa#cpap","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs19-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","o":1,"x":"Sleep apnoea with severe daytime sleepiness. Driving advice: {{c1::stop driving}} until stable on treatment.","e":"The same for operating machinery.","nt":"rs-osa#drive","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"},{"id":"rs20-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"Progressive breathlessness, dry cough and fine velcro-like crackles suggest {{c1::interstitial lung disease::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-ild#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"The crackles of interstitial lung disease are typically {{c1::fine::fine or coarse}}.","e":"Coarse crackles suggest bronchiectasis.","nt":"rs-ild#suspect","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"The urinary antibiotic known to cause interstitial lung disease is {{c1::nitrofurantoin::drug}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-ild#cause","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"The antiarrhythmic known to cause interstitial lung disease is {{c1::amiodarone::drug}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-ild#cause","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"Two antirheumatic drugs that cause interstitial lung disease are leflunomide and {{c1::methotrexate::drug}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-ild#cause","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"Breathlessness in a person who keeps birds suggests {{c1::hypersensitivity pneumonitis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Bird fancier's lung.","nt":"rs-ild#cause","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis typically presents after the age of {{c1::65}}.","e":"More often in men who have smoked.","nt":"rs-ild#cause","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"Spirometry in interstitial lung disease shows a {{c1::restrictive::obstructive or restrictive}} pattern.","e":"Reduced FVC with a normal or raised ratio.","nt":"rs-ild#workup","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"In restrictive lung disease the FEV1/FVC ratio is {{c1::normal or raised}}.","e":"The FVC is reduced.","nt":"rs-ild#workup","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"The pivotal imaging test for interstitial lung disease is {{c1::high-resolution CT::test}}.","e":"A chest X-ray may be normal early.","nt":"rs-ild#workup","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"Suspected interstitial lung disease in general practice: after first tests, {{c1::refer::next step}}.","e":"Diagnosis is made by a multidisciplinary team.","nt":"rs-ild#workup","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"A fall in FVC of {{c1::10%}} suggests progressive interstitial lung disease.","e":"","nt":"rs-ild#workup","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"The CT pattern of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is called {{c1::usual interstitial pneumonia}}.","e":"Basal, subpleural reticulation with honeycombing.","nt":"rs-ild#ipf","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"The two antifibrotic drugs for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis are nintedanib and {{c1::pirfenidone::drug}}.","e":"They slow progression.","nt":"rs-ild#ipf","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"Is prednisolone with azathioprine recommended for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis? {{c1::No}}","e":"With acetylcysteine it increased deaths.","nt":"rs-ild#ipf","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"Untreated idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis usually causes death within {{c1::3 to 5 years}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-ild#ipf","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"Enlarged hilar lymph nodes found by chance in a well young adult suggest {{c1::sarcoidosis::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-ild#sarcoid","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"Is finger clubbing a feature of pulmonary sarcoidosis? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-ild#sarcoid","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"The electrolyte to check in sarcoidosis is {{c1::calcium}}.","e":"Hypercalcaemia and hypercalciuria can occur.","nt":"rs-ild#sarcoid","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"Asymptomatic sarcoidosis limited to hilar lymph nodes is usually managed by {{c1::observation}}.","e":"Many cases resolve spontaneously.","nt":"rs-ild#sarcoid","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"The dust from cutting artificial stone benchtops causes {{c1::silicosis::diagnosis}}.","e":"Worst with dry cutting.","nt":"rs-ild#exposure","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"The main treatment for hypersensitivity pneumonitis is {{c1::avoiding the exposure}}.","e":"Corticosteroids for acute severe or progressive disease.","nt":"rs-ild#exposure","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs20-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Interstitial lung disease","o":1,"x":"The main treatment for smoking-related interstitial lung disease is {{c1::stopping smoking}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-ild#exposure","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"},{"id":"rs21-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Everyone with allergic rhinitis should be assessed for {{c1::asthma::diagnosis}}.","e":"Present in 15 to 38%.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#asthma","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"The pollen allergy linked to thunderstorm asthma is {{c1::rye grass}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#asthma","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Persistent allergic rhinitis: symptoms on more than {{c1::4}} days a week and for more than 4 weeks.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#dx","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Allergic rhinitis that disturbs sleep is classed as {{c1::moderate to severe}}.","e":"Also if it impairs work, school, sport or daily activities.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#dx","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"The usual year-round indoor trigger for allergic rhinitis is {{c1::dust mite}}.","e":"Also pet dander and mould.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#dx","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"A one-sided blocked nose with bloody discharge. Is this consistent with allergic rhinitis? {{c1::No}}","e":"Look for another diagnosis.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#flags","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Is a sinus CT recommended for uncomplicated rhinitis? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#flags","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"For mild intermittent allergic rhinitis with sneezing and itch, use a less-sedating oral {{c1::antihistamine::drug class}}.","e":"Fast onset; can be taken as needed.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#oral","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"The less-sedating antihistamine most likely to cause sedation is {{c1::cetirizine::drug}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#oral","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Oral antihistamines are least effective for which symptom of rhinitis? {{c1::Nasal blockage}}","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#oral","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Does adding an antihistamine nasal spray to an oral antihistamine give extra benefit? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#oral","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Adult dose of loratadine for allergic rhinitis: {{c1::10 mg}} daily.","e":"Cetirizine is the same.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#oral","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"The most effective treatment for nasal blockage in allergic rhinitis is an intranasal {{c1::corticosteroid::drug class}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#incs","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"The full effect of an intranasal corticosteroid takes {{c1::2 to 4 weeks}}.","e":"Relief begins within days.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#incs","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"For severe seasonal rhinitis, start the intranasal corticosteroid at least {{c1::2 weeks}} before the pollen season.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#incs","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Before starting a combined antihistamine and corticosteroid nasal spray, stop the {{c1::oral antihistamine}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#incs","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Trial a new rhinitis treatment for about {{c1::4 weeks}} before judging response.","e":"Then check adherence and technique before stepping up.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#incs","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"The most common adverse effect of intranasal corticosteroids is minor {{c1::nosebleeds}}.","e":"Also dryness and stinging.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#incs","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"A nasal spray is aimed {{c1::away from}} the septum.","e":"Towards the outer wall and the ear, using the opposite hand.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#technique","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"For a nasal spray the head is tilted {{c1::forward::forward or back}}.","e":"Looking down.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#technique","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Wait {{c1::10 minutes}} between two different nasal sprays.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#technique","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Should vasoconstrictor eye drops be used for allergic conjunctivitis? {{c1::No}}","e":"They cause rebound redness.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#eyes","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Do decongestants have a role in allergic rhinitis? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#eyes","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Rebound congestion from prolonged decongestant spray is called {{c1::rhinitis medicamentosa}}.","e":"It can occur after 3 to 5 days of use.","nt":"rs-rhinitis#eyes","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Decongestant nasal sprays should not be used for more than {{c1::3 to 5 days}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#eyes","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-26","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"The leukotriene antagonist that needs a warning about mood and behaviour change is {{c1::montelukast::drug}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#eyes","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-27","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"The only treatment that alters the course of allergic rhinitis is {{c1::allergen immunotherapy}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#refer","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-28","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"A course of allergen immunotherapy lasts {{c1::3 to 5 years}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#refer","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs21-29","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Allergic rhinitis","o":1,"x":"Subcutaneous immunotherapy is always given under medical supervision because of the risk of {{c1::anaphylaxis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-rhinitis#refer","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"},{"id":"rs22-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"Before stepping up inhaled treatment, always check {{c1::inhaler technique}}.","e":"And adherence.","nt":"rs-devices#principles","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"A patient cannot use a device correctly despite repeated teaching. Next step: {{c1::change the device}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-devices#principles","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"The inhaler type with the lower carbon footprint is the {{c1::dry powder inhaler}}.","e":"Compared with pressurised metered dose inhalers.","nt":"rs-devices#principles","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"Dry powder inhalers need a {{c1::strong}} inspiratory flow.","e":"Weak flow means little drug reaches the lungs.","nt":"rs-devices#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"Can a dry powder inhaler be used with a spacer? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-devices#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"Can a mist inhaler (Respimat) be used with a spacer? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"","nt":"rs-devices#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"Can a breath-actuated puffer (Autohaler) be used with a spacer? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-devices#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"Dry powder inhalers must be kept {{c1::dry}}, and the patient must not breathe out into them.","e":"Moisture stops the powder dispersing.","nt":"rs-devices#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"A new pressurised puffer should be {{c1::primed}} before first use.","e":"By spraying into the air.","nt":"rs-devices#types","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"A spacer reduces local adverse effects of inhaled corticosteroids by reducing {{c1::oropharyngeal}} deposition.","e":"","nt":"rs-devices#spacers","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"The usually preferred spacer is the {{c1::small-volume}} spacer.","e":"It needs fewer breaths.","nt":"rs-devices#spacers","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"Children take {{c1::4 to 6}} normal breaths per puff through a spacer.","e":"","nt":"rs-devices#spacers","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"After inhaled corticosteroid given by face mask, {{c1::wash the face}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-devices#spacers","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"The preferred device for children of all ages is a puffer with a {{c1::spacer}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-devices#kids","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"A face mask is added to the spacer for children under {{c1::4}} years.","e":"Or any child who cannot seal the lips.","nt":"rs-devices#kids","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"An older adult with weak inspiratory flow should avoid {{c1::dry powder}} inhalers.","e":"And breath-actuated puffers.","nt":"rs-devices#older","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"An adult who cannot coordinate pressing and breathing should use a puffer with a {{c1::spacer}}.","e":"Or a breath-actuated device.","nt":"rs-devices#older","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"The aid that helps arthritic hands press a puffer is a {{c1::Haleraid}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-devices#older","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"For reliever in an acute wheezing episode, a nebuliser is {{c1::less}} effective than a puffer with a spacer.","e":"","nt":"rs-devices#nebs","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"Nebulised ipratropium by face mask can precipitate acute {{c1::angle-closure glaucoma}}.","e":"Use a mouthpiece.","nt":"rs-devices#nebs","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs22-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Inhaler devices","o":1,"x":"Should a nebuliser be used in a patient with an acute respiratory infection? {{c1::No}}","e":"It can spread infective organisms.","nt":"rs-devices#nebs","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"},{"id":"rs23-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"The purpose of long-term home oxygen is to {{c1::prolong life}}.","e":"Not to relieve breathlessness.","nt":"rs-oxygen#who","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"Home oxygen is indicated in stable COPD when resting PaO2 on air is {{c1::55 mmHg}} or less.","e":"Or 56 to 59 with complications of hypoxaemia.","nt":"rs-oxygen#who","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"Before assessment for home oxygen, the patient must have stopped smoking for at least {{c1::1 month}}.","e":"And be clinically stable on best treatment.","nt":"rs-oxygen#who","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"The test needed to establish eligibility for home oxygen is {{c1::arterial blood gases::test}} on room air.","e":"Consider when SpO2 is under 90%.","nt":"rs-oxygen#who","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"PaO2 of 57 mmHg with polycythaemia in stable COPD. Is home oxygen indicated? {{c1::Yes}}","e":"Also with pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure.","nt":"rs-oxygen#who","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"A breathless patient with COPD has a PaO2 of 65 mmHg. Is home oxygen indicated? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-oxygen#not","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"Home oxygen is not prescribed to people who {{c1::smoke}}.","e":"Risk of fire.","nt":"rs-oxygen#not","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"Long-term oxygen should be used for at least {{c1::15 hours}} a day.","e":"Preferably 18 or more, including sleep.","nt":"rs-oxygen#use","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"The usual starting flow for home oxygen is {{c1::2 litres}} a minute.","e":"By nasal cannulae.","nt":"rs-oxygen#use","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"The usual source of long-term home oxygen is an oxygen {{c1::concentrator}}.","e":"Cylinders for backup and outings.","nt":"rs-oxygen#use","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"A dry nose on home oxygen is treated with saline spray or a {{c1::water-based}} lubricant.","e":"","nt":"rs-oxygen#use","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"Before prescribing night-time oxygen, arrange {{c1::a sleep study::test}}.","e":"To exclude sleep-disordered breathing.","nt":"rs-oxygen#use","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"After starting home oxygen, specialist review is due within {{c1::4 months}}.","e":"With blood gases on room air, when stable. Then yearly.","nt":"rs-oxygen#review","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"Oxygen started at hospital discharge should be regarded as {{c1::short term}}.","e":"Over a third no longer need it at 4 to 8 weeks.","nt":"rs-oxygen#review","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"},{"id":"rs23-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"Stable lung disease, no past in-flight problems. No testing before flying if resting SpO2 is at least {{c1::95%}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-oxygen#fly","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"},{"id":"rs23-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"At cruising altitude, gas trapped in body cavities expands by about {{c1::one-third}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-oxygen#fly","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"},{"id":"rs23-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"Are large lung bullae a contraindication to commercial air travel? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-oxygen#fly","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"},{"id":"rs23-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"Can a person with a current pneumothorax fly? {{c1::No}}","e":"","nt":"rs-oxygen#nofly","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"},{"id":"rs23-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"After a spontaneous pneumothorax, delay flying until {{c1::1 week}} after full resolution on X-ray.","e":"","nt":"rs-oxygen#nofly","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"},{"id":"rs23-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"After a traumatic or post-surgical pneumothorax, delay flying until {{c1::2 weeks}} after full resolution on X-ray.","e":"","nt":"rs-oxygen#nofly","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"},{"id":"rs23-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"A patient who cannot walk {{c1::50 metres}} on level ground is unfit to fly.","e":"","nt":"rs-oxygen#nofly","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"},{"id":"rs23-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"Resting SpO2 under 95% before a flight: consider a {{c1::hypoxic challenge test::test}}.","e":"In a respiratory laboratory.","nt":"rs-oxygen#nofly","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"},{"id":"rs23-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"In-flight oxygen is usually indicated if SpO2 falls below {{c1::85%}} during a hypoxic challenge test.","e":"","nt":"rs-oxygen#nofly","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"},{"id":"rs23-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","o":1,"x":"A person needing oxygen at {{c1::4 litres}} a minute or more at sea level is unfit to fly.","e":"","nt":"rs-oxygen#nofly","src":"Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"},{"id":"rs24-01","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Unexplained respiratory symptoms should be investigated for lung cancer if they persist beyond {{c1::3 weeks}}.","e":"Earlier with risk factors or more than one symptom.","nt":"rs-lungca#symptoms","src":"Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer (2020)"},{"id":"rs24-02","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"The first investigation for suspected lung cancer in general practice is an urgent {{c1::chest X-ray::test}}.","e":"Result within 1 week.","nt":"rs-lungca#investigate","src":"Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer (2020)"},{"id":"rs24-03","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Does a normal chest X-ray exclude lung cancer? {{c1::No}}","e":"It misses up to 25%.","nt":"rs-lungca#investigate","src":"Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer (2020)"},{"id":"rs24-04","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"A chest X-ray misses up to {{c1::25%}} of lung cancers.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#investigate","src":"Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer (2020)"},{"id":"rs24-05","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Normal chest X-ray but strong suspicion of lung cancer: arrange {{c1::CT chest::test}}.","e":"With contrast unless contraindicated.","nt":"rs-lungca#investigate","src":"Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer (2020)"},{"id":"rs24-06","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Suspected lung cancer: the first specialist appointment should be within {{c1::2 weeks}} of referral.","e":"A specialist linked to a lung cancer multidisciplinary team.","nt":"rs-lungca#investigate","src":"Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer (2020)"},{"id":"rs24-07","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Massive haemoptysis or stridor: {{c1::emergency department::next step}} immediately.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#investigate","src":"Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer (2020)"},{"id":"rs24-08","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"After treating consolidation in a patient at risk of lung cancer, repeat the chest X-ray within {{c1::6 weeks}}.","e":"Refer if it has not cleared.","nt":"rs-lungca#investigate","src":"Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer (2020)"},{"id":"rs24-09","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"A blood count finding that can be a sign of lung cancer is {{c1::thrombocytosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#symptoms","src":"Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer (2020)"},{"id":"rs24-10","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Hoarseness with a changed cough in a smoker should prompt investigation for {{c1::lung cancer::diagnosis}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#symptoms","src":"Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer (2020)"},{"id":"rs24-11","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Three dust or fume exposures at work that raise lung cancer risk are asbestos, diesel exhaust and {{c1::silica}}.","e":"Also radon.","nt":"rs-lungca#risk","src":"Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer (2020)"},{"id":"rs24-12","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Lung cancer screening is offered between the ages of {{c1::50 and 70}} years.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#eligible","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-13","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Lung cancer screening requires a smoking history of at least {{c1::30}} pack-years.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#eligible","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-14","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"A former smoker is eligible for lung cancer screening if they quit within the last {{c1::10 years}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#eligible","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-15","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"One pack-year is {{c1::20}} cigarettes a day for one year.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#eligible","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-16","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"A 60-year-old smoker with haemoptysis asks for lung screening. Is the screening program appropriate? {{c1::No}}","e":"Symptoms are investigated, not screened.","nt":"rs-lungca#eligible","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-17","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Must a person quit smoking to take part in lung cancer screening? {{c1::No}}","e":"Offer cessation support at every contact.","nt":"rs-lungca#eligible","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-18","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Lung screening is deferred after a symptomatic chest infection in the previous {{c1::12 weeks}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#eligible","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-19","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Lung screening is deferred if a full chest CT was done in the last {{c1::12 months}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#eligible","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-20","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"The lung cancer screening test is {{c1::low-dose CT::test}}.","e":"Without contrast.","nt":"rs-lungca#process","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-21","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Lung cancer screening is repeated every {{c1::2 years}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#process","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-22","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Participants in lung cancer screening are enrolled in the National Cancer Screening {{c1::Register}}.","e":"","nt":"rs-lungca#process","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-23","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"A screening CT reported as moderate risk is followed by a scan in {{c1::3 months}}.","e":"Low to moderate: 6 months. Low: 12 months.","nt":"rs-lungca#process","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-24","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"A screening CT reported as high risk: {{c1::refer::next step}} to a respiratory physician.","e":"Linked to a lung cancer multidisciplinary team.","nt":"rs-lungca#process","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"},{"id":"rs24-25","t":"Respiratory health","l":"Lung cancer","o":1,"x":"Of people with a high-risk nodule on screening, about {{c1::half}} have lung cancer.","e":"About 3% of those screened have such a nodule.","nt":"rs-lungca#harms","src":"National Lung Cancer Screening Program, GP resource guide (2025)"}],"questions":[{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","s":"Callum Reid, aged 24 years, is an apprentice electrician. Over the past four months he has had episodes of wheeze and chest tightness, mostly in the early morning and after indoor soccer. Each episode settles within an hour. He had eczema as a child and his sister has asthma. He has never smoked and does not vape. Today he feels well. Spirometry was performed this morning before and after salbutamol.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Cetirizine 10 mg daily in spring","No inhalers"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 14/min, SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear, no wheeze"]}],"tb":{"h":["Spirometry","Before salbutamol","After salbutamol","Predicted"],"r":[["FEV1","4.10 L","4.22 L","4.20 L"],["FVC","5.05 L","5.10 L","5.00 L"],["FEV1/FVC","0.81","0.83",">0.75"]]},"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a chest X-ray to look for another cause","Reassure him that normal spirometry has excluded asthma","Repeat spirometry with bronchodilator on a day when he has symptoms","Request skin prick testing to confirm allergic asthma","Start a daily preventer and review him in twelve months without further testing"],"a":2,"x":["Imaging is not part of diagnosing asthma. It is used for unusual features or when another diagnosis is suspected, and nothing here suggests one.","Normal spirometry on a well day does not exclude asthma. Airflow limitation in asthma varies, and he was tested between episodes.","Correct. His history is typical, so the test is repeated when airflow limitation is most likely to be present: during symptoms or in the early morning.","Allergy tests neither confirm nor exclude asthma. They guide trigger management once the diagnosis is made.","Treating without objective evidence makes the diagnosis harder to confirm later. This would be reasonable only as a structured trial with review at about four weeks."],"e":"Problem: a young man with a typical history, a clear chest and normal spirometry while well. Task: decide how to get objective evidence. The normal result is the distractor. What if spirometry were normal while he was wheezy and breathless? Then another diagnosis should be considered.","k":"Normal spirometry on a well day does not exclude asthma: repeat it when symptomatic.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Asthma diagnosis"],"c":["rs1-04","rs1-05","rs1-06","rs1-07","rs1-08"],"id":"rs-a01","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","s":"Priya Nair, aged 37 years, is a primary school teacher with three months of cough and occasional wheeze at night. She has allergic rhinitis and has never smoked. Her general practitioner arranged spirometry at the practice, performed by the practice nurse with three acceptable blows before and 15 minutes after four puffs of salbutamol through a spacer.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Mometasone nasal spray daily","Combined oral contraceptive pill","Ibuprofen 400 mg occasionally for headaches"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"tb":{"h":["Spirometry","Before salbutamol","After salbutamol"],"r":[["FEV1","2.60 L","2.84 L"],["FVC","3.60 L","3.66 L"],["FEV1/FVC","0.72","0.78"]]},"q":"What is the MOST appropriate interpretation of the bronchodilator response?","o":["Negative, because the rise in FEV1 is less than 12%","Negative, because the rise in FEV1 is less than 400 mL","Positive, because FEV1 rose by at least 200 mL and at least 12%","Positive, because FEV1 rose by more than 200 mL","Uninterpretable, because the FEV1/FVC ratio changed"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. FEV1 rose by 240 mL, which is 9.2% of 2.60 L. Both parts are required, and the percentage falls short. Asthma is not excluded.","A rise over 400 mL strongly supports asthma, but it is not the threshold for a positive test.","The rise is 240 mL but only 9.2%, so the second condition is not met.","The volume criterion is met, but the percentage criterion is not. One without the other is a negative test.","The ratio is expected to rise when FEV1 improves more than FVC. It does not invalidate the test."],"e":"Problem: a pre and post bronchodilator result to read. Task: apply both criteria. 240 mL passes the volume test and tempts a positive call. 240 divided by 2,600 is 9.2%, which fails. What if her baseline FEV1 were 1.90 L? The same 240 mL would be 12.6% and the test would be positive.","k":"A positive bronchodilator response needs both: at least 12% and at least 200 mL.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs1-04","rs1-05","rs1-06","rs1-07","rs1-08"],"id":"rs-a02","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","s":"Dung Pham, aged 41 years, has worked in a commercial bakery for six years. For the past five months he has had wheeze, cough and a tight chest that start a few hours into each shift and ease on his days off. His symptoms cleared during two weeks of leave and returned in his first week back. He had no asthma as a child. He stopped smoking ten years ago after an eight pack-year history.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Loratadine 10 mg daily","Fluticasone nasal spray daily","Pantoprazole 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Scattered expiratory wheeze","Nasal mucosa pale and swollen"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Advise a P2 mask at work and review in three months","Arrange a chest X-ray and full blood count","Prescribe a salbutamol inhaler to use before each shift","Refer him now to a respiratory or occupational physician","Start a four-week trial of inhaled corticosteroid and review"],"a":3,"x":["A mask does not reliably prevent sensitiser exposure, and three months of continued exposure risks permanent loss of lung function.","Neither test confirms occupational asthma, and waiting for them delays the step that matters.","Pre-treating so that he can keep working in the exposure masks the problem. Reliever-only treatment is also no longer recommended for adults.","Correct. Symptoms that follow the pattern of work and improve away from it suggest occupational asthma, which needs prompt specialist confirmation while he is still exposed.","A treatment trial is a fallback when testing is not possible. It would not identify the cause, and exposure would continue."],"e":"Problem: adult-onset wheeze in a baker that tracks his roster. Task: recognise work-related asthma and act on it. His rhinitis and the response to leave are the deciding facts. What if his symptoms were unrelated to work? Spirometry with bronchodilator in the practice would be the next step.","k":"Work-related asthma symptoms: refer immediately.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs1-23","rs1-24","rs1-25","rs1-26","rs1-27"],"id":"rs-a03","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#mimics","s":"Sophie Laurent, aged 19 years, is a university student and competitive netballer. For six months she has had sudden episodes of noisy breathing and throat tightness during hard matches, which settle within minutes of stopping. Her voice goes hoarse during episodes. A salbutamol inhaler before games and eight weeks of a regular inhaled corticosteroid have made no difference. Spirometry performed at courtside during an episode was normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide 200 micrograms twice daily","Salbutamol two puffs before sport","Etonogestrel implant"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["At rest: chest clear, SpO2 99% on room air","Video from her coach: noise is loudest on breathing in"]}],"q":"What is the MOST likely diagnosis?","o":["Dysfunctional breathing with hyperventilation","Exercise-induced bronchoconstriction","Inducible laryngeal obstruction","Poorly controlled asthma needing a higher corticosteroid dose","Tracheobronchomalacia"],"a":2,"x":["This usually brings dizziness, tingling and sighing rather than a noise on breathing in with voice change.","This typically peaks after exercise stops, responds to salbutamol, and shows airflow limitation on spirometry during symptoms.","Correct. Throat tightness, voice change, noise on inspiration, normal spirometry during symptoms and no response to asthma treatment point to the larynx.","No response to eight weeks of inhaled corticosteroid, with normal spirometry while symptomatic, argues against asthma. More steroid is not the answer.","A rare structural cause of wheeze. It would not explain the voice change or the rapid recovery."],"e":"Problem: exertional breathing difficulty labelled as asthma that has not responded to treatment. Task: name the mimic. The deciding facts are the inspiratory noise, hoarse voice and normal spirometry during an episode. What if spirometry during an episode showed a reduced FEV1/FVC that reversed with salbutamol? Exercise-induced bronchoconstriction.","k":"Throat tightness, voice change and normal spirometry during symptoms: inducible laryngeal obstruction.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Asthma diagnosis","National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs1-23","rs1-24","rs1-25","rs1-26","rs1-27"],"id":"rs-a04","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#spirometry","s":"Marlene Kowalski, aged 52 years, runs a sheep property four hours from the nearest town with a respiratory laboratory. For two months she has woken three or four nights a week with wheeze and cough, and she is breathless carrying feed. She had asthma as a child. She has never smoked. The visiting spirometry service is next in the district in five weeks. Her practice has no spirometer.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Perindopril 5 mg daily","Atorvastatin 20 mg daily","Salbutamol inhaler borrowed from her daughter, used most days"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 16/min, SpO2 97% on room air","Scattered expiratory wheeze","No ankle oedema, heart sounds normal"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a chest CT before deciding on treatment","Continue salbutamol alone until spirometry is done in five weeks","Give a peak flow meter and withhold all treatment until four weeks of twice-daily readings are complete","Start an inhaled corticosteroid-containing treatment now and book the first available spirometry","Stop perindopril and review her in four weeks"],"a":3,"x":["Imaging does not diagnose asthma and would add delay.","Reliever-only treatment is not recommended for adults, and frequent night waking should not go untreated for five weeks.","A peak flow diary is a reasonable substitute when spirometry is delayed, but treatment that is clinically needed should not be withheld to complete it.","Correct. Significant symptoms are treated now. Testing follows as soon as it can be arranged, accepting that treatment may blunt the result.","ACE inhibitor cough is dry and without wheeze. Her wheeze and night waking are not explained by perindopril."],"e":"Problem: frequent night symptoms and no access to spirometry for five weeks. Task: balance confirming the diagnosis against the need to treat. The ideal order is test, then treat, but not at the cost of leaving significant symptoms untreated. What if her symptoms were mild and occasional? Waiting for spirometry before a preventer would be reasonable.","k":"Test before treating where possible, but do not delay treatment that is needed now.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Asthma diagnosis"],"c":["rs1-04","rs1-05","rs1-06","rs1-07","rs1-08"],"id":"rs-a05","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#next","s":"Amira Haddad, aged 30 years, is 16 weeks pregnant with her second child. Since early pregnancy she has had bouts of chest tightness and cough at night, twice a week. She had a wheezy chest with colds as a teenager. Spirometry with bronchodilator at the practice was normal three weeks ago and again last week, when she had mild symptoms. She asks what other tests can be done. A respiratory laboratory is 20 minutes away.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Pregnancy multivitamin with folic acid and iodine","Doxylamine 12.5 mg at night for nausea"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear","Fundal height consistent with dates"]}],"q":"Which investigation is MOST appropriate to arrange next?","o":["Chest X-ray with abdominal shielding","Exercise challenge test","Laboratory spirometry with exhaled nitric oxide","Mannitol bronchial provocation test","Serum total IgE and skin prick tests to common aeroallergens"],"a":2,"x":["Imaging does not diagnose asthma and is not indicated by her presentation.","An exercise challenge is a form of bronchial provocation, which is not arranged in pregnancy.","Correct. When practice spirometry is non-diagnostic and suspicion remains, repeat testing at an accredited laboratory with FeNO is the next step, and it is safe in pregnancy.","Bronchial provocation is contraindicated in pregnancy.","Allergy tests show sensitisation. They do not confirm or exclude asthma."],"e":"Problem: suspected asthma, two normal practice spirometry tests, and a pregnancy. Task: choose the next objective test. Provocation testing is the usual step after non-diagnostic spirometry and FeNO, which is why two forms of it are offered. Pregnancy rules them out. What if she were not pregnant and laboratory spirometry and FeNO were also normal? Bronchial provocation could then be requested.","k":"Bronchial provocation is contraindicated in pregnancy and when FEV1 is under 60% predicted.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Diagnosing asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs1-11","rs1-12","rs1-13","rs1-14","rs1-15"],"id":"rs-a06","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Asthma: diagnosis","nt":"rs-asthma-dx#history","n":3,"s":"Jordan Blake, aged 28 years, is a barista who presents with four months of intermittent cough and breathlessness. You are taking a history to judge how likely asthma is before arranging tests. Jordan uses no regular medicines and takes ibuprofen for occasional back pain.","q":"Which THREE of the following findings on history would MOST increase the probability of asthma? Select three.","o":["Breathlessness with tingling in the fingers and light-headedness","Cough that is productive every day, with no wheeze","Hay fever every spring since childhood","Symptoms that are worse in the early morning","Symptoms that worsen with talking and laughing","Throat tightness with a change in voice","Wheeze within an hour or two of taking ibuprofen"],"sc":[0,0,1,1,0,0,1],"x":["Scores 0. These suggest hyperventilation or dysfunctional breathing and lower the probability of asthma.","Scores 0. A chronic productive cough without wheeze or breathlessness points towards bronchiectasis or chronic bronchitis.","Scores 1. A personal history of atopy raises the probability.","Scores 1. Night and early-morning symptoms are typical of asthma.","Scores 0. This pattern suggests a laryngeal or functional cause.","Scores 0. These point to inducible laryngeal obstruction.","Scores 1. Symptoms after aspirin or NSAIDs raise the probability, and suggest aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease."],"e":"Three features raise the odds: atopy, a night and early-morning pattern, and symptoms triggered by NSAIDs. The other four each point to a mimic. History shapes the probability but never completes the diagnosis: spirometry follows.","k":"Atopy, early-morning symptoms and NSAID-triggered wheeze raise the probability of asthma.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Asthma diagnosis, Table 9.1"],"c":["rs1-22","rs1-01","rs1-02","rs1-03","rs1-04"],"id":"rs-k01","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#patterns","s":"Graham Whitlock, aged 66 years, is a retired fitter who has become breathless walking up the hill to his letterbox over the past two years. He coughs up a little clear sputum most mornings. He smoked 25 cigarettes a day from age 18 until he stopped at 60. He has no chest pain or ankle swelling. Spirometry was performed after salbutamol.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amlodipine 5 mg daily","Rosuvastatin 10 mg daily","Paracetamol 1 g as needed for knee pain"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 95% on room air","Quiet breath sounds, prolonged expiration","BMI 23 kg/m2"]}],"tb":{"h":["Post-bronchodilator spirometry","Result","Percent predicted"],"r":[["FEV1","1.62 L","56%"],["FVC","3.40 L","92%"],["FEV1/FVC","0.48",""]]},"q":"What pattern does the spirometry show?","o":["Mixed obstructive and restrictive defect","Normal for his age","Obstructive ventilatory defect","Poor effort, uninterpretable","Restrictive ventilatory defect"],"a":2,"x":["A mixed pattern or gas trapping is suggested when the ratio and the FVC are both low. His FVC is preserved.","A ratio of 0.48 with an FEV1 of 56% predicted is well outside normal at any age.","Correct. The FEV1/FVC ratio is low and the FVC is normal. That is airflow obstruction.","Nothing in the result suggests poor effort, and the FVC is close to predicted.","Restriction is a low FVC with a normal or raised ratio. His is the reverse."],"e":"Problem: a spirometry report in a breathless ex-smoker. Task: name the pattern. Read the ratio first: 0.48 is low, so there is obstruction. Then the FVC: 92% is normal, so there is no suggestion of restriction. What if the ratio were 0.85 and the FVC 58% predicted? A restrictive pattern.","k":"Low FEV1/FVC ratio is obstruction. Low FVC with a normal or high ratio suggests restriction.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing, Table 9.27"],"c":["rs2-03","rs2-04","rs2-05","rs2-06","rs2-07"],"id":"rs-a07","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#lab","s":"Helen Papadakis, aged 71 years, has had atrial fibrillation for four years and has taken amiodarone for the past 18 months. She reports a dry cough and breathlessness on the stairs for three months. She has never smoked. Her echocardiogram last month showed normal left ventricular function. Practice spirometry shows an FEV1 of 62% predicted, an FVC of 60% predicted and an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.84.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amiodarone 200 mg daily","Apixaban 5 mg twice daily","Perindopril 2.5 mg daily","Levothyroxine 75 micrograms daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 94% on room air","Fine crackles at both bases","No ankle oedema, JVP not raised"]}],"q":"Which additional lung function test BEST assesses the gas-exchanging capacity of her lungs?","o":["Bronchial provocation test","Diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide","Hypoxic challenge test","Peak expiratory flow diary","Repeat spirometry after a bronchodilator"],"a":1,"x":["This looks for airway hyperresponsiveness in suspected asthma. Her spirometry is not obstructive.","Correct. DLCO measures transfer of gas from the air sacs to the blood. It is the laboratory test for suspected lung toxicity from drugs such as amiodarone.","This simulates altitude to assess fitness to fly.","Peak flow tracks variable obstruction in asthma. It adds nothing to a restrictive pattern.","Reversibility testing is for obstruction. Her ratio is normal and her FVC is low."],"e":"Problem: breathlessness, basal crackles and a restrictive spirometry pattern in a woman on amiodarone. Task: pick the test of gas exchange. The normal echocardiogram and absence of oedema steer away from heart failure. What if the question were how to assess the restriction itself? Static lung volumes.","k":"DLCO measures gas exchange. Static lung volumes assess restriction and hyperinflation.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-26","rs2-27","rs2-28","rs2-01","rs2-02"],"id":"rs-a08","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","s":"Robert Ainsley, aged 74 years, attends for his annual COPD review. The practice nurse has him booked for spirometry today. He mentions that he had cataract surgery on his right eye five days ago and is using eye drops. His breathing is unchanged from last year and he has had no exacerbations. He is keen to have the test today because his daughter has driven him 40 minutes to the appointment.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium 18 micrograms inhaled daily","Salbutamol as needed","Prednisolone-phenylephrine eye drops four times daily","Metformin 1 g twice daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 95% on room air","Chest: reduced breath sounds, no wheeze"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Defer spirometry until at least four weeks after the surgery","Defer spirometry until at least one week after the surgery","Perform spirometry today with a nose clip and gentle effort","Perform spirometry today, as cataract surgery is not relevant","Replace spirometry with a peak flow reading"],"a":1,"x":["Four weeks applies to chest and abdominal surgery. For eye, sinus and middle ear surgery the period is one week.","Correct. Eye surgery in the previous week is a relative contraindication. His review is routine, so a short delay costs nothing.","Spirometry requires maximal forced effort. A gentle blow produces an unusable result.","Forced expiration raises pressure in the eye. Recent eye surgery is a recognised reason to defer.","Peak flow is not useful in COPD, where the obstruction does not vary."],"e":"Problem: routine spirometry booked five days after eye surgery. Task: apply the deferral periods. The long drive adds pressure to proceed, but nothing about a stable annual review is urgent. What if he had had a laparoscopic hernia repair two weeks ago? Defer until four weeks have passed.","k":"Defer spirometry 1 week after eye, sinus or ear surgery and 4 weeks after chest or abdominal surgery.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-08","rs2-09","rs2-10","rs2-11","rs2-12"],"id":"rs-a09","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#oximetry","s":"Tevita Fifita, aged 45 years, is brought to a rural practice by a neighbour after being found drowsy in his shed, where he had been running a petrol generator with the door closed during a power cut. He has a throbbing headache, nausea and feels unsteady. He takes no regular medicines and does not smoke. A fingertip pulse oximeter is applied.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert but slow to answer","Heart rate 108/min, blood pressure 128/78 mmHg, respiratory rate 20/min","SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear, no burns, lips pink"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate interpretation of the oxygen saturation reading?","o":["It confirms adequate oxygen delivery to his tissues, so a viral illness is the more likely cause","It is falsely high because the oximeter reads carboxyhaemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin","It is falsely low because of poor peripheral perfusion","It is unreliable because methaemoglobin absorbs light unpredictably","It rules out carbon dioxide retention"],"a":1,"x":["The exposure and symptoms fit carbon monoxide poisoning. A normal reading does not show that oxygen is reaching the tissues.","Correct. Carboxyhaemoglobin is counted as oxygenated haemoglobin by a standard pulse oximeter, so the reading is falsely reassuring.","Poor perfusion makes readings unreliable, but he is warm and normotensive, and the error with carbon monoxide runs in the other direction.","Methaemoglobinaemia follows drugs such as dapsone or benzocaine, not exhaust fumes.","Pulse oximetry never gives information about carbon dioxide, with or without carbon monoxide exposure."],"e":"Problem: headache and drowsiness after generator exhaust in a closed shed, with a saturation of 99%. Task: know what the oximeter cannot see. The normal reading and pink lips are the trap. What if he had been using benzocaine throat spray heavily and looked slate-grey? Methaemoglobinaemia, where the reading can be wrong in either direction.","k":"Pulse oximetry reads falsely high in carbon monoxide poisoning.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-16","rs2-17","rs2-18","rs2-19","rs2-20"],"id":"rs-a10","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#pef","s":"Lorraine Simmons, aged 69 years, has COPD confirmed on spirometry three years ago, with a post-bronchodilator FEV1 of 54% predicted. She smoked for 40 years and stopped at diagnosis. Her neighbour has asthma and measures her peak flow every morning. Lorraine asks for a peak flow meter so that she can monitor her own lungs at home and know when a flare-up is coming.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Umeclidinium-vilanterol inhaler daily","Salbutamol as needed","Alendronate 70 mg weekly","Colecalciferol 1000 units daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 94% on room air","Reduced breath sounds throughout"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate response to her request?","o":["Agree, and ask her to record the best of three readings each morning and each evening in a diary","Agree, but only for use during chest infections","Explain that peak flow is not useful in COPD because the obstruction does not vary","Explain that peak flow is unreliable in people over 65","Suggest a home spirometer instead"],"a":2,"x":["Twice-daily readings help in asthma, where airflow varies from day to day. In COPD they add nothing.","Peak flow does not reliably detect a COPD exacerbation. Symptoms do: more breathlessness, more sputum, or a change in its colour.","Correct. Airflow obstruction in COPD is largely fixed, so serial peak flow readings do not track the disease.","Peak flow is unreliable in children. Age over 65 is not the reason here.","Home spirometry is not part of routine COPD self-management."],"e":"Problem: a request to copy an asthma monitoring habit in COPD. Task: know where peak flow helps. It tracks variable obstruction. COPD obstruction is not variable. What if she had asthma? A meter, used with the same device each time, would be reasonable for monitoring.","k":"Peak flow monitoring is for asthma. It has no role in COPD.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-13","rs2-14","rs2-15","rs2-01","rs2-02"],"id":"rs-a11","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#gases","s":"Neville Carter, aged 72 years, has severe COPD and is brought to a small rural hospital where you are the on-call doctor. He has had two days of worsening breathlessness and green sputum. Ambulance officers gave oxygen by mask during the 50-minute trip. He is now drowsy but rouses to voice. A venous blood gas is taken with his routine bloods.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium-olodaterol inhaler daily","Fluticasone-salmeterol inhaler twice daily","Salbutamol as needed","Furosemide 40 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 24/min, heart rate 104/min, temperature 37.9 degrees","SpO2 97% on oxygen 6 L/min by mask","Widespread wheeze, prolonged expiration"]}],"tb":{"h":["Venous blood gas","Result"],"r":[["pH","7.29"],["PCO2","64 mmHg"],["Bicarbonate","30 mmol/L"]]},"q":"Which investigation is MOST appropriate to perform next?","o":["Arterial blood gas","Chest CT","D-dimer","Repeat venous blood gas in four hours","Spirometry"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. A venous gas alone cannot accurately assess respiratory failure. An abnormal venous result is followed by an arterial sample.","CT does not assess ventilation and would delay management of a drowsy patient.","Pulmonary embolism is not the leading concern, and a D-dimer would not explain his drowsiness.","Venous values do not track arterial values reliably. Repeating the same test does not answer the question.","He is too unwell to perform a forced manoeuvre, and spirometry does not measure gas exchange."],"e":"Problem: a drowsy man with COPD on oxygen, a reassuring saturation and an abnormal venous gas. Task: choose the test that defines his ventilation. The saturation of 97% says nothing about carbon dioxide. What if the venous gas had been normal? Arterial sampling would be less pressing, though a normal venous result does not fully exclude carbon dioxide retention.","k":"An abnormal venous gas in suspected respiratory failure is followed by an arterial gas.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-21","rs2-22","rs2-23","rs2-24","rs2-25"],"id":"rs-a12","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Lung function tests","nt":"rs-lungfn#contra","n":3,"s":"You supervise the spirometry clinic at your practice. The nurse shows you this afternoon's list of seven patients booked for routine spirometry and asks whether any should be rebooked.","q":"For which THREE patients should spirometry be deferred today? Select three.","o":["A 34-year-old who had a laparoscopic appendicectomy 16 days ago","A 45-year-old with well-controlled hypertension on perindopril","A 52-year-old who had a cold that cleared three weeks ago","A 58-year-old who had endoscopic sinus surgery four days ago","A 63-year-old who had a heart attack and a stent five days ago","A 70-year-old who had cataract surgery three months ago","A 77-year-old with stable angina and no recent change in symptoms"],"sc":[1,0,0,1,1,0,0],"x":["Scores 1. Abdominal surgery in the previous four weeks is a relative contraindication.","Scores 0. Controlled hypertension is not a reason to defer.","Scores 0. The infection has resolved. A current infection would call for infection-control measures.","Scores 1. Sinus surgery in the previous week is a relative contraindication.","Scores 1. A heart attack in the previous week is a relative contraindication.","Scores 0. The one-week period after eye surgery has long passed.","Scores 0. Stable angina is not on the list of reasons to defer."],"e":"Two timeframes cover most of the list: one week for a heart attack and for eye, sinus or middle ear surgery, and four weeks for chest or abdominal surgery. These are relative contraindications. If a result is needed urgently, a respiratory laboratory can test with support on hand.","k":"Defer spirometry: 1 week after MI or eye, sinus or ear surgery; 4 weeks after chest or abdominal surgery.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Pulmonary function testing"],"c":["rs2-08","rs2-09","rs2-10","rs2-11","rs2-12"],"id":"rs-k02","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#start","s":"Ethan Marsh, aged 26 years, is a graphic designer whose asthma was confirmed last week on spirometry with a positive bronchodilator response. He gets wheeze and chest tightness about twice a month, usually when visiting a friend who has cats, and has never woken at night with symptoms. He has had no flare-ups needing oral corticosteroids and has never been to hospital with his breathing. He does not smoke or vape.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Loratadine 10 mg as needed","No inhalers"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear","Demonstrates good technique with a dry powder inhaler"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate initial treatment?","o":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, one inhalation as needed","Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily, plus one as needed","Fluticasone propionate-salmeterol 250/25 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily","Montelukast 10 mg daily","Salbutamol 100 micrograms, two inhalations as needed"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Most adults start on low-dose budesonide-formoterol taken only when symptoms occur. It treats inflammation every time the reliever is used.","This is medium-dose maintenance and reliever therapy. It is for frequent or severe symptoms or a serious past flare-up, none of which he has.","A fixed-dose combination at this strength is more treatment than infrequent symptoms need, and it cannot be used as a reliever.","Montelukast is less effective than inhaled corticosteroid-based treatment and is an alternative for people who cannot or will not use one.","A reliever alone is no longer recommended for anyone aged 12 or over, however infrequent the symptoms."],"e":"Problem: newly confirmed asthma with symptoms twice a month and no risk factors. Task: choose the first treatment. The mildness of his asthma is what makes salbutamol alone tempting. The 2025 Handbook removes that option. What if he had needed prednisolone for a flare-up eight months ago? Start with maintenance and reliever therapy.","k":"First treatment for most people aged 12 and over: low-dose budesonide-formoterol as needed.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Initial asthma treatment after diagnosis"],"c":["rs3-12","rs3-13","rs3-14","rs3-15","rs3-16"],"id":"rs-a13","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#principles","s":"Deborah Lang, aged 48 years, is a hairdresser who has had asthma since her teens. She asks for another salbutamol prescription and says her asthma is 'fine'. She uses salbutamol three or four times a week and has no other inhaler. Pharmacy records show five salbutamol inhalers dispensed in the past 12 months. She had a course of prednisolone from an after-hours clinic last winter. She has never smoked.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol 100 micrograms, two puffs as needed","Sertraline 50 mg daily","Levonorgestrel intrauterine device"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear today","Uses her inhaler without a spacer; technique otherwise adequate"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add montelukast 10 mg daily and continue salbutamol as needed","Change her treatment to a regimen that contains an inhaled corticosteroid","Prescribe salbutamol with a spacer and review in 12 months","Refer her to a respiratory physician for assessment of difficult-to-treat asthma","Supply a home nebuliser for use when her symptoms are worse"],"a":1,"x":["Montelukast is less effective than an inhaled corticosteroid and is not the first choice when she has never tried one.","Correct. Reliever-only treatment is not recommended. Five canisters a year, use on more than two days a week and a recent oral corticosteroid course all mark her as at risk.","A spacer improves delivery, but leaves her on a reliever alone with poor control and a recent flare-up.","Referral is for asthma that stays uncontrolled on an optimised medium-dose regimen. She has not yet had any preventer.","Nebulisers are not used for routine treatment. A pressurised inhaler with a spacer is as effective."],"e":"Problem: an adult who feels fine on salbutamol alone but uses it most days. Task: recognise uncontrolled, under-treated asthma. Her own assessment is the distractor. The dispensing record and the prednisolone course are the deciding facts. What if she were already on medium-dose maintenance and reliever therapy with good technique? Then eosinophils and referral.","k":"Salbutamol alone is not acceptable asthma treatment in anyone aged 12 or over.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Initial asthma treatment after diagnosis; Treatment levels"],"c":["rs3-01","rs3-02","rs3-03","rs3-04","rs3-05"],"id":"rs-a14","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepup","s":"Mohammed Aziz, aged 39 years, drives a delivery van and has had asthma for 15 years. Over the past two months he has woken with wheeze once or twice a week and uses salbutamol on four days a week. He takes his preventer every morning and evening, which his dispensing history confirms. You watch him use his inhaler with a spacer and his technique is correct. He has no nasal symptoms and has never smoked. His weight is stable.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone propionate-salmeterol 50/25 micrograms, two puffs twice daily","Salbutamol 100 micrograms, two puffs as needed","Esomeprazole 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear","BMI 26 kg/m2"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add tiotropium by soft mist inhaler","Continue the same treatment and add montelukast","Increase to fluticasone propionate-salmeterol 250/25 micrograms, two puffs twice daily","Refer to a respiratory physician","Switch to low-dose maintenance and reliever therapy with budesonide-formoterol"],"a":4,"x":["A long-acting muscarinic antagonist is an add-on at higher treatment levels, guided by spirometry.","Montelukast is not the recommended next step from a low-dose combination.","Raising the corticosteroid dose is not the first move. Changing the reliever to corticosteroid-formoterol comes before a dose increase.","Referral follows failure of an optimised medium-dose regimen. He is on a low-dose regimen.","Correct. For anyone on a fixed-dose combination with a salbutamol reliever, a switch to maintenance and reliever therapy is considered before increasing the dose."],"e":"Problem: poor control on a low-dose fixed combination with salbutamol, with adherence and technique confirmed. Task: choose the step up. The stem removes the usual reasons to hold off. The Handbook's preferred move is to change the kind of regimen, not the dose. What if he had not been taking the preventer? Address adherence first and leave the regimen alone.","k":"Uncontrolled on fixed corticosteroid-LABA plus salbutamol: switch to MART before raising the dose.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment"],"c":["rs3-24","rs3-25","rs3-26","rs3-01","rs3-02"],"id":"rs-a15","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#refer","s":"Natalie Brooks, aged 44 years, is an accountant with asthma since childhood. Despite four months on her current treatment she wakes with symptoms twice a week and has needed two courses of prednisolone this year. She takes every dose, and the practice nurse confirmed correct technique last week. Her allergic rhinitis is well controlled, she has never smoked, and there are no pets or mould at home. Spirometry shows an FEV1 of 71% predicted.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily and one as needed","Mometasone nasal spray daily","Cetirizine 10 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 97% on room air","Scattered expiratory wheeze","BMI 24 kg/m2"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add a second inhaled corticosteroid inhaler to reach a high daily dose","Arrange a blood eosinophil count and refer to a respiratory physician","Change to a different brand of budesonide-formoterol inhaler","Prescribe prednisolone 10 mg daily long term","Step down to as-needed budesonide-formoterol to reduce her exposure"],"a":1,"x":["Most of the benefit of an inhaled corticosteroid comes at low dose, and long-term high doses add harm. This is not the recommended response.","Correct. Asthma uncontrolled on medium-dose maintenance and reliever therapy, with adherence, technique, triggers and comorbidities addressed, needs specialist assessment. Eosinophils are checked while waiting.","Her technique is correct and the drug would be the same. A brand change does not address the problem.","Maintenance oral corticosteroids carry serious long-term harm and are not started in primary care for this.","Stepping down requires good control for two to three months. Hers is poor."],"e":"Problem: persistent poor control and repeated oral corticosteroid courses on a medium-dose regimen, taken properly. Task: know when general practice has reached its limit. Every modifiable factor has been ruled out in the stem. What if her eosinophil count came back at 1,600 per microlitre? The referral becomes urgent.","k":"Uncontrolled on an optimised medium-dose regimen: blood eosinophils and specialist referral.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment"],"c":["rs3-27","rs3-28","rs3-29","rs3-01","rs3-02"],"id":"rs-a16","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#doses","s":"Lucy Tran, aged 31 years, is a nurse who started as-needed budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms by dry powder inhaler three months ago for mild asthma. She has a cold and has been using more of the inhaler. She phones the practice to ask how many inhalations she can safely take, because she used five yesterday and is worried she is overdoing it. She is speaking in full sentences and says the inhaler is working each time.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms dry powder, one inhalation as needed","Combined oral contraceptive pill","Paracetamol for the cold"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate advice about the usual maximum number of inhalations?","o":["Up to 12 inhalations on one occasion and 16 in a day","Up to 12 inhalations on one occasion and 24 in a day","Up to 2 inhalations on one occasion and 4 a day","Up to 4 inhalations on one occasion and 6 in a day","Up to 6 inhalations on one occasion and 8 in a day"],"a":4,"x":["These are the limits for the 100/3 pressurised inhaler, which delivers half the dose per puff.","Twenty-four is the short-term daily ceiling for the 100/3 pressurised inhaler, not for hers.","This is lower than the approved limits and would leave her undertreated during a cold.","This is lower than the approved limits for the dry powder inhaler.","Correct. For the 200/6 dry powder inhaler the limits are 6 on one occasion and 8 in a day. Up to 12 in a day is allowed briefly."],"e":"Problem: more reliever use during a cold, and a question about the ceiling. Task: recall the limits for her device. Two sets of numbers exist because the pressurised inhaler has half the strength per puff. What if she kept needing extra doses for three days without improving? She needs to be seen.","k":"Budesonide-formoterol 200/6: up to 6 at a time and 8 a day (12 briefly). The 100/3 inhaler doubles each number.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), table of budesonide-formoterol combinations approved as anti-inflammatory relievers"],"c":["rs3-17","rs3-18","rs3-19","rs3-20","rs3-21"],"id":"rs-a17","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepdown","s":"George Petrou, aged 57 years, is a high school teacher with asthma diagnosed at 30. For the past four months he has had no daytime symptoms, no night waking and has not needed extra reliever doses. His last flare-up was three years ago. He walks 5 km most days. He asks whether he can stop his inhaler altogether, because he feels cured and dislikes taking medicines. It is late summer and he has no hay fever.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, one inhalation twice daily and one as needed","Rosuvastatin 5 mg daily","Telmisartan 40 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear","Spirometry: FEV1 94% predicted"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Change to salbutamol as needed and stop budesonide-formoterol","Continue the current regimen indefinitely, as asthma is a lifelong inflammatory condition","Reduce to budesonide-formoterol as needed only, and review in 4 to 8 weeks","Stop all inhalers and review if symptoms return","Switch to montelukast 10 mg daily"],"a":2,"x":["This removes the inhaled corticosteroid, which is never withdrawn completely, and returns him to reliever-only treatment.","He meets the criteria to step down: good control for more than two to three months and low risk. Staying on more treatment than needed is not required.","Correct. Low-dose maintenance and reliever therapy steps down to the same inhaler used as needed. Review follows in four to eight weeks.","Stopping everything leaves him with no anti-inflammatory treatment and no reliever.","Montelukast is less effective and is not a step-down option from a well-tolerated inhaler."],"e":"Problem: four months of good control on low-dose maintenance and reliever therapy, and a wish to stop. Task: step down safely. He can reduce, but there is a floor. What if he were asking in the first week of a chest infection? Stepping down waits until he is well.","k":"Step down after 2 to 3 months of good control, one level at a time, and never below as-needed budesonide-formoterol.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment"],"c":["rs3-30","rs3-31","rs3-32","rs3-33","rs3-01"],"id":"rs-a18","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Asthma: treatment (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-adult#stepup","n":4,"s":"Karen Whitfield, aged 35 years, has asthma treated with low-dose maintenance and reliever therapy. Over six weeks her control has slipped: she now has symptoms on four days a week and woke twice last week. She asks for 'something stronger'.","q":"Before stepping up her treatment, which FOUR of the following should you assess? Select four.","o":["Adherence to her maintenance doses","Bone mineral density","Exposure to triggers at home and work","Her inhaler technique, by watching her use the device","Serum total IgE","Symptoms of rhinitis and other comorbidities","Thyroid function"],"sc":[1,0,1,1,0,1,0],"x":["Scores 1. Poor adherence to maintenance doses is common and is checked before any step up.","Scores 0. Bone density is relevant to long-term oral corticosteroid use, not to this decision.","Scores 1. Smoke, allergens, infections and work exposures can explain a loss of control.","Scores 1. Technique is often poor and can only be judged by watching.","Scores 0. Total IgE is a specialist test for selecting monoclonal antibody treatment.","Scores 1. Rhinitis, obesity, reflux and laryngeal obstruction can each worsen control or mimic it.","Scores 0. Thyroid function has no routine role here."],"e":"Four checks come before any step up: is she taking it, is she taking it correctly, is something setting her off, and is something else going on. Only when all four are satisfactory does the regimen move up a level.","k":"Before stepping up: adherence, technique, triggers, comorbidities.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment"],"c":["rs3-24","rs3-25","rs3-26","rs3-01","rs3-02"],"id":"rs-k03","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#control","s":"Aroha Ngata, aged 33 years, is a childcare worker attending for her yearly asthma review. In the past four weeks she has had daytime chest tightness on one day a week, relieved by a single dose of her inhaler. She used her reliever before netball twice a week as well. She woke once with wheeze after a late shift. She has missed no work and plays a full game without stopping. She does not smoke.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, one inhalation as needed","Etonogestrel implant","Ferrous sulfate 325 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear","Technique with her dry powder inhaler is correct"]}],"q":"How is her asthma symptom control BEST classified?","o":["Good, because daytime symptoms occur on fewer than three days a week","Good, because she has no limitation of activity","Poor, because she has had a night waking with asthma","Poor, because she uses her reliever before exercise","Poor, because she uses her reliever on three days a week in total"],"a":2,"x":["Daytime symptoms are within the limit, but good control requires every criterion to be met.","Activity is unrestricted, but one criterion being met does not make control good.","Correct. Any night or waking symptom in the previous four weeks places her outside good control.","Doses taken before exercise are not counted when judging reliever use.","Pre-exercise doses are excluded. Her symptom-driven use is one day a week, which is within the limit."],"e":"Problem: a four-week history to classify. Task: apply all four criteria. Three are met: daytime symptoms and reliever use on two days a week or fewer, and no activity limit. The single night waking is the one that fails. What if she had not woken at night? Good control.","k":"Good control needs all four: symptoms and reliever on 2 days a week or fewer, no night waking, no activity limit.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Assessing and reviewing asthma"],"c":["rs4-01","rs4-02","rs4-03","rs4-04","rs4-05"],"id":"rs-a19","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#ocs","s":"Stuart Mackie, aged 46 years, is a chef with asthma treated with maintenance and reliever therapy. He has had a head cold for four days. Since yesterday his wheeze has returned within two to three hours of each reliever dose, and he took nine inhalations in the past 24 hours. He is speaking in sentences. He follows a written action plan but has run out of the tablets it mentions.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily and one as needed","Pantoprazole 40 mg daily","Ibuprofen avoided since a reaction in his twenties"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 20/min, heart rate 96/min","SpO2 95% on room air","Widespread expiratory wheeze, no accessory muscle use"]}],"q":"Which prescription is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily for 5 days","Prednisolone 25 mg each morning for 3 days","Prednisolone 50 mg each morning for 5 days, then reduce by 10 mg every second day","Prednisolone 50 mg each morning for 5 days, with no taper","Prednisolone 50 mg each morning for 5 days, with two repeats to keep at home"],"a":3,"x":["A viral cold is the trigger. Antibiotics are not part of treating an asthma flare-up.","This is below the adult dose of 37.5 to 50 mg and shorter than the 5 to 10 days recommended for adults.","A taper is not needed after a short course and only prolongs exposure.","Correct. Symptoms returning within four hours of the reliever call for oral corticosteroids: 37.5 to 50 mg each morning for 5 to 10 days.","Repeats are not provided. Each course should be prescribed and counted, because frequent courses signal a need to step up or refer."],"e":"Problem: a flare-up that extra reliever doses are no longer holding. Task: prescribe the oral corticosteroid course correctly. Three of the options are variations on the right drug with one wrong detail each: dose, taper or repeats. What if he were 14 years old? 1 mg/kg, to a maximum of 50 mg, for 3 days.","k":"Adult flare-up: prednisolone 37.5 to 50 mg each morning for 5 to 10 days, no taper, no repeats.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs4-14","rs4-15","rs4-16","rs4-17","rs4-18"],"id":"rs-a20","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#pregnancy","s":"Hannah Schultz, aged 29 years, is 10 weeks pregnant with her first child. She has had asthma since childhood, well controlled for two years. She stopped her preventer when she found out she was pregnant because she was worried about steroids harming the baby. For the past two weeks she has had a tight chest most mornings and has woken twice at night.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide 400 micrograms twice daily (stopped four weeks ago)","Salbutamol two puffs, now used daily","Folic acid 0.5 mg and iodine 150 micrograms daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Scattered wheeze on forced expiration","Blood pressure 108/64 mmHg"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate advice?","o":["Avoid all inhaled corticosteroids until after the first trimester","Rely on salbutamol alone until delivery","Restart an inhaled corticosteroid-containing regimen now","Take a course of oral prednisolone in place of the inhaler","Use montelukast in place of the inhaled corticosteroid"],"a":2,"x":["There is no reason to wait. Poorly controlled asthma is the risk in pregnancy, and it has already returned.","Reliever-only treatment is not recommended for any adult, and daily use shows that her asthma is uncontrolled.","Correct. Asthma in pregnancy is managed as in any adult. The preventer is not stopped, and treatment is stepped up as needed.","Oral corticosteroids are for flare-ups that do not settle. They do not replace an inhaled preventer.","Montelukast is less effective than an inhaled corticosteroid, and there is no reason to switch."],"e":"Problem: a pregnant woman who has stopped her preventer out of concern for the baby. Task: correct the belief and restore control. Flare-ups are linked to low birth weight and preterm birth. What if she were well controlled and asking to reduce her dose? Stepping down is deferred until after the pregnancy.","k":"Asthma in pregnancy: continue the inhaled corticosteroid, step up if needed, do not step down.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment; Assessing and reviewing asthma"],"c":["rs4-20","rs4-21","rs4-22","rs4-01","rs4-02"],"id":"rs-a21","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","s":"Raymond Cheng, aged 62 years, is a retired engineer with asthma managed for a decade on a single inhaler. He travels to a remote fishing camp for three weeks each winter and wants his action plan updated before he goes. He perceives his symptoms reliably. He asks what the plan should tell him to do first if his chest tightens for several days and he needs his reliever most days.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Ciclesonide 160 micrograms once daily by pressurised inhaler with spacer","Salbutamol two puffs as needed","Tamsulosin 400 micrograms daily","Atorvastatin 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear","Technique correct"]}],"q":"For a persistent, non-urgent increase in symptoms, which instruction is MOST appropriate for his written plan?","o":["Double the ciclesonide dose for 3 days","Quadruple the ciclesonide dose for 1 to 2 weeks","Start amoxicillin and continue usual inhalers","Stop ciclesonide and start prednisolone 50 mg daily","Use salbutamol every 2 hours until symptoms settle"],"a":1,"x":["Doubling the inhaled dose is not enough to prevent progression.","Correct. For a person on inhaled corticosteroid alone, quadrupling the dose for one to two weeks is the recommended first response to persistent worsening.","Antibiotics have no routine role in an asthma flare-up.","Prednisolone is added if the higher inhaled dose does not work. The inhaled corticosteroid is not stopped.","More reliever treats symptoms but does nothing for the inflammation driving them."],"e":"Problem: writing the middle section of an action plan for someone on inhaled corticosteroid alone. Task: know the size of the increase. What if he were on a fixed corticosteroid-LABA inhaler? A prednisolone course is usually more practical, because raising the corticosteroid would need a second inhaler.","k":"On inhaled corticosteroid alone, the plan quadruples the dose for 1 to 2 weeks. Doubling is not enough.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents"],"v":"This instruction comes from the 2020 Therapeutic Guidelines. The 2025 Australian Asthma Handbook pages read for this unit did not cover it, and the Handbook now prefers regimens where the reliever itself carries the corticosteroid.","c":["rs4-09","rs4-10","rs4-11","rs4-12","rs4-13"],"id":"rs-a22","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#general","s":"Vincent Russo, aged 58 years, has had asthma since his twenties, well controlled on maintenance and reliever therapy with no flare-ups in five years. He was discharged from hospital three days ago after a heart attack treated with a stent. The discharge summary notes that a beta blocker was withheld 'because of asthma' and asks the general practitioner to review. His echocardiogram showed mildly reduced left ventricular function.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, one inhalation twice daily and one as needed","Aspirin 100 mg daily","Ticagrelor 90 mg twice daily","Atorvastatin 80 mg daily","Ramipril 2.5 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Heart rate 84/min, blood pressure 124/76 mmHg","SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate approach to beta-blocker treatment?","o":["Avoid beta blockers permanently and use diltiazem","Start a beta blocker at a low dose under supervision","Start a beta blocker at the full target dose, as his asthma is controlled","Start a beta blocker only after bronchial provocation testing","Start a beta blocker only if his asthma treatment is first stepped up"],"a":1,"x":["Asthma does not remove the benefit of a beta blocker after a heart attack. Avoidance is not required when an indication exists.","Correct. When a person with asthma develops an indication for a beta blocker, it is started at a low dose under supervision.","Starting at full dose ignores the risk of bronchoconstriction. The start is cautious.","Provocation testing is a diagnostic test for uncertain asthma, not a safety screen for beta blockers.","His asthma is well controlled. There is no reason to increase asthma treatment pre-emptively."],"e":"Problem: a clear cardiac indication for a beta blocker in a man with well-controlled asthma. Task: weigh the two conditions. Beta blockers are a trigger to minimise, not an absolute ban. What if he had no cardiac indication and was offered a beta blocker for tremor? Another drug would be chosen.","k":"Asthma with a new indication for a beta blocker: start low, under supervision.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents, Table 9.7"],"c":["rs4-23","rs4-24","rs4-25","rs4-26","rs4-27"],"id":"rs-a23","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#review","s":"Eleni Markou, aged 41 years, attends for a cervical screening test. Her record shows asthma, and her last asthma-specific visit was three years ago. She uses budesonide-formoterol as needed about once a fortnight, has had no night symptoms or flare-ups, and plays tennis weekly. Her last spirometry was at diagnosis six years ago. She smokes socially, about five cigarettes a week. She has no written action plan.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, one inhalation as needed","No other regular medicines"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"q":"Which is the MOST appropriate plan for her asthma?","o":["Add a daily maintenance dose, because she smokes","Arrange a chest X-ray, because she smokes","Arrange a review with spirometry, an action plan and smoking cessation support","No action is needed today, because her symptoms are well controlled and she rarely needs her inhaler","Refer to a respiratory physician, because spirometry is overdue"],"a":2,"x":["Her control is good. Smoking is addressed directly, not by adding inhaled corticosteroid.","Imaging is not part of routine asthma review.","Correct. Asthma is reviewed at least yearly, spirometry is repeated about every two years, everyone has a written plan, and smoking is addressed at every opportunity.","Good symptom control does not remove the need for review. She is overdue on three counts and smokes.","Overdue spirometry is arranged in primary care. Referral is for uncertain diagnosis or uncontrolled disease."],"e":"Problem: well-controlled asthma noticed during an unrelated visit, with no review for three years. Task: list what a review must cover. Feeling well is the distractor. What if she had frequent symptoms with normal spirometry? The diagnosis would need reconsidering.","k":"Asthma review at least yearly; spirometry about every 2 years; a written plan for everyone; address smoking.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Assessing and reviewing asthma"],"c":["rs4-06","rs4-07","rs4-08","rs4-01","rs4-02"],"id":"rs-a24","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Asthma: review and action plans","nt":"rs-asthma-review#plan","n":4,"s":"You are writing an asthma action plan for Michelle Okafor, aged 27 years, who has just started low-dose maintenance and reliever therapy with budesonide-formoterol.","q":"Which FOUR of the following must her written action plan include? Select four.","o":["Her predicted peak flow from a height chart","Her usual maintenance and reliever doses","The date of her next influenza vaccination","The maximum number of reliever inhalations per occasion and per day","The results of her allergy tests","When to seek medical care and when to call an ambulance","When to start a course of oral corticosteroids"],"sc":[0,1,0,1,0,1,1],"x":["Scores 0. Peak-flow plans are for selected patients and use personal best, not a predicted value.","Scores 1. The plan starts with usual treatment.","Scores 0. Useful to arrange, but not part of an action plan.","Scores 1. Limits are specific to the inhaler and must be stated.","Scores 0. Allergy results do not guide what to do in a flare-up.","Scores 1. Every person with asthma must know when to get help.","Scores 1. The plan states the trigger for oral corticosteroids."],"e":"A plan answers four questions: what do I take when well, how much extra can I take, when do I add prednisolone, and when do I get help. It is matched to the regimen and reviewed yearly and whenever treatment changes.","k":"An action plan lists usual doses, reliever limits, when to start oral corticosteroids, and when to seek help.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Management: adults and adolescents: Adjusting treatment","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Maintenance management of asthma in adults and adolescents"],"c":["rs4-09","rs4-10","rs4-11","rs4-12","rs4-13"],"id":"rs-k04","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#treat","s":"Bianca Rossi, aged 34 years, walks into your suburban practice at 10 am with a tight chest and wheeze that began overnight during a cold. She has asthma and ran out of her preventer a month ago. She can say only four or five words between breaths and is sitting forward on the chair. The nearest emergency department is 25 minutes away by road.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol inhaler, about 20 puffs since midnight","Fluticasone-salmeterol inhaler (not taken for four weeks)","Sertraline 100 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, anxious","Respiratory rate 28/min, heart rate 118/min","SpO2 93% on room air","Widespread wheeze, using neck muscles to breathe"]}],"q":"In addition to calling an ambulance, what is the MOST appropriate immediate treatment?","o":["Intramuscular adrenaline 0.5 mg","Nebulised salbutamol 2.5 mg, driven by air","Oral prednisolone 50 mg, then reassess in one hour before giving a bronchodilator","Salbutamol 12 puffs through a spacer, repeated every 20 minutes","Salbutamol 4 puffs through a spacer, repeated every 4 hours"],"a":3,"x":["Adrenaline is first-line for anaphylaxis, or for a peri-arrest patient who cannot inhale. She has neither.","A nebuliser is used only when a spacer cannot be. She can use one, and 2.5 mg is not the adult dose.","Prednisolone is given early, but never in place of a bronchodilator and never with a delay to treatment.","Correct. Speech in short phrases, accessory muscle use, a pulse of 118 and saturation of 93% make this severe. The dose is 12 puffs through a spacer, at least every 20 minutes for the first hour.","Four puffs is the first aid dose. A four-hourly interval is far too long for a severe attack."],"e":"Problem: an acute attack in the treatment room. Task: grade it and dose it. Any one severe feature is enough: here there are four. Severe means 12 puffs, every 20 minutes, with an ambulance on the way. What if she were speaking full sentences with a saturation of 97%? Mild to moderate: 4 to 12 puffs, managed in the practice.","k":"Severe acute asthma: salbutamol 12 puffs via spacer every 20 minutes for the first hour, and an ambulance.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"c":["rs5-12","rs5-13","rs5-14","rs5-15","rs5-16"],"id":"rs-a25","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#steroids","s":"Darren Holt, aged 42 years, is a landscaper who presents with wheeze and breathlessness that came on while mowing a client's lawn this morning. He has asthma treated with as-needed budesonide-formoterol and had taken four inhalations before arriving. On arrival he could speak in full sentences with a saturation of 96%. He was given eight puffs of salbutamol through a spacer. Twenty minutes later he feels back to normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms as needed","Cetirizine 10 mg daily in spring","No other regular medicines"]},{"h":"Examination findings (after treatment)","i":["Respiratory rate 16/min, heart rate 84/min","SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a chest X-ray before he leaves","Discharge now with advice to use his inhaler as needed","Give a further 12 puffs of salbutamol to prevent a relapse","Give oral prednisolone and observe him before discharge","Start oral amoxicillin for five days"],"a":3,"x":["Imaging is not part of managing an uncomplicated asthma attack that has responded to treatment.","He has responded, but the episode was significant enough to bring him in. Leaving without a corticosteroid or a period of observation risks relapse.","He is symptom-free. More bronchodilator adds side effects without benefit.","Correct. Systemic corticosteroids are started for every adult who presents with acute asthma, whatever the first severity grading, and the patient is observed after settling.","Antibiotics have no role. The trigger was pollen and exertion."],"e":"Problem: a mild to moderate attack that cleared with one dose. Task: decide what else he needs. The rapid recovery makes sending him straight home attractive. The 2025 Handbook gives prednisolone to all who present and asks for at least four hours of observation. What if this were a brief wheeze that settled at home with his own inhaler? No presentation, no prednisolone: his action plan covers it.","k":"Every adult who presents with acute asthma gets a systemic corticosteroid, even after a good response.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"v":"The 2020 Therapeutic Guidelines allow corticosteroids to be omitted in the mildest cases that respond quickly and completely. This question follows the 2025 Australian Asthma Handbook.","c":["rs5-21","rs5-22","rs5-23","rs5-01","rs5-02"],"id":"rs-a26","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#severity","s":"Kylie Brennan, aged 27 years, has been in your rural practice treatment room for 40 minutes with a severe asthma attack. She has had three doses of 12 puffs of salbutamol through a spacer and oral prednisolone. The ambulance is 15 minutes away. The nurse calls you back because Kylie has 'calmed down': she has stopped talking, her eyes are closing, and the wheeze that was loud on arrival can no longer be heard.","b":[{"h":"Observations on arrival","i":["Respiratory rate 32/min, heart rate 126/min","SpO2 91% on room air","Loud wheeze, speaking in single words"]},{"h":"Observations now","i":["Respiratory rate 12/min, heart rate 58/min","SpO2 86% on oxygen 6 L/min","Breath sounds very soft on both sides"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate interpretation of the change in her condition?","o":["A panic attack has resolved, unmasking mild asthma","She has developed a pneumothorax on one side","She is exhausted and close to respiratory arrest","She is over-sedated by salbutamol","The bronchodilator has taken effect and she is recovering"],"a":2,"x":["Falling saturation and a slowing pulse do not occur when anxiety settles.","A pneumothorax is a recognised complication, but it gives one-sided signs. Her breath sounds are soft on both sides.","Correct. Drowsiness, a silent chest, a slow respiratory rate and bradycardia are life-threatening features. Bradycardia may come just before respiratory arrest.","Salbutamol causes tremor and tachycardia. It does not sedate or slow the pulse.","Recovery brings easier speech and better saturation. Hers is falling as the chest goes quiet."],"e":"Problem: a patient who looks calmer after 40 minutes of treatment. Task: tell recovery from exhaustion. Every number has moved the wrong way for the wrong reason: the breathing has slowed because she is tiring, and the wheeze has gone because too little air is moving. What if her saturation had risen to 96% and she were chatting? That is recovery.","k":"Silent chest, drowsiness, slow breathing and bradycardia in acute asthma mean impending arrest.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Secondary severity assessment in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"c":["rs5-04","rs5-05","rs5-06","rs5-07","rs5-08"],"id":"rs-a27","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#anaphylaxis","s":"Jasmine Wu, aged 19 years, is carried into the practice by friends from the food court next door, ten minutes after starting a satay lunch. She has asthma and a peanut allergy diagnosed in childhood. She is wheezing loudly and says her throat feels tight. Her friends gave six puffs of her salbutamol inhaler in the car park without improvement. She did not have her adrenaline autoinjector with her.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms as needed","Salbutamol inhaler as needed","Adrenaline autoinjector (at home)"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 30/min, heart rate 124/min, blood pressure 92/58 mmHg","SpO2 93% on room air","Widespread wheeze","Raised red wheals on the neck and chest; lips swollen"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate immediate treatment?","o":["Intramuscular adrenaline into the outer thigh","Intravenous hydrocortisone 100 mg","Nebulised salbutamol 5 mg with ipratropium 500 micrograms","Oral cetirizine 10 mg and prednisolone 50 mg","Salbutamol 12 puffs through a spacer"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Sudden breathlessness with hives and lip swelling after a known allergen is anaphylaxis. Intramuscular adrenaline is first-line and is given without delay.","Corticosteroids act too slowly to treat anaphylaxis and are never the first drug.","Bronchodilators can follow, but they do not treat the airway swelling or the falling blood pressure.","Antihistamines relieve itch and hives only. They do not treat airway or circulatory compromise.","This is the right dose for severe asthma, but treating this as asthma alone misses the diagnosis."],"e":"Problem: wheeze in a known asthmatic that has not responded to salbutamol. Task: recognise anaphylaxis. The skin signs, lip swelling, low blood pressure and timing after food are the deciding facts. What if there were no skin signs but she had vomiting and dizziness with the wheeze? Anaphylaxis is still suspected and adrenaline is still given.","k":"Wheeze with skin, gut or circulation signs is anaphylaxis: intramuscular adrenaline first.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute asthma","National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"c":["rs5-24","rs5-25","rs5-01","rs5-02","rs5-03"],"id":"rs-a28","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","s":"Walter Henare, aged 61 years, presents to your rural practice at 4 pm with an asthma attack graded mild to moderate. He has had three doses of salbutamol through a spacer and oral prednisolone. Two hours later he can speak in sentences, but wheeze has returned about 90 minutes after each dose. He lives alone on a property 45 minutes away with patchy phone reception. He was admitted to intensive care with asthma four years ago.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily and as needed","Metformin 1 g twice daily","Ramipril 5 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings (two hours after arrival)","i":["Respiratory rate 20/min, heart rate 98/min","SpO2 95% on room air","Scattered wheeze"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange transfer to hospital for further observation and treatment","Discharge home with a nebuliser on loan from the practice","Discharge home with instructions to take salbutamol every two hours overnight","Discharge home with prednisolone and review in the morning","Keep him in the practice until closing, then discharge"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. He still needs reliever more often than every four hours, lives alone far from help, and has a past intensive care admission. Each of these weighs against going home.","A nebuliser at home does not make an unstable patient safe, and encourages delay in seeking help.","Needing reliever every two hours is itself the reason not to discharge.","Review tomorrow assumes a safe night. His reliever need and isolation make that unsafe.","A few more hours do not change his overnight risk if he is still needing frequent reliever."],"e":"Problem: partial response to treatment in a man with risk factors and no one at home. Task: decide where he spends the night. The reasonable saturation is the distractor. The deciding fact is that wheeze returns well inside four hours. What if he had needed no reliever for four hours and lived in town with his partner? Home, with written instructions and review in 3 to 5 days.","k":"No discharge while reliever is needed more often than every 4 hours. Weigh distance, isolation and past severe attacks.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute asthma","National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"c":["rs5-26","rs5-27","rs5-28","rs5-29","rs5-30"],"id":"rs-a29","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#storm","s":"Lachlan Burke, aged 23 years, is an agronomy student in the Riverina. He attends in late August for a medical certificate. He mentions sneezing and itchy eyes every spring, and that for the past two Octobers he has had a few days of chest tightness and wheeze, which he treated with a salbutamol inhaler bought from the pharmacy. He is well between seasons and has never been told he has asthma. Spirometry today is normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol inhaler, used in October only","Fexofenadine 180 mg as needed","No preventer inhaler"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear","Pale, swollen nasal turbinates"]}],"q":"Which advice is MOST appropriate to reduce his risk during the coming pollen season?","o":["Avoid outdoor exercise in winter","Carry salbutamol and take two puffs before going outdoors on each day of the pollen season","Start a nasal corticosteroid spray as his only preventive treatment","Take prednisolone 25 mg on days when storms are forecast","Use an inhaled corticosteroid-containing treatment through the pollen season"],"a":4,"x":["His risk period is spring and early summer, when grass pollen counts are high.","Reliever-only self-management with grass pollen allergy is the highest-risk pattern for severe thunderstorm asthma.","A nasal spray will help his rhinitis, but it is not known whether it protects against thunderstorm asthma. He has lower airway symptoms each spring.","Pre-emptive oral corticosteroids are not recommended and expose him to avoidable harm.","Correct. Springtime rhinitis with seasonal wheeze marks him as at risk. Inhaled corticosteroid treatment is protective, and should be in place before and during the season."],"e":"Problem: seasonal wheeze self-treated with pharmacy salbutamol in a high-pollen region. Task: prevent a severe attack. Normal spirometry in August is expected out of season and does not clear him. What if he had rhinitis alone and had never wheezed? He is still at risk, and needs advice about storm days and what to do if symptoms start.","k":"Springtime hay fever with seasonal wheeze: inhaled corticosteroid through the pollen season, not salbutamol alone.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Thunderstorm asthma"],"c":["rs5-31","rs5-32","rs5-33","rs5-34","rs5-01"],"id":"rs-a30","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#oxygen","s":"Fiona Gallagher, aged 55 years, is brought to your practice by her husband with a severe asthma attack. An ambulance has been called and the first dose of salbutamol is being given through a spacer. She has had asthma since childhood, has never smoked and has no other lung disease. The practice has an oxygen cylinder with a range of masks and nasal prongs.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-formoterol 200/6 micrograms, two inhalations twice daily and as needed","Candesartan 8 mg daily","Estradiol 50 microgram patch twice weekly"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, speaking in short phrases","Respiratory rate 28/min, heart rate 116/min","SpO2 90% on room air"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate use of oxygen?","o":["Give high-flow oxygen by non-rebreather mask to keep saturation at 100%","Give oxygen only if she becomes drowsy","Start oxygen and titrate to a saturation of 88 to 92%","Start oxygen and titrate to a saturation of 92 to 96%","Withhold oxygen, because it suppresses respiratory drive in asthma"],"a":3,"x":["Over-oxygenation in adults raises the risk of carbon dioxide retention. The aim is a range, not a maximum.","Oxygen is started on the saturation reading, without waiting for drowsiness, which is a late sign.","The lower range of 88 to 92% is for people at risk of carbon dioxide retention, such as those with COPD. She has no such risk.","Correct. Oxygen is started when saturation is under 92% and titrated to 92 to 96%.","A saturation of 90% is treated. Controlled oxygen titrated to a target is safe."],"e":"Problem: saturation of 90% in a severe attack. Task: start and target oxygen correctly. Two targets exist, and the lower one is the distractor. What if she also had COPD with previous carbon dioxide retention? Target 88 to 92%.","k":"Acute asthma: oxygen if saturation is under 92%, titrated to 92 to 96% (88 to 92% if at risk of CO2 retention).","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute asthma"],"c":["rs5-19","rs5-20","rs5-01","rs5-02","rs5-03"],"id":"rs-a31","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Acute asthma (12+)","nt":"rs-asthma-acute#after","n":4,"s":"Tamara Singh, aged 30 years, has recovered in your practice from a moderate asthma attack triggered by a cold. She has been observed for four hours, needs no further reliever and is ready to go home with her partner. Before the attack she used salbutamol alone.","q":"Which FOUR of the following should be part of her discharge plan? Select four.","o":["A course of amoxicillin for the cold","A maintenance inhaled corticosteroid-containing preventer","A review appointment within 3 to 5 days","A written interim action plan, including when to call an ambulance","Instructions to take salbutamol every four hours by the clock for a week","Peak flow readings four times a day for a month","Prednisolone each morning to complete a 5 to 10 day course"],"sc":[0,1,1,1,0,0,1],"x":["Scores 0. A viral trigger does not call for antibiotics.","Scores 1. After an attack she starts maintenance treatment with at least a medium corticosteroid dose. Salbutamol alone is not acceptable.","Scores 1. First follow-up is within 3 to 5 days, with a full review about two weeks later.","Scores 1. Written instructions cover what to do if symptoms return.","Scores 0. Reliever is used as needed, not on a schedule.","Scores 0. Not part of routine post-acute care.","Scores 1. The corticosteroid course is completed, with written timing."],"e":"Four things leave with her: a preventer, the rest of the prednisolone course, a written plan, and an appointment. The attack is also the moment to end reliever-only treatment.","k":"After an attack: preventer, prednisolone course, written plan, review in 3 to 5 days.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in adults and adolescents in primary care"],"c":["rs5-26","rs5-27","rs5-28","rs5-29","rs5-30"],"id":"rs-k05","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#ages","s":"Oliver Grant, aged 9 months, is brought in by his parents with a third episode of noisy, wheezy breathing in three months. Each has come with a runny nose and lasted about a week. Between episodes he feeds well, is active and is growing along the 50th centile. He was born at term. His father has asthma and asks whether Oliver can have 'a puffer like mine' to keep at home.","b":[{"h":"History","i":["No eczema or food reactions","Attends childcare three days a week","No smokers at home"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, smiling","Respiratory rate 36/min, SpO2 98% on room air","Scattered wheeze, mild subcostal recession","No murmur, no clubbing"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate response to the father's request?","o":["Diagnose asthma and start a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid","Diagnose asthma and supply salbutamol with a spacer and mask","Explain that asthma is not diagnosed or treated before 12 months","Prescribe montelukast granules as a trial","Supply a home nebuliser with salbutamol for future episodes"],"a":2,"x":["Asthma is not diagnosed under 12 months, and asthma treatment is not prescribed at this age.","A reliever is not prescribed for asthma under 12 months, whatever the family history.","Correct. Wheeze in infants is most often viral bronchiolitis. Asthma is not diagnosed or treated before 12 months, and persistent wheeze is referred.","Montelukast is an alternative preventer for older children whose parents decline corticosteroids. It is not for infants.","Nebulised medicines are not prescribed for home use in children."],"e":"Problem: recurrent viral wheeze in a thriving infant with an asthmatic father. Task: resist the label. The family history makes asthma feel likely, but the age rules it out for now. What if he were 3 years old with the same story and settled within minutes of salbutamol? A reliever, and a preventer trial if episodes are frequent.","k":"No asthma diagnosis and no asthma treatment under 12 months.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years: Diagnosing asthma in children 1 to 5 years; Initial asthma treatment"],"c":["rs6-01","rs6-02","rs6-03","rs6-04","rs6-05"],"id":"rs-a32","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","s":"Mia Patel, aged 3 years, has had wheeze and cough since she turned two. Her mother reports a night cough that wakes Mia two or three times a week, and wheeze when she runs at the park on most days, even when she has no cold. Salbutamol through a spacer and mask clears the wheeze within minutes. She has had one emergency department visit and has never had oral corticosteroids. She has eczema.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol 100 micrograms, two puffs by spacer and mask as needed","Emollient cream daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Growing along the 25th centile","Chest clear today, SpO2 99% on room air","Dry eczema in elbow creases"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add montelukast 4 mg daily","Continue salbutamol alone and review in six months","Refer for spirometry with bronchodilator","Start fluticasone propionate 50 micrograms twice daily by spacer and mask","Start fluticasone propionate-salmeterol 50/25 micrograms twice daily by spacer and mask"],"a":3,"x":["Montelukast is an alternative only if parents decline an inhaled corticosteroid after discussion.","Daytime symptoms more than twice a week and night symptoms more than twice a month both call for a preventer.","Children under 6 cannot perform spirometry reliably. The response to salbutamol is the evidence at this age.","Correct. Frequent symptoms that respond to salbutamol are an indication for a trial of daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, reviewed at about three months.","A LABA combination is a level 3 option from age 4, not a first preventer for a 3-year-old."],"e":"Problem: a preschooler with frequent, salbutamol-responsive symptoms between colds. Task: decide whether she needs a preventer and which. Her thresholds are met twice over. What if she wheezed only with colds, three times a year, and was well in between? Salbutamol as needed, without a preventer.","k":"Ages 1 to 5: start low-dose inhaled corticosteroid for daytime symptoms twice a week or night symptoms twice a month.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years: Initial asthma treatment for children 1 to 5 years"],"c":["rs6-14","rs6-15","rs6-16","rs6-17","rs6-18"],"id":"rs-a33","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#young","s":"Noah Williams, aged 4 years, started a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid three months ago for frequent wheeze. His parents give every dose through a spacer and their technique is good. Since starting he has had no wheeze, no night cough and no reliever use, including through two colds and the start of the pollen season. He is growing along his centile. His parents ask how long he must stay on the inhaler.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone propionate 50 micrograms twice daily by spacer","Salbutamol as needed (not used for 10 weeks)"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Chest clear","Height and weight on the 50th centile"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Consider pausing the inhaled corticosteroid and watching for a return of symptoms","Continue the same dose every day until he is old enough to perform spirometry reliably","Double the dose over winter to prevent flare-ups","Replace the inhaled corticosteroid with daily montelukast","Switch to budesonide-formoterol as needed"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. After a good response through his usual triggers, treatment can be paused to see whether symptoms return. Some preschool children go into remission.","There is no need to continue for two more years without testing whether he still needs it.","He is fully controlled. More corticosteroid adds risk with no gain.","Montelukast is less effective and is not a step-down option.","Budesonide-formoterol as needed is for people aged 12 and over."],"e":"Problem: a preschooler fully controlled after a three-month trial that included colds. Task: decide what the trial has shown. A good response through typical triggers is the cue to test whether treatment is still needed. What if symptoms had continued despite good technique? Check triggers at home, then question the diagnosis.","k":"Preschool preventer trial: review at 3 months; if well through usual triggers, consider a pause.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years: Initial asthma treatment for children 1 to 5 years"],"c":["rs6-14","rs6-15","rs6-16","rs6-17","rs6-18"],"id":"rs-a34","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","s":"Zara Ahmed, aged 7 years, has had a cough every day for seven weeks since a cold. Her mother describes it as 'chesty and rattly', worst in the morning, and Zara sometimes swallows what she coughs up. There is no wheeze, no breathlessness with sport and no night waking. A salbutamol inhaler from an after-hours clinic made no difference. She is growing well and has had no previous chest problems.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol two puffs as needed for two weeks, now stopped"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, SpO2 99% on room air","Coarse crackles that clear with coughing","No clubbing, no chest deformity","Weight on the 50th centile"]}],"q":"What is the MOST likely diagnosis?","o":["Cough-variant asthma needing a preventer","Habit cough","Inhaled foreign body","Pertussis","Protracted bacterial bronchitis"],"a":4,"x":["An isolated moist cough with no wheeze or breathlessness, and no response to salbutamol, makes asthma less likely.","A habit cough is dry, present only in the daytime, and stops in sleep.","This usually starts abruptly and gives one-sided signs.","Pertussis gives bouts of dry coughing, often ending in a whoop or vomiting, not a daily wet cough.","Correct. A persistent wet cough in an otherwise well child, with no features of asthma, points to protracted bacterial bronchitis."],"e":"Problem: seven weeks of daily wet cough in a well child who has been given an asthma reliever. Task: recognise that cough alone is not asthma. The moist quality is the deciding fact. What if she also had clubbing and poor growth? Cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis would need excluding.","k":"A child's persistent wet cough without wheeze is protracted bacterial bronchitis until shown otherwise, not asthma.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Signs and symptoms that suggest an alternative diagnosis in children"],"c":["rs6-09","rs6-10","rs6-11","rs6-12","rs6-13"],"id":"rs-a35","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#levels","s":"Jack Morrison, aged 8 years, had asthma confirmed on spirometry six months ago and uses salbutamol as needed. Over the past two months he has coughed at night about once a week and needs his reliever at school sport three times a week. He had one course of prednisolone for a flare-up in winter. His technique with a spacer is good. His older brother, aged 14, uses budesonide-formoterol as needed, and his mother asks whether Jack can have the same.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol 100 micrograms, two to four puffs by spacer as needed","Intranasal mometasone in spring"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Chest clear, SpO2 99% on room air","Height on the 60th centile"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate change to his treatment?","o":["Add a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, with salbutamol as needed","Add daily montelukast, with salbutamol as needed","Change to budesonide-formoterol as needed, like his brother","Change to fluticasone propionate-salmeterol 50/25 micrograms, two puffs twice daily","Continue salbutamol alone and add a peak flow diary"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. At 6 to 11 years, frequent symptoms or a flare-up needing oral corticosteroids in the past year call for a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, reviewed in 8 to 12 weeks.","Montelukast is an alternative only when parents decline an inhaled corticosteroid.","As-needed budesonide-formoterol is recommended from age 12. Under 12 the reliever remains salbutamol.","A LABA combination is level 3. He has not yet tried level 2.","His symptoms and flare-up already meet the threshold for a preventer."],"e":"Problem: an 8-year-old with weekly night cough, reliever use three times a week and a recent prednisolone course. Task: step up correctly for his age. His brother's regimen is the distractor. What if Jack were 12? As-needed budesonide-formoterol, or maintenance and reliever therapy, would be the choice.","k":"Under 12: level 2 is daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid plus salbutamol. AIR and MART start at 12.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years: Treatment levels and Initial asthma treatment for children 6 to 11 years"],"c":["rs6-20","rs6-21","rs6-22","rs6-23","rs6-24"],"id":"rs-a36","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#never","s":"Chloe Bennett, aged 5 years, has asthma treated with a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid and salbutamol by spacer. Her grandmother, who minds her after kindergarten, finds the spacer fiddly. She asks for salbutamol syrup, which her own children had, or a nebuliser machine to keep at her house. Chloe's asthma is well controlled, with reliever use about once a fortnight.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone propionate 50 micrograms twice daily by spacer","Salbutamol two puffs by spacer as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Chest clear","Chloe shows good technique with her spacer and mouthpiece"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate response?","o":["Prescribe a home nebuliser with salbutamol nebules","Prescribe salbutamol syrup for use at her grandmother's house after kindergarten each day","Replace both inhalers with daily montelukast","Stop the preventer so that only one inhaler is needed","Teach her grandmother to use the spacer, and give her a copy of the action plan"],"a":4,"x":["Nebulised medicines are not prescribed for home use in children.","Oral salbutamol is not recommended for asthma.","Montelukast is less effective and is reserved for families who decline corticosteroids.","She is well controlled because of the preventer. Removing it for convenience invites flare-ups.","Correct. A pressurised inhaler and spacer remains the device. Everyone who cares for the child is taught to use it and holds a copy of the plan."],"e":"Problem: a carer who wants an older, easier-seeming treatment. Task: keep the right device and fix the real barrier, which is training. What if Chloe herself could not manage the mouthpiece? Add a mask to the spacer.","k":"Children with asthma: spacer, not nebuliser or syrup. Teach every carer and share the action plan.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), children 1 to 5 years and 6 to 11 years: Initial asthma treatment"],"c":["rs6-27","rs6-28","rs6-01","rs6-02","rs6-03"],"id":"rs-a37","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids#redflags","n":3,"s":"Leo Fischer, aged 6 years, is referred to you by a locum as 'asthma not responding to treatment' after three months on an inhaled corticosteroid.","q":"Which THREE of the following findings would MOST strongly suggest a diagnosis other than asthma? Select three.","o":["Eczema in the elbow creases","Finger clubbing","His mother has hay fever","Night cough that is worse in the early morning","Symptoms present since birth","Wheeze after running that eases with salbutamol","Wheeze heard only over the right lower chest"],"sc":[0,1,0,0,1,0,1],"x":["Scores 0. Atopy raises the probability of asthma.","Scores 1. Clubbing points to cystic fibrosis, bronchiectasis, primary ciliary dyskinesia or immunodeficiency.","Scores 0. A family history of allergy raises the probability of asthma.","Scores 0. This pattern is typical of asthma.","Scores 1. Symptoms from birth suggest cystic fibrosis, a structural abnormality or ciliary disease.","Scores 0. Exercise wheeze that responds to a reliever supports asthma.","Scores 1. Focal or one-sided wheeze suggests an inhaled foreign body or a structural lesion and needs urgent attention."],"e":"When a child's 'asthma' does not respond, the first question is whether it is asthma. Clubbing, onset at birth and focal wheeze each point away from it, and each needs investigation or referral.","k":"Clubbing, symptoms from birth and focal wheeze point away from asthma in a child.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Signs and symptoms that suggest an alternative diagnosis in children"],"c":["rs6-09","rs6-10","rs6-11","rs6-12","rs6-13"],"id":"rs-k06","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#dose","s":"Ruby Chen, aged 3 years, is carried into your practice by her father with wheeze and fast breathing that began this morning during a cold. She has had two previous wheezy episodes that settled with salbutamol. She is clinging to her father and crying in short bursts, managing two or three words at a time. She weighs 15 kg.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol two puffs by spacer and mask, given twice at home today"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, distressed","Respiratory rate 48/min, heart rate 150/min","SpO2 93% on room air","Widespread wheeze with subcostal recession and tracheal tug"]}],"q":"In addition to calling an ambulance, what is the MOST appropriate immediate treatment?","o":["Nebulised salbutamol 5 mg, driven by air","Oral prednisolone 15 mg, then reassess in 30 minutes","Salbutamol 12 puffs through a spacer and mask, repeated every 20 minutes","Salbutamol 2 puffs through a spacer and mask, repeated every 4 hours","Salbutamol 6 puffs through a spacer and mask, repeated every 20 minutes"],"a":4,"x":["A nebuliser is for a child who cannot breathe through a spacer and mask, and the dose under 6 years is 2.5 mg.","A corticosteroid may follow if she needs admission, but the bronchodilator comes first and is not delayed.","Twelve puffs is the severe dose from age 6. Under 6 the dose is 6 puffs.","Two puffs is her usual home reliever dose, and a four-hourly interval is far too long for a severe attack.","Correct. Recession, tracheal tug, broken speech and a saturation of 93% make this severe. Under 6 years the dose is 6 puffs, at least every 20 minutes for the first hour."],"e":"Problem: a 3-year-old with a severe attack. Task: pick the age-correct dose and interval. Two of the options are right for an older child. What if she were 7 years old? Twelve puffs every 20 minutes.","k":"Severe acute asthma: 6 puffs under 6 years, 12 puffs from 6 years, every 20 minutes for the first hour.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in children in primary care: children 1 to 5 years, immediate treatment table"],"c":["rs7-04","rs7-05","rs7-06","rs7-07","rs7-08"],"id":"rs-a38","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","s":"Archie Dunn, aged 4 years, presents with wheeze on the second day of a cold. He has had three similar episodes, each settling with salbutamol. On arrival he was running around the waiting room, talking in sentences, with a saturation of 97% and mild wheeze. He was given four puffs of salbutamol through a spacer. Thirty minutes later his chest is clear. His mother asks whether he should have 'the steroid syrup' he was given in hospital last year.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol two puffs by spacer as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings (after treatment)","i":["Playing, talking freely","Respiratory rate 26/min, SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear, no recession"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate advice about oral corticosteroids?","o":["Give a single dose of dexamethasone","Give prednisolone 1 mg/kg daily for 3 days","Give prednisolone 1 mg/kg daily for 5 to 10 days","Give prednisolone only if he wheezes again tonight","They are not needed for this episode"],"a":4,"x":["Dexamethasone is an alternative when a corticosteroid is indicated. Here none is.","This is the right dose and duration when a corticosteroid is indicated. In a preschooler that means an attack needing hospital admission.","Five to ten days is the adult course. Children have three days, when a course is needed at all.","Leaving a parent to start corticosteroids for a recurrence overnight is not the advice. Returning symptoms need reassessment.","Correct. In children aged 1 to 5, systemic corticosteroids are started only if hospital admission is required. A mild episode that clears with salbutamol does not need them."],"e":"Problem: a mild preschool wheeze that cleared with one dose, and a parent expecting steroids. Task: know who benefits. In preschoolers with mild to moderate wheeze, corticosteroids may add nothing. What if he were 8 years old with a moderate attack? Prednisolone 1 mg/kg for 3 days.","k":"Ages 1 to 5: oral corticosteroids only if the child needs hospital admission.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in children in primary care: children 1 to 5 years","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute asthma"],"c":["rs7-14","rs7-15","rs7-16","rs7-17","rs7-18"],"id":"rs-a39","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#steroids","s":"Ella Robinson, aged 9 years, has asthma treated with a daily low-dose inhaled corticosteroid. She presents after school with wheeze that began at lunchtime during cross-country training. On arrival she spoke in full sentences but was breathing fast, with a saturation of 95% and widespread wheeze. Her attack was graded moderate. She has had two doses of eight puffs of salbutamol through a spacer and is improving. She weighs 30 kg.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone propionate 50 micrograms twice daily by spacer","Salbutamol by spacer as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings (after two doses)","i":["Respiratory rate 22/min, heart rate 104/min","SpO2 97% on room air","Scattered wheeze, no recession"]}],"q":"Which corticosteroid prescription is MOST appropriate?","o":["Dexamethasone 8 mg orally each morning for 5 days","No corticosteroid, as she is improving","Prednisolone 30 mg each morning for 3 days","Prednisolone 30 mg each morning for 7 days, then taper","Prednisolone 50 mg each morning for 5 days"],"a":2,"x":["Dexamethasone is an accepted alternative, but as 0.6 mg/kg (maximum 16 mg) in a single dose, repeated once the next day if needed. It is not given as a five-day course.","At 6 to 11 years, corticosteroids are omitted only when the attack is graded mild at first assessment. Hers was moderate.","Correct. Prednisolone 1 mg/kg, to a maximum of 50 mg, each morning for 3 days: 30 mg for a 30 kg child.","Seven days with a taper is longer than a child needs. The course is 3 days with no taper.","Fifty milligrams is the adult dose and the paediatric ceiling, not the dose for a 30 kg child."],"e":"Problem: a moderate attack in a school-age child who is responding. Task: prescribe the corticosteroid by weight and for the right time. What if her attack had been mild at first assessment? No corticosteroid would be needed.","k":"Child's acute asthma: prednisolone 1 mg/kg (maximum 50 mg) each morning for 3 days.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in children in primary care: children 6 to 11 years","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute asthma"],"c":["rs7-14","rs7-15","rs7-16","rs7-17","rs7-18"],"id":"rs-a40","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#severity","s":"Samuel Okoye, aged 5 years, has been in your treatment room for 25 minutes with a severe asthma attack. He has had two doses of salbutamol through a spacer and mask, and oxygen by mask. An ambulance is ten minutes away. His mother says he 'seems to be settling', because he has stopped crying and is lying still on the bed. You re-examine him.","b":[{"h":"Observations on arrival","i":["Crying, speaking in single words","Respiratory rate 52/min, heart rate 158/min","SpO2 91% on room air","Loud wheeze with marked recession"]},{"h":"Observations now","i":["Eyes closed, rouses briefly to voice","Respiratory rate 18/min, heart rate 70/min","SpO2 87% on oxygen 5 L/min","Very quiet breath sounds, lips dusky"]}],"q":"How should his current condition be classified?","o":["Improving, as his respiratory rate and heart rate have fallen","Life-threatening","Mild","Severe, unchanged from arrival","Sleeping normally after exertion"],"a":1,"x":["The falls in breathing and heart rate reflect exhaustion, not recovery. His saturation has dropped despite oxygen.","Correct. Reduced consciousness, a silent chest, cyanosis, slow breathing, bradycardia and a saturation under 90% are each life-threatening features.","He meets none of the criteria: he is not alert, not vocalising, and his saturation is far below 94%.","He has moved from severe to life-threatening. New drowsiness and a quiet chest mark the change.","A child with a saturation of 87% and dusky lips is not simply asleep."],"e":"Problem: a child who has gone quiet during treatment. Task: read the quiet correctly. Every feature that looks like calm is a feature of exhaustion. Bradycardia may come just before respiratory arrest. What if he were alert, pink and chatting with a saturation of 96%? That would be improvement.","k":"A drowsy child with a quiet chest, slow pulse and falling saturation has life-threatening asthma.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Secondary severity assessment of acute asthma in children 1 to 5 in primary care"],"c":["rs7-01","rs7-02","rs7-03","rs7-04","rs7-05"],"id":"rs-a41","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#oxygen","s":"Isla McKenzie, aged 7 years, is brought to a rural practice with an asthma attack. The first dose of salbutamol is being given through a spacer and an ambulance has been called. She is alert, speaking a few words at a time, with recession. Her saturation on room air is 90%. The practice has oxygen, simple paediatric masks and nasal prongs. She has no other medical conditions.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol by spacer as needed","No preventer"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 38/min, heart rate 136/min","SpO2 90% on room air","Widespread wheeze"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate use of oxygen?","o":["Give oxygen by simple mask at 15 L/min, with the aim of reaching a saturation of 100%","Give oxygen by simple mask at 5 L/min, titrated to a saturation of 92 to 96%","Give oxygen only if her saturation falls below 85%","Titrate oxygen to a saturation of 88 to 92%","Withhold oxygen until salbutamol has had 20 minutes to work"],"a":1,"x":["The aim is a target range, not a maximum, and a simple mask is run at 5 L/min.","Correct. Oxygen starts when saturation is under 92% on room air, by simple mask at 5 L/min or nasal prongs at 3 L/min, aiming for 92 to 96%.","The threshold for starting is under 92%, not 85%.","The 88 to 92% range is for adults at risk of carbon dioxide retention. It is not the target for a child with asthma.","Oxygen is given alongside the bronchodilator, not after it."],"e":"Problem: a saturation of 90% in a child with a severe attack. Task: start, deliver and target oxygen. What if her saturation were 93%? The attack is still severe, but oxygen is not yet required.","k":"Children with acute asthma: oxygen if under 92%, simple mask 5 L/min or prongs 3 L/min, target 92 to 96%.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in children in primary care: children 6 to 11 years"],"c":["rs7-12","rs7-13","rs7-01","rs7-02","rs7-03"],"id":"rs-a42","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Acute asthma in children","nt":"rs-asthma-kids-acute#after","n":3,"s":"Harper Lewis, aged 6 years, has been treated in your practice for a moderate asthma attack. Two hours after her last dose of salbutamol she is playing, speaking normally, and has a saturation of 98% with a clear chest. Her parents are keen to take her home.","q":"Which THREE of the following should happen before or as she leaves? Select three.","o":["Arrange a review appointment within 3 to 5 days","Give written instructions on reliever, prednisolone and when to call an ambulance","Keep her under observation until 4 hours have passed since she settled","Lend the family a nebuliser for the next week","Order a chest X-ray to exclude pneumonia","Prescribe amoxicillin in case of bacterial infection","Tell the parents to give salbutamol every 3 hours by the clock for 3 days"],"sc":[1,1,1,0,0,0,0],"x":["Scores 1. First follow-up is within 3 to 5 days, with a full review about two weeks later.","Scores 1. An interim written plan goes home with every child.","Scores 1. Observation continues for at least 4 hours after the breathing has settled. She is at 2 hours.","Scores 0. Nebulisers are not prescribed or recommended for home use.","Scores 0. Imaging is not routine after an attack that has responded.","Scores 0. Antibiotics have no routine role in acute asthma.","Scores 0. Reliever is given as needed, not on a schedule."],"e":"Three things stand between a recovered child and the car park: enough observation time, a written plan, and a booked review.","k":"After a child's attack: observe 4 hours, give a written plan, review in 3 to 5 days.","src":["National Asthma Council Australia. Australian Asthma Handbook v3.0 (2025), Managing acute asthma in children in primary care"],"c":["rs7-21","rs7-22","rs7-01","rs7-02","rs7-03"],"id":"rs-k07","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#dx","s":"Kevin Doyle, aged 58 years, is a truck driver who attends for a licence medical. On questioning he admits to a cough with clear sputum most mornings for three years and says he now stops for breath on the stairs to his flat. He has smoked 20 cigarettes a day since he was 17. He has had two courses of antibiotics for 'bronchitis' in the past year. A chest X-ray six months ago was reported as normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Perindopril 5 mg daily","Atorvastatin 40 mg daily","Pantoprazole 40 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 96% on room air","Chest: quiet breath sounds, no crackles","BMI 29 kg/m2, no ankle oedema"]}],"q":"Which investigation is MOST appropriate to make the diagnosis?","o":["CT scan of the chest","Peak expiratory flow diary for two weeks","Repeat chest X-ray with a lateral view, to look for hyperinflation and flattened diaphragms","Serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level","Spirometry before and after a bronchodilator"],"a":4,"x":["CT can show emphysema but is not required for diagnosis, and cannot confirm airflow obstruction.","Peak flow does not diagnose COPD and is not useful for monitoring it.","A chest X-ray helps exclude other causes. A normal film does not exclude COPD, and no film can diagnose it.","Alpha-1 antitrypsin is tested when COPD is diagnosed before 40 or runs in the family. The diagnosis itself comes first.","Correct. COPD cannot be diagnosed without spirometry. A post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC under 0.7 confirms it."],"e":"Problem: a 58-year-old smoker with cough, sputum, breathlessness and recurrent chest infections. Task: choose the test that makes the diagnosis. The normal X-ray is the distractor. What if he were 37 with the same picture? Spirometry first, then alpha-1 antitrypsin testing and referral.","k":"COPD is diagnosed on spirometry: post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC under 0.7.","src":["Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"c":["rs8-01","rs8-02","rs8-04","rs8-05","rs8-06"],"id":"rs-a43","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#steps","s":"Margaret Liu, aged 67 years, has moderate COPD diagnosed two years ago, with an FEV1 of 55% predicted. She stopped smoking at diagnosis and completed pulmonary rehabilitation last year. She has had no exacerbations, but in recent months she has to stop halfway to the shops to catch her breath. She takes her inhaler every morning, and you confirm her technique is correct. Her vaccinations are up to date.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium 18 micrograms inhaled once daily","Salbutamol two puffs as needed, used most days","Amlodipine 5 mg daily","Calcium and vitamin D daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 95% on room air","Reduced breath sounds, no wheeze or crackles","No ankle oedema"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add a long-acting beta agonist to her long-acting muscarinic antagonist","Add an inhaled corticosteroid to her long-acting muscarinic antagonist","Add regular ipratropium four times daily to her current inhalers","Add theophylline 200 mg twice daily and check a serum level after one week of treatment","Start prednisolone 5 mg daily"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Persisting breathlessness on one long-acting bronchodilator is treated by adding the other. LAMA with LABA comes before any corticosteroid.","An inhaled corticosteroid is added for exacerbations with ongoing symptoms on dual bronchodilators. She has had none, and it raises pneumonia risk.","Ipratropium is not combined with a long-acting muscarinic antagonist.","Theophylline is not recommended for COPD.","Long-term oral corticosteroids are not used for maintenance. They cause bone loss and muscle weakness."],"e":"Problem: breathlessness on a single long-acting bronchodilator, with adherence, technique and non-drug measures all in place. Task: take the next step in order. The absence of exacerbations is what rules out the corticosteroid. What if she had been admitted with an exacerbation this year and was already on both bronchodilators? Triple therapy.","k":"COPD steps: reliever, then LAMA or LABA, then both, then add inhaled corticosteroid for exacerbations.","src":["Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"c":["rs8-16","rs8-17","rs8-18","rs8-19","rs8-20"],"id":"rs-a44","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#steps","s":"Brian Foster, aged 71 years, has COPD with an FEV1 of 44% predicted. In the past 12 months he has had two exacerbations treated at home with prednisolone and antibiotics. Between them he remains breathless walking to his letterbox. He stopped smoking five years ago and attends a maintenance exercise class. His inhaler technique is good and pharmacy records show regular dispensing. He has not had pneumonia.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Umeclidinium-vilanterol 62.5/25 micrograms, one inhalation daily","Salbutamol as needed","Metoprolol 50 mg twice daily","Rivaroxaban 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 93% on room air","Hyperinflated chest, quiet breath sounds","No peripheral oedema"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add an inhaled corticosteroid, as a single triple-therapy inhaler","Add oral prednisolone 10 mg daily for three months, then reassess his exacerbation rate","Change to an inhaled corticosteroid alone","Continue current inhalers and review in 12 months","Stop metoprolol, as beta blockers worsen COPD"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Two moderate exacerbations in 12 months with significant symptoms on LAMA with LABA meet both conditions for adding an inhaled corticosteroid.","Maintenance oral corticosteroids are not recommended in COPD.","An inhaled corticosteroid alone is not a COPD treatment. The bronchodilators stay.","He meets the threshold for stepping up now. Each exacerbation costs lung function.","Cardioselective beta blockers are safe in COPD when there is a cardiac indication."],"e":"Problem: two exacerbations and ongoing symptoms on dual bronchodilators. Task: recognise the threshold for triple therapy. Both parts of the rule are present. What if he had symptoms but no exacerbations? No corticosteroid: review technique, rehabilitation and other causes of breathlessness.","k":"Add inhaled corticosteroid in COPD for 2 moderate or 1 hospitalised exacerbation in a year, with symptoms on LAMA plus LABA.","src":["Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"c":["rs8-16","rs8-17","rs8-18","rs8-19","rs8-20"],"id":"rs-a45","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#refer","s":"Daniel Kovac, aged 38 years, is a carpenter who presents with two years of breathlessness on exertion and a daily cough. He smoked ten cigarettes a day for eight years and stopped at 30. His father died of 'emphysema' at 52. Spirometry at your practice shows a post-bronchodilator FEV1/FVC of 0.58 and an FEV1 of 61% predicted, with an FEV1 rise of 4% after salbutamol.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol as needed, supplied by a locum last month","No regular medicines"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 96% on room air","Reduced breath sounds at both bases","No clubbing"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a sleep study","Reassure him that this degree of obstruction is expected in an ex-smoker and repeat spirometry in a year","Refer to a respiratory physician and test for alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Start an inhaled corticosteroid for presumed asthma","Start long-term azithromycin"],"a":2,"x":["Nothing in the history suggests a sleep disorder.","An eight pack-year history does not explain this obstruction at 38. COPD before 40 always needs explanation.","Correct. COPD under 40, with a parent who died young of emphysema, suggests alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. Young patients are referred.","A 4% bronchodilator response and fixed obstruction do not support asthma.","A long-term macrolide is a specialist option for frequent exacerbations. He has had none."],"e":"Problem: fixed airflow obstruction at 38 with a light smoking history and a family history. Task: recognise early-onset COPD. Age is the deciding fact. What if he were 62 with a 40 pack-year history? Ordinary COPD, managed in general practice.","k":"COPD before age 40: think alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency and refer.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)"],"c":["rs8-30","rs8-31","rs8-01","rs8-02","rs8-03"],"id":"rs-a46","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#oxygen","s":"Shirley Watson, aged 74 years, has severe COPD and attends for a routine review. She has not had an exacerbation for three months. She stopped smoking eight years ago. She is breathless dressing and has noticed her ankles swelling in the evenings. Her inhaler technique is correct and she has completed pulmonary rehabilitation twice.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone furoate-umeclidinium-vilanterol, one inhalation daily","Salbutamol as needed","Furosemide 20 mg daily","Alendronate 70 mg weekly"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 88% on room air at rest, repeated after 10 minutes","Pitting oedema to mid-shin","Raised jugular venous pressure","Quiet chest, no crackles"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add theophylline","Increase furosemide and review in three months","Prescribe a portable oxygen cylinder to use at home whenever she feels breathless","Refer for arterial blood gases and assessment for long-term oxygen therapy","Start a course of prednisolone and amoxicillin"],"a":3,"x":["Theophylline is not recommended for COPD.","The oedema may improve, but the cause, which is persistent low oxygen with right heart strain, would be left untreated.","Oxygen 'as needed' for breathlessness is not the indication. Long-term oxygen is prescribed on blood gas criteria and used for more than 18 hours a day.","Correct. A resting saturation of 90% or less while stable calls for assessment. With signs of right heart failure she qualifies at a PaO2 of up to 59 mmHg.","She is stable, with no change in cough, sputum or breathlessness. This is not an exacerbation."],"e":"Problem: resting saturation of 88% and right heart failure in stable severe COPD. Task: recognise the indication for long-term oxygen assessment. Stability matters: testing waits four weeks after an exacerbation, and she is three months clear. What if she still smoked? Home oxygen is contraindicated because of fire risk.","k":"Stable COPD with resting saturation of 90% or less: arterial gases and long-term oxygen assessment.","src":["Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"c":["rs8-26","rs8-27","rs8-28","rs8-29","rs8-01"],"id":"rs-a47","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#nondrug","s":"Gary Simmons, aged 60 years, was diagnosed with mild COPD last month, with an FEV1 of 72% predicted. He smokes 25 cigarettes a day, has his first within ten minutes of waking and has tried to quit 'cold turkey' three times. He gets breathless hurrying but has had no exacerbations. He asks which of the things you have discussed will make the most difference to how long he lives.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol as needed, used twice a week","Irbesartan 150 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 97% on room air","Chest clear","BMI 26 kg/m2"]}],"q":"Which intervention has the GREATEST effect on his survival?","o":["Flu vaccine","Long-acting muscarinic antagonist inhaler","Pulmonary rehabilitation","Smoking cessation","Triple inhaled therapy with a corticosteroid and two long-acting bronchodilators"],"a":3,"x":["Vaccination reduces exacerbations and is recommended, but it does not match the effect of stopping smoking.","Bronchodilators relieve symptoms and reduce exacerbations. Their effect on mortality is unclear.","Rehabilitation improves exercise capacity and quality of life. It is recommended for symptomatic patients.","Correct. Smoking cessation is the single most important intervention. It limits lung damage and reduces deaths.","Triple therapy is for exacerbations despite dual bronchodilators. He has mild disease with none."],"e":"Problem: a newly diagnosed heavy smoker asking what matters most. Task: rank the interventions. His dependence is high, so the plan is counselling with a nicotine patch plus a fast-acting form, or varenicline. What if he had already quit and had resting hypoxaemia? Long-term oxygen would be the intervention that improves survival.","k":"Smoking cessation is the most important intervention in COPD.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)"],"c":["rs8-11","rs8-12","rs8-13","rs8-14","rs8-15"],"id":"rs-a48","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#severity","s":"Patricia Nolan, aged 64 years, has COPD confirmed three years ago. She returns for results of her annual spirometry, performed when she was well, eight weeks after her last chest infection. She becomes breathless walking on level ground at her own pace and had two exacerbations this year, each treated with prednisolone. She stopped smoking two years ago.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium-olodaterol, two puffs daily","Salbutamol as needed","Sertraline 50 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 94% on room air","Reduced breath sounds"]}],"tb":{"h":["Post-bronchodilator spirometry","Result","Percent predicted"],"r":[["FEV1","1.05 L","47%"],["FVC","2.60 L","88%"],["FEV1/FVC","0.40",""]]},"q":"How is the severity of her COPD BEST classified?","o":["Mild","Moderate","Severe","Unable to be graded, because the test was done after an exacerbation","Very severe, with a need for home oxygen"],"a":1,"x":["Mild COPD has an FEV1 of 60 to 80% predicted and breathlessness only on moderate exertion.","Correct. An FEV1 of 40 to 59% predicted, breathlessness on the flat and exacerbations needing corticosteroids fit moderate COPD.","Severe COPD has an FEV1 under 40% predicted and breathlessness on minimal exertion.","Grading waits at least four weeks after an exacerbation. She was tested at eight weeks.","Home oxygen is decided by blood gases, not by FEV1. Her saturation is 94%."],"e":"Problem: an annual spirometry result with a symptom and exacerbation history. Task: grade severity. All three strands agree: FEV1 47%, breathless on the flat, exacerbations needing prednisolone. What if her FEV1 were 36% predicted? Severe, with review at least twice a year.","k":"COPD severity by FEV1: mild 60 to 80%, moderate 40 to 59%, severe under 40%.","src":["Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"c":["rs8-07","rs8-08","rs8-09","rs8-10","rs8-01"],"id":"rs-a49","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"COPD: long-term management","nt":"rs-copd#refer","n":4,"s":"You are reviewing the care of Ronald Hughes, aged 70 years, who has moderate COPD and attends once a year. He stopped smoking three years ago.","q":"Which FOUR of the following should be part of his annual COPD review? Select four.","o":["Bronchial provocation test","Check of inhaler technique and adherence","CT scan of the chest","Review and update of his written action plan","Serum theophylline level","Spirometry","Vaccination status, including influenza and pneumococcal"],"sc":[0,1,0,1,0,1,1],"x":["Scores 0. This is a test for uncertain asthma.","Scores 1. Most people use their device incorrectly, and this is checked at every visit.","Scores 0. CT is not a routine monitoring test.","Scores 1. Every person with COPD has a written plan, reviewed at least yearly.","Scores 0. Theophylline is not recommended for COPD.","Scores 1. Spirometry is repeated every year in everyone with COPD.","Scores 1. Influenza each year, pneumococcal and COVID-19."],"e":"The yearly review covers smoking, symptoms, exacerbations, technique, adherence, the action plan, vaccination, mood and weight, with spirometry each year. In severe disease the review is twice a year and adds oxygen saturation, signs of right heart failure and advance care planning.","k":"Annual COPD review: spirometry, technique and adherence, action plan, vaccination.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Stable chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)"],"c":["rs8-30","rs8-31","rs8-01","rs8-02","rs8-03"],"id":"rs-k08","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","s":"Dennis Clarke, aged 68 years, has moderate COPD. For two days he has been more breathless than usual and has used his reliever five or six times a day. His cough is unchanged, and he brings up a small amount of white sputum each morning, as he always does. He has had no fever. His granddaughter, who stayed last weekend, had a cold. He is eating, sleeping and walking around the house.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Umeclidinium-vilanterol, one inhalation daily","Salbutamol as needed","Rosuvastatin 10 mg daily","Aspirin 100 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 36.9 degrees, respiratory rate 20/min, heart rate 88/min","SpO2 93% on room air (usually 94%)","Scattered wheeze, no crackles","No ankle oedema"]}],"q":"In addition to regular salbutamol through a spacer, which treatment is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours for 5 days","Amoxicillin with clavulanate 875+125 mg twice daily for 7 days, to cover resistant organisms","Doxycycline 100 mg daily for 5 days and prednisolone 50 mg daily for 5 days","Prednisolone 30 to 50 mg daily for 5 days","Roxithromycin 300 mg daily for 5 days"],"a":3,"x":["Antibiotics are considered when sputum becomes more purulent together with more volume or more breathlessness. His sputum has not changed.","This is not a first-line antibiotic for an exacerbation, and he does not meet the criteria for any antibiotic.","The prednisolone is right. The antibiotic is not indicated without a change in sputum purulence.","Correct. An exacerbation not settling with bronchodilator alone is treated with a 5-day course of prednisolone. A likely viral trigger with unchanged sputum does not need an antibiotic.","Macrolides are not recommended as initial antibiotic treatment, and no antibiotic is indicated here."],"e":"Problem: more breathlessness and reliever use, with unchanged white sputum after contact with a cold. Task: decide what to add. Purulence is the trigger for antibiotics, and it is absent. What if his sputum had turned green and doubled in volume? Add amoxicillin or doxycycline for 5 days.","k":"COPD exacerbation: prednisolone if bronchodilator is not enough; antibiotics only with more purulent sputum plus more volume or dyspnoea.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-08","rs9-09","rs9-10","rs9-11","rs9-12"],"id":"rs-a50","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#abx","s":"Beverley Hart, aged 72 years, has moderate COPD with an FEV1 of 52% predicted. For three days her sputum has changed from clear to thick and green, there is about twice as much of it, and she is more breathless. She has had one exacerbation in the past two years and no hospital admissions. She has no drug allergies. She is managing at home and her husband is with her.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium 18 micrograms daily","Salbutamol as needed, now every 3 to 4 hours","Prednisolone 37.5 mg daily, started yesterday under her action plan","Atenolol 25 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.6 degrees, respiratory rate 22/min","SpO2 92% on room air","Coarse crackles that clear with coughing, scattered wheeze"]}],"q":"Which antibiotic regimen is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours for 7 days","Amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours for 5 days","Amoxicillin with clavulanate 875+125 mg every 12 hours for 10 days","Azithromycin 500 mg daily for 3 days","Cefalexin 500 mg every 6 hours for 5 days"],"a":1,"x":["One gram every 8 hours is the dose for pneumonia. Exacerbation doses are lower, and the course is 5 days.","Correct. More purulent sputum with more volume and more breathlessness meets the criteria. Amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours, or doxycycline 100 mg daily, for 5 days.","This is reserved for severe COPD that has not responded at 48 to 72 hours, and then for 5 days.","Macrolides are not recommended for initial treatment of an exacerbation.","Cephalosporins are not recommended for initial treatment. They are no more effective and are broader than needed."],"e":"Problem: an exacerbation with purulent sputum, more of it, and more breathlessness. Task: choose the drug, dose and duration. Crackles that clear with coughing argue against pneumonia. What if the crackles persisted with rigors and a fast pulse? Chest X-ray, and treat as pneumonia if confirmed.","k":"COPD exacerbation antibiotic: amoxicillin 500 mg 8-hourly or doxycycline 100 mg daily, for 5 days.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-08","rs9-09","rs9-10","rs9-11","rs9-12"],"id":"rs-a51","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#oxygen","s":"Leonard Marsh, aged 76 years, has severe COPD and was found breathless at home by his daughter. Paramedics gave oxygen at 8 L/min by mask during the 30-minute trip to your rural hospital. On arrival he is drowsy, rousing to voice, with a flushed face and a bounding pulse. His usual saturation at the practice is 91%. He has not been on home oxygen.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone furoate-umeclidinium-vilanterol, one inhalation daily","Salbutamol as needed","Furosemide 40 mg daily","Apixaban 5 mg twice daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 12/min, heart rate 104/min","SpO2 99% on oxygen 8 L/min by mask","Widespread wheeze, poor air entry"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Continue oxygen at 8 L/min, as his saturation is now satisfactory","Give oxygen-driven nebulised salbutamol","Increase oxygen to 15 L/min by non-rebreather mask until he is more alert","Reduce oxygen and titrate to a saturation of 88 to 92%","Remove oxygen completely and observe on room air for 30 minutes"],"a":3,"x":["A saturation of 99% in a drowsy patient with severe COPD is the problem. Excess oxygen is driving carbon dioxide retention.","Nebulisers in COPD are driven by air. An oxygen-driven nebuliser adds to the over-oxygenation.","More oxygen will worsen carbon dioxide retention. His drowsiness is from carbon dioxide, not low oxygen.","Correct. In a COPD exacerbation oxygen is titrated to 88 to 92%. Excess oxygen raises carbon dioxide and increases deaths.","Stopping oxygen abruptly risks severe hypoxaemia. The dose is reduced to the target range, not removed."],"e":"Problem: drowsiness with a saturation of 99% on high-flow oxygen in severe COPD. Task: recognise oxygen-induced carbon dioxide retention and correct it. The 'good' saturation is the trap. What if his arterial gas then showed a PaCO2 of 68 mmHg and a pH of 7.28? Non-invasive ventilation.","k":"COPD exacerbation: titrate oxygen to 88 to 92%. A high saturation in a drowsy patient means too much oxygen.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-21","rs9-22","rs9-01","rs9-02","rs9-03"],"id":"rs-a52","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#slow","s":"Joyce Palmer, aged 69 years, has mild COPD with an FEV1 of 68% predicted. Four days ago she started amoxicillin and prednisolone under her action plan for an exacerbation with green sputum. She returns because she 'still has a cough'. Her sputum is now pale yellow and less in volume, her breathlessness is improving, and she slept through last night. She has had no fever. She asks for 'a stronger antibiotic'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours, day 4","Prednisolone 37.5 mg daily, day 4","Tiotropium 18 micrograms daily","Salbutamol as needed, now twice a day"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 36.8 degrees, respiratory rate 16/min","SpO2 95% on room air","Chest: a few scattered wheezes, no crackles"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add doxycycline to the amoxicillin","Change to amoxicillin with clavulanate for a further 7 days","Change to ciprofloxacin to cover Pseudomonas","Complete the 5-day courses and explain that symptoms can last 7 to 10 days","Send sputum for culture and continue amoxicillin until the result is available"],"a":3,"x":["Combining two first-line antibiotics has no role.","Broader treatment is considered only in severe COPD with no response at 48 to 72 hours. She has mild disease and is improving.","Ciprofloxacin is for people with Pseudomonas in recent sputum who are not responding.","Correct. She is improving. In mild COPD the symptoms of an exacerbation commonly last 7 to 10 days, and no change of treatment is needed.","Sputum culture is not routine, and she has none of the indications for it. Therapy is not modified when symptoms are improving."],"e":"Problem: a lingering cough on day 4 in someone who is clearly getting better. Task: resist escalation. Every marker has moved the right way. What if she had severe COPD, was worse at 72 hours, and a bacterial cause still seemed likely? Then amoxicillin with clavulanate for 5 days.","k":"A slow-to-settle COPD exacerbation rarely needs a broader antibiotic. Symptoms last 7 to 10 days in mild disease.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-07","rs9-16","rs9-17","rs9-18","rs9-19"],"id":"rs-a53","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#treat","s":"Harold Benson, aged 73 years, has moderate COPD. He attends your practice with a day of worse breathlessness and wheeze. He left his spacer at his daughter's house. The practice nurse suggests a salbutamol nebuliser and asks how you would like it set up. The treatment room has piped oxygen and a portable air compressor. His usual saturation is 93%.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium-olodaterol, two puffs daily","Salbutamol as needed","Lercanidipine 10 mg daily","Metformin 500 mg twice daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, speaking in sentences","Respiratory rate 22/min, heart rate 96/min","SpO2 91% on room air","Widespread wheeze"]}],"q":"How should the nebuliser be given?","o":["Ipratropium 500 micrograms, driven by oxygen at 6 L/min","Salbutamol 10 mg continuously, driven by oxygen","Salbutamol 5 mg with ipratropium 500 micrograms, driven by oxygen at 8 L/min, repeated every 20 minutes","Salbutamol 5 mg, driven by air from the compressor","Salbutamol 5 mg, driven by oxygen at 8 L/min"],"a":3,"x":["Ipratropium is not given to people taking a long-acting muscarinic antagonist, and the nebuliser should not be driven by oxygen.","Continuous high-dose nebulisation is for life-threatening asthma.","This is the severe asthma regimen. He takes a LAMA, so ipratropium is avoided, and oxygen should not drive the nebuliser.","Correct. If a nebuliser is needed in COPD, it is driven by compressed air, to avoid dangerous over-oxygenation. Salbutamol 2.5 to 5 mg is the dose.","The dose is right. The driving gas is wrong: 8 L/min of oxygen can cause carbon dioxide retention."],"e":"Problem: a nebuliser is needed for a COPD exacerbation in a patient on a LAMA. Task: choose drug and driving gas. Two rules apply: air, not oxygen, and no ipratropium with a LAMA. What if his saturation were 85%? Give controlled oxygen by nasal prongs alongside the air-driven nebuliser, aiming for 88 to 92%.","k":"Nebulisers in COPD are driven by air. Ipratropium is not used with a LAMA.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-01","rs9-02","rs9-03","rs9-04","rs9-05"],"id":"rs-a54","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#hospital","s":"Elsie Thornton, aged 81 years, has severe COPD and lives alone in a retirement unit. Her neighbour has called you to a home visit. Over two days Elsie has become too breathless to walk from her bedroom to the kitchen, which she normally manages with a frame. She has not eaten since yesterday. She started prednisolone and salbutamol yesterday under her action plan. She has no advance care directive and says she wants 'whatever will help'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide-glycopyrronium-formoterol, two puffs twice daily","Salbutamol 8 puffs by spacer, every 2 hours since last night","Prednisolone 37.5 mg daily, day 2","Warfarin as charted"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, speaking in short phrases","Respiratory rate 26/min, heart rate 108/min, irregular","SpO2 87% on room air","New pitting oedema to both knees"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add amoxicillin and review tomorrow","Arrange a chest X-ray and blood tests for tomorrow morning, then decide","Arrange transfer to hospital by ambulance now","Increase prednisolone to 75 mg daily and salbutamol to hourly","Organise a home oxygen concentrator for delivery this afternoon"],"a":2,"x":["She may need an antibiotic, but she has failed treatment at home and has several indications for admission.","Waiting a day for tests leaves an unstable woman alone overnight.","Correct. No response to treatment at home, unable to walk between rooms or eat, low saturation, new oedema, a new irregular pulse and living alone are each indications for hospital.","Higher doses do not address the failure of home treatment, and hourly salbutamol with an irregular pulse adds risk.","Home oxygen is prescribed on blood gases when stable, not started during an exacerbation."],"e":"Problem: an exacerbation that has not responded to a day of correct home treatment. Task: recognise the indications for admission. There are six in the stem. Her stated wish for treatment matters too. What if she had an advance care plan declining hospital? Her preference would guide care at home, with palliative support.","k":"COPD exacerbation: hospital if not responding, cannot walk between rooms or eat, hypoxic, confused, new oedema or arrhythmia.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), COPD exacerbations","Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)"],"c":["rs9-20","rs9-01","rs9-02","rs9-03","rs9-04"],"id":"rs-a55","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"COPD: exacerbations","nt":"rs-copd-exac#after","n":4,"s":"Albert Dimitriou, aged 70 years, was discharged four days ago after his first hospital admission for a COPD exacerbation. He attends your practice with his wife. He has stopped smoking since the admission.","q":"Which FOUR of the following should be addressed at this visit or arranged from it? Select four.","o":["A check of his inhaler technique and adherence","A discussion about advance care planning","A long course of oral prednisolone to prevent relapse","A referral to pulmonary rehabilitation","A review and update of his written action plan","A routine sputum culture","Prophylactic doxycycline through winter"],"sc":[1,1,0,1,1,0,0],"x":["Scores 1. Technique and adherence are reviewed after every exacerbation, before changing treatment.","Scores 1. A hospital admission is a sentinel event that should prompt this discussion.","Scores 0. The course is 5 days. Long-term oral corticosteroids cause harm without benefit.","Scores 1. Rehabilitation after an exacerbation reduces readmission and can start within 2 to 4 weeks of discharge.","Scores 1. The plan is updated and its use is checked.","Scores 0. Sputum culture is not routine.","Scores 0. There is no evidence for doxycycline as prophylaxis. A long-term macrolide is a specialist decision for frequent exacerbations."],"e":"The post-discharge visit happens within 7 days. It covers technique, adherence, whether maintenance treatment should step up, the action plan, rehabilitation, vaccination, smoking and advance care planning.","k":"After a COPD admission: review within 7 days, technique, action plan, pulmonary rehabilitation, advance care planning.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), COPD exacerbations","Lung Foundation Australia. COPD-X Handbook (2024)","Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of COPD exacerbations"],"c":["rs9-23","rs9-24","rs9-25","rs9-26","rs9-01"],"id":"rs-k09","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#features","s":"Tina Marshall, aged 36 years, is a retail manager with five days of cough that began with a sore throat and runny nose. For two days she has coughed up yellow-green sputum and her chest feels sore when she coughs. She has had no fever at home and is still going to work. She does not smoke and has no medical conditions. She asks for antibiotics because 'it has gone to my chest'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Combined oral contraceptive pill","Paracetamol and throat lozenges this week"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.2 degrees","Respiratory rate 14/min, heart rate 76/min","SpO2 98% on room air","Chest: a few scattered wheezes that clear with coughing, no focal signs"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a chest X-ray today and start amoxicillin while waiting for the radiology result","Explain that this is acute bronchitis and that antibiotics will not help","Prescribe amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours for 5 days","Prescribe doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 days","Send sputum for culture and prescribe according to the result"],"a":1,"x":["Her observations and chest are normal. Imaging is for an unclear diagnosis, and antibiotics should not be started for a presumed viral illness.","Correct. Coloured sputum with normal temperature, pulse, breathing rate and saturation, and no focal signs, is acute bronchitis. It resolves without antibiotics.","This is the right regimen for low-severity pneumonia. She has no features of pneumonia.","This is the alternative for pneumonia with penicillin allergy or a suspected atypical organism. Neither applies.","Sputum culture has no role in acute bronchitis."],"e":"Problem: an acute cough with coloured sputum in a well young adult. Task: tell bronchitis from pneumonia. Sputum colour is the distractor. The deciding facts are the normal observations and the absence of focal signs. What if her temperature were 38.6 with a pulse of 108 and crackles at the right base? Pneumonia: assess severity and treat.","k":"Coloured sputum with normal observations and no focal signs is acute bronchitis: no antibiotics.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-17","rs10-18","rs10-19","rs10-01","rs10-02"],"id":"rs-a56","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#treat","s":"Andrew Phillips, aged 45 years, is a plumber with three days of fever, rigors and a cough productive of rust-coloured sputum, and pain on the right side of his chest when he breathes in. He lives with his wife, is eating and drinking, and has no medical conditions or drug allergies. He does not smoke. A chest X-ray this morning shows consolidation in the right lower lobe only.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Ibuprofen 400 mg as needed this week","No regular medicines"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 38.7 degrees, alert and oriented","Respiratory rate 20/min, heart rate 96/min, blood pressure 124/78 mmHg","SpO2 95% on room air","Bronchial breathing and crackles at the right base"]}],"q":"Which treatment is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 1 g orally every 8 hours for 5 days","Amoxicillin 1 g orally every 8 hours plus doxycycline 100 mg orally every 12 hours for 7 days","Amoxicillin 500 mg orally every 8 hours for 5 days","Amoxicillin with clavulanate 875+125 mg orally every 12 hours for 10 days","Roxithromycin 300 mg orally daily for 7 days"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. He has none of the features that suggest admission, so this is low-severity pneumonia. Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours for 5 days is the drug of choice.","Two drugs from the start are for people with significant comorbidity whose follow-up is uncertain. He has neither, and the course is 5 days.","This is the dose for a COPD exacerbation. Pneumonia needs 1 g.","Broader cover is not needed for low-severity pneumonia, and 10 days is twice the usual course.","Macrolide resistance in pneumococcus is rising, and roxithromycin is not the recommended agent."],"e":"Problem: lobar pneumonia in a fit 45-year-old with reassuring observations. Task: grade severity and choose the regimen. Check the list: respiratory rate under 22, pulse not over 100, systolic not under 90, no confusion, saturation not under 92%, one lobe. Low severity. What if he had had hives with amoxicillin? Doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 days.","k":"Low-severity pneumonia: amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours for 5 days.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-01","rs10-02","rs10-04","rs10-05","rs10-06"],"id":"rs-a57","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#severity","s":"Dorothy Evans, aged 79 years, lives alone in her own home. Her daughter brings her in because she has been 'muddled' since yesterday, calling her by her sister's name, and has a new cough. She has not eaten today. She has type 2 diabetes and hypertension and normally manages her own medicines and shopping. A chest X-ray taken on the way shows left lower lobe consolidation.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Metformin 1 g twice daily","Perindopril 5 mg daily","Atorvastatin 20 mg daily","Temazepam 10 mg at night"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.9 degrees; disoriented to day and place","Respiratory rate 24/min, heart rate 104/min, blood pressure 118/70 mmHg","SpO2 93% on room air","Crackles at the left base"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange admission to hospital for monitoring and treatment","Prescribe amoxicillin and ask her daughter to phone tomorrow with an update on how she is going","Prescribe amoxicillin with doxycycline and review at home in three days","Prescribe doxycycline alone and arrange a repeat chest X-ray in six weeks","Stop temazepam and review in the morning"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. New confusion, a respiratory rate of 24 and a pulse of 104 are each features that may need admission. She also lives alone and is not eating.","A phone call tomorrow is not enough observation for moderate-severity pneumonia in a confused 79-year-old who lives alone.","Adding a second antibiotic does not replace the monitoring she needs. Admission features call for support, not broader cover.","This would under-treat and under-observe her.","Sedatives are worth reviewing after recovery. Her confusion is new and is explained by the pneumonia."],"e":"Problem: pneumonia in an older woman whose main symptom is confusion. Task: grade severity and choose the place of care. Three admission features are present, and her circumstances add weight. What if she were lucid, with a respiratory rate of 18, a pulse of 88, and her daughter staying with her? Low severity: amoxicillin at home with review.","k":"Pneumonia with any of RR 22 or more, HR over 100, SBP under 90, new confusion or SpO2 under 92%: consider admission.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-10","rs10-11","rs10-12","rs10-13","rs10-14"],"id":"rs-a58","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#review","s":"Marcus Lee, aged 52 years, is an office worker treated for right middle lobe pneumonia with amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours. He finished the 5-day course eleven days ago. His fever settled on the second day and his appetite is back. He returns because he is still coughing, mostly dry, gets tired by mid-afternoon and is a little puffed on the stairs. He has never smoked. He asks whether he needs another course of antibiotics.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amoxicillin (completed eleven days ago)","Esomeprazole 20 mg daily","Fexofenadine as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 36.7 degrees","Respiratory rate 14/min, heart rate 72/min","SpO2 98% on room air","Chest: a few crackles at the right base, no dullness"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a CT pulmonary angiogram today","Prescribe a 7-day course of amoxicillin with clavulanate, as the first antibiotic was too narrow","Prescribe a second 5-day course of amoxicillin","Prescribe prednisolone 25 mg daily for 5 days","Reassure him that cough and fatigue can last weeks, and arrange follow-up"],"a":4,"x":["He has no pleuritic pain, tachycardia or hypoxia. Nothing suggests a pulmonary embolism.","He responded to amoxicillin: the fever settled and he is recovering. A broader antibiotic is not indicated.","Prolonged symptoms after a good response are not a reason to extend or repeat antibiotics.","Corticosteroids have no role in post-pneumonia cough.","Correct. Cough, breathlessness and sputum can take several weeks to settle, and fatigue can last months. He is afebrile with normal observations."],"e":"Problem: lingering cough and tiredness eleven days after a successful course. Task: separate slow recovery from treatment failure. His normal observations and settled fever are the deciding facts. What if his fever had returned with pleuritic pain and dullness at the base? Look for an effusion or empyema.","k":"After pneumonia, cough can last weeks and fatigue months. That is not a reason for more antibiotics.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-03","rs10-23","rs10-24","rs10-25","rs10-01"],"id":"rs-a59","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#treat","s":"Rachel Stein, aged 29 years, is a primary school teacher with a week of dry cough, headache, low-grade fever and tiredness. Several children in her class have been diagnosed with Mycoplasma infection this term. She had facial swelling and wheeze with amoxicillin as a teenager. A chest X-ray shows patchy change at the left base. She is not pregnant, and a pregnancy test today is negative.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Levonorgestrel intrauterine device","Paracetamol as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.9 degrees, alert","Respiratory rate 16/min, heart rate 88/min, blood pressure 112/70 mmHg","SpO2 97% on room air","Fine crackles at the left base"]}],"q":"Which antibiotic is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours for 5 days","Cefalexin 500 mg every 6 hours for 5 days","Cefuroxime 500 mg every 12 hours for 7 days, as she has only had a reaction to amoxicillin","Ciprofloxacin 500 mg every 12 hours for 5 days","Doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 days"],"a":4,"x":["She has had a severe immediate reaction to amoxicillin, and beta-lactams do not treat Mycoplasma.","A cephalosporin is avoided after a severe immediate penicillin reaction, and it has no activity against atypical organisms.","Cefuroxime is an option after a non-severe reaction, as part of combination therapy. Hers was severe, and it would not cover Mycoplasma.","Ciprofloxacin is not a recommended treatment for community-acquired pneumonia.","Correct. Low-severity pneumonia with a suspected atypical organism, or with penicillin hypersensitivity, is treated with doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 days."],"e":"Problem: low-severity pneumonia with two reasons to avoid amoxicillin: a likely atypical organism and a severe penicillin reaction. Task: choose the alternative. Either reason alone leads to the same answer. What if she had no allergy and no Mycoplasma contact? Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours.","k":"Low-severity pneumonia with penicillin allergy or a suspected atypical organism: doxycycline 100 mg twice daily for 5 days.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-01","rs10-02","rs10-04","rs10-05","rs10-06"],"id":"rs-a60","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#notbetter","s":"Peter Vasquez, aged 63 years, is a retired fisherman with heavy alcohol use and poor dentition. He was started on amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours six days ago for right lower lobe pneumonia, and has taken every dose. He still has fevers each evening and a heavy ache on the right side of his chest. His cough now brings up foul-tasting sputum. He stopped smoking ten years ago.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours, day 6","Thiamine 100 mg daily","Pantoprazole 40 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 38.4 degrees","Respiratory rate 20/min, heart rate 98/min, blood pressure 126/74 mmHg","SpO2 94% on room air","Dull percussion note and absent breath sounds at the right base"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add doxycycline and review in one week","Arrange a repeat chest X-ray and urgent hospital assessment for a complication","Continue amoxicillin for a further 5 days and reassure him","Stop the antibiotic, as the persistent fever is likely to be a drug reaction to amoxicillin","Switch to roxithromycin for atypical cover"],"a":1,"x":["Adding atypical cover does not address a likely collection, and a week is too long to wait.","Correct. Fever and chest discomfort that persist on appropriate treatment, with a dull base and foul sputum, suggest empyema or lung abscess. He needs imaging and hospital assessment.","He is not clinically stable at day 6, which carries higher risk. More of the same is not the answer.","Drug fever is possible in principle, but new dullness, absent breath sounds and foul sputum point to a complication.","Atypical organisms would not explain the examination findings."],"e":"Problem: no response to six days of correct treatment. Task: reassess the diagnosis instead of extending the antibiotic. The dull base, foul sputum, alcohol use and poor teeth point to an anaerobic complication. What if he were afebrile and simply still coughing? Slow recovery, with no change needed.","k":"Pneumonia with persistent fever and chest discomfort on treatment: look for effusion, empyema or abscess.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-26","rs10-27","rs10-28","rs10-01","rs10-02"],"id":"rs-a61","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Pneumonia in adults","nt":"rs-cap#prevent","n":4,"s":"Gwen Holloway, aged 71 years, has recovered from her second episode of community-acquired pneumonia in 18 months. She smokes five cigarettes a day and takes temazepam nightly and esomeprazole, which was started for reflux four years ago and has not been reviewed.","q":"Which FOUR of the following measures would help to prevent a further episode? Select four.","o":["A daily low-dose antibiotic through winter","Advice and support to stop smoking","Pneumococcal vaccination, if not up to date","Review of the ongoing need for esomeprazole","Review of the ongoing need for temazepam","Routine chest X-ray every six months","Vitamin C 1 g daily"],"sc":[0,1,1,1,1,0,0],"x":["Scores 0. Prophylactic antibiotics are not a preventive strategy for community-acquired pneumonia.","Scores 1. Stopping smoking is a listed preventive measure.","Scores 1. Pneumococcal, influenza and COVID-19 vaccination are all reviewed.","Scores 1. Proton pump inhibitors are associated with a higher incidence of pneumonia.","Scores 1. Benzodiazepines and other sedatives are associated with a higher incidence of pneumonia.","Scores 0. Surveillance imaging does not prevent pneumonia.","Scores 0. Not a recommended preventive measure."],"e":"Prevention after pneumonia is mostly a review of what is already there: vaccines, smoking, and medicines that raise risk. Aspiration risk, exercise and nutrition are on the same list.","k":"Prevent pneumonia: vaccinate, stop smoking, and review proton pump inhibitors and sedatives.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in adults"],"c":["rs10-29","rs10-30","rs10-01","rs10-02","rs10-03"],"id":"rs-k10","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","nt":"rs-cough#bronchitis","s":"Simon Hartley, aged 41 years, is an accountant who caught a cold from his children five weeks ago. The sore throat and runny nose cleared in a week, but he still coughs through the day, bringing up a little clear sputum. The cough is slowly improving. He sleeps through the night, has had no fever, weight loss or breathlessness, and has never smoked. He is worried that it has 'gone on too long'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Rosuvastatin 5 mg daily","A pharmacy cough mixture, without benefit"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 36.6 degrees","Respiratory rate 14/min, heart rate 70/min","SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a chest X-ray and spirometry","Explain that the cough can last up to 8 weeks, and review if it has not settled by then","Prescribe a 5-day course of amoxicillin","Prescribe a salbutamol inhaler for use four times a day until the cough has completely settled","Start a trial of a proton pump inhibitor"],"a":1,"x":["These are the tests for chronic cough, which means more than 8 weeks. He is at 5 weeks, improving, with no red flags.","Correct. After acute bronchitis about 1 in 10 people cough for up to 8 weeks while airway inflammation settles. He is improving and has a normal examination.","Antibiotics do not help acute bronchitis or a post-viral cough.","Beta agonists are not recommended in acute bronchitis without airflow limitation.","A proton pump inhibitor is used for chronic cough only when there are reflux symptoms."],"e":"Problem: a subacute cough after a viral infection, improving, in a well non-smoker. Task: reassure with accurate timeframes and set a review point. What if he were a smoker with hoarseness? Those are red flags: investigate now and refer.","k":"After acute bronchitis, cough usually clears by 3 to 4 weeks and can take up to 8.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Acute bronchitis","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in adults"],"c":["rs11-04","rs11-05","rs11-06","rs11-07","rs11-08"],"id":"rs-a62","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","nt":"rs-cough#chronic","s":"Angela Rossi, aged 47 years, is a florist with a dry cough for three months. It is worse when she talks on the phone and in cold air. She has no heartburn, no nasal symptoms, no wheeze and no breathlessness. She has never smoked, has lost no weight and has not travelled or lived overseas. She takes no blood pressure medicines. Her examination is normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Levothyroxine 100 micrograms daily","Vitamin D 1000 units daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear, throat normal","No lymphadenopathy"]}],"q":"Which investigations are MOST appropriate at this visit?","o":["24-hour oesophageal pH monitoring","Chest X-ray and spirometry","CT scan of chest","Nasendoscopy and bronchoscopy, by referral to a respiratory physician and an ear, nose and throat surgeon","Sputum culture and pertussis serology"],"a":1,"x":["She has no reflux symptoms, and this is not a first-line test for cough.","Correct. Every adult with a cough lasting more than 8 weeks has a chest X-ray and spirometry, with a blood eosinophil count and FeNO where available.","CT is not a routine first investigation for chronic cough.","Specialist procedures follow if first-line assessment finds no cause or treatment fails.","She produces no sputum, and these tests are not part of the routine work-up."],"e":"Problem: three months of dry cough with no pointers on history or examination. Task: know the baseline work-up. Triggers such as talking and cold air suggest an oversensitive cough reflex, but the baseline tests come first. What if both tests were normal and the cough continued? Referral to a respiratory physician or cough clinic, with speech therapy.","k":"Chronic cough in an adult: chest X-ray and spirometry for everyone.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in adults"],"c":["rs11-15","rs11-01","rs11-02","rs11-03","rs11-04"],"id":"rs-a63","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","nt":"rs-cough#causes","s":"Frank Mills, aged 66 years, has had a dry, tickly cough for four months that keeps his wife awake. He started perindopril ten months ago after a heart attack. He has no heartburn, wheeze or breathlessness, stopped smoking 20 years ago, and his weight is stable. A chest X-ray and spirometry last week were normal. His blood pressure is well controlled and his echocardiogram showed mildly reduced left ventricular function.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Perindopril 5 mg daily","Bisoprolol 2.5 mg daily","Aspirin 100 mg daily","Atorvastatin 80 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Blood pressure 122/74 mmHg, heart rate 62/min","SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear, no oedema"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add a proton pump inhibitor for 8 weeks","Continue perindopril, as the cough began six months after it was started and so is unlikely to be related","Prescribe a low-dose inhaled corticosteroid","Replace perindopril with an angiotensin receptor blocker","Stop bisoprolol"],"a":3,"x":["Without reflux symptoms a proton pump inhibitor is not recommended for chronic cough.","The timing between starting an ACE inhibitor and the onset of cough is often loose. A delay does not exclude it.","He has no features of asthma, and cough alone is not treated as asthma.","Correct. ACE inhibitors are the commonest drug cause of chronic cough. He still needs the drug class after his heart attack, so an angiotensin receptor blocker replaces it.","Beta blockers are not a recognised cause of isolated dry cough here, and he needs it after his heart attack."],"e":"Problem: a dry chronic cough with normal baseline tests in a man on an ACE inhibitor. Task: act on the commonest drug cause. The six-month gap is the distractor. What if the cough persisted three months after the switch? Look again at the other causes and consider referral.","k":"Bothersome chronic cough on an ACE inhibitor: stop it, and use an ARB if the indication remains.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in adults"],"c":["rs11-16","rs11-17","rs11-18","rs11-19","rs11-20"],"id":"rs-a64","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","nt":"rs-cough#redflags","s":"Carol Jennings, aged 58 years, is a bar manager who has smoked 20 cigarettes a day since her twenties. She has had a 'smoker's cough' for years, but over the past two months it has become harsher and more frequent, and her voice has been hoarse for six weeks. She has lost 3 kg without trying. She has had no fever. She asks for a cough mixture to get her through work.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amlodipine 5 mg daily","Nicotine lozenges, used occasionally"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 96% on room air","Hoarse voice","Chest: scattered wheeze, no focal signs","No palpable neck nodes"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a chest X-ray and refer urgently for specialist assessment","Prescribe a course of amoxicillin and review in two weeks","Recommend voice rest, steam inhalation and honey, and review her in six weeks if the hoarseness persists","Start a combination inhaler for presumed COPD","Start a proton pump inhibitor for laryngopharyngeal reflux"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. A changed cough in a smoker over 50 with hoarseness and weight loss suggests lung or laryngeal cancer. Urgent referral is needed.","There is no fever or purulent sputum, and antibiotics would delay the diagnosis.","Six more weeks of observation is unsafe with several red flags present.","She may well have COPD, and spirometry is needed, but red flags come first.","Reflux can cause hoarseness, but not weight loss, and treating it first delays investigation."],"e":"Problem: a long-standing smoker whose cough has changed. Task: recognise red flags. There are four: smoking, hoarseness, weight loss and a changed cough after 50. What if her voice were normal, her weight stable and the cough unchanged for years? Spirometry for COPD, and help to stop smoking.","k":"Smoker over 50 with a new or changed cough or a hoarse voice: urgent referral.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in adults"],"c":["rs11-24","rs11-01","rs11-02","rs11-03","rs11-04"],"id":"rs-a65","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","nt":"rs-cough#causes","s":"Yusuf Demir, aged 39 years, is a chef with a cough for four months. It is mainly at night and after his evening meal. He gets heartburn several times a week and sometimes wakes with a choking feeling. He does not smoke and takes no regular medicines. He has no wheeze or nasal symptoms. A chest X-ray and spirometry are normal. He eats late after his shift and drinks four coffees a day.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Antacid tablets most nights"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["BMI 31 kg/m2","Chest clear, SpO2 99% on room air"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Refer for gastroscopy before any treatment","Refer to a speech pathologist for laryngeal retraining","Start a proton pump inhibitor and continue it for 12 months before assessing whether the cough has responded","Start a proton pump inhibitor with lifestyle advice, and review in 8 to 12 weeks","Start an inhaled corticosteroid and review in 2 to 4 weeks"],"a":3,"x":["He has typical symptoms and no alarm features. An empirical trial is appropriate first.","Speech pathology is for laryngeal obstruction or refractory cough. His picture points to reflux.","The trial is reviewed at 8 to 12 weeks, and the drug is stopped if it has not helped.","Correct. Chronic cough with typical reflux symptoms is treated with an empirical proton pump inhibitor and lifestyle measures, reviewed at 8 to 12 weeks and stopped if there is no response.","He has no features of asthma. This is the trial for asthma-associated cough."],"e":"Problem: chronic cough with heartburn, night choking and worsening after meals. Task: match the trial to the features. What if he had the same cough with no reflux symptoms at all? No proton pump inhibitor: it is not recommended for cough alone.","k":"Chronic cough with reflux symptoms: PPI trial plus lifestyle change, review at 8 to 12 weeks, stop if no response.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in adults"],"c":["rs11-16","rs11-17","rs11-18","rs11-19","rs11-20"],"id":"rs-a66","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","nt":"rs-cough#otc","s":"Megan Fraser, aged 33 years, has had a cough for six days with a cold. She is otherwise well, with normal observations and a clear chest, and you have explained that this is a viral infection that needs no antibiotic. She is not pregnant. She asks what she can take for the cough, which is keeping her awake, and mentions that a friend gave her some codeine linctus.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Sertraline 50 mg daily","Paracetamol as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.1 degrees, SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"q":"Which advice about cough remedies is MOST appropriate?","o":["A sedating antihistamine at night will suppress the cough","Codeine linctus is the most effective cough suppressant and is appropriate for a few nights","Dextromethorphan is safe and reliably effective, including for people taking sertraline","Honey is safe and may help, and most cough medicines have little or no benefit","Salbutamol by inhaler will settle the cough"],"a":3,"x":["Antihistamines are no more effective than placebo for cough.","Codeine is no more effective than placebo for cough and carries adverse effects.","The evidence for dextromethorphan is conflicting, so it cannot be called reliably effective.","Correct. Over-the-counter cough medicines are not generally recommended. Honey has some evidence of benefit and is safe to try.","Beta agonists are not recommended for acute cough without airflow limitation."],"e":"Problem: a request for something to stop a viral cough. Task: give honest advice about what works. What if she insisted on a pharmacy product? Bromhexine may reduce cough frequency. Expectations should stay modest.","k":"For acute cough: honey may help; codeine and antihistamines are no better than placebo.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in adults"],"c":["rs11-13","rs11-14","rs11-01","rs11-02","rs11-03"],"id":"rs-a67","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Cough and acute bronchitis","nt":"rs-cough#redflags","n":4,"s":"Trevor Banks, aged 62 years, presents with a cough that has lasted three months.","q":"Which FOUR of the following features on history would be red flags requiring further investigation and early referral? Select four.","o":["Cough is worse after his evening meal","Difficulty swallowing, with occasional choking","He drinks three cups of coffee a day","Hoarse voice for the past month","Smoking history of 30 pack-years","The cough began after a cold","Unintentional weight loss of 5 kg"],"sc":[0,1,0,1,1,0,1],"x":["Scores 0. This suggests reflux-associated cough, which is treated empirically.","Scores 1. Swallowing difficulty, choking or vomiting is a red flag.","Scores 0. Not a red flag.","Scores 1. Hoarseness is a red flag for laryngeal or lung cancer.","Scores 1. A smoking history, including e-cigarettes, is a red flag.","Scores 0. A post-viral onset is common and usually benign.","Scores 1. Systemic symptoms such as weight loss or fever are red flags."],"e":"The red flags are coughing blood, a smoking history, prominent breathlessness, a lot of sputum, hoarseness, systemic symptoms, swallowing difficulty, recurrent infections, and abnormal chest signs or X-ray.","k":"Cough red flags: haemoptysis, smoking, hoarseness, weight loss, dysphagia, breathlessness at rest.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in adults"],"c":["rs11-24","rs11-01","rs11-02","rs11-03","rs11-04"],"id":"rs-k11","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronchadmit","s":"Henry Collins, aged 4 months, is brought in by his mother on the second day of a cough and runny nose. Since last night he has been breathing fast and has taken about a third of his usual breastfeeds, coming off the breast to catch his breath. He has had three wet nappies in 24 hours. He was born at term and is fully immunised for his age. His older sister has a cold.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert but tiring with feeds","Respiratory rate 62/min, heart rate 160/min, temperature 37.8 degrees","SpO2 93% on room air","Moderate subcostal and intercostal recession","Widespread fine crackles and wheeze"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange admission to hospital for observation","Give salbutamol 6 puffs through a spacer and mask, and review the response in 20 minutes","Prescribe amoxicillin and review in 48 hours","Prescribe prednisolone for 3 days","Send home with advice on small, frequent feeds and review tomorrow"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Feeding less than half of usual and moderate work of breathing are each indications for admission, and he is on day 2, when the illness is still worsening.","Bronchodilators are not recommended in bronchiolitis. They do not shorten the illness or reduce oxygen need.","Antibiotics are not routinely given. This is a viral illness.","Corticosteroids are not recommended in bronchiolitis.","This is right for mild bronchiolitis. His feeding and work of breathing place him beyond mild."],"e":"Problem: an infant with bronchiolitis on day 2. Task: decide home or hospital. The saturation of 93% is just above the threshold, which makes home tempting. Feeding and work of breathing decide it, and the peak is still ahead. What if he were feeding at three-quarters of normal with mild recession? Home, with small frequent feeds and review.","k":"Bronchiolitis: admit for feeding under half of usual, moderate work of breathing, apnoea, or saturation of 92% or less.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute bronchiolitis"],"c":["rs12-05","rs12-06","rs12-07","rs12-08","rs12-01"],"id":"rs-a68","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronchtreat","s":"Amelia Wright, aged 8 months, has had a runny nose for three days and a wheezy cough since yesterday. She is feeding at about 80% of normal and is playful between coughing bouts. Her father has asthma and asks whether she can have 'a puffer or some steroid syrup, like I get'. She was born at term, is growing well and has no other medical problems. No one smokes at home.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, smiling","Respiratory rate 44/min, SpO2 97% on room air","Mild subcostal recession","Scattered wheeze and crackles on both sides"]}],"q":"Which management is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin for 5 days","Nebulised adrenaline in the practice, then observation for 4 hours before deciding on discharge","Oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg daily for 3 days","Salbutamol through a spacer and mask, as needed","Supportive care at home, with small frequent feeds and advice on when to return"],"a":4,"x":["Antibiotics are not routinely given for a viral illness.","Adrenaline has no role in bronchiolitis outside arrest. This is the treatment for severe croup.","Corticosteroids are not recommended in bronchiolitis.","Bronchodilators are not recommended in bronchiolitis, whatever the family history.","Correct. Mild bronchiolitis is managed at home with small frequent feeds, minimal handling, and clear advice on when to return."],"e":"Problem: mild bronchiolitis and a parent who expects asthma treatment. Task: explain that the wheeze of bronchiolitis does not respond to asthma medicines. What if she were 3 years old with recurrent wheeze that cleared with salbutamol? That is preschool wheeze, and a reliever is appropriate.","k":"Bronchiolitis treatment is supportive: no bronchodilators, steroids or antibiotics.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute bronchiolitis"],"c":["rs12-09","rs12-10","rs12-11","rs12-01","rs12-02"],"id":"rs-a69","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","s":"Lily Nguyen, aged 2 years, woke at 2 am with a harsh, barking cough and a hoarse cry after a day of runny nose. Her parents brought her to your rural hospital, where you are the on-call doctor. She is sitting on her mother's lap watching a phone. There is no noise on breathing while she is settled, but a harsh sound on breathing in when she cries. She weighs 12 kg and is immunised.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, interactive","Respiratory rate 28/min, SpO2 98% on room air","No stridor at rest; mild suprasternal retraction","Temperature 37.9 degrees"]}],"q":"Which treatment is MOST appropriate?","o":["A single dose of oral prednisolone 12 mg","Amoxicillin 25 mg/kg three times daily","Nebulised adrenaline 5 mL of 1:1000, followed by oral dexamethasone and observation for 4 hours","Nebulised salbutamol 2.5 mg","Reassurance alone, with advice to use steam in the bathroom"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. This is mild croup: no stridor at rest. Every child with croup gets a single dose of corticosteroid. Prednisolone 1 mg/kg is 12 mg.","Croup is viral. Antibiotics are not indicated.","Adrenaline is for severe croup, with persisting stridor at rest. She has none.","Croup is upper airway swelling, not bronchospasm. Salbutamol does not help.","Steam is not recommended, and even mild croup is given a corticosteroid, which reduces return visits."],"e":"Problem: a barking cough at night with stridor only on crying. Task: grade it and treat it. Mild croup still earns a steroid. What if she had stridor at rest that persisted 30 minutes after the steroid? Treat as severe: nebulised adrenaline and transfer.","k":"Mild to moderate croup: one dose of prednisolone 1 mg/kg or dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Croup"],"c":["rs12-17","rs12-18","rs12-19","rs12-20","rs12-21"],"id":"rs-a70","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","nt":"rs-kids-airway#crouptreat","s":"Mason Taylor, aged 18 months, presents to your general practice at 8 am with croup. He was given oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg on arrival. Forty minutes later he still has a harsh noise on every breath in while lying quietly against his father, and he looks tired. The nearest hospital is 35 minutes away. He weighs 11 kg. The practice has a nebuliser, oxygen and an emergency drug kit.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings (40 minutes after prednisolone)","i":["Quiet, tired, eyes open","Respiratory rate 44/min, heart rate 162/min","SpO2 94% on room air","Stridor at rest; marked suprasternal and sternal retraction","Reduced air entry on both sides"]}],"q":"In addition to calling an ambulance, what is the MOST appropriate immediate treatment?","o":["A further dose of oral prednisolone 1 mg/kg","Intramuscular adrenaline 0.01 mg/kg into the thigh","Nebulised adrenaline 5 mL of 1:1000 solution","Nebulised budesonide 2 mg, repeated every 12 hours for up to 48 hours if he still has stridor","Nebulised salbutamol 2.5 mg"],"a":2,"x":["He has had a corticosteroid, which takes time to work. The airway needs something faster.","Intramuscular adrenaline is the treatment for anaphylaxis, not for croup.","Correct. Persisting stridor at rest with fatigue and reduced air entry is severe croup. Nebulised adrenaline 5 mL of 1:1000 is given while transfer is arranged.","Nebulised budesonide is an alternative when an oral steroid cannot be taken. It is not the treatment for severe croup.","Salbutamol does not relieve upper airway obstruction."],"e":"Problem: stridor at rest 40 minutes after a steroid, with tiring. Task: escalate. If stridor at rest has not improved 30 minutes after the steroid, the child is treated as severe. What if he then needed a second adrenaline dose? Intensive care is notified and he is admitted.","k":"Severe croup: nebulised adrenaline 5 mL of 1:1000 plus steroid, and immediate transfer.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Croup"],"c":["rs12-17","rs12-18","rs12-19","rs12-20","rs12-21"],"id":"rs-a71","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","nt":"rs-kids-airway#stridor","s":"Ethan Brooks, aged 20 months, is rushed in by his grandmother. He was well this morning and was eating mixed nuts at a family lunch when he suddenly coughed, choked and went red in the face. Since then, for 30 minutes, he has had noisy breathing and a persistent cough. He has had no fever or cold symptoms. He is immunised and has no medical history.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, anxious, clinging to his grandmother","Respiratory rate 36/min, SpO2 95% on room air","Inspiratory stridor at rest","Wheeze and reduced breath sounds on the right","Temperature 36.8 degrees"]}],"q":"What is the MOST likely diagnosis?","o":["Acute epiglottitis","Anaphylaxis to tree nuts","Croup","Inhaled foreign body","Spasmodic croup, which typically comes on suddenly without a preceding viral illness"],"a":3,"x":["Epiglottitis brings fever and a toxic appearance. He is afebrile and was well this morning.","There is no rash, swelling, vomiting or circulatory change, and the signs are one-sided.","Croup follows a viral prodrome and gives a barking cough with signs on both sides.","Correct. Sudden stridor and cough after a choking episode while eating, with one-sided wheeze and reduced breath sounds, is an inhaled foreign body. He needs urgent referral.","Spasmodic croup has no prodrome, but it comes on at night, is recurrent, and does not give one-sided signs."],"e":"Problem: sudden stridor in a toddler. Task: separate croup from its mimics. The choking episode and the one-sided chest signs decide it. What if he had hives and lip swelling within minutes of eating the nuts? Anaphylaxis: intramuscular adrenaline.","k":"Stridor after a choking episode is an inhaled foreign body: urgent referral.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Croup","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in children"],"c":["rs12-27","rs12-28","rs12-29","rs12-01","rs12-02"],"id":"rs-a72","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","nt":"rs-kids-airway#bronchadmit","s":"Noah Anderson, aged 7 weeks, was born at 33 weeks' gestation and went home from the special care nursery three weeks ago. His mother brings him in on the first day of a snuffly nose and a mild cough. He is taking his full bottles and has had normal wet nappies. His father and two siblings have colds, and his father smokes outside. Noah has not yet had his first immunisations.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, settled","Respiratory rate 48/min, SpO2 97% on room air","Mild subcostal recession","A few scattered crackles","Temperature 37.4 degrees"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a chest X-ray, full blood count and C-reactive protein, then decide on a plan when the results are back","Arrange early hospital assessment, because he is at high risk of rapid deterioration","Give his 6-week immunisations today and review in a week","Prescribe salbutamol through a spacer and mask for home","Send home with feeding advice and review in 48 hours"],"a":1,"x":["A chest X-ray is not routine in bronchiolitis, and tests would not change the need for observation.","Correct. He is under 2 months, was born preterm and is exposed to tobacco smoke. Each is a reason to consider early admission even when the illness looks mild, and day 1 is before the peak.","Immunisation matters, but not ahead of assessing a high-risk infant with bronchiolitis.","Bronchodilators are not recommended.","This would suit a low-risk infant with mild illness. His risk factors change the plan."],"e":"Problem: mild bronchiolitis on day 1 in a 7-week-old born preterm. Task: weigh risk factors, not just today's signs. Everything on examination is reassuring, and that is the trap. What if he were a 9-month-old born at term with the same findings? Home with feeding advice and review.","k":"Bronchiolitis under 2 months or in a preterm infant: consider early admission even if mild.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Acute bronchiolitis"],"c":["rs12-05","rs12-06","rs12-07","rs12-08","rs12-01"],"id":"rs-a73","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Bronchiolitis and croup","nt":"rs-kids-airway#croup","n":3,"s":"You are assessing Sophie Martin, aged 3 years, who has a barking cough and hoarse voice.","q":"Which THREE of the following findings would indicate that her croup is severe or life-threatening? Select three.","o":["A temperature of 38.2 degrees","Drowsiness and floppiness","Markedly reduced air entry on auscultation","Stridor that is heard only when she cries","Stridor that is very loud","Stridor that persists at rest, with increasing fatigue","The cough is worse at night"],"sc":[0,1,1,0,0,1,0],"x":["Scores 0. Fever is common in croup and does not grade severity.","Scores 1. Reduced consciousness and hypotonia are signs of life-threatening obstruction.","Scores 1. Markedly decreased air entry is a feature of severe croup.","Scores 0. No stridor at rest is mild croup.","Scores 0. Loudness of stridor is not a guide to the degree of obstruction.","Scores 1. Persisting stridor at rest with fatigue defines severe croup.","Scores 0. Croup is typically worse at night at any severity."],"e":"Severity is judged by stridor at rest, retraction, fatigue, air entry and conscious state. Volume of stridor and height of fever are not part of it.","k":"Severe croup: persisting stridor at rest, fatigue, poor air entry. Life-threatening: drowsy, floppy, pale or blue.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Croup"],"c":["rs12-12","rs12-13","rs12-14","rs12-15","rs12-16"],"id":"rs-k12","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","nt":"rs-kids-cough#pbb","s":"Charlie Evans, aged 3 years, has had a 'rattly' cough every day for six weeks since a cold. It is worse when he lies down at night and first thing in the morning. He has no wheeze, runs around without getting puffed, eats well and is growing along the 50th centile. He has had no choking episodes. No one smokes at home. He has no drug allergies and weighs 15 kg.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["A pharmacy cough syrup, stopped after a week"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, SpO2 99% on room air","Coarse crackles that shift with coughing","No clubbing, no chest deformity"]}],"q":"Which treatment is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 25 mg/kg every 8 hours for 3 days","Amoxicillin with clavulanate every 12 hours for 2 weeks","Azithromycin once daily for 3 days, repeated in a month if the cough has returned by then","Fluticasone propionate 50 micrograms twice daily by spacer for 3 months","No treatment, as this is a post-viral cough"],"a":1,"x":["This is the regimen for low-severity pneumonia. He is well, afebrile and has had the cough for six weeks.","Correct. An isolated wet cough for more than 4 weeks in a well child is protracted bacterial bronchitis. Amoxicillin with clavulanate is given for 2 weeks.","A 3-day course is too short. Symptoms return when treatment is shortened.","He has no wheeze, breathlessness or dry night cough. A wet cough alone is not asthma.","A post-viral cough is usually dry and is settling. Six weeks of wet cough needs treatment."],"e":"Problem: six weeks of wet cough in a thriving 3-year-old. Task: name the diagnosis and treat for long enough. What if the cough were still there after 2 weeks of treatment? Discuss with a paediatrician and continue to 4 weeks in total.","k":"Protracted bacterial bronchitis: amoxicillin with clavulanate for 2 weeks.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Protracted bacterial bronchitis in children"],"c":["rs13-08","rs13-09","rs13-10","rs13-11","rs13-12"],"id":"rs-a74","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","nt":"rs-kids-cough#pbb","s":"Ruby Scott, aged 4 years, returns with her fourth episode this year of a wet cough lasting more than a month. Each episode cleared after two weeks of amoxicillin with clavulanate, then came back within six to eight weeks. Between episodes she is well. Her weight has slipped from the 50th to the 25th centile over the year. She was born at term and has no history of choking.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amoxicillin with clavulanate, four courses this year"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, SpO2 98% on room air","Coarse crackles at both bases","Early clubbing of the fingers"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Prescribe a 4-week course of amoxicillin with clavulanate and review her when the course is complete","Prescribe a fifth 2-week course of amoxicillin with clavulanate","Refer to a paediatrician or paediatric respiratory physician","Start a daily inhaled corticosteroid","Start long-term prophylactic azithromycin"],"a":2,"x":["A longer course is for a single episode that has not cleared at 2 weeks. Hers clear and recur.","Another course treats the episode but ignores the pattern.","Correct. Three or more episodes a year, with falling growth and clubbing, call for referral to exclude bronchiectasis and its causes.","A recurrent wet cough is not asthma.","Long-term antibiotics are a specialist decision made after investigation."],"e":"Problem: recurrent protracted bacterial bronchitis with clubbing and poor growth. Task: recognise when it stops being a simple diagnosis. What if this were her first episode, with normal growth and no clubbing? Two weeks of antibiotic and review.","k":"Three or more episodes of protracted bacterial bronchitis a year, or no clearance at 4 weeks: refer.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Protracted bacterial bronchitis in children","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in children"],"c":["rs13-08","rs13-09","rs13-10","rs13-11","rs13-12"],"id":"rs-a75","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","nt":"rs-kids-cough#cap","s":"Oscar Bennett, aged 5 years, has had fever and cough for three days. He is drinking well and watched cartoons in the waiting room. He has no medical conditions, is fully immunised and has no drug allergies. He weighs 20 kg. His mother can bring him back at any time and lives five minutes away. A chest X-ray arranged by a colleague yesterday shows consolidation in the left lower lobe.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Alert, interactive","Temperature 38.4 degrees","Respiratory rate 24/min, heart rate 104/min","SpO2 97% on room air","No recession; crackles at the left base"]}],"q":"Which treatment is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 500 mg orally every 8 hours for 10 days, with a repeat chest X-ray at the end of the course","Amoxicillin 500 mg orally every 8 hours for 3 days","Amoxicillin with clavulanate orally every 12 hours for 14 days","Azithromycin 200 mg orally daily for 3 days","Intravenous benzylpenicillin in hospital"],"a":1,"x":["The dose is right. The duration for low-severity pneumonia in a child is 3 days.","Correct. This is low-severity pneumonia: minimal tachypnoea, saturation of 95% or more. Amoxicillin 25 mg/kg, which is 500 mg, every 8 hours for 3 days.","This is the regimen for protracted bacterial bronchitis.","Azithromycin is for children with severe penicillin hypersensitivity.","He has none of the features of moderate severity and is drinking well."],"e":"Problem: lobar pneumonia in a well 5-year-old. Task: grade severity and prescribe by weight. The short duration is what catches people. What if he were no better at 48 to 72 hours? Reassess the diagnosis and severity, and consider admission.","k":"Low-severity pneumonia in a child: amoxicillin 25 mg/kg (maximum 1 g) every 8 hours for 3 days.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in children 2 months or older"],"c":["rs13-16","rs13-17","rs13-18","rs13-19","rs13-20"],"id":"rs-a76","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","nt":"rs-kids-cough#meds","s":"Zoe Harris, aged 3 years, has had a cough and runny nose for four days. She is eating, playing and sleeping, though the cough wakes her parents. Her examination is normal. Her mother asks you to recommend a cough medicine, and mentions that the pharmacy has a children's cough and cold syrup containing an antihistamine and a decongestant.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear, ears and throat normal"]}],"q":"Which advice is MOST appropriate?","o":["A decongestant alone is safe from 2 years of age","Cough and cold medicines should not be used under 6 years, and a teaspoon of honey may be tried","Half the adult dose of a codeine linctus at night is acceptable for up to three nights, provided she is supervised","The children's cough and cold syrup is appropriate at the dose on the label","Use salbutamol syrup at night"],"a":1,"x":["Decongestants are among the products that should not be given to children under 6.","Correct. Cough and cold medicines, including antihistamines, decongestants and cough suppressants, should not be given to children under 6 years. Honey has some evidence and can be tried after 12 months of age.","Cough suppressants are included in the advice against use under 6 years.","These products have little or no proven benefit and can cause harm at this age, whatever the label says.","Oral salbutamol is not recommended for any indication in children with cough or asthma."],"e":"Problem: a parent asking for a cough medicine for a preschooler with a cold. Task: give the regulator's age rule and a safe alternative. What if she were 10 months old? No honey either, because it is only for children over 12 months.","k":"No cough and cold medicines under 6 years. Honey may be tried after 12 months.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in children"],"c":["rs13-24","rs13-25","rs13-01","rs13-02","rs13-03"],"id":"rs-a77","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","nt":"rs-kids-cough#classify","s":"Isaac Morgan, aged 6 years, has had a dry cough for five weeks. It happens many times an hour during the day, especially at school and when he is watching television, and his teacher has complained. It disappears completely when he is asleep and he can hold it back for a minute or two when asked. He has no wheeze, breathlessness or night symptoms, and plays sport normally. He is growing well.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol inhaler, tried for two weeks without effect"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Chest clear, SpO2 99% on room air","No cough during ten minutes of play with building blocks","Throat and nose normal"]}],"q":"What is the MOST likely diagnosis?","o":["Asthma","Pertussis","Protracted bacterial bronchitis","Tic (habit) cough","Upper airway cough syndrome caused by allergic rhinitis, with postnasal drip that worsens during the day"],"a":3,"x":["There is no wheeze, breathlessness or night cough, and no response to salbutamol.","Pertussis causes bouts of coughing, often at night, with a whoop or vomiting.","This is excluded by a cough that is solely dry.","Correct. A dry cough that is suppressible, stops with distraction and is absent in sleep is a tic cough.","He has no nasal symptoms, and this would not vanish with distraction or sleep."],"e":"Problem: a frequent dry daytime cough that vanishes in sleep. Task: recognise the pattern. Suppressibility and distractibility are the deciding facts. What if the cough were wet and present on waking? Protracted bacterial bronchitis.","k":"A child's dry cough that is suppressible, distractible and absent in sleep is a tic cough.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in children"],"c":["rs13-01","rs13-02","rs13-03","rs13-04","rs13-05"],"id":"rs-a78","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","nt":"rs-kids-cough#cap","s":"Maya Cooper, aged 2 years, has had fever and cough for two days. Today she has refused most drinks and has been sleepy. She was well before this and is fully immunised. Her father brings her to your practice, which is 15 minutes from a hospital with a children's ward. She weighs 12 kg.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Lethargic, cries weakly when examined","Temperature 39.1 degrees","Respiratory rate 52/min, heart rate 168/min","SpO2 91% on room air","Nasal flaring and intercostal recession","Bronchial breathing at the right base"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange transfer to hospital now","Give the first dose of oral amoxicillin and review her in the practice tomorrow morning before school drop-off","Prescribe azithromycin","Prescribe oral amoxicillin for 3 days and review in 48 to 72 hours","Send a nasopharyngeal swab and await the result before deciding on antibiotics"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Increased work of breathing, fast breathing and pulse, lethargy and a saturation of 91% place her well beyond low severity. She needs hospital care.","Review tomorrow is unsafe for a lethargic, hypoxic child who is not drinking.","Changing the antibiotic does not address severity.","This is the plan for low-severity pneumonia, with a saturation of 95% or more and minimal tachypnoea.","A swab may be useful in hospital. It must not delay transfer."],"e":"Problem: pneumonia in a toddler with hypoxia and nasal flaring. Task: grade severity before reaching for a prescription. What if she were alert, drinking, with a saturation of 97% and a respiratory rate of 30? Low severity: oral amoxicillin for 3 days if an antibiotic is needed.","k":"Child with pneumonia and increased work of breathing or saturation of 95% or lower: hospital.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Community-acquired pneumonia in children 2 months or older"],"c":["rs13-16","rs13-17","rs13-18","rs13-19","rs13-20"],"id":"rs-a79","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Cough and pneumonia in children","nt":"rs-kids-cough#chronic","n":3,"s":"Jacob Phillips, aged 5 years, has had a cough for seven weeks. You are taking a history to look for a cause other than a simple post-viral cough.","q":"Which THREE of the following findings would MOST strongly suggest a serious underlying cause needing investigation or referral? Select three.","o":["Finger clubbing","He has had three colds this winter","His weight has fallen across two centile lines","The cough began suddenly while he was playing with small toy parts","The cough is absent during sleep","The cough is dry","The cough is worse after running"],"sc":[1,0,1,1,0,0,0],"x":["Scores 1. Clubbing suggests bronchiectasis or cystic fibrosis.","Scores 0. Frequent colds are normal at this age.","Scores 1. Poor growth is a systemic feature that needs investigation.","Scores 1. Sudden onset while playing with small objects suggests a retained foreign body, which needs urgent referral.","Scores 0. This points to a tic cough.","Scores 0. A dry cough is common after viral infection.","Scores 0. This may suggest asthma, which is assessed in primary care first."],"e":"The pointers to serious disease in a child with chronic cough are clubbing, chest deformity, poor growth, recurrent wet cough, systemic symptoms, and a sudden onset that suggests a foreign body.","k":"Chronic cough in a child with clubbing, poor growth or sudden onset: investigate or refer.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (December 2020), Cough in children"],"c":["rs13-05","rs13-06","rs13-07","rs13-01","rs13-02"],"id":"rs-k13","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","nt":"rs-urti#arf","s":"Tyrone Watson, aged 9 years, is an Aboriginal boy who lives with his extended family in a remote community in the Kimberley. He is brought to the clinic with two days of sore throat, a runny nose and a cough. He is eating and drinking. His cousin, who lives in the same house, was treated for acute rheumatic fever last year. Tyrone has no drug allergies and weighs 30 kg.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.6 degrees","Throat red, tonsils not enlarged, no exudate","Clear nasal discharge","No rash, heart sounds normal, no joint swelling"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Give antibiotic treatment for streptococcal infection, with a throat swab first if possible","Give symptomatic treatment and review in 7 days","Give symptomatic treatment only, because cough and a runny nose indicate a viral infection in a child of this age","Take a throat swab and treat only if group A streptococcus is grown","Take blood for an antistreptolysin O titre"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. He is under 40, Aboriginal, living remotely, and shares a house with someone who had rheumatic fever. Every sore throat in a high-risk person is treated, even when it looks viral.","Waiting leaves a high-risk child untreated.","This reasoning applies to people not at high risk. For him, the risk of rheumatic fever outweighs the harm of a possibly unnecessary antibiotic.","A swab is taken if possible, but treatment is not delayed for the result in a high-risk patient.","Serology has no role in deciding treatment of an acute sore throat."],"e":"Problem: a sore throat that looks viral, in a child at high risk of acute rheumatic fever. Task: let risk, not appearance, drive the decision. What if he were a 9-year-old in suburban Sydney with no risk factors? Symptomatic treatment only.","k":"High risk of acute rheumatic fever: antibiotics for every sore throat, even if it looks viral.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic, Sore throat"],"c":["rs14-05","rs14-06","rs14-07","rs14-01","rs14-02"],"id":"rs-a80","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","nt":"rs-urti#decide","s":"Hannah Kim, aged 31 years, is a solicitor who lives in inner Melbourne. She has had a sore throat and fever for two days, with no cough or runny nose. She is drinking and managing soft food, and took yesterday off work. She was born in Australia, has no history of rheumatic fever and no relevant travel. She asks for antibiotics so she can 'get back to court by Thursday'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Combined oral contraceptive pill","Ibuprofen 400 mg as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 38.3 degrees","Enlarged tonsils with white exudate on both sides","Tender anterior cervical nodes","Mouth opens fully, voice normal, no rash"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Explain that antibiotics shorten symptoms by less than a day, and treat symptoms","Prescribe amoxicillin with clavulanate for 7 days, as tonsillar exudate indicates bacterial infection","Prescribe phenoxymethylpenicillin for 10 days","Refer to the emergency department for assessment of a peritonsillar abscess","Take a throat swab and start azithromycin"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. She is not at high risk of rheumatic fever, her symptoms are not severe and she has no scarlet fever rash. Antibiotics offer less than a day of benefit.","Streptococcal infection is likely, but that alone does not make antibiotics necessary, and this is not the recommended agent.","This is the right regimen when antibiotics are indicated. For her they are not required.","She has no trismus, voice change or one-sided pain.","Azithromycin is for severe penicillin hypersensitivity when treatment is indicated."],"e":"Problem: probable streptococcal tonsillitis in a low-risk adult who expects antibiotics. Task: have the conversation the guideline asks for. What if she had severe pain and could barely swallow? Antibiotics can be considered, with a throat swab, and a single dose of corticosteroid for pain.","k":"Not at high risk of rheumatic fever: antibiotics for sore throat only if severe or with a scarlet fever rash.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic, Sore throat"],"c":["rs14-08","rs14-09","rs14-01","rs14-02","rs14-03"],"id":"rs-a81","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","nt":"rs-urti#treat","s":"Jarrah Thompson, aged 14 years, is an Aboriginal boy living in a regional town in a house with nine relatives. He has a sore throat, fever and tender neck glands. His grandmother, who has brought him, says he had 'heart fever' when he was seven and used to have monthly injections but stopped two years ago. He has no drug allergies and weighs 58 kg. A throat swab has been taken.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["None currently"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 38.6 degrees","Tonsillar exudate, tender cervical nodes","Soft systolic murmur at the apex, unchanged from his records"]}],"q":"Which antibiotic regimen is MOST appropriate?","o":["Amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours for 5 days","Azithromycin 500 mg daily for 3 days, as a short course is more likely to be completed by an adolescent","Cefalexin 500 mg every 6 hours for 7 days","Phenoxymethylpenicillin 500 mg every 12 hours for 10 days","Phenoxymethylpenicillin 500 mg every 12 hours for 5 days"],"a":3,"x":["Amoxicillin is a second choice for children who cannot take the penicillin liquid, and 5 days is too short to prevent rheumatic fever.","Azithromycin is for severe penicillin hypersensitivity, and the course is 5 days.","Cefalexin is for non-severe penicillin hypersensitivity, at 1 g every 12 hours for 10 days.","Correct. Phenoxymethylpenicillin for a full 10 days clears streptococcus and prevents rheumatic fever. A single injection of benzathine benzylpenicillin is the alternative if adherence is doubtful.","The drug and dose are right. Five days is acceptable only for people not at high risk who are treated for symptoms."],"e":"Problem: streptococcal tonsillitis in a boy with past rheumatic fever who has lapsed from prophylaxis. Task: choose the drug and, above all, the duration. What if the family doubted he would take tablets for 10 days? One intramuscular dose of benzathine benzylpenicillin, and a plan to restart his prophylaxis.","k":"Streptococcal sore throat: phenoxymethylpenicillin 500 mg every 12 hours for 10 days.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic, Sore throat"],"c":["rs14-10","rs14-11","rs14-12","rs14-13","rs14-14"],"id":"rs-a82","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","nt":"rs-urti#danger","s":"Liam Foster, aged 22 years, is a university student with four days of sore throat that has become much worse on the left since yesterday. He can barely open his mouth, is spitting out saliva because swallowing hurts, and his housemate says his voice sounds 'like he has a hot potato in his mouth'. He has no medical history and takes no medicines.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 38.9 degrees, heart rate 112/min","Mouth opening limited to two fingers' width","Left tonsil pushed towards the midline, uvula deviated to the right","Tender swelling at the left angle of the jaw","No stridor, SpO2 98% on room air"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange urgent transfer to hospital for assessment and drainage","Prescribe phenoxymethylpenicillin for 10 days and review in 48 hours","Prescribe prednisolone 50 mg for two days with phenoxymethylpenicillin, and review him tomorrow morning","Take a throat swab and monospot test, and review with results","Treat symptoms and reassure him that it is viral"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Trismus, a muffled voice, drooling and one-sided pain point to a peritonsillar abscess. He needs hospital assessment for drainage and airway monitoring.","Oral antibiotics alone will not treat an abscess.","A corticosteroid may ease pain, but sending him home leaves a deep neck space infection undrained.","Tests would delay transfer. Glandular fever can coexist, but the examination shows an abscess.","The features are those of a deep neck space infection, not a viral sore throat."],"e":"Problem: a sore throat that has become one-sided, with trismus and a changed voice. Task: recognise the red flags for a deep neck space infection. What if he also had stridor? The airway takes priority, and he travels with an escort who can manage it.","k":"Sore throat with trismus, muffled voice, drooling or one-sided pain: urgent hospital transfer.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic, Sore throat"],"c":["rs14-04","rs14-01","rs14-02","rs14-03","rs14-05"],"id":"rs-a83","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","nt":"rs-urti#sinus","s":"Deborah Quinn, aged 44 years, is a receptionist with six days of blocked nose, thick yellow-green nasal discharge and a feeling of pressure across both cheeks, which is worse when she bends forward. She has had no fever, feels tired but is working, and has had similar episodes twice before. She says her previous doctor 'always gave amoxicillin'. She has no drug allergies and no asthma.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Pseudoephedrine tablets for three days","Paracetamol as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 36.9 degrees","Swollen nasal mucosa with purulent discharge","Mild tenderness over both maxillary sinuses","Eyes and eye movements normal"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a CT scan of the sinuses","Prescribe amoxicillin 500 mg every 8 hours for 5 days","Prescribe amoxicillin with clavulanate for 10 days, as the discharge is purulent and she has had previous episodes","Prescribe an oral antihistamine","Recommend saline rinses and an intranasal corticosteroid, and review if no better by day 10"],"a":4,"x":["Imaging is not needed to diagnose uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis.","This is the right regimen when antibiotics are indicated. At six days, afebrile and working, she meets none of the three criteria.","Purulent discharge does not indicate bacterial infection, and this is not the recommended agent.","Antihistamines do not relieve rhinosinusitis unless allergy is contributing.","Correct. Uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis is viral and settles within 7 to 14 days. Symptom relief is offered, with review if she has not improved by 10 days or worsens."],"e":"Problem: six days of sinus symptoms with coloured discharge and an expectation of antibiotics. Task: apply the three criteria. She is not very unwell, has not worsened after improving, and has not reached 10 days. What if her fever and facial pain returned on day 8 after a few better days? Antibiotics would then be considered.","k":"Acute rhinosinusitis: antibiotics only if very unwell, worse after improving, or no better at 10 days.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Acute rhinosinusitis"],"c":["rs14-17","rs14-18","rs14-19","rs14-20","rs14-21"],"id":"rs-a84","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","nt":"rs-urti#sinuscomp","s":"Nathan Price, aged 17 years, has had a cold with a blocked nose and frontal headache for five days. This morning his mother noticed that his left upper eyelid was swollen and red. By this afternoon he has pain when he looks to the side and says he is 'seeing double'. He has been vomiting and wants the lights off. He has no medical history.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 38.8 degrees, heart rate 108/min","Left eyelid swollen and red; left eye pushed forward","Painful, restricted movement of the left eye","Alert and oriented; mild neck stiffness"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange urgent transfer to hospital for intravenous antibiotics and surgical review","Prescribe an intranasal corticosteroid and a decongestant spray","Prescribe oral amoxicillin 1 g every 12 hours for 5 days and review tomorrow","Prescribe oral amoxicillin with clavulanate and chloramphenicol eye ointment, and review him in 48 hours","Request an outpatient CT scan of the sinuses for later this week"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Eyelid swelling, a bulging eye, painful eye movements, double vision and neck stiffness mean the infection has spread beyond the sinuses. This is an emergency.","Symptom relief is for uncomplicated disease.","Oral antibiotics do not treat orbital or intracranial spread.","This treats a presumed eyelid infection and leaves a sight-threatening and life-threatening complication.","Imaging is needed, but urgently and in hospital."],"e":"Problem: sinus symptoms followed by eye signs and meningism. Task: recognise complicated rhinosinusitis. Complications arise early in the illness and are not prevented by earlier oral antibiotics. What if he had only a blocked nose and headache at day 5, with normal eyes? Symptom relief and review.","k":"Sinusitis with eyelid swelling, eye pain, double vision, confusion or meningism: hospital now.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Acute rhinosinusitis"],"c":["rs14-27","rs14-28","rs14-01","rs14-02","rs14-03"],"id":"rs-a85","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Sore throat and sinusitis","nt":"rs-urti#arf","n":3,"s":"You work in a regional practice and see many patients with sore throat. You are deciding which patients should be treated as being at high risk of acute rheumatic fever.","q":"Which THREE of the following patients are at high risk of acute rheumatic fever? Select three.","o":["A 12-year-old Aboriginal girl living in a remote community","A 19-year-old Samoan man living in an overcrowded house","A 25-year-old woman whose brother, in the same household, had rheumatic fever six months ago","A 30-year-old man born in Australia who had his tonsils removed as a child","A 35-year-old teacher with three sore throats this year","A 55-year-old Aboriginal man living in a remote community","A 6-year-old boy from a city suburb with a scarlet fever rash"],"sc":[1,1,1,0,0,0,0],"x":["Scores 1. Under 40, Aboriginal, and living in a remote area.","Scores 1. Under 40, a Pacific person, and living with household overcrowding.","Scores 1. Under 40, with a household member who recently had rheumatic fever.","Scores 0. No risk factor.","Scores 0. Recurrent sore throats alone do not place an adult at high risk.","Scores 0. The high-risk definition applies to people under 40.","Scores 0. A scarlet fever rash is a reason to consider antibiotics, but it is not a risk factor for rheumatic fever."],"e":"High risk means under 40 with one of: Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander and rural or remote; Aboriginal, Torres Strait Islander, Maori or Pacific with overcrowding or disadvantage; past rheumatic fever or rheumatic heart disease; or a household member with recent disease.","k":"High risk of rheumatic fever: under 40 with defined ethnicity and living conditions, past disease, or an affected household.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic, Sore throat"],"c":["rs14-05","rs14-06","rs14-07","rs14-01","rs14-02"],"id":"rs-k14","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Influenza and pertussis","nt":"rs-flu#flutreat","s":"Emily Carter, aged 29 years, is 26 weeks pregnant with her first child. In mid-July she developed fever, cough, headache and aching muscles 36 hours ago. Her partner tested positive for influenza A three days ago. She had the influenza vaccine in April. She is drinking well and her baby is moving normally. A rapid antigen test in the practice is positive for influenza A.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Pregnancy multivitamin","Iron supplement","Paracetamol 1 g as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 38.4 degrees","Respiratory rate 18/min, heart rate 102/min","SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear; fetal heart rate 150/min"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Admit to hospital for intravenous fluids and monitoring","Prescribe baloxavir as a single dose","Prescribe oseltamivir 75 mg once daily for 10 days","Prescribe oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days","Treat symptoms only, as she has been vaccinated and it is more than 24 hours since her symptoms began"],"a":3,"x":["She is hydrated with normal saturation and a clear chest. Admission is not needed.","There are no data to support baloxavir in pregnancy.","Once daily for 10 days is the prophylaxis regimen.","Correct. Antiviral treatment is recommended for influenza in pregnancy and up to two weeks after birth. Oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days.","Vaccination does not rule out influenza, and she is within 48 hours. Pregnancy is one of the two groups in whom treatment is recommended."],"e":"Problem: confirmed influenza at 36 hours in a pregnant woman. Task: know who should be treated and with what. What if she were a healthy non-pregnant 29-year-old? Symptom relief, with at most a day's benefit from an antiviral.","k":"Influenza in pregnancy: oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Influenza"],"c":["rs15-03","rs15-04","rs15-05","rs15-06","rs15-07"],"id":"rs-a86","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Influenza and pertussis","nt":"rs-flu#flutreat","s":"Jake Morrison, aged 30 years, is a personal trainer with four days of fever, cough, sore throat and muscle aches during the influenza season. He has no medical conditions, does not smoke and takes no medicines. He was not vaccinated this year. He is drinking and eating a little, and is improving slightly today. He asks for 'the flu tablets' because a client told him they work.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.9 degrees","Respiratory rate 14/min, heart rate 84/min","SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Advise rest, fluids and paracetamol, and explain when to return","Arrange a chest X-ray to exclude pneumonia","Prescribe amoxicillin to prevent secondary bacterial infection","Prescribe oseltamivir 75 mg twice daily for 5 days","Prescribe oseltamivir for 10 days, as he is beyond the first 48 hours and needs a longer course"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. He is a healthy adult at low risk, four days in and improving. Antivirals would not help, and symptom relief is the treatment.","His observations and chest are normal.","Antibiotics do not prevent bacterial complications of influenza.","In healthy adults the benefit is about one day, and only when started within 48 hours.","There is no extended course for late presenters. Ten days is the prophylaxis duration."],"e":"Problem: uncomplicated influenza in a healthy adult on day 4. Task: recognise that antivirals have no role. What if he were 70 with COPD on day 2? An antiviral would be considered.","k":"Healthy adults with influenza: symptomatic care. Antivirals shorten illness by about a day if started within 48 hours.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Influenza"],"c":["rs15-03","rs15-04","rs15-05","rs15-06","rs15-07"],"id":"rs-a87","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Influenza and pertussis","nt":"rs-flu#perttreat","s":"Greg Lawson, aged 38 years, is a builder who has had bouts of coughing for 16 days. The bouts come in runs that leave him red in the face and have twice ended in vomiting. He is well between them. His wife is 37 weeks pregnant. He had his last pertussis-containing vaccine at school. A nasopharyngeal swab taken three days ago is positive for Bordetella pertussis on PCR. He has no drug allergies.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Ibuprofen as needed for back pain"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear","A subconjunctival haemorrhage in the right eye"]}],"q":"Which treatment is MOST appropriate for him?","o":["Amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours for 5 days","Azithromycin 500 mg on day 1, then 250 mg daily for 4 more days","Doxycycline 100 mg every 12 hours for 7 days","No antibiotic, because treatment does not shorten the illness and the cough has already been present for two weeks","Roxithromycin 300 mg daily for 7 days"],"a":1,"x":["Amoxicillin is not active against Bordetella pertussis.","Correct. Pertussis diagnosed within 3 weeks of cough onset is treated to stop transmission. Azithromycin for 5 days is first line.","Doxycycline is not a recommended treatment for pertussis.","Antibiotics may not shorten his cough, but he is still infectious at 16 days and lives with a woman in the last month of pregnancy.","There is no evidence to support roxithromycin for pertussis."],"e":"Problem: confirmed pertussis at day 16 in a man whose wife is about to give birth. Task: treat him, and think beyond him. His wife is a contact in the last month of pregnancy and needs prophylaxis. What if he were at day 30 of cough? No antibiotic for him, as he is rarely infectious by then.","k":"Pertussis within 3 weeks of onset: azithromycin for 5 days, and prophylaxis for contacts who could infect an infant.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Pertussis"],"c":["rs15-17","rs15-18","rs15-19","rs15-20","rs15-01"],"id":"rs-a88","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Influenza and pertussis","nt":"rs-flu#pertcontacts","s":"The Nguyen household has five members: both parents, a grandmother, a 4-year-old and a 3-month-old baby. The 4-year-old was diagnosed with pertussis by PCR yesterday, nine days after her cough began, and has started azithromycin. Everyone has been at home together throughout. The baby had her 6-week and has not yet had her 4-month immunisations. No one else has symptoms. The public health unit has been notified.","b":[{"h":"Household","i":["Mother: fully immunised, booster in pregnancy","Father and grandmother: last booster more than 10 years ago","Baby aged 3 months: well, feeding normally"]}],"q":"Who in the household should receive antibiotic prophylaxis?","o":["All four other household members","No one, because the baby's mother was vaccinated during pregnancy and the baby has had a first dose","Only the baby","Only the baby and her mother","Only the father and grandmother, who are not up to date with vaccination"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. When a household contains an infant under 6 months and the case is a household member, every household member receives prophylaxis.","Maternal vaccination and one infant dose reduce risk but do not replace prophylaxis after household exposure.","The baby is the person being protected, but adults who could carry infection to her are treated too.","The father and grandmother could also transmit infection to the baby.","Prophylaxis is decided by who could infect the infant, not by vaccination status alone."],"e":"Problem: pertussis in a preschooler who lives with a 3-month-old. Task: apply the household rule. What if the youngest person in the house were 5 years old and no one was pregnant? Prophylaxis would not be routine.","k":"Pertussis case in a household with an infant under 6 months: prophylaxis for the whole household.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Pertussis"],"c":["rs15-21","rs15-22","rs15-23","rs15-24","rs15-25"],"id":"rs-a89","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Influenza and pertussis","nt":"rs-flu#pertdx","s":"Susan Wright, aged 52 years, is a childcare centre director with a cough for three and a half weeks. It comes in bursts, often at night, and she gags at the end of a burst. She has no fever, wheeze or breathlessness and has never smoked. Two children at her centre were diagnosed with whooping cough last month. Her last pertussis booster was 15 years ago. Her chest is clear.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Candesartan 8 mg daily","Estradiol gel daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear"]}],"q":"Which test is MOST appropriate to confirm the diagnosis?","o":["Anterior nasal swab for a rapid antigen test","Chest X-ray","PCR on a posterior nasopharyngeal swab","Sputum culture, sent to the laboratory with a request for prolonged incubation on selective media","Throat swab for culture"],"a":2,"x":["An anterior nasal swab is less sensitive for pertussis.","Imaging does not diagnose pertussis.","Correct. PCR on a posterior nasopharyngeal swab or aspirate is the test of choice. It is most sensitive in the first 3 to 4 weeks of cough, so she is still within the window.","Culture is used for surveillance, not routine diagnosis.","A throat swab is less sensitive than a nasopharyngeal sample."],"e":"Problem: a paroxysmal cough at three and a half weeks in a childcare worker with known exposure. Task: choose the right sample. What about treatment? She is past 3 weeks, so antibiotics are not indicated for her, but the diagnosis matters for notification and for the centre.","k":"Pertussis test: PCR on a posterior nasopharyngeal swab, best in the first 3 to 4 weeks.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Pertussis"],"c":["rs15-13","rs15-14","rs15-15","rs15-16","rs15-01"],"id":"rs-a90","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Influenza and pertussis","nt":"rs-flu#fluprev","s":"Mavis Henderson, aged 84 years, lives in a residential aged-care facility. She has heart failure and stage 3 chronic kidney disease. Her roommate was confirmed to have influenza A yesterday and has been moved. Mavis has no symptoms. She received this year's influenza vaccine eight days ago, when the facility ran a late clinic. The facility manager has declared an outbreak and asks for your advice about Mavis.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Furosemide 40 mg daily","Bisoprolol 2.5 mg daily","Perindopril 2.5 mg daily","Apixaban 2.5 mg twice daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, chest clear, SpO2 96% on room air"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Give a second influenza vaccine today","No action is needed, because she has been vaccinated this season","Offer antiviral prophylaxis, with the dose adjusted for her kidney function","Start oseltamivir at the treatment dose of 75 mg twice daily for 5 days","Take a swab and start antibiotics if she develops a fever within the next 7 days of close observation"],"a":2,"x":["A second dose does not provide rapid protection.","She was vaccinated under two weeks ago, so she is not yet protected.","Correct. She is at high risk, was exposed within 48 hours, and was vaccinated less than two weeks ago. Prophylaxis is considered, and it is part of outbreak management in residential care.","She has no symptoms. The treatment regimen is for people with influenza.","Antibiotics do not prevent or treat influenza."],"e":"Problem: a high-risk aged-care resident exposed to influenza during an outbreak, eight days after vaccination. Task: recognise an indication for prophylaxis. What if she had been vaccinated three months ago and there were no outbreak? Prophylaxis would not be routine.","k":"Influenza prophylaxis: high-risk person exposed within 48 hours and not protected by vaccine, or a residential care outbreak.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Influenza"],"c":["rs15-11","rs15-12","rs15-01","rs15-02","rs15-03"],"id":"rs-a91","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Influenza and pertussis","nt":"rs-flu#flutreat","n":4,"s":"During the influenza season you see several patients with confirmed, non-severe influenza. None requires ventilatory support.","q":"For which FOUR of the following patients is antiviral treatment recommended or to be considered? Select four.","o":["A 22-year-old university student with no medical conditions, on day 3","A 34-year-old woman who gave birth 8 days ago","A 40-year-old office worker with well-controlled hypertension, on day 4","A 58-year-old man admitted to hospital with influenza and dehydration","A 68-year-old woman with COPD, on day 2","A 9-year-old boy with no medical conditions, on day 3","An Aboriginal man aged 45 years, on day 1"],"sc":[0,1,0,1,1,0,1],"x":["Scores 0. A healthy young adult beyond 48 hours gains nothing.","Scores 1. Treatment is recommended in pregnancy and up to two weeks after birth.","Scores 0. Controlled hypertension alone is not on the higher-risk list.","Scores 1. Treatment is recommended for patients admitted to hospital.","Scores 1. Age 65 and over and chronic lung disease are both higher-risk factors.","Scores 0. Children under 5 are higher risk. A healthy 9-year-old is not.","Scores 1. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people of any age are in the higher-risk group."],"e":"Antivirals are recommended for people admitted to hospital and for pregnancy and the two weeks after birth. They are considered for others at higher risk: 65 and over, under 5, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people, residential care, and chronic disease.","k":"Influenza antivirals: recommended if admitted or pregnant; considered for other higher-risk groups.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Influenza"],"c":["rs15-03","rs15-04","rs15-05","rs15-06","rs15-07"],"id":"rs-k15","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiectasis","nt":"rs-bronch#diagnose","s":"Margaret Doyle, aged 61 years, is a retired teacher who has coughed up a few teaspoons of yellow sputum most mornings for four years. She has had antibiotics for 'bronchitis' three times in the past year, each time with slow recovery. She has never smoked. She had severe pneumonia at the age of six. A chest X-ray last month was reported as normal, and spirometry today shows no airflow obstruction.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Salbutamol inhaler as needed, with little effect","Esomeprazole 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 97% on room air","Coarse crackles at the left base that do not clear with coughing","No clubbing"]}],"q":"Which investigation is MOST appropriate to establish the diagnosis?","o":["Bronchial provocation testing","Bronchoscopy with washings","High-resolution CT of the chest","Repeat chest X-ray in 6 weeks, with inspiratory and expiratory films to look for areas of air trapping","Sputum cytology"],"a":2,"x":["This tests for asthma. Her picture is a chronic productive cough with coarse crackles, not variable airflow limitation.","Bronchoscopy does not diagnose bronchiectasis.","Correct. Chronic productive cough, coarse crackles and recurrent infections suggest bronchiectasis. High-resolution CT is the gold standard, and a normal X-ray and normal spirometry do not exclude it.","A chest X-ray is usually not adequate to diagnose bronchiectasis and may be normal.","Cytology looks for malignancy and does not show airway widening."],"e":"Problem: years of wet cough with recurrent infections, and two normal first-line tests. Task: know that neither excludes bronchiectasis. What if the CT confirms it? Send sputum for culture including mycobacteria, check immunoglobulins and Aspergillus serology, and refer for airway clearance.","k":"Suspected bronchiectasis: high-resolution CT. A normal chest X-ray and spirometry do not exclude it.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"],"c":["rs16-05","rs16-06","rs16-07","rs16-08","rs16-09"],"id":"rs-a92","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiectasis","nt":"rs-bronch#manage","s":"Peter Vasiliou, aged 58 years, was diagnosed with bronchiectasis on CT two months ago after years of productive cough. He has never smoked and has no history of asthma. Spirometry is normal. He feels well today, with his usual morning sputum. He is up to date with influenza and pneumococcal vaccines. He asks what he should be doing to 'stop it getting worse', and whether he needs 'a puffer like my brother has for his COPD'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Atorvastatin 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Coarse crackles at the right base"]}],"q":"Which management step is MOST appropriate now?","o":["Prescribe a long-acting bronchodilator and inhaled corticosteroid combination inhaler to reduce airway inflammation","Prescribe azithromycin three times a week","Prescribe doxycycline to keep at home and take whenever his sputum is yellow","Refer to a respiratory physiotherapist for an airway clearance program","Start nebulised hypertonic saline at home"],"a":3,"x":["Bronchodilators and inhaled corticosteroids are not routinely prescribed unless asthma or COPD is also present.","Long-term macrolides are a specialist decision for people with more than three exacerbations a year after other measures are optimised.","Purulent sputum alone is not an indication for antibiotics, and colonising organisms are not treated when he is stable.","Correct. Airway clearance, taught by a respiratory physiotherapist, is central to management, along with regular exercise and a written action plan.","Hypertonic saline can cause bronchospasm and is started after a supervised challenge."],"e":"Problem: newly diagnosed, stable bronchiectasis with no other lung disease. Task: start the treatments that work and avoid the ones that are reflexively prescribed. What if he also had asthma? Inhaled treatment would be given for the asthma.","k":"Stable bronchiectasis: airway clearance and exercise. Inhalers only for coexisting asthma or COPD.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis","Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of bronchiectasis in adults"],"c":["rs16-14","rs16-15","rs16-16","rs16-17","rs16-18"],"id":"rs-a93","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiectasis","nt":"rs-bronch#exac","s":"Helen Brooks, aged 67 years, has bronchiectasis. For four days she has been coughing more, producing twice her usual amount of sputum, which has turned from pale yellow to dark green, and she is too tired to do her daily walk. She is eating and drinking. She has no drug allergies. Her records show no sputum culture in the past two years. Her kidney function is normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Alendronate 70 mg weekly","Vitamin D 1000 units daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.4 degrees, respiratory rate 18/min","SpO2 96% on room air, her usual","Coarse crackles at both bases, unchanged"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange admission for intravenous ceftriaxone","Collect sputum for culture and wait for the result before choosing an antibiotic","Collect sputum for culture, then start amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours","Increase her airway clearance and review in one week","Start amoxicillin with clavulanate 875+125 mg every 12 hours without a sputum sample, as broad cover is needed in bronchiectasis"],"a":2,"x":["Intravenous therapy is for worsening respiratory distress or hypoxaemia, or failure of oral treatment.","Sputum is collected before treatment, but treatment is not delayed for the result.","Correct. She has four of the six features for more than 48 hours. Sputum is collected first, then empirical amoxicillin or doxycycline is started.","She meets the criteria for antibiotic treatment.","Amoxicillin with clavulanate is for suspected beta-lactamase-producing organisms, such as after a recent exacerbation caused by one. Sputum is still collected."],"e":"Problem: an exacerbation of bronchiectasis that meets the antibiotic criteria, with no past cultures to guide choice. Task: sample, then treat empirically. What if last month's sputum had grown a beta-lactamase-producing Haemophilus influenzae? Amoxicillin with clavulanate.","k":"Bronchiectasis exacerbation: sputum culture first, then amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours or doxycycline.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of bronchiectasis in adults"],"c":["rs16-20","rs16-21","rs16-22","rs16-23","rs16-24"],"id":"rs-a94","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiectasis","nt":"rs-bronch#manage","s":"Frank Nowak, aged 72 years, has bronchiectasis and attends for a routine review. He feels well. His cough and sputum are 'the same as always', he walks 3 km most days and does his airway clearance exercises each morning. A sputum sample sent at his last visit as a baseline has grown Haemophilus influenzae, sensitive to amoxicillin. He has had one exacerbation in the past year.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Perindopril 5 mg daily","Metformin 1 g twice daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, SpO2 97% on room air","Scattered coarse crackles, unchanged"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Continue current management and record the result to guide future exacerbations","Prescribe amoxicillin 1 g every 8 hours","Prescribe doxycycline 100 mg daily long term to suppress the organism and reduce his risk of exacerbations","Refer to a respiratory physician because of the sputum result","Repeat the sputum culture every month"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Airways in bronchiectasis are often colonised. Colonising organisms are not treated in a clinically stable patient, and the result will guide the choice at his next exacerbation.","He has no features of an exacerbation.","Long-term antibiotics are a specialist decision for frequent exacerbations. He has had one.","Referral is advised for resistant or unusual organisms such as Pseudomonas aeruginosa or nontuberculous mycobacteria.","Monthly cultures are not recommended."],"e":"Problem: a positive sputum culture in a well patient. Task: resist treating a result. What if the culture had grown Pseudomonas aeruginosa? Refer to a respiratory physician.","k":"Stable bronchiectasis with a positive sputum culture: do not treat colonising organisms.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (December 2025), Antibiotic management of bronchiectasis in adults","Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"],"c":["rs16-14","rs16-15","rs16-16","rs16-17","rs16-18"],"id":"rs-a95","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiectasis","nt":"rs-bronch#kids","s":"Ruby Anderson, aged 5 years, is brought by her father with a wet-sounding cough that has been present every day for seven weeks. She completed four weeks of amoxicillin with clavulanate prescribed at another practice, with no change. She had similar episodes lasting over a month twice last year. She was born at term, is fully immunised, and is growing along the 25th centile. No one at home smokes.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, respiratory rate 22/min, SpO2 98% on room air","Crackles at the right base","No clubbing, no chest wall deformity"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a high-resolution CT of the chest","Prescribe a further 4 weeks of amoxicillin with clavulanate","Reassure her father that a post-viral cough can last up to 3 months in a child of this age and review if it persists","Refer to a paediatric specialist for further investigation","Start a trial of inhaled salbutamol"],"a":3,"x":["Children are more sensitive to radiation. The decision to scan is made with the paediatric specialist.","A wet cough that persists beyond 4 weeks despite antibiotics is an indication for referral, not for more of the same.","A daily wet cough for seven weeks with focal crackles is not a post-viral cough.","Correct. A wet cough over 4 weeks despite antibiotics, recurrent prolonged episodes and persistent crackles each warrant paediatric referral to investigate for bronchiectasis.","A wet cough is not treated as asthma."],"e":"Problem: a chronic wet cough in a child that has not cleared with a long antibiotic course. Task: refer early, because airway damage may be reversible in children. What if this were her first wet cough, at three weeks? A course of antibiotics for protracted bacterial bronchitis would be considered at four weeks.","k":"Child with wet cough over 4 weeks despite antibiotics: paediatric referral for possible bronchiectasis.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"],"c":["rs16-12","rs16-13","rs16-01","rs16-02","rs16-03"],"id":"rs-a96","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Bronchiectasis","nt":"rs-bronch#refer","s":"Lorraine Hughes, aged 70 years, has bronchiectasis and has had her usual morning sputum for years. For three days it has been thicker and greener than usual, with thin streaks of blood mixed through it on two occasions. She is coughing more and feels tired. She is not breathless. She had a knee replacement two years ago and has atrial fibrillation.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Apixaban 5 mg twice daily","Metoprolol 50 mg twice daily","Naproxen 500 mg twice daily for knee pain, started last month"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Heart rate 78/min irregular, blood pressure 128/76 mmHg","SpO2 96% on room air","Coarse crackles at both bases"]}],"q":"In addition to assessing and treating her for an exacerbation, what is the MOST appropriate action?","o":["Arrange a CT pulmonary angiogram today","Arrange urgent transfer to hospital for bronchial artery embolisation","Reassure her and make no change, as minor haemoptysis is expected in bronchiectasis and needs no action","Review her need for naproxen and treat the infection","Stop apixaban until the bleeding settles"],"a":3,"x":["She is not breathless, and streaks in purulent sputum during an exacerbation do not suggest pulmonary embolism.","This is for massive haemoptysis.","Minor streaking is common, but medicines that worsen bleeding should still be reviewed.","Correct. Streaks of blood in purulent sputum are common and often signal infection. Drugs that may worsen bleeding, such as NSAIDs, are reviewed.","The guidance is to review such drugs. Stopping her stroke prevention for minor streaking is not justified, while the NSAID is the easier one to stop."],"e":"Problem: minor haemoptysis during an exacerbation in a woman on an anticoagulant and a recently started NSAID. Task: treat the infection and remove the avoidable bleeding risk. What if she coughed up a cup of bright blood? Immediate transfer to hospital.","k":"Minor haemoptysis in bronchiectasis: assess for infection and review NSAIDs and anticoagulants.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"],"c":["rs16-26","rs16-27","rs16-28","rs16-29","rs16-01"],"id":"rs-a97","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Bronchiectasis","nt":"rs-bronch#refer","n":3,"s":"You look after several adults with bronchiectasis in your practice and are reviewing who should be referred to a respiratory physician.","q":"Which THREE of the following patients should be referred to a respiratory physician? Select three.","o":["A man who asks for a written action plan","A man who has had four exacerbations in the past 12 months","A man whose spirometry is unchanged from last year","A woman who has had one exacerbation this year, treated with amoxicillin","A woman whose sputum has grown amoxicillin-sensitive Haemophilus influenzae while she is well","A woman whose sputum has grown Pseudomonas aeruginosa","A woman with repeated episodes of small-volume bright red haemoptysis"],"sc":[0,1,0,0,0,1,1],"x":["Scores 0. Every patient should have an action plan, written in general practice.","Scores 1. More than three exacerbations a year is an indication for referral.","Scores 0. Stable lung function is reassuring.","Scores 0. An occasional exacerbation that responds to treatment is managed in general practice.","Scores 0. This is colonisation in a stable patient and needs no treatment or referral.","Scores 1. Resistant or unusual organisms, including Pseudomonas aeruginosa, warrant referral.","Scores 1. Recurrent small-volume bright haemoptysis needs specialist assessment."],"e":"Stable bronchiectasis is managed in primary care. Refer for rapid progression, hospital admission, severe symptoms, poor response, more than three exacerbations a year, unusual organisms and haemoptysis.","k":"Refer bronchiectasis for frequent exacerbations, Pseudomonas or mycobacteria, and haemoptysis.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Bronchiectasis"],"c":["rs16-26","rs16-27","rs16-28","rs16-29","rs16-01"],"id":"rs-k16","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Tuberculosis","nt":"rs-tb#test","s":"Arjun Thapa, aged 27 years, moved from Nepal 14 months ago and works in a commercial kitchen. He has had a cough for two months, now with yellow sputum, and has woken with drenching sweats most nights for three weeks. His clothes are loose. He shares a flat with four other men. He does not smoke and takes no medicines. A chest X-ray shows a cavity in the right upper lobe.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Temperature 37.8 degrees, weight 54 kg, down 5 kg since his visa medical","SpO2 97% on room air","Crackles over the right upper zone"]}],"q":"Which request is MOST appropriate for his sputum?","o":["Acid-fast bacilli microscopy, mycobacterial culture and PCR","Cytology on three early-morning samples","Microscopy, culture and sensitivity with atypical pneumonia PCR","Microscopy, culture and sensitivity, with a note asking the laboratory to extend incubation for slow-growing organisms","No sputum test; request an interferon gamma release assay"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Tuberculosis is not usually detected by standard microscopy and culture. The request names acid-fast microscopy, mycobacterial culture and PCR, which can also detect resistance.","Cytology looks for malignancy.","This looks for the usual causes of pneumonia, not tuberculosis.","Standard culture will not usually detect mycobacteria, whatever the incubation time.","An interferon gamma release assay shows infection, not active disease, and does not replace sputum testing."],"e":"Problem: a classic picture of pulmonary tuberculosis with an upper-lobe cavity. Task: ask the laboratory for the right tests. What else is needed today? Advice from the tuberculosis service, notification, and infection control, as pulmonary disease is usually infectious.","k":"Suspected pulmonary tuberculosis: sputum for acid-fast microscopy, mycobacterial culture and PCR.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), Clinical presentations of tuberculosis; Investigations for tuberculosis"],"c":["rs17-04","rs17-05","rs17-06","rs17-07","rs17-08"],"id":"rs-a98","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Tuberculosis","nt":"rs-tb#monitor","s":"Grace Okafor, aged 46 years, started treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis three weeks ago under the hospital chest clinic. She presents to you because she has felt sick for four days, has vomited twice and has an ache under her right ribs. She has no rash and her vision is normal. She drinks no alcohol. Her liver tests were normal before treatment. Blood taken yesterday is shown.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Isoniazid 300 mg daily","Rifampicin 600 mg daily","Ethambutol 1200 mg daily","Pyrazinamide 1500 mg daily","Pyridoxine 25 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, no jaundice","Tender in the right upper quadrant"]}],"tb":{"h":["Test","Result","Reference range"],"r":[["ALT","168 U/L","under 40"],["AST","142 U/L","under 40"],["Bilirubin","16 micromol/L","under 20"]]},"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Continue all four drugs and add an antiemetic, as a rise in liver enzymes is common early in treatment and usually settles","Continue all four drugs and repeat the liver tests in two weeks","Stop all tuberculosis drugs and contact her treating team today","Stop isoniazid only and continue the others","Stop pyrazinamide only and continue the others"],"a":2,"x":["A minor rise is common, but hers is over 3 times the upper limit with symptoms.","Continuing risks severe liver injury.","Correct. Transaminases of 3 times the upper limit with symptoms, or 5 times without, or any jaundice, mean all drugs are withdrawn immediately. Her ALT is over 4 times the limit and she is symptomatic.","Isoniazid, rifampicin and pyrazinamide can each cause hepatitis. All drugs are stopped.","Stopping one drug leaves two other hepatotoxic drugs running."],"e":"Problem: symptomatic hepatitis in week 3 of tuberculosis treatment. Task: know the threshold for stopping everything. What happens next? Treatment is reintroduced by the specialist once ALT falls below 2 times the upper limit.","k":"Tuberculosis treatment: stop all drugs for jaundice, ALT 5 times normal, or 3 times normal with symptoms.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), Principles of managing tuberculosis; Monitoring tuberculosis treatment"],"c":["rs17-19","rs17-20","rs17-21","rs17-22","rs17-23"],"id":"rs-a99","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Tuberculosis","nt":"rs-tb#infection","s":"Sandra Mitchell, aged 54 years, has rheumatoid arthritis that has not responded to methotrexate. Her rheumatologist plans to start adalimumab and has asked you to arrange screening. She was born in Australia and worked as a nurse in Papua New Guinea for three years in her twenties. She has no cough, fever, sweats or weight loss. Her interferon gamma release assay is positive.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Methotrexate 20 mg weekly","Folic acid 5 mg weekly","Celecoxib 200 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, chest clear, no lymphadenopathy"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Advise the rheumatologist that adalimumab can start, as she has no symptoms","Arrange a chest X-ray and clinical assessment to exclude active tuberculosis","Notify her as a case of tuberculosis and arrange contact tracing for her household and close workplace contacts","Repeat the interferon gamma release assay in 3 months","Start the standard four-drug tuberculosis regimen"],"a":1,"x":["A positive test before immune suppression needs assessment, and treatment of infection is recommended in this group.","Correct. A positive test means infection. Active disease is excluded first, with history, examination and chest X-ray, before preventive treatment is planned with expert advice.","A positive assay without disease is not an infectious case.","Repeating the test does not answer the question of active disease.","Four-drug treatment is for active disease, which has not been shown."],"e":"Problem: a positive screening test before a biologic. Task: exclude active disease before anything else. What follows a normal chest X-ray? Preventive treatment, such as rifampicin for 4 months, chosen with specialist advice and ideally begun before the biologic.","k":"Positive interferon gamma release assay: exclude active tuberculosis first, then consider preventive treatment.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), Tuberculosis infection (previously known as latent tuberculosis)"],"c":["rs17-26","rs17-27","rs17-28","rs17-29","rs17-30"],"id":"rs-a100","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Tuberculosis","nt":"rs-tb#monitor","s":"Nguyen Van Minh, aged 63 years, is six weeks into treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis and attends for a repeat prescription of his blood pressure tablets. He mentions that for the past week the newspaper has looked blurred and the traffic lights 'look washed out'. He has stage 3 chronic kidney disease. He has no eye pain, headache or nausea.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Isoniazid 300 mg daily","Rifampicin 600 mg daily","Ethambutol 1200 mg daily","Pyrazinamide 1500 mg daily","Amlodipine 10 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Visual acuity 6/12 in each eye, previously 6/6","Makes several errors on colour plates","Eyes white, pupils equal and reactive"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a routine optometry review for new glasses","Check his blood glucose and HbA1c","Stop ethambutol today and refer urgently to an ophthalmologist","Stop isoniazid and add pyridoxine","Stop rifampicin, as it colours body fluids orange and can alter colour perception, and review his vision in a fortnight"],"a":2,"x":["New loss of acuity and colour vision on ethambutol is not a refractive problem.","This does not address a known drug toxicity.","Correct. Reduced acuity and colour vision on ethambutol indicate ocular toxicity. Ethambutol is stopped immediately with ophthalmology referral. Kidney impairment raises the risk because the drug accumulates.","Isoniazid rarely affects the eye. Ethambutol is the usual cause.","Rifampicin is not the cause of optic toxicity."],"e":"Problem: blurred vision and altered colour perception six weeks into four-drug treatment, in a man with kidney disease. Task: link the symptom to the drug and act the same day. Who else needs to know? His tuberculosis team, so the regimen can be adjusted.","k":"Visual change on ethambutol: stop it at once and refer to an ophthalmologist.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), Principles of managing tuberculosis; Monitoring tuberculosis treatment"],"c":["rs17-19","rs17-20","rs17-21","rs17-22","rs17-23"],"id":"rs-a101","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Tuberculosis","nt":"rs-tb#suspect","s":"Tevita Fifita, aged 41 years, moved from Tonga eight years ago and works as a labourer. Over six months he has seen three doctors for urinary frequency and stinging, and has had three courses of antibiotics without lasting benefit. He has seen blood in his urine twice. He has type 2 diabetes. He has no fever, cough or weight loss. Three midstream urine results are available.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Metformin 1 g twice daily","Gliclazide MR 60 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, abdomen soft, no loin tenderness","Prostate normal on examination"]}],"tb":{"h":["Midstream urine","Leucocytes","Red cells","Culture"],"r":[["March",">100 x10^6/L","40 x10^6/L","No growth"],["June",">100 x10^6/L","20 x10^6/L","No growth"],["September",">100 x10^6/L","60 x10^6/L","No growth"]]},"q":"Which investigation is MOST appropriate next?","o":["A fourth midstream urine for culture, taken before any further antibiotics and with extended incubation","Early-morning urine for acid-fast microscopy, mycobacterial culture and PCR","Prostate-specific antigen","Urine cytology on one sample","Urodynamic studies"],"a":1,"x":["Three sterile cultures have already answered this question.","Correct. Persistent sterile pyuria with haematuria and urinary symptoms, in a man from a country where tuberculosis is common, suggests genitourinary tuberculosis. Systemic symptoms are usually absent. Testing is best discussed with a microbiologist.","This does not explain sterile pyuria.","Haematuria does need a cancer work-up in its own right, but one cytology sample is not that work-up and does not explain the pyuria.","Urodynamics assess bladder function, not the cause of pyuria."],"e":"Problem: repeated 'urinary infections' with pus cells but no growth. Task: recognise sterile pyuria and think of tuberculosis. What if he also had a cough and night sweats? Sputum testing and a chest X-ray as well.","k":"Sterile pyuria with haematuria in a person at risk: test early-morning urine for tuberculosis.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), Clinical presentations of tuberculosis; Investigations for tuberculosis"],"c":["rs17-01","rs17-02","rs17-03","rs17-04","rs17-05"],"id":"rs-a102","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Tuberculosis","nt":"rs-tb#treat","s":"Amara Deng, aged 24 years, has just been diagnosed with drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis and started on standard four-drug treatment by the chest clinic. She attends your practice for a repeat of her contraceptive pill. She is a university student, has a regular partner and does not want to become pregnant. Her HIV test is negative and her baseline liver tests are normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Isoniazid, rifampicin, ethambutol and pyrazinamide daily","Levonorgestrel 150 micrograms with ethinylestradiol 30 micrograms daily"]}],"q":"Which statement about her treatment is MOST accurate?","o":["Ethambutol will be continued for the full 6 months","Her household contacts need no follow-up because she has started treatment","Her treatment will last 12 months in total, with all four drugs taken for the first six months and two thereafter","Rifampicin interacts with her contraceptive pill, so her contraception needs review","She will remain infectious until she completes treatment"],"a":3,"x":["Ethambutol and pyrazinamide are given for the first 2 months only.","Contact tracing is part of managing every case and is usually run by the tuberculosis program.","Standard treatment is 6 months: 2 months of four drugs, then 4 months of isoniazid and rifampicin.","Correct. Contraception is discussed before treatment because rifampicin interacts with oral contraceptives.","Fully susceptible pulmonary disease is usually non-infectious after about 5 days of daily standard treatment."],"e":"Problem: a young woman on the pill starting rifampicin. Task: spot the interaction that the chest clinic may assume the GP will handle. What else belongs to the GP? Watching adherence, weight, liver symptoms and vision between clinic visits.","k":"Rifampicin interacts with oral contraceptives: review contraception before tuberculosis treatment.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), Principles of managing tuberculosis; Monitoring tuberculosis treatment","Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), Short-course treatments for tuberculosis"],"c":["rs17-11","rs17-12","rs17-13","rs17-14","rs17-15"],"id":"rs-a103","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Tuberculosis","nt":"rs-tb#treat","n":4,"s":"Daniel Reyes, aged 35 years, has confirmed drug-susceptible pulmonary tuberculosis. The chest clinic will start standard four-drug treatment tomorrow and has asked you to complete his baseline work-up today.","q":"Which FOUR of the following should be done before treatment starts? Select four.","o":["Audiometry","Electrocardiogram","HIV test","Liver function tests","Thyroid function tests","Visual acuity and colour vision","Weight"],"sc":[0,0,1,1,0,1,1],"x":["Scores 0. Hearing is monitored with aminoglycosides, which are not part of standard treatment.","Scores 0. Not part of the baseline work-up for the standard regimen.","Scores 1. Tuberculosis is an indicator condition for HIV testing.","Scores 1. Three of the four drugs can cause hepatitis.","Scores 0. Not part of the baseline work-up.","Scores 1. Ethambutol can cause optic neuritis, so a baseline is needed.","Scores 1. Doses are based on weight."],"e":"Baseline before tuberculosis treatment: weight, liver and kidney function, full blood count, visual acuity and colour vision, HIV and hepatitis B and C serology, plus a discussion of adherence and contraception.","k":"Before tuberculosis treatment: weight, liver and kidney tests, vision, HIV and hepatitis serology.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Antibiotic (April 2026), Principles of managing tuberculosis; Monitoring tuberculosis treatment"],"c":["rs17-11","rs17-12","rs17-13","rs17-14","rs17-15"],"id":"rs-k17","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","nt":"rs-pleural#primary","s":"Callum Reid, aged 23 years, is a tall, slim apprentice electrician who felt a sudden sharp pain in the right side of his chest while watching television two hours ago. It catches when he breathes in. He feels slightly short of breath when he walks. He smokes ten cigarettes a day, has no medical history and takes no medicines. You work in a rural hospital emergency department. An inspiratory chest X-ray shows a right pneumothorax with no mediastinal shift.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Heart rate 88/min, blood pressure 124/72 mmHg","Respiratory rate 18/min, SpO2 97% on room air","Speaking in full sentences","Reduced breath sounds at the right apex"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange an expiratory chest X-ray to measure the pneumothorax more accurately","Give analgesia and observe him in the department","Give high-flow oxygen by non-rebreather mask for 4 hours to speed reabsorption of the pleural air","Insert an intercostal catheter","Perform needle decompression"],"a":1,"x":["Expiratory films are not recommended because they exaggerate the size.","Correct. He is clinically stable with no lung disease. A primary spontaneous pneumothorax of any size is managed conservatively at first, with analgesia, and oxygen only if hypoxaemic.","Oxygen is given if the patient is hypoxaemic, titrated to target saturation. His saturation is normal.","Drainage is considered for clinically unstable patients. It is painful and carries risk.","Needle decompression is for a tension pneumothorax in an unstable patient."],"e":"Problem: a first primary spontaneous pneumothorax in a stable young smoker. Task: resist the drain. What advice does he need before he leaves? Stop smoking, return promptly if symptoms recur, and review every 2 weeks until it has resolved.","k":"Stable primary spontaneous pneumothorax: analgesia and observation, regardless of size.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"],"c":["rs18-13","rs18-14","rs18-15","rs18-16","rs18-01"],"id":"rs-a104","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","nt":"rs-pleural#tension","s":"You are the doctor at a country football ground when a 28-year-old player is brought off after a heavy collision to the left side of his chest. Within minutes he becomes severely breathless and agitated, then drowsy. An ambulance is 25 minutes away. You have a first-response kit with oxygen, large-bore cannulas and a pulse oximeter. He has no known medical conditions.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Heart rate 138/min, blood pressure 78/50 mmHg","Respiratory rate 36/min, SpO2 82% on high-flow oxygen","No breath sounds on the left; trachea deviated to the right"]}],"q":"Where should the cannula be inserted?","o":["Left side, above the third rib in the midclavicular line","Left side, below the fifth rib in the midaxillary line, directed downwards towards the diaphragm","Left side, immediately beside the sternum in the fifth intercostal space","Right side, above the third rib in the midclavicular line","Right side, midaxillary line"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. He has a tension pneumothorax on the left: collapse, hypoxaemia, hypotension and absent breath sounds. Urgent needle decompression is performed above the third rib in the midclavicular line on the affected side, without waiting for imaging.","This is not the recommended site, and the needle should pass above a rib.","This is not a decompression site.","The trachea is pushed away from the affected side. The pneumothorax is on the left.","This is the wrong side."],"e":"Problem: collapse after chest trauma with signs of a left tension pneumothorax. Task: decompress without imaging, on the correct side and at the correct site. What follows? A chest drain as soon as possible.","k":"Tension pneumothorax: needle above the third rib, midclavicular line, on the affected side.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"],"c":["rs18-07","rs18-08","rs18-09","rs18-10","rs18-11"],"id":"rs-a105","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","nt":"rs-pleural#secondary","s":"Ronald Baxter, aged 71 years, has severe COPD. He presents to your rural practice with sudden right-sided chest pain and worsening breathlessness since this morning. He can speak in short sentences. He stopped smoking three years ago. The practice has an X-ray facility next door, and the film shows a moderate right pneumothorax. The nearest hospital is 40 minutes away by road.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium 18 micrograms daily","Budesonide with formoterol 400/12 micrograms twice daily","Salbutamol as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Heart rate 104/min, blood pressure 132/80 mmHg","Respiratory rate 26/min, SpO2 86% on room air","Reduced breath sounds on the right"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Give analgesia and oxygen titrated to his target saturation, and arrange transfer to hospital","Give analgesia and send him home with a repeat chest X-ray in two weeks, as conservative care is now standard for spontaneous pneumothorax","Give nebulised salbutamol and oral prednisolone, and review tomorrow","Perform needle decompression before transfer","Start bilevel positive airway pressure while awaiting the ambulance"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. This is a secondary spontaneous pneumothorax. Initial care is analgesia and oxygen if hypoxaemic, but hospital admission and an intercostal catheter are usually needed.","Conservative outpatient care applies to stable primary spontaneous pneumothorax, not to a hypoxaemic man with severe COPD.","This treats an exacerbation and ignores the pneumothorax on the film.","He is not clinically unstable. Needle decompression is for tension pneumothorax.","Positive pressure ventilation should not be used with an untreated pneumothorax."],"e":"Problem: a pneumothorax in a man with severe COPD. Task: recognise that underlying lung disease changes the plan. What if his blood pressure fell and he became drowsy on the way? Treat as tension pneumothorax and decompress.","k":"Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax: analgesia, oxygen if hypoxaemic, hospital, usually a chest drain.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"],"c":["rs18-20","rs18-21","rs18-01","rs18-02","rs18-03"],"id":"rs-a106","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","nt":"rs-pleural#recur","s":"Marcus Lindgren, aged 26 years, is a graphic designer who had a left-sided spontaneous pneumothorax 14 months ago that resolved with observation. Last month he had a second pneumothorax on the left, again managed conservatively, and yesterday's chest X-ray shows that it has fully resolved. He feels well. He smokes cannabis mixed with tobacco most days. He has no other medical history and takes no medicines.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Chest clear with equal breath sounds"]}],"q":"In addition to advising him to stop smoking, what is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange spirometry every 6 months","Prescribe a salbutamol inhaler to use before exertion","Reassure him that no further action is needed now that the X-ray is clear, and review if symptoms return","Refer for consideration of pleurodesis","Repeat the chest X-ray every 3 months for a year"],"a":3,"x":["Spirometry does not predict or prevent recurrence.","He has no airway disease.","After a recurrence in the same lung the risk of further episodes rises sharply.","Correct. Recurrence in the same lung is the point at which referral for pleurodesis is recommended.","Surveillance films do not prevent recurrence."],"e":"Problem: a second pneumothorax on the same side. Task: know when prevention becomes surgical. What was his risk after the first episode? About 30 to 50%, higher because he smokes.","k":"Pneumothorax recurring in the same lung: refer for pleurodesis.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"],"c":["rs18-17","rs18-18","rs18-19","rs18-01","rs18-02"],"id":"rs-a107","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","nt":"rs-pleural#effusion","s":"Dorothy Whitfield, aged 74 years, is a retired seamstress with six weeks of increasing breathlessness and a dull ache in the right side of her chest. She has lost 4 kg. She smoked for 40 years and stopped at 60. She has no orthopnoea or ankle swelling and no fever. A chest X-ray shows a moderate right pleural effusion with a normal heart size. Her full blood count, kidney function and liver tests are normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amlodipine 5 mg daily","Paracetamol as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 94% on room air","Dull percussion note and reduced breath sounds at the right base","No peripheral oedema, jugular venous pressure not raised"]}],"q":"Which investigation is MOST appropriate to establish the cause?","o":["A trial of furosemide for two weeks with a repeat chest X-ray to see whether the effusion resolves","Bronchoscopy","Echocardiogram","Sputum cytology","Ultrasound-guided pleural aspiration"],"a":4,"x":["Nothing suggests heart failure, and a one-sided effusion with weight loss needs a diagnosis.","Bronchoscopy does not sample pleural fluid.","Her heart size is normal and she has no signs of heart failure.","This has a low yield and does not explain the effusion.","Correct. Management depends on the cause. Fluid is sampled under direct ultrasound guidance and sent for biochemistry, cytology and culture, including mycobacteria."],"e":"Problem: an unexplained one-sided effusion with weight loss in an ex-smoker. Task: sample the fluid. What if cytology shows malignant cells? Drainage for symptoms, with pleurodesis or an indwelling catheter if it refills.","k":"Unexplained pleural effusion: ultrasound-guided aspiration for biochemistry, cytology and culture.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pleural effusion in adults"],"c":["rs18-22","rs18-23","rs18-24","rs18-25","rs18-01"],"id":"rs-a108","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Pneumothorax and pleural effusion","nt":"rs-pleural#tension","n":3,"s":"A 34-year-old woman is brought to your rural emergency department after a fall from a horse onto her right side. She is in pain and breathing quickly.","q":"Which THREE of the following findings would indicate a tension pneumothorax needing immediate needle decompression? Select three.","o":["Blood pressure 80/50 mmHg","Bruising over the right ribs","Pain on taking a deep breath","Reduced breath sounds at the right apex with normal observations","Severe breathlessness that is rapidly worsening","SpO2 84% despite high-flow oxygen","Tenderness over two ribs"],"sc":[1,0,0,0,1,1,0],"x":["Scores 1. Hypotension is a sign of clinical instability.","Scores 0. This shows injury, not instability.","Scores 0. Pleuritic pain is expected with any rib injury or pneumothorax.","Scores 0. A pneumothorax in a stable patient does not need needle decompression.","Scores 1. Severe, rapidly worsening breathlessness is a sign of instability.","Scores 1. Hypoxaemia is a sign of instability.","Scores 0. Rib tenderness alone does not indicate tension."],"e":"Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis: a large pneumothorax in an unstable patient, with severe breathlessness, hypoxaemia, hypotension or collapse. Imaging is not needed first.","k":"Tension pneumothorax: severe breathlessness, hypoxaemia and hypotension. Diagnose clinically.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Pneumothorax"],"c":["rs18-07","rs18-08","rs18-09","rs18-10","rs18-11"],"id":"rs-k18","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","nt":"rs-osa#test","s":"Darren Kovac, aged 48 years, is a warehouse manager whose wife has moved to the spare room because of his snoring. She has seen him stop breathing at night. He wakes unrefreshed, has a headache most mornings and falls asleep in front of the television by 7 pm. His body mass index is 34 kg/m2 and his collar size is 46 cm. He has no heart or lung disease. His Epworth Sleepiness Scale score is 14 and his STOP-Bang score is 6.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Telmisartan 80 mg daily","Amlodipine 10 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Blood pressure 152/94 mmHg","Crowded oropharynx","Chest clear, heart sounds normal"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange overnight pulse oximetry at home and treat only if desaturation is recorded on at least two nights","Order a home-based sleep study through Medicare","Order an in-laboratory sleep study with carbon dioxide monitoring","Prescribe a trial of CPAP","Refer for palatal surgery"],"a":1,"x":["The standard diagnostic test is a sleep study.","Correct. His Epworth score is at least 8 and his STOP-Bang score at least 4, so a GP can order the study directly. A home-based study suits most patients.","A laboratory study is chosen for conditions such as heart failure, lung or neuromuscular disease, or suspected respiratory failure. He has none.","The diagnosis comes first.","Palatal surgery is now considered only in highly selected cases."],"e":"Problem: a classic history with positive questionnaires. Task: know that the GP can order the test, and which type. What should he be told about driving meanwhile? If his daytime sleepiness is severe, he should not drive until stable on treatment.","k":"Epworth 8 or more plus STOP-Bang 4 or more: the GP can order a home sleep study.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"],"c":["rs19-06","rs19-07","rs19-08","rs19-09","rs19-10"],"id":"rs-a109","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","nt":"rs-osa#drive","s":"Wayne Sutcliffe, aged 52 years, drives a delivery van for a courier company. His sleep study last week showed an apnoea-hypopnoea index of 48 events an hour. He tells you he has 'micro-sleeps' at red lights most afternoons and last month drifted onto the gravel shoulder of a highway. His appointment at the sleep clinic to start treatment is in three weeks. He is the sole earner for his family and asks you not to 'make a fuss'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Rosuvastatin 10 mg daily","Metformin 1 g twice daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Body mass index 37 kg/m2","Blood pressure 146/90 mmHg"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate advice about driving?","o":["He may continue to drive if he limits himself to daylight hours and takes a break every hour","He may continue to drive provided he drinks coffee before each shift and keeps the window open for fresh air","He may drive his own car but not the work van","He should stop driving permanently","He should stop driving until his condition is stabilised on treatment"],"a":4,"x":["His sleepiness occurs in the daytime, and breaks do not make severe sleepiness safe.","Caffeine and fresh air do not treat severe sleepiness.","The risk is the same in any vehicle.","The restriction lasts until treatment is working, not for life.","Correct. People with sleep apnoea causing severe daytime sleepiness are advised to stop driving and operating machinery until stabilised on treatment."],"e":"Problem: severe sleep apnoea with near-misses in a man who drives for a living. Task: give clear advice despite the pressure. What helps him most? Bringing forward his treatment, so ring the clinic. The Austroads standards and expert advice guide the detail.","k":"Sleep apnoea with severe daytime sleepiness: no driving until stable on treatment.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"],"c":["rs19-21","rs19-01","rs19-02","rs19-03","rs19-04"],"id":"rs-a110","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","nt":"rs-osa#cpap","s":"Linda Marchetti, aged 59 years, had a home sleep study after her husband complained about her snoring. It shows an apnoea-hypopnoea index of 11 events an hour. She sleeps seven hours, wakes refreshed and does not nap. Her Epworth score is 4. She works full-time as a bookkeeper. Her blood pressure has been normal at every visit. Her body mass index is 31 kg/m2 and she drinks two glasses of wine most evenings.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Estradiol patch 50 micrograms twice weekly","Micronised progesterone 100 mg at night"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Blood pressure 122/76 mmHg","Nasal airway clear"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Advise weight loss and less evening alcohol, and review","Arrange a CPAP titration study","Refer for a mandibular advancement splint and for palatal surgery, since she will need treatment for life","Repeat the sleep study in a laboratory","Start CPAP, because any index above 5 requires treatment"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. With mild to moderate sleep apnoea and no daytime dysfunction or hypertension, general measures and a watch-and-wait approach are reasonable.","CPAP is considered for severe disease, daytime dysfunction or hypertension. She has none.","Palatal surgery is rarely used, and she has no symptoms to treat.","The home study was adequate and nothing suggests a need for a laboratory study.","An index above 5 defines significant apnoea. The decision to treat depends on severity, symptoms and blood pressure."],"e":"Problem: a sleep study number without symptoms. Task: treat the patient, not the index. What would change the plan? Daytime sleepiness, hypertension, or an index over 30.","k":"Mild to moderate sleep apnoea without daytime symptoms or hypertension: general measures and review.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"],"c":["rs19-16","rs19-17","rs19-18","rs19-19","rs19-20"],"id":"rs-a111","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","nt":"rs-osa#cpap","s":"Graham Tully, aged 63 years, started CPAP two months ago for severe obstructive sleep apnoea. His daytime sleepiness has improved, but he wakes every night with a very dry mouth and is thinking of giving up. He already uses the machine's heated humidifier at its highest setting. His wife says his mouth falls open when he sleeps. He uses a nasal mask. His nose feels clear.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Perindopril 10 mg daily","Tamsulosin 400 micrograms daily"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add a chin strap","Advise him to stop CPAP and refer for a mandibular advancement splint","Prescribe an intranasal corticosteroid","Prescribe temazepam 10 mg at night for a week to help him sleep through the discomfort and settle into therapy","Stop tamsulosin"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. A dry mouth on CPAP usually indicates a mouth leak. A chin strap is tried. Humidification will not relieve it while the leak continues.","Splints are effective only in mild to moderate disease. His is severe and CPAP is working.","This helps nasal symptoms. His nose is clear.","A sedative does not address the leak.","Nothing links his symptom to tamsulosin, and the history points to a mouth leak."],"e":"Problem: a CPAP user about to abandon effective treatment over a fixable problem. Task: recognise mouth leak. What if his complaint were a blocked, dry nose? Humidification, intranasal saline or a corticosteroid spray.","k":"Dry mouth on CPAP means mouth leak: try a chin strap.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"],"c":["rs19-16","rs19-17","rs19-18","rs19-19","rs19-20"],"id":"rs-a112","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","nt":"rs-osa#suspect","s":"Tom Fraser, aged 29 years, has Down syndrome and lives in supported accommodation. His support worker reports that over six months he has become irritable in the afternoons, has stopped enjoying his day program and often falls asleep on the bus. Staff on night shift say he snores but have not noticed anything else. His weight is stable. His thyroid function, checked last month, is normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Levothyroxine 75 micrograms daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Body mass index 29 kg/m2","Large tongue, small oropharynx","Heart sounds normal, chest clear"]}],"q":"Which condition should be investigated FIRST?","o":["Absence seizures","Depression","Early-onset dementia","Obstructive sleep apnoea","Under-replacement of thyroid hormone, with a repeat thyroid function test after a six-week trial of a higher dose"],"a":3,"x":["Falling asleep on the bus is sleepiness, not a brief lapse of awareness.","Daytime sleepiness can be a marker of depression, but snoring with sleepiness in a person with Down syndrome points first to sleep apnoea.","Snoring with daytime sleepiness points to a sleep disorder, which is treatable and assessed first.","Correct. Obstructive sleep apnoea is close to universal in adults with Down syndrome and should be considered even without overt symptoms. Irritability and sleepiness are typical daytime effects.","His thyroid function was normal last month."],"e":"Problem: behaviour change and sleepiness in a man with Down syndrome. Task: think of sleep apnoea before attributing change to mood or cognition. Which sleep study? In-laboratory, because of his intellectual disability.","k":"Down syndrome: consider obstructive sleep apnoea in every adult, with or without symptoms.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"],"c":["rs19-01","rs19-02","rs19-03","rs19-04","rs19-05"],"id":"rs-a113","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Obstructive sleep apnoea","nt":"rs-osa#general","n":4,"s":"A 45-year-old man has been diagnosed with mild obstructive sleep apnoea, worse when he lies on his back. He has no daytime sleepiness and his blood pressure is normal. His body mass index is 32 kg/m2. He smokes and drinks three beers each night. He asks what he can do himself.","q":"Which FOUR of the following measures are recommended? Select four.","o":["A 'snore-easy' pillow","A sedative to deepen sleep","Avoiding alcohol in the evening","Nasal dilator strips","Sleeping on his side","Stopping smoking","Weight reduction"],"sc":[0,0,1,0,1,1,1],"x":["Scores 0. These have little supporting data.","Scores 0. Sedating drugs are avoided in sleep apnoea.","Scores 1. Avoiding alcohol and drugs that affect sleep is a general measure.","Scores 0. These are marketed for snoring but have little supporting data.","Scores 1. Positional therapy helps when apnoea is mainly supine.","Scores 1. Stopping smoking is recommended for all and can reduce nasal resistance.","Scores 1. Weight reduction is beneficial."],"e":"General measures for every adult with sleep apnoea: weight loss, stopping smoking, good sleep habits, avoiding alcohol and sedatives, an intranasal corticosteroid, and positional therapy for supine apnoea.","k":"Sleep apnoea self-care: lose weight, stop smoking, avoid alcohol, sleep on the side.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Obstructive sleep apnoea in adults"],"c":["rs19-14","rs19-15","rs19-01","rs19-02","rs19-03"],"id":"rs-k19","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Interstitial lung disease","nt":"rs-ild#workup","s":"Kenneth Rowe, aged 71 years, is a retired accountant with breathlessness on hills for 12 months and a dry cough. He smoked for 30 years and stopped at 55. He has never worked with dust, keeps no birds and takes no regular medicines other than those listed. He has no joint pain or rash. His chest X-ray shows reticular shadowing at both bases. Spirometry is shown.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Atorvastatin 40 mg daily","Aspirin 100 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 94% on room air, falling to 89% after walking the corridor","Fine crackles at both bases that do not clear with coughing","Finger clubbing","No ankle oedema"]}],"tb":{"h":["Spirometry","Result","Predicted"],"r":[["FEV1","2.05 L","72%"],["FVC","2.40 L","64%"],["FEV1/FVC","0.85",""]]},"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange high-resolution CT of the chest and refer to a respiratory physician","Prescribe a long-acting bronchodilator and review in 6 weeks","Prescribe prednisolone 50 mg daily for 4 weeks and repeat spirometry to assess whether the fibrosis is steroid responsive","Refer for pulmonary rehabilitation and review in 6 months","Start furosemide and arrange an echocardiogram"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Fine basal crackles, clubbing and restrictive spirometry suggest interstitial lung disease, probably idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. High-resolution CT is the pivotal test, and all suspected cases are referred.","His ratio is raised with a low FVC. This is restriction, not airflow obstruction.","Corticosteroid-based regimens are not recommended for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, and the diagnosis has not been made.","Rehabilitation helps, but diagnosis must not be delayed.","He has no signs of heart failure."],"e":"Problem: an older ex-smoker with velcro crackles, clubbing and restriction. Task: recognise the pattern and refer without delay, because antifibrotic drugs slow progression. What does a low FVC with a ratio of 0.85 mean? Restriction.","k":"Fine basal crackles, clubbing and restrictive spirometry: high-resolution CT and referral.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"],"c":["rs20-08","rs20-09","rs20-10","rs20-11","rs20-12"],"id":"rs-a114","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Interstitial lung disease","nt":"rs-ild#cause","s":"Beryl Hanson, aged 76 years, has had a dry cough and increasing breathlessness for three months. She can no longer hang out the washing without resting. She has never smoked, worked as a librarian, and has no pets. She had recurrent urinary tract infections until a urologist started a preventive antibiotic 14 months ago, and has had none since. Her chest X-ray shows interstitial shadowing at both bases.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Nitrofurantoin 50 mg at night","Vaginal estriol cream twice weekly","Candesartan 8 mg daily","Calcium and vitamin D"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 93% on room air","Fine crackles at both bases","No clubbing, no peripheral oedema"]}],"q":"In addition to referral, what is the MOST appropriate immediate action?","o":["Add an inhaled corticosteroid","Change candesartan to a calcium channel blocker, as drugs acting on the renin-angiotensin system are a recognised cause of cough","Start a course of doxycycline for atypical pneumonia","Stop estriol cream","Stop nitrofurantoin"],"a":4,"x":["Inhaled corticosteroids do not treat interstitial disease.","Candesartan does not explain interstitial shadowing and crackles.","Three months of progressive symptoms without fever is not pneumonia.","Topical estriol is not a cause of lung disease.","Correct. Nitrofurantoin is a recognised cause of drug-induced interstitial lung disease. The drug history is central to assessment, and the suspected drug is stopped."],"e":"Problem: new interstitial lung disease in a woman on long-term nitrofurantoin. Task: find the cause in the medication list. Which other common drugs do this? Amiodarone, methotrexate and leflunomide.","k":"New interstitial lung disease: check the drug list. Nitrofurantoin, amiodarone, methotrexate, leflunomide.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"],"c":["rs20-03","rs20-04","rs20-05","rs20-06","rs20-07"],"id":"rs-a115","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Interstitial lung disease","nt":"rs-ild#exposure","s":"Jayden Mills, aged 31 years, attends for a pre-employment medical. For nine years he has cut and polished engineered stone kitchen benchtops in a small workshop, mostly with a dry angle grinder and a paper dust mask. He feels well apart from a cough in the mornings, which he puts down to smoking five cigarettes a day. He runs twice a week. His new employer installs benchtops.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air","Chest clear","No clubbing"]}],"q":"Which condition is he MOST at risk of?","o":["Asbestosis","Coal worker's pneumoconiosis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Occupational asthma, which would be expected to improve on weekends and holidays away from the workshop","Silicosis from respirable crystalline silica"],"a":4,"x":["This follows asbestos exposure.","This follows coal dust exposure.","This follows repeated inhalation of an antigen, such as bird proteins.","His exposure is to respirable silica, and the feared outcome is fibrosis.","Correct. Cutting artificial stone, especially dry and without adequate respiratory protection, releases very high concentrations of respirable crystalline silica. Silicosis can be symptomless at first and can progress to massive fibrosis."],"e":"Problem: a well young man with nine years of dry-cutting engineered stone. Task: take the occupational history seriously when the examination is normal. What next? Health screening through the workplace safety scheme and respiratory referral.","k":"Engineered stone benchtop workers: silicosis, even when well and with a normal examination.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"],"c":["rs20-21","rs20-22","rs20-23","rs20-01","rs20-02"],"id":"rs-a116","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Interstitial lung disease","nt":"rs-ild#sarcoid","s":"Priya Nair, aged 34 years, had a chest X-ray for a visa application. It shows enlarged lymph nodes at both lung roots with clear lung fields. She feels well, runs 5 km three times a week and has no cough, fever, sweats or weight loss. She was born in Australia and has no known contact with tuberculosis. A respiratory physician has made a diagnosis of sarcoidosis and asked you to share her care. Her spirometry is normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Levonorgestrel intrauterine device"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air, chest clear","No rash, no red eyes, no enlarged peripheral nodes"]}],"q":"Which management is MOST appropriate now?","o":["Azathioprine","Inhaled budesonide twice daily","Observation, with a check of serum calcium and an eye review","Prednisolone 40 mg daily, reducing over six months, to prevent progression to pulmonary fibrosis","Standard four-drug tuberculosis treatment"],"a":2,"x":["Immunosuppression is not used for symptomless nodal disease.","Inhaled corticosteroids are not the treatment for sarcoidosis.","Correct. Disease limited to hilar nodes often resolves without treatment, so patients are observed. Sarcoidosis can affect the eyes and cause hypercalcaemia, so these are checked.","Oral corticosteroids are reserved for progressive symptoms, persistent infiltrates or falling lung function.","The diagnosis is sarcoidosis, made by the specialist."],"e":"Problem: symptomless sarcoidosis found by chance. Task: know that watching is the treatment, and what to look for outside the chest. What would prompt corticosteroids? Progressive breathlessness or declining lung function.","k":"Symptomless hilar sarcoidosis: observe, check calcium and eyes.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"],"c":["rs20-17","rs20-18","rs20-19","rs20-20","rs20-01"],"id":"rs-a117","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Interstitial lung disease","nt":"rs-ild#ipf","s":"Harold Simmons, aged 73 years, was diagnosed with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis four months ago by a multidisciplinary team. He is breathless walking to the letterbox. His daughter has read about treatments online and asks whether he should be on 'steroids and an immune suppressant like her friend's father was years ago'. His SpO2 is 93% at rest. He stopped smoking 20 years ago and has heartburn most nights.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Pantoprazole 40 mg daily, taken irregularly","Paracetamol as needed"]}],"q":"Which statement about his treatment is MOST accurate?","o":["An antifibrotic drug such as nintedanib or pirfenidone can slow progression","Home oxygen will relieve his breathlessness and should be started now","Prednisolone with azathioprine and acetylcysteine remains the standard first-line treatment and should be started by his specialist","Pulmonary rehabilitation is unsafe in pulmonary fibrosis","Treating his reflux has no bearing on his lung disease"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Nintedanib and pirfenidone slow progression in mild to moderate disease and are prescribed by specialists under eligibility criteria.","Long-term oxygen is for resting hypoxaemia and may not improve breathlessness.","This combination is no longer recommended because it increased morbidity and mortality.","Pulmonary rehabilitation is one of the beneficial therapies.","Reflux is a treatable comorbidity that can worsen symptoms."],"e":"Problem: a family asking for an outdated treatment. Task: know what helps and what harms in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. What belongs to the GP? Reflux, vaccination, rehabilitation referral, symptom control and early talk about advance care planning.","k":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis: antifibrotics slow it; prednisolone with azathioprine is harmful.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"],"c":["rs20-13","rs20-14","rs20-15","rs20-16","rs20-01"],"id":"rs-a118","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Interstitial lung disease","nt":"rs-ild#workup","n":4,"s":"A 58-year-old woman has eight months of exertional breathlessness, a dry cough and fine crackles at both bases. You suspect interstitial lung disease and plan to refer her.","q":"Which FOUR of the following initial investigations are recommended in general practice? Select four.","o":["Antinuclear antibodies and rheumatoid factor","Chest X-ray","D-dimer","Pulse oximetry","Serum tryptase","Spirometry","Sputum culture"],"sc":[1,1,0,1,0,1,0],"x":["Scores 1. Autoimmune serology helps identify connective tissue disease as a cause.","Scores 1. Part of the initial assessment, though it can be normal in early disease.","Scores 0. Not part of the assessment of chronic interstitial disease.","Scores 1. Part of the initial assessment.","Scores 0. Not relevant.","Scores 1. Looks for a restrictive defect.","Scores 0. She has a dry cough and no features of infection."],"e":"Initial assessment before referral: history, examination, pulse oximetry, spirometry, chest X-ray and blood tests including full blood count, autoimmune serology and calcium.","k":"Suspected interstitial lung disease: oximetry, spirometry, chest X-ray, autoimmune bloods, then refer.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Interstitial lung disease"],"c":["rs20-08","rs20-09","rs20-10","rs20-11","rs20-12"],"id":"rs-k20","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Allergic rhinitis","nt":"rs-rhinitis#incs","s":"Chloe Bennett, aged 26 years, is a primary school teacher in Canberra. Every spring for five years she has had a blocked nose, sneezing and itchy eyes from September to December. This year the blocked nose is waking her at night and she is tired in class. She has taken loratadine 10 mg daily for three weeks, which helps the sneezing but not the blockage. She has no asthma. She is not pregnant.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Loratadine 10 mg daily","Combined oral contraceptive pill"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Pale, swollen nasal mucosa on both sides with clear discharge","No polyps seen","Chest clear"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add an intranasal corticosteroid daily and teach her how to use it","Add an oxymetazoline nasal spray twice daily until the end of the pollen season in December","Add azelastine nasal spray to her loratadine","Change loratadine to promethazine at night","Refer for a CT scan of the sinuses"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. Her symptoms disturb sleep, so they are moderate to severe, and blockage is her main problem. Intranasal corticosteroids are the most effective option for obstruction.","Decongestants have no role in allergic rhinitis, and use beyond 3 to 5 days causes rebound congestion.","Combining an oral and an intranasal antihistamine gives no extra benefit.","Sedating antihistamines are not recommended.","CT is not recommended for uncomplicated rhinitis."],"e":"Problem: seasonal allergic rhinitis with blockage that an antihistamine has not relieved. Task: step up to the right class and teach technique. What should she be told about timing? Relief builds over days and peaks at 2 to 4 weeks. Next year, start 2 weeks before the season.","k":"Moderate to severe allergic rhinitis or nasal blockage: intranasal corticosteroid.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"],"c":["rs21-13","rs21-14","rs21-15","rs21-16","rs21-17"],"id":"rs-a119","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Allergic rhinitis","nt":"rs-rhinitis#technique","s":"Mitchell Green, aged 35 years, has perennial allergic rhinitis from dust mite allergy. He started mometasone nasal spray six weeks ago. His nose is clearer, but he has had streaks of blood on his tissue most mornings for two weeks, always from the right nostril. He is right-handed and shows you how he uses the spray: head tilted back, nozzle pointing straight up the middle of each nostril, with a hard sniff.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Mometasone 50 micrograms per spray, two sprays each nostril daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Small crusted area on the right side of the nasal septum","No perforation, no mass"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Change to an oral corticosteroid for two weeks","Correct his technique and reduce to the maintenance dose","Refer to an ear, nose and throat surgeon for cautery","Stop the spray permanently and use a decongestant spray","Stop the spray permanently and use loratadine alone, as nosebleeds mean he cannot tolerate nasal corticosteroids"],"a":1,"x":["Oral corticosteroids are not a substitute for correct use of a spray.","Correct. Minor bleeding is the commonest local effect. Pointing the nozzle away from the septum, using the opposite hand, head forward and a gentle sniff, reduces it. After the first 2 to 4 weeks the dose is stepped down.","The cause is spray landing on the septum, which technique will fix.","Decongestants have no role and cause rebound congestion.","The spray is working. The bleeding has a correctable cause."],"e":"Problem: septal bleeding from poor spray technique. Task: watch the patient use the device. Why the right nostril? A right-handed person spraying the right nostril aims at the septum. Using the left hand for the right nostril points the spray outwards.","k":"Nosebleeds on a nasal corticosteroid: fix technique (opposite hand, aim away from the septum) and step down.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"],"c":["rs21-19","rs21-20","rs21-21","rs21-01","rs21-02"],"id":"rs-a120","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Allergic rhinitis","nt":"rs-rhinitis#eyes","s":"Sharon Ellis, aged 57 years, has had a blocked nose 'for months'. She started an over-the-counter oxymetazoline spray during a cold in winter and now uses it four or five times a day, because her nose blocks completely within hours of each dose. She has no sneezing, itch or eye symptoms, no facial pain and no bleeding. Both sides are equally affected. She has hypertension.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Oxymetazoline nasal spray several times daily","Ramipril 5 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Blood pressure 138/84 mmHg","Red, swollen nasal mucosa on both sides","No polyps, no discharge"]}],"q":"What is the MOST likely diagnosis?","o":["Allergic rhinitis","Chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps","Drug-induced rhinitis from ramipril, which would be expected to resolve on changing to another class of antihypertensive","Nasal tumour","Rhinitis medicamentosa"],"a":4,"x":["She has no sneezing, itch or eye symptoms.","No polyps are seen, and she has no facial pain or discharge.","Some antihypertensives can cause rhinitis, but the pattern of blockage returning hours after each decongestant dose points to the spray.","Symptoms on both sides without bleeding make this unlikely.","Correct. Prolonged use of an intranasal decongestant causes rebound congestion, which can take several weeks to reverse. These sprays should not be used for more than 3 to 5 days."],"e":"Problem: months of blockage sustained by the spray used to treat it. Task: recognise rebound congestion. What would make you look harder? One-sided blockage, pain, or bloody discharge.","k":"Daily decongestant spray with worsening blockage: rhinitis medicamentosa. Limit use to 3 to 5 days.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"],"c":["rs21-22","rs21-23","rs21-24","rs21-25","rs21-26"],"id":"rs-a121","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Allergic rhinitis","nt":"rs-rhinitis#flags","s":"Giovanni Russo, aged 64 years, is a retired cabinet-maker. For three months his left nostril has been blocked, with a blood-stained discharge on that side most days. His right nostril is clear. He has no sneezing or itching and has never had hay fever. A pharmacist recommended an antihistamine and then a corticosteroid spray, which he has used correctly for six weeks with no change.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone nasal spray daily","Cetirizine 10 mg daily","Aspirin 100 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Left nasal airway blocked; view limited by crusting","Right side normal","No cervical lymphadenopathy"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add montelukast 10 mg daily","Arrange skin prick testing and refer for allergen immunotherapy","Change to a combined azelastine and fluticasone spray and review him again in a further four weeks","Refer to an ear, nose and throat surgeon","Stop aspirin and review in a month"],"a":3,"x":["This escalates treatment for a diagnosis he does not have.","Immunotherapy is for confirmed allergic rhinitis.","More of the same delays diagnosis.","Correct. One-sided obstruction with bloody discharge and no response to treatment points away from allergic rhinitis. He needs specialist assessment, and imaging may be considered.","Aspirin may add to bleeding but does not explain one-sided blockage."],"e":"Problem: a one-sided blocked nose with blood-stained discharge treated as hay fever. Task: recognise the features of another diagnosis. Which features are they? One-sided symptoms, pain, bloody or purulent discharge and recurrent nosebleeds.","k":"One-sided nasal blockage with bloody discharge is not allergic rhinitis: refer.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"],"c":["rs21-06","rs21-07","rs21-01","rs21-02","rs21-03"],"id":"rs-a122","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Allergic rhinitis","nt":"rs-rhinitis#refer","s":"Lucy Tran, aged 19 years, is a veterinary nursing student with year-round sneezing, a blocked nose and itchy eyes that are much worse at work. Skin prick testing through a previous clinic was strongly positive to cat and dog dander. For a year she has used a combined azelastine and fluticasone spray daily, with good technique, plus olopatadine eye drops. She still misses classes and sleeps poorly. She wants to stay in her career.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Azelastine with fluticasone nasal spray twice daily","Olopatadine eye drops twice daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Swollen pale nasal mucosa on both sides","Chest clear; spirometry normal"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add a sedating antihistamine at night","Add pseudoephedrine on work days","Advise her to change career, as no treatment can alter the course of animal dander allergy","Prescribe a two-week course of prednisolone each term","Refer to an allergist or immunologist for allergen immunotherapy"],"a":4,"x":["Sedating antihistamines are not recommended.","Decongestants have no role in allergic rhinitis.","Immunotherapy does alter the course of the disease and is effective for animal dander.","Repeated oral corticosteroids are not part of the recommended approach.","Correct. She has moderate to severe symptoms poorly controlled on treatment and cannot avoid the allergen. These are indications for referral for immunotherapy, the only treatment that alters the course of the condition."],"e":"Problem: persistent, disabling allergic rhinitis despite good treatment, with an allergen she cannot avoid. Task: know when to refer. What should she expect? A course of 3 to 5 years, by injection under supervision or under the tongue at home.","k":"Allergic rhinitis uncontrolled on sprays, or unavoidable allergen: refer for immunotherapy.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"],"c":["rs21-27","rs21-28","rs21-29","rs21-01","rs21-02"],"id":"rs-a123","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Allergic rhinitis","nt":"rs-rhinitis#technique","n":4,"s":"A 30-year-old man is starting an intranasal corticosteroid for allergic rhinitis. You are teaching him how to use it.","q":"Which FOUR of the following instructions are correct? Select four.","o":["Aim the nozzle towards the septum in the middle of the nose","Blow your nose gently before using the spray","Sniff as hard as you can while spraying","Tilt your head back as far as possible","Tilt your head forward and look down","Use it every day; the full benefit takes 2 to 4 weeks","Use your right hand for the left nostril and your left hand for the right"],"sc":[0,1,0,0,1,1,1],"x":["Scores 0. Aim away from the septum, towards the outer wall and the ear.","Scores 1. Clearing the nose first lets the spray reach the lining.","Scores 0. A hard sniff sends the liquid down the throat.","Scores 0. The head is tilted forward.","Scores 1. This is the correct head position.","Scores 1. Relief is not immediate and depends on consistent use.","Scores 1. The crossover technique directs the spray away from the septum."],"e":"Nasal spray technique: prime and shake, blow the nose, head forward, opposite hand, aim outwards, sniff gently. Poor technique is a common reason for apparent treatment failure and for nosebleeds.","k":"Nasal spray: head forward, opposite hand, aim away from the septum, gentle sniff, every day.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory (updated June 2026), Allergic rhinitis"],"c":["rs21-19","rs21-20","rs21-21","rs21-01","rs21-02"],"id":"rs-k21","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Inhaler devices","nt":"rs-devices#older","s":"Edna Pritchard, aged 83 years, has COPD and lives alone. Her breathlessness has worsened over three months and she has had two exacerbations. She was changed last year from a puffer and spacer to a once-daily dry powder inhaler 'to make things simpler'. She is thin and frail, and tires quickly when talking. When you ask her to show you how she uses the inhaler, her breath in is slow and shallow and you hear no click or rattle from the device.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Umeclidinium with vilanterol dry powder inhaler, one inhalation daily","Salbutamol puffer as needed, used without a spacer","Furosemide 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 93% on room air","Quiet breath sounds, no wheeze","Hands move freely, no deformity"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add an inhaled corticosteroid as a second dry powder inhaler","Add oral prednisolone 10 mg daily","Change to the same drug classes delivered by a puffer or mist inhaler with a spacer","Continue the same inhaler and ask her to breathe in harder and faster, with a review of her technique in three months","Refer for home oxygen"],"a":2,"x":["Stepping up treatment comes after technique is checked, and a second powder device adds the same problem.","Long-term oral corticosteroids are not the answer to a delivery problem.","Correct. Dry powder inhalers need a strong inspiratory flow. With reduced flow the choice is a puffer with or without a spacer, and dry powder and breath-actuated devices are avoided.","She cannot generate the flow, so more instruction will not solve it. When a device cannot be used correctly, change it.","Her saturation does not suggest a need for oxygen, and the problem is drug delivery."],"e":"Problem: worsening COPD control in a frail woman on a dry powder inhaler she cannot inhale from. Task: check technique before stepping up, and match the device to the patient. What else needs fixing? Her salbutamol puffer should go through the spacer too.","k":"Weak inspiratory flow: avoid dry powder inhalers; use a puffer with a spacer.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"],"c":["rs22-16","rs22-17","rs22-18","rs22-01","rs22-02"],"id":"rs-a124","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Inhaler devices","nt":"rs-devices#kids","s":"Oscar Daly, aged 2 years, has had three episodes of wheeze with colds that responded to salbutamol in the emergency department. His parents want to be ready for the next episode. His mother asks whether they should buy a nebuliser, because 'that is what the hospital used the first time'. He is otherwise well and growing normally. Nobody at home smokes.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Well, chest clear today","Weight 13 kg"]}],"q":"Which device is MOST appropriate for giving him salbutamol at home?","o":["A breath-actuated puffer","A dry powder inhaler","A home nebuliser with a face mask, because children of this age cannot cooperate with any hand-held device","A puffer with a spacer and a tightly fitting face mask","A puffer with a spacer and mouthpiece"],"a":3,"x":["A two-year-old cannot trigger a breath-actuated device, and it cannot be used with a spacer.","Dry powder devices need a strong, deliberate breath in.","A puffer with a spacer is more effective than a nebuliser for reliever in acute wheeze, and needs no cooperation beyond normal breathing.","Correct. A puffer with a spacer is preferred for all children, with a face mask under 4 years or when the child cannot seal the lips around a mouthpiece.","At two he cannot reliably seal his lips around a mouthpiece."],"e":"Problem: choosing a reliever device for a toddler. Task: know that a spacer and mask beats a nebuliser. How many breaths per puff? Four to six normal breaths.","k":"Children under 4: puffer with spacer and face mask.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"],"c":["rs22-14","rs22-15","rs22-01","rs22-02","rs22-03"],"id":"rs-a125","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Inhaler devices","nt":"rs-devices#older","s":"Joyce Kemp, aged 74 years, has asthma that was well controlled until this year. She has rheumatoid arthritis with marked deformity of both hands. She admits she often gives up on her preventer because she 'cannot squeeze the canister any more'. Her inspiratory effort is good: she can blow out candles and sing in her choir. She manages her own medicines and her memory is sharp.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone with salmeterol puffer 125/25 micrograms, two puffs twice daily through a spacer","Salbutamol puffer as needed","Methotrexate 15 mg weekly","Folic acid 5 mg weekly"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Ulnar deviation and weak pinch grip in both hands","Scattered expiratory wheeze"]}],"q":"Which change is MOST appropriate?","o":["Add montelukast and continue the same puffer","Change to a breath-actuated dry powder inhaler containing the same drug classes","Change to nebulised budesonide twice daily","Double the prescribed dose of her current puffer so that the doses she does manage to take deliver more drug","Refer to a respiratory physician for biologic therapy"],"a":1,"x":["Adding a drug does not solve the problem that she cannot take her preventer.","Correct. For reduced manual dexterity the options are a breath-actuated device, or help with a puffer such as a Haleraid or leaving it attached to the spacer. Her inspiratory flow is good, so a dry powder inhaler suits her.","Nebulisers need higher doses, take longer and need servicing.","A higher dose does not help if she cannot actuate the device.","Her asthma is uncontrolled because of a device problem, not because standard treatment has failed."],"e":"Problem: asthma control lost because arthritic hands cannot press a puffer. Task: match the device to hand function and breath strength. What if her inspiratory flow were also poor? Keep the puffer, left attached to the spacer, with a Haleraid.","k":"Poor hand function with good inspiratory flow: breath-actuated or dry powder inhaler.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"],"c":["rs22-16","rs22-17","rs22-18","rs22-01","rs22-02"],"id":"rs-a126","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Inhaler devices","nt":"rs-devices#principles","s":"Brett Callaghan, aged 44 years, has asthma. He attends because he is waking with wheeze twice a week and using salbutamol most days. He collects his preventer every month and says he takes it morning and night. He does not smoke and has no new pets or work exposures. He asks for 'something stronger'. He has never been shown how to use an inhaler since he was a teenager.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Budesonide with formoterol puffer 100/3 micrograms, two puffs twice daily","Salbutamol puffer as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Chest clear, SpO2 98% on room air","Peak flow 80% of his best"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Add a long-acting muscarinic antagonist","Add montelukast 10 mg at night","Ask him to show you how he uses his inhaler","Double the dose of his preventer","Prescribe a short course of prednisolone and review him in two weeks to decide whether a higher preventer dose is needed"],"a":2,"x":["Adding a drug comes after technique and adherence are checked.","The same applies.","Correct. Poor technique is common and causes apparent treatment failure. Technique is assessed at every opportunity and always before stepping up.","A higher dose through poor technique delivers little more and adds adverse effects.","He is not having an exacerbation that needs oral corticosteroids."],"e":"Problem: poorly controlled asthma in a man who is collecting and taking his preventer. Task: watch him use it before changing anything. What simple addition helps most puffer users? A spacer.","k":"Poor asthma control: check inhaler technique before stepping up.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"],"c":["rs22-01","rs22-02","rs22-03","rs22-04","rs22-05"],"id":"rs-a127","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Inhaler devices","nt":"rs-devices#nebs","s":"Albert Schofield, aged 79 years, has severe COPD. His daughter bought him a home nebuliser, and for a month he has used nebulised salbutamol and ipratropium four times a day through a face mask. This morning he developed severe pain in his right eye with blurred vision, haloes around lights and nausea. He has not injured the eye.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Nebulised salbutamol 5 mg and ipratropium 500 micrograms four times daily by face mask","Tiotropium 18 micrograms daily","Amlodipine 5 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Right eye red, cornea hazy, pupil mid-dilated and fixed","Visual acuity: right hand movements, left 6/9"]}],"q":"What is the MOST likely cause of his eye condition?","o":["Corneal abrasion from the edge of the mask","Giant cell arteritis","Ipratropium mist reaching the eye from the face mask","Salbutamol-induced rise in blood pressure causing a retinal vein occlusion, which would be expected to be painless","Viral conjunctivitis"],"a":2,"x":["An abrasion does not cause a fixed mid-dilated pupil or haloes.","This does not explain a red eye with a hazy cornea and a fixed pupil.","Correct. A nebulised short-acting muscarinic antagonist given by mask can cause local adverse effects, including acute angle-closure glaucoma. A mouthpiece avoids this. He needs urgent eye assessment today.","The picture is a painful red eye, not painless visual loss.","Conjunctivitis does not reduce vision to hand movements or fix the pupil."],"e":"Problem: acute angle-closure glaucoma in a man nebulising ipratropium by mask. Task: link the device to the complication. What should replace the nebuliser? A puffer with a spacer, which delivers reliever at least as well.","k":"Nebulised ipratropium by mask can cause acute glaucoma: use a mouthpiece, or a puffer and spacer.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"],"c":["rs22-19","rs22-20","rs22-21","rs22-01","rs22-02"],"id":"rs-a128","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Inhaler devices","nt":"rs-devices#spacers","n":4,"s":"A 60-year-old woman with asthma uses a preventer puffer directly into her mouth. You recommend a spacer and she asks why.","q":"Which FOUR of the following are benefits of using a spacer with a puffer? Select four.","o":["It allows a dry powder inhaler to be used by a person with weak breath","It improves the amount of drug reaching the lungs","It means the dose can be halved","It reduces local adverse effects of inhaled corticosteroids","It reduces the amount of drug deposited in the mouth and throat","It removes the need to shake the inhaler","It removes the need to time the puff with the breath in"],"sc":[0,1,0,1,1,0,1],"x":["Scores 0. Dry powder inhalers cannot be used with a spacer.","Scores 1. Lung delivery improves.","Scores 0. The prescribed dose stays the same.","Scores 1. Less local deposition means fewer local effects.","Scores 1. Less drug lands in the mouth and throat.","Scores 0. The inhaler is still prepared in the usual way.","Scores 1. Coordination of actuation and inhalation is no longer needed."],"e":"A spacer is used with a puffer or mist inhaler. It removes the coordination problem, improves lung delivery and reduces oropharyngeal deposition. A small-volume spacer is usually preferred.","k":"Spacer: better lung delivery, less mouth deposition, no coordination needed.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Inhalational drug delivery devices"],"c":["rs22-10","rs22-11","rs22-12","rs22-13","rs22-01"],"id":"rs-k22","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","nt":"rs-oxygen#not","s":"Leonard Marsh, aged 69 years, has severe COPD and is breathless walking to the bathroom. He stopped smoking five years ago, has completed pulmonary rehabilitation and uses his inhalers correctly. He has been stable for three months. His neighbour has home oxygen and 'swears by it', and he asks you to arrange it. Arterial blood gases taken at rest on room air are shown.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Fluticasone furoate, umeclidinium and vilanterol inhaler daily","Salbutamol as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 93% on room air at rest","No ankle oedema, jugular venous pressure not raised"]}],"tb":{"h":["Arterial blood gas on room air","Result"],"r":[["pH","7.40"],["PaO2","66 mmHg"],["PaCO2","42 mmHg"],["Haemoglobin","148 g/L"]]},"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange an oxygen concentrator for use 15 hours a day","Arrange portable oxygen cylinders for use only when he is walking or showering, to relieve his breathlessness on exertion","Explain that oxygen will not help him, and review other ways to manage breathlessness","Prescribe oxygen for use overnight only","Repeat the blood gases after a 6-minute walk"],"a":2,"x":["Long-term oxygen prolongs life when PaO2 is 55 mmHg or less, or 56 to 59 with complications of hypoxaemia. His is 66.","Oxygen should not be prescribed to treat breathlessness in the absence of hypoxaemia.","Correct. Home oxygen is not indicated for a patient whose main complaint is breathlessness and whose PaO2 is above 60 mmHg.","Nocturnal oxygen is considered for demonstrated night-time hypoxaemia after a sleep study.","Eligibility is based on resting blood gases when stable."],"e":"Problem: a breathless man with severe COPD who is not hypoxaemic. Task: explain that oxygen treats hypoxaemia, not breathlessness. What would change the answer? A resting PaO2 of 55 mmHg or less, or 56 to 59 with polycythaemia, pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure.","k":"Home oxygen is for hypoxaemia (PaO2 55 mmHg or less), not for breathlessness.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"],"c":["rs23-06","rs23-07","rs23-01","rs23-02","rs23-03"],"id":"rs-a129","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","nt":"rs-oxygen#review","s":"Pamela Ogden, aged 72 years, was admitted seven weeks ago with a severe exacerbation of COPD and sent home with an oxygen concentrator, to use 16 hours a day. No follow-up was arranged. She has recovered well, is back to her usual walking and has not smoked for eight years. She finds the tubing a nuisance and asks whether she needs to keep using it. Her SpO2 on room air in your rooms, after 20 minutes off oxygen, is 94%.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium with olodaterol mist inhaler daily","Salbutamol as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Respiratory rate 16/min, chest clear","No peripheral oedema"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Advise her to continue oxygen for life, because stopping long-term oxygen once started shortens survival in COPD","Advise her to stop oxygen today and return the concentrator","Arrange reassessment with arterial blood gases on room air now that she is stable","Continue oxygen and review in 12 months","Reduce her oxygen use to night-time only"],"a":2,"x":["More than a third of people started on oxygen at discharge no longer need it at 4 to 8 weeks.","The decision rests on blood gases on room air, which have not been done since she recovered.","Correct. Oxygen started at discharge is short term. Review when stable includes repeat blood gases on room air to see whether it is still indicated.","Annual review follows the first reassessment, which is overdue.","Changing the pattern without reassessment is guesswork."],"e":"Problem: oxygen started in hospital and never reviewed. Task: reassess need once stable. How should the news be framed if it is stopped? As a sign that she has recovered well.","k":"Oxygen started at hospital discharge: reassess with blood gases on air when stable.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"],"c":["rs23-13","rs23-14","rs23-01","rs23-02","rs23-03"],"id":"rs-a130","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","nt":"rs-oxygen#not","s":"Raymond Cutler, aged 66 years, has had home oxygen for COPD for two years. At his annual review his wife tells you, in front of him, that he has started smoking again, 'four or five a day on the back step', and that last week he lit one with the cannula still on. He says the oxygen is the only thing that helps and that he is careful. His SpO2 on 2 litres a minute is 93%.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Home oxygen 2 L/min, 16 hours a day","Tiotropium 18 micrograms daily","Budesonide with formoterol 400/12 micrograms twice daily"]}],"q":"Which statement is MOST accurate?","o":["He can continue oxygen safely provided he smokes only outdoors and turns the concentrator off before lighting a cigarette","His oxygen flow should be increased to compensate for the effect of smoking","Home oxygen is not prescribed for people who continue to smoke, because of the risk of fire","Smoking affects only the benefit of oxygen and carries no safety concern","The fire risk applies to cylinders but not to concentrators"],"a":2,"x":["Continuing to smoke is the contraindication, wherever he does it, and he has already lit a cigarette with the cannula on.","Flow is not adjusted for smoking.","Correct. Continuing to smoke is a contraindication to home oxygen because of fire. His treating specialist needs to know, and he needs help to stop.","Fire is the central concern.","A concentrator delivers 90 to 95% oxygen. The risk is the same."],"e":"Problem: a man on home oxygen who has relapsed to smoking. Task: recognise a contraindication and act on it with him, not around him. What comes with that conversation? An offer of smoking cessation treatment and prompt contact with his specialist.","k":"Home oxygen is contraindicated in people who continue to smoke: fire risk.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"],"c":["rs23-06","rs23-07","rs23-01","rs23-02","rs23-03"],"id":"rs-a131","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","nt":"rs-oxygen#nofly","s":"Ethan Wallace, aged 25 years, had a spontaneous left pneumothorax 12 days ago that was managed conservatively. A chest X-ray taken four days ago showed that it had fully resolved. He feels well and is back at work. He is booked on a flight from Brisbane to Bali in two days for a friend's wedding and asks whether he can go. He does not smoke and has no other medical history.","b":[{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 99% on room air","Equal breath sounds"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate advice?","o":["He can fly now if he carries a letter from you and sits near the front of the cabin where pressure changes are smaller","He can fly now, because the X-ray shows full resolution","He should delay flying until 1 week after the X-ray that showed full resolution","He should delay flying until 6 weeks after the pneumothorax occurred","He should never fly again"],"a":2,"x":["Neither a letter nor seat position changes the advice.","Air travel is delayed for a week after full radiological resolution of a spontaneous pneumothorax. That X-ray was four days ago.","Correct. Gas in the pleural space expands by about a third at cabin altitude. After a spontaneous pneumothorax, flying is delayed for 1 week after full resolution on X-ray. He can fly in three days.","This is longer than recommended.","A resolved pneumothorax is not a permanent bar to flying."],"e":"Problem: a flight booked six days after X-ray resolution of a pneumothorax. Task: apply the 1-week rule, counted from resolution. What if it had followed a rib fracture? Two weeks from full resolution.","k":"Flying after spontaneous pneumothorax: wait 1 week from full resolution on X-ray; 2 weeks after trauma or surgery.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"],"c":["rs23-18","rs23-19","rs23-20","rs23-21","rs23-22"],"id":"rs-a132","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","nt":"rs-oxygen#nofly","s":"Valerie Stone, aged 70 years, has moderate COPD and plans to fly from Sydney to London in two months to meet her first grandchild. She has not flown for ten years. She walks 400 metres on the flat before stopping and has had no exacerbations this year. She does not use oxygen at home. She asks whether she needs 'a note for the airline'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Umeclidinium with vilanterol inhaler daily","Salbutamol as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 92% on room air at rest","FEV1 48% predicted","Chest clear"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Advise her that she is unfit to fly","Give her a letter stating she is fit to fly without oxygen, since she can walk further than 50 metres","Prescribe a course of prednisolone to start the day before the flight to protect against an exacerbation at altitude","Reassure her that no assessment is needed","Refer for assessment for in-flight oxygen, including a hypoxic challenge test"],"a":4,"x":["She can walk well over 50 metres and does not need high-flow oxygen at sea level.","The walk test is used only where hypoxic challenge testing is unavailable, and her resting saturation is below 95%.","This is not a recommended preparation for flying.","No assessment is needed only when disease is stable, there were no past problems and resting SpO2 is 95% or more. Hers is 92%.","Correct. A resting SpO2 under 95% is a reason to consider in-flight oxygen, assessed by a hypoxic challenge test in a respiratory laboratory. Oxygen is usually given if SpO2 falls below 85% in the test."],"e":"Problem: stable COPD with a resting saturation of 92% before a long-haul flight. Task: know the 95% threshold for further assessment. What else should she plan? Reliever in hand luggage, and oxygen for airports and stopovers arranged with the airline well ahead.","k":"Resting SpO2 under 95% before flying: assess for in-flight oxygen with a hypoxic challenge test.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Fitness to fly for patients with respiratory disease"],"c":["rs23-18","rs23-19","rs23-20","rs23-21","rs23-22"],"id":"rs-a133","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Home oxygen and fitness to fly","nt":"rs-oxygen#who","n":3,"s":"You are reviewing patients with COPD to decide who should be referred for assessment for long-term home oxygen. All have stable disease on optimal treatment and resting blood gases on room air.","q":"Which THREE of the following patients meet the criteria for long-term oxygen? Select three.","o":["A man with PaO2 52 mmHg who stopped smoking 2 years ago","A man with PaO2 54 mmHg who smokes 10 cigarettes a day","A man with PaO2 58 mmHg and right heart failure, who stopped smoking 6 months ago","A man with PaO2 68 mmHg and SpO2 of 86% during a walk test","A woman with PaO2 55 mmHg who has never smoked","A woman with PaO2 58 mmHg and no complications of hypoxaemia","A woman with PaO2 63 mmHg and severe breathlessness"],"sc":[1,0,1,0,1,0,0],"x":["Scores 1. PaO2 is 55 mmHg or less and he no longer smokes.","Scores 0. Oxygen is not prescribed to people who continue to smoke.","Scores 1. PaO2 56 to 59 mmHg with right heart failure meets the criterion.","Scores 0. Long-term continuous oxygen is based on resting PaO2.","Scores 1. PaO2 of 55 mmHg or less meets the criterion.","Scores 0. Between 56 and 59 mmHg, oxygen is indicated only with complications of hypoxaemia.","Scores 0. Breathlessness without hypoxaemia is not an indication."],"e":"Long-term oxygen prolongs survival in stable COPD with resting PaO2 of 55 mmHg or less, or 56 to 59 with polycythaemia, pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure. The patient must not smoke.","k":"Long-term oxygen: PaO2 55 or less, or 56 to 59 with complications, in a non-smoker.","src":["Therapeutic Guidelines: Respiratory, Domiciliary oxygen therapy"],"c":["rs23-01","rs23-02","rs23-03","rs23-04","rs23-05"],"id":"rs-k23","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung cancer","nt":"rs-lungca#investigate","s":"Trevor Baines, aged 64 years, is a retired bricklayer who has smoked a pack a day since he was 18. For six weeks his usual morning cough has been more frequent and 'sounds different', and his voice has become hoarse. He has lost 3 kg without trying. He has had no fever and no haemoptysis. He says he did not come sooner because he expected 'a lecture about the smokes'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Perindopril 5 mg daily","Atorvastatin 20 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 96% on room air","Chest clear","No clubbing, no palpable lymph nodes"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange an urgent chest X-ray, with the result within a week","Arrange spirometry","Enrol him in the National Lung Cancer Screening Program for a low-dose CT","Prescribe a 7-day course of doxycycline and review if the cough has not settled at the end of the course","Stop perindopril and review in four weeks"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. A changed cough, hoarseness and weight loss lasting more than 3 weeks in a smoker need investigation for lung cancer, starting with an urgent chest X-ray reported within a week.","Spirometry does not investigate these symptoms.","Screening is for people without symptoms. He has symptoms, so he is investigated.","He has no features of infection, and a trial of antibiotics delays diagnosis.","An ACE inhibitor cough does not explain hoarseness and weight loss."],"e":"Problem: a changed cough with hoarseness and weight loss in a long-term smoker who feared being judged. Task: investigate now, and without the lecture. What if the X-ray is normal? Up to a quarter of lung cancers are missed on X-ray, so CT and referral follow if suspicion remains.","k":"Unexplained cough, hoarseness or weight loss over 3 weeks in a smoker: urgent chest X-ray.","src":["Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer: a guide for all health professionals (2020)"],"c":["rs24-02","rs24-03","rs24-04","rs24-05","rs24-06"],"id":"rs-a134","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung cancer","nt":"rs-lungca#investigate","s":"Maureen Fitzgerald, aged 68 years, smoked 20 cigarettes a day for 40 years and stopped last year. She has had a dull ache in her right shoulder and upper chest for two months, increasing breathlessness on stairs, and on three mornings this fortnight has coughed up small streaks of blood. Her appetite is poor. A chest X-ray performed four days ago has been reported as normal.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Tiotropium 18 micrograms daily","Paracetamol 1 g as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 95% on room air","Reduced breath sounds throughout, no focal signs","A firm 1 cm lymph node above the right clavicle"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange an urgent CT of the chest and refer to a specialist linked to a lung cancer multidisciplinary team","Prescribe amoxicillin with clavulanate and repeat the chest X-ray in six weeks to confirm the lungs remain clear","Reassure her that the normal X-ray excludes lung cancer","Refer for a shoulder ultrasound","Repeat the chest X-ray in three months"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. She has several symptoms, a supraclavicular node and a heavy smoking history. A chest X-ray can be falsely negative in up to 25% of cases. CT is arranged urgently, with referral at the same time and a first appointment within 2 weeks.","The repeat-film rule applies to consolidation, which she does not have.","A normal X-ray does not exclude lung cancer.","Shoulder pain with haemoptysis and a neck node is not a shoulder problem.","Waiting three months is an avoidable delay."],"e":"Problem: persistent haemoptysis, shoulder pain and a supraclavicular node with a normal X-ray. Task: trust the clinical picture over the film. What would send her to the emergency department today? Massive haemoptysis or stridor.","k":"High suspicion of lung cancer with a normal chest X-ray: urgent CT and referral within 2 weeks.","src":["Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer: a guide for all health professionals (2020)"],"c":["rs24-02","rs24-03","rs24-04","rs24-05","rs24-06"],"id":"rs-a135","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung cancer","nt":"rs-lungca#eligible","s":"Gary Whitlam, aged 57 years, is a long-haul truck driver who attends for a script. He smoked 30 cigarettes a day from the age of 17 and stopped four years ago after a friend's heart attack. He has no cough, breathlessness, chest pain or weight loss, and has had no chest infections this year. He saw an advertisement about 'the new lung scan' and asks whether it applies to him. He has never had a CT of his chest.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Rosuvastatin 10 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 98% on room air, chest clear","Body mass index 31 kg/m2"]}],"q":"Which statement about his eligibility for the National Lung Cancer Screening Program is MOST accurate?","o":["He is eligible, and can be offered a low-dose CT every 2 years","He is not eligible, because he has no symptoms and screening requires at least one respiratory symptom lasting three weeks","He is not eligible, because he no longer smokes","He is not eligible, because the program starts at the age of 60","He will become eligible only if spirometry shows airflow obstruction"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. He is between 50 and 70, has no symptoms, quit within the last 10 years, and has about 54 pack-years (1.5 packs a day for 36 years).","The reverse is true. Screening is for people without symptoms.","Former smokers are eligible if they quit within the last 10 years.","The age range is 50 to 70 years.","Spirometry is not part of eligibility."],"e":"Problem: a well ex-smoker asking about screening. Task: apply the four criteria and calculate pack-years. What happens next? A shared decision about benefits and harms, enrolment in the National Cancer Screening Register, and a program request form.","k":"Lung screening: age 50 to 70, no symptoms, 30 pack-years, smoking now or quit within 10 years.","src":["National Lung Cancer Screening Program, general practitioner resource guide (2025)"],"c":["rs24-12","rs24-13","rs24-14","rs24-15","rs24-16"],"id":"rs-a136","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung cancer","nt":"rs-lungca#process","s":"Christine Paull, aged 61 years, smokes 15 cigarettes a day and has 40 pack-years. She had her first low-dose CT through the National Lung Cancer Screening Program two weeks ago. She feels well. The report describes a nodule in the left upper lobe, classifies the result as moderate risk, and recommends an interval scan. It also notes mild emphysema. She is anxious and asks what it means.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Sertraline 50 mg daily"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["SpO2 97% on room air, chest clear"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a low-dose CT in 3 months, as the report recommends","Arrange a PET scan","Reassure her and return her to screening in 2 years","Refer immediately to a thoracic surgeon for resection, because a moderate-risk nodule in a current smoker is usually malignant","Repeat the low-dose CT in 12 months"],"a":0,"x":["Correct. A moderate-risk result is followed by a 3-month interval scan. Most nodules are not cancer. The visit is also a chance to offer help to quit and to act on the emphysema.","Further imaging beyond the interval scan is a specialist decision for higher-risk results.","Two-yearly screening applies to a very low risk result.","Referral is for high or very high risk results. Most nodules found on screening are not cancer.","Twelve months is the interval for a low-risk result."],"e":"Problem: a moderate-risk nodule on a first screening scan. Task: know the follow-up interval and explain it calmly. What about the emphysema? It is an additional finding to assess on its merits, and another reason to offer help to quit.","k":"Screening CT, moderate risk: repeat low-dose CT in 3 months. High risk: refer.","src":["National Lung Cancer Screening Program, general practitioner resource guide (2025)"],"c":["rs24-20","rs24-21","rs24-22","rs24-23","rs24-24"],"id":"rs-a137","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"akt","l":"Lung cancer","nt":"rs-lungca#investigate","s":"Stanley Mokbel, aged 66 years, has smoked since his teens. He was treated nine days ago for a right lower lobe pneumonia confirmed on chest X-ray, and has finished his antibiotics. His fever has gone and he feels 'about 80 percent'. He still has a cough. He asks whether he needs to do anything else or can 'just get on with it'.","b":[{"h":"Medications","i":["Amoxicillin, course completed","Salbutamol as needed"]},{"h":"Examination findings","i":["Afebrile, SpO2 96% on room air","A few crackles at the right base"]}],"q":"What is the MOST appropriate next step?","o":["Arrange a CT of the chest today","Arrange a repeat chest X-ray within 6 weeks of the first to confirm that the consolidation has cleared","Prescribe a second course of antibiotics","Reassure him that no follow-up is needed as he is improving","Refer him to the National Lung Cancer Screening Program for a low-dose CT in place of any further chest X-ray"],"a":1,"x":["He is improving, and the consolidation fitted the clinical picture. CT follows if it does not clear.","Correct. When consolidation fits the clinical picture it is treated, and the X-ray is repeated within 6 weeks to confirm resolution. Consolidation or symptoms that persist need referral.","He is recovering as expected.","A cancer can sit behind a pneumonia, and he is a long-term smoker.","Screening is for people without symptoms, and is deferred for 12 weeks after a chest infection."],"e":"Problem: pneumonia in an older smoker who is getting better. Task: make sure the film clears. What if it has not cleared at six weeks? CT and referral to a specialist linked to a lung cancer team.","k":"Consolidation in a person at risk of lung cancer: repeat the chest X-ray within 6 weeks.","src":["Cancer Australia, Investigating symptoms of lung cancer: a guide for all health professionals (2020)","National Lung Cancer Screening Program, general practitioner resource guide (2025)"],"c":["rs24-02","rs24-03","rs24-04","rs24-05","rs24-06"],"id":"rs-a138","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"},{"y":"kfp","l":"Lung cancer","nt":"rs-lungca#eligible","n":3,"s":"Several patients ask you about the National Lung Cancer Screening Program. All are well today unless stated.","q":"Which THREE of the following patients can be offered a screening low-dose CT now? Select three.","o":["A 52-year-old woman who smokes 25 cigarettes a day and has done so for 30 years","A 58-year-old man with 40 pack-years who quit 6 years ago","A 60-year-old man with 35 pack-years who quit 15 years ago","A 63-year-old woman with 45 pack-years who has coughed up blood twice this month","A 66-year-old man with 50 pack-years who had pneumonia 5 weeks ago","A 69-year-old woman who smokes a pack a day and has done so for 45 years","A 74-year-old man with 60 pack-years who still smokes"],"sc":[1,1,0,0,0,1,0],"x":["Scores 1. She is 50 to 70, smokes now and has about 37 pack-years.","Scores 1. He quit within the last 10 years and has at least 30 pack-years.","Scores 0. He quit more than 10 years ago.","Scores 0. Haemoptysis is a symptom. She needs investigation, not screening.","Scores 0. The scan is deferred for 12 weeks after a symptomatic chest infection.","Scores 1. She is within the age range, smokes now and has 45 pack-years.","Scores 0. He is over 70."],"e":"Eligibility needs all four: age 50 to 70, no symptoms, 30 pack-years or more, and smoking now or quit within 10 years. The scan is deferred after a recent chest infection or chest CT.","k":"Lung screening: 50 to 70, symptom-free, 30 pack-years, current or quit within 10 years.","src":["National Lung Cancer Screening Program, general practitioner resource guide (2025)"],"c":["rs24-12","rs24-13","rs24-14","rs24-15","rs24-16"],"id":"rs-k24","t":"Respiratory health","rev":"v4 2026-11-15"}]});
